/* The heavy half of the bio cards — GENERATED, never hand-edited.
 *
 * abstract / sources / why / quote / wiki / image / places, for the cards whose ids begin `bio-`. None of it is
 * read until a reader REVEALS a card in this collection, so it is fetched then (bundle
 * `cardExtra:bio`) rather than downloaded by every visitor before they can flip one.
 *
 * IT STAGES ONTO A QUEUE rather than assigning, for the reason glossary-extra.js does: app.js
 * snapshots PRISTINE_CARDS at boot, which is BEFORE this file lands, so a plain assignment would
 * leave the editor's revert baseline empty and Revert would DELETE a shipped abstract rather than
 * restore it. cardExtraIngest drains the queue and re-seeds that baseline.
 *
 * An id data.js does not carry is not resurrected — the light half decides what a card is. */
(function () {
  var CARD_EXTRA = {
    "bio-001": {"questions":["Order, response, reproduction, growth, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution are the eight properties textbooks list to mark out what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> takes as its subject matter.","About 86% of species were still undescribed on one 2011 estimate, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a science whose own subject matter has mostly not yet been catalogued."],"abstract":"<b>Biology</b> is the study of life, and its scope is unusually wide even among the sciences, reaching from a single molecule inside one cell to whole ecosystems and the living planet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No one worker covers that range, so it is divided into fields defined by the scale or the group they work on, among them cell biology, physiology, botany, zoology, microbiology, ecology and palaeontology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What they all study is picked out less by a definition than by a list of shared properties: order, response to the environment, reproduction, growth and development, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Biological_evolution\">evolution</span>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those properties appear along a hierarchy running from atoms and molecules through organelles, cells, tissues, organs and organisms to populations, communities, ecosystems and the biosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each level is assembled from the one below, so a question asked at one level rarely has its answer there and a physiologist and an ecologist studying the same animal may be asking unrelated questions. <br><br> What holds so scattered a subject together is heredity, which separates a living process from the growth of a crystal or the burning of a candle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Every cell yet examined stores its hereditary information as double-stranded DNA built from the same four units, transcribes it into RNA and translates that into protein by the same machinery, and reads very nearly the same genetic code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That uniformity is inherited rather than coincidental, and the pattern of resemblances lets biologists reconstruct a family tree of living species with three great branches, the bacteria, the archaea and the eukaryotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Common descent is therefore the frame the rest of the subject is written inside, and why a result obtained in yeast or a fruit fly bears on human medicine at all. It is also why the work is nowhere near done: one widely cited 2011 estimate put the number of eukaryote species at about 8.7 million against some 1.2 million then catalogued, leaving roughly 86% undescribed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Diversity of Genomes and the Tree of Life,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26866/. [Open access]","Camilo Mora, Derek P. Tittensor, Sina Adl, Alastair G. B. Simpson, and Boris Worm, “How Many Species Are There on Earth and in the Ocean?” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 9, no. 8 (2011): e1001127, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001127. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/PhylogeneticTree.png/960px-PhylogeneticTree.png","title":"A phylogenetic tree of living things, from ribosomal RNA","desc":"The three domains of life drawn from ribosomal RNA sequence comparisons: bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes, all descending from a single trunk. Redrawn from a NASA Astrobiology Institute figure.","credit":"MPF, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PhylogeneticTree.png","alt":"A branching diagram on a white ground with three coloured groups of branches rising from one black trunk, labelled Bacteria in purple on the left, Archaea in red in the middle and Eucarya in dark red on the right, each branch tipped with the name of a group such as Cyanobacteria, Halophiles, Animalia, Fungi and Plantae"},"why":[{"q":"Why is a subject this scattered treated as one science?","a":"Because heredity holds it together: every cell yet examined stores its information as double-stranded DNA of the same four units and reads very nearly the same genetic code, and that uniformity is inherited rather than coincidental."},{"q":"Why does a result obtained in yeast bear on human medicine?","a":"Because common descent is the frame the subject is written inside — the pattern of shared machinery lets biologists reconstruct one family tree, so a mechanism found in one branch is likely the same mechanism in another."},{"q":"Why is the work nowhere near finished?","a":"Because a widely cited 2011 estimate put eukaryote species at about 8.7 million against some 1.2 million then catalogued, leaving roughly 86% of them undescribed."}],"wiki":"Biology"},
    "bio-002": {"questions":["Textbooks introduce <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a list of eight properties rather than a definition, among them order, response to the environment, reproduction, growth, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution.","A committee working for NASA settled on a self-sustaining chemical system capable of Darwinian evolution as a working definition of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, chosen because it is the requirement fire and growing crystals fail."],"abstract":"<b>Life</b> has no definition biologists agree on, so textbooks introduce it with a list of properties instead: order, response to the environment, reproduction, growth and development, regulation, energy processing, adaptation and evolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each item does real work: a living thing is highly organised, holds its internal conditions steady, takes in energy and passes on what it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The trouble is that no item settles the matter by itself, since fire consumes fuel, gives off waste, moves and grows, and a crystal in solution grows and hands its handedness on to those that form from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Definitions built as checklists inherit that trouble, since a list requiring every property at once will not admit a single rabbit, which neither reproduces on its own nor evolves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Behind all of it lies a harder problem: with only one example to generalise from, which features of life on Earth are necessary and which are accidents of its history cannot be told apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The most used working definition calls life a self-sustaining chemical system capable of Darwinian evolution, which fire fails because nothing it produces inherits from it and the crystal because its defects are not passed on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a working definition rather than a settled one, framed so it can be applied without knowing which of the familiar properties are universal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In practice most definitions rest on the cell, the minimal self-reproducing unit and the vehicle by which genetic information is handed on in every living species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why viruses are the standing hard case: they reproduce, evolve and carry genes, but a virion has no metabolism of its own, and whether they are alive is a decision about the definition rather than a discovery about viruses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The list is therefore not a failure of rigour but an honest report of where the subject stands, and biology proceeds without settling it, because what makes one organism work is answerable where the general question is not.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Steven A. Benner, “Defining Life,” <i>Astrobiology</i> 10, no. 10 (2010): 1021–30, https://doi.org/10.1089/ast.2010.0524. [Open access]","Chris P. McKay, “What Is Life—and How Do We Search for It in Other Worlds?” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 2, no. 9 (2004): e302, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0020302. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Patrick Forterre, “Defining Life: The Virus Viewpoint,” <i>Origins of Life and Evolution of Biospheres</i> 40, no. 2 (2010): 151–60, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11084-010-9194-1. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “22.1 Prokaryotic Diversity,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-1-prokaryotic-diversity. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “4.4 Gram-Positive Bacteria,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/4-4-gram-positive-bacteria. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Onion_Root_Tip_Cells_Mitosis.jpg/1920px-Onion_Root_Tip_Cells_Mitosis.jpg","title":"Onion root tip cells under a phase-contrast microscope","desc":"Cells from the growing tip of an onion root, with blue arrows marking two of them caught dividing","credit":"Photograph by Catfaster, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Onion_Root_Tip_Cells_Mitosis.jpg","alt":"A pale blue-grey micrograph of about a dozen box-shaped plant cells packed together, their walls picked out as bright lines, with two blue arrows labelled A and B pointing at cells whose interiors are divided or filled with fibrous strands"},"why":[{"q":"How did life begin?","a":"Nobody knows for certain. Earth is about 4.54 billion years old, and fossil microbial mats show cells by about 3.5 billion years ago, so something simpler that could copy itself probably came first; the earliest cells were prokaryotes, likely sheltered in the deep ocean or underground.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it so hard to define the term ‘life’?","a":"Every single property also turns up in things nobody calls alive, from fire to a growing crystal, and with only Earth’s life to study, no one can tell which of its features any life must have and which are accidents of history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What is the smallest living thing?","a":"If life means a cell, the smallest are bacteria of the genus <i>Mycoplasma</i>, some about 0.2 micrometres across, smaller than some large viruses; viruses are tinier still, but a virion has no metabolism of its own, so whether it counts depends on the definition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Life"},
    "bio-003": {"questions":["Because each works at a different level of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a biochemist, a cytologist, a physiologist and an ecologist can study the same animal and never once overlap.","Philosophers of biology point out that no single ladder of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fits every organism, so the textbook hierarchy is best read as a teaching scaffold rather than a law."],"abstract":"<b>Biological organisation</b> is the nesting of living matter into levels, each composed of the one below, from atoms and molecules through organelles, cells, tissues, organs and organ systems to organisms, populations, communities, ecosystems and the biosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The lower rungs are chemistry: atoms bond into molecules, molecules into the macromolecules and organelles a cell is built from, and a human red blood cell is about eight micrometres across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Above the cell the definitions turn functional: a tissue is a group of similar cells doing one job, an organ a structure of two or more tissues, and an organ system a set of organs meeting one need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The organism is the level at which the assembly can independently perform every function life requires, and where the ladder stops describing a body and starts describing a world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Above it the units are ecological: a population is one species interbreeding in one place, a community the species sharing it and their interactions, and an ecosystem the non-living surroundings as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The Convention on Biological Diversity defines the last of those as a dynamic complex of plant, animal and micro-organism communities and their non-living environment interacting as a functional unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The ladder's use is organising the subject, since each level has its own methods and questions, and a biochemist and an ecologist can work on one animal without ever meeting. It organises explanation too, though it usually works in the background as an implicit framework rather than an explicit theory, and general definitions of it have not met wide acceptance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The uniformity of composition a single ladder assumes does not hold across all of biology, which is the standing objection, and recent work treats levels contextually instead, case by case rather than as one layer cake.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> None of that makes the hierarchy useless: it is how the subject is taught and how a question is placed, but it maps the science rather than a structure discovered in the world.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “1.2 Structural Organization of the Human Body,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-2-structural-organization-of-the-human-body. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “44.1 The Scope of Ecology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/44-1-the-scope-of-ecology. [Open access]","United Nations, <i>Convention on Biological Diversity</i>, Rio de Janeiro, 5 June 1992, article 2, https://www.cbd.int/convention/articles/?a=cbd-02. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Daniel Stephen Brooks, “Levels of Organization in Biology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Summer 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2024/entries/levels-org-biology/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the definitions change character above the level of the cell?","a":"Because the lower rungs are chemistry — atoms into molecules into organelles — while a tissue, an organ and an organ system are defined by the job they do rather than by what they are made of."},{"q":"Why is the ladder useful even to workers who never use it?","a":"Because each level has its own methods and questions, so it places a question and organises how the subject is taught, letting a biochemist and an ecologist work on one animal without ever meeting."},{"q":"Why is the hierarchy said to map the science rather than the world?","a":"Because the uniformity of composition a single ladder assumes does not hold across all of biology, and recent work treats levels contextually, case by case, rather than as one layer cake."}],"wiki":"Biological organisation"},
    "bio-004": {"questions":["Everything alive is either one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or an assembly of them, and every one of them comes from a pre-existing one rather than forming out of nothing.","Enclosed by a membrane that separates its chemistry from the outside, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the smallest thing that can grow, respond and reproduce on its own."],"abstract":"The <b>cell</b> is the smallest unit that is alive, and the three claims making that precise are that every living thing is made of cells, that the cell is biology’s basic unit, and that new cells arise only from existing ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What makes one a unit rather than a patch of chemistry is its plasma membrane, a bilayer of phospholipids and proteins that provides the functional separation between inside and outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Everything needed to keep going and to copy itself sits within that boundary, which is why it is the minimal self-reproducing unit and the vehicle by which genetic information passes on in every living species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The requirement is not a light one: the bacterium with the smallest known genome is a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Parasitism\">parasite</span> handed many of its small molecules ready-made, and it still carries 477 genes, with the floor for a viable cell probably 200 to 300.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cells are correspondingly small, an <i>Escherichia coli</i> measuring about one micrometre across and two long, so a body is not one thing but an enormous number of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> One recent estimate puts a 70 kg reference adult at about 30 trillion human cells, alongside roughly 38 trillion bacterial ones living in and on the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That second figure retires a much-repeated claim that bacteria outnumber human cells ten to one, since the ratio turns out to be close to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every organism yet examined is cellular, across the bacteria, the archaea and the eukaryotes, and the resemblance of their machinery is why all three are taken to descend from one ancestral cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A multicellular body is an assembly of that same unit rather than a different kind of thing, its cells specialised for different jobs but each of them still a cell. The unit is therefore where biology’s explanations bottom out: below it there is chemistry and above it there are assemblies, but the cell is the smallest thing that does what living things do.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Origin and Evolution of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9841/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Ron Sender, Shai Fuchs, and Ron Milo, “Revised Estimates for the Number of Human and Bacteria Cells in the Body,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 14, no. 8 (2016): e1002533, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002533. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Diversity of Genomes and the Tree of Life,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26866/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Ecoli_dividing.jpg/1920px-Ecoli_dividing.jpg","title":"A dividing Escherichia coli, magnified 21,674 times","desc":"A scanning electron micrograph of one Escherichia coli cell part-way through dividing in two, with a scale bar of one micrometre.","credit":"CDC Public Health Image Library, image 7137, by Evangeline Sowers and Janice Haney Carr. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecoli_dividing.jpg","alt":"A grey scanning electron micrograph in which a single pale rod-shaped bacterium lies against a pitted background, pinched at its middle into two rounded halves still joined, with a one-micrometre scale bar and the microscope settings printed along the bottom"},"why":[{"q":"Why is a cell a unit rather than a patch of chemistry?","a":"Because its plasma membrane, a bilayer of phospholipids and proteins, provides the functional separation between inside and outside, and everything needed to keep going and copy itself sits within that boundary."},{"q":"Why is being the minimal self-reproducing unit not a light requirement?","a":"Because the bacterium with the smallest known genome is a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Parasitism\">parasite</span> handed many small molecules ready-made and still carries 477 genes, with the floor for a viable cell probably 200 to 300."},{"q":"Why does the ten-to-one bacteria claim no longer stand?","a":"Because one recent estimate puts a 70 kg reference adult at about 30 trillion human cells against roughly 38 trillion bacterial ones, which is a ratio close to one."}],"wiki":"Cell (biology)"},
    "bio-005": {"questions":["A sensor, a control centre and an effector are the parts every loop of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needs, and the loop works by reversing the deviation rather than switching on and off.","Insulin released after a meal brings blood glucose back into its normal range, one of the loops of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a body runs continuously and without any conscious control."],"abstract":"<b>Homeostasis</b> is the maintenance of a relatively stable internal environment in the face of a changing outside world and varying internal activity, worked by negative feedback that reduces an error signal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A loop needs a sensor measuring the regulated variable, a mechanism setting its normal range, a detector comparing the two, a controller deciding what to do and effectors that do it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Temperature is the familiar case and it divides the animal kingdom: an ectotherm takes its body temperature from its surroundings, an endotherm holds one from internal sources, and heat moves by radiation, evaporation, convection and conduction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a human that comes to sensors reporting a departure, a control centre comparing it with the normal range, and effectors sweating, shivering or altering blood flow until the departure is reversed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blood glucose runs on the same shape, cells in the pancreas detecting a rise after a meal and releasing insulin that takes glucose out of circulation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Positive feedback does the opposite and is much rarer: in childbirth the stretching of the cervix releases oxytocin, contractions strengthen, the stretching increases, and the loop stops only when the baby is born.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two things commonly taught are wrong: a set point is a range rather than a single value and can be shifted, and the loops are not switches, since control signals are present the whole time and continuously set the output.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A fever is the clearest case of the first, an actively driven rise of 1 to 4 °C (2 to 7 °F) rather than the temperature loop breaking down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the most famous set point has moved, since the 37 °C (98.6 °F) standard rests on measurements taken in Leipzig in 1851, and one analysis of American records finds men born in the 1990s about 0.59 °C (1.1 °F) cooler than men born in the early 19th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word therefore names regulation rather than constancy, and what looks like a body holding still is a body correcting continuously.","sources":["Harold Modell, William Cliff, Joel Michael, Jenny McFarland, Mary Pat Wenderoth, and Ann Wright, “A Physiologist’s View of Homeostasis,” <i>Advances in Physiology Education</i> 39, no. 4 (2015): 259–66, https://doi.org/10.1152/advan.00107.2015. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “33.3 Homeostasis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/33-3-homeostasis. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “1.5 Homeostasis,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-5-homeostasis. [Open access]","Sharon S. Evans, Elizabeth A. Repasky, and Daniel T. Fisher, “Fever and the Thermal Regulation of Immunity: The Immune System Feels the Heat,” <i>Nature Reviews Immunology</i> 15, no. 6 (2015): 335–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/nri3843. [Open access]","Myroslava Protsiv, Catherine Ley, Joanna Lankester, Trevor Hastie, and Julie Parsonnet, “Decreasing Human Body Temperature in the United States since the Industrial Revolution,” <i>eLife</i> 9 (2020): e49555, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.49555. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Chuckwalla_%2854609766149%29.jpg/1920px-Chuckwalla_%2854609766149%29.jpg","title":"A chuckwalla basking on a rock in Joshua Tree National Park","desc":"An ectotherm at work: the lizard takes its body temperature from its surroundings, and flattening itself against sunlit rock is how it raises one. Photo by NPS/Robert Zuniga, public domain.","credit":"Photo by NPS/Robert Zuniga, Joshua Tree National Park. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chuckwalla_%2854609766149%29.jpg","alt":"A dark grey lizard with a pale speckled back lying flattened along the top of a sunlit granite boulder, its head turned towards the camera and a shadowed rock face behind it"},"why":[{"q":"Why does the word name regulation rather than constancy?","a":"Because what looks like a body holding still is a body correcting continuously: negative feedback loops are not switches, since control signals are present the whole time and continuously set the output."},{"q":"Why is a fever not the temperature loop breaking down?","a":"Because a set point is a range rather than a single value and can be shifted, and a fever is an actively driven rise of 1 to 4 °C rather than a failure of control."},{"q":"Why is the 37 °C standard itself unreliable?","a":"Because it rests on measurements taken in Leipzig in 1851, and one analysis of American records finds men born in the 1990s about 0.59 °C cooler than men born in the early 19th century."}],"wiki":"Homeostasis"},
    "bio-006": {"questions":["Enzymes let reactions run at body temperature that would otherwise need far hotter conditions, which is what makes <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> possible inside a cell at all.","A single gut bacterium’s <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has been reconstructed as 2,251 reactions among 1,136 different molecules, which is what a cell runs simply to stay alive."],"abstract":"<b>Metabolism</b> is the whole set of chemical reactions going on inside a living thing, both those that release energy and those that use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It falls into two halves that depend on each other: catabolic pathways break foodstuffs down into smaller molecules, yielding a usable form of energy and some of the building blocks a cell needs, while anabolic pathways spend that energy synthesising the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost none of it would happen unaided, since these reactions would normally proceed only at temperatures far above the one inside a cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Enzymes are what make it possible at body temperature, and a pathway is a series of them, the product of one reaction becoming the starting material for the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those series are linked to one another into a maze of interconnected reactions, which is why metabolism is better pictured as a network than as a line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The size of that network can be counted: a genome-scale reconstruction of <i>Escherichia coli</i> accounts for 1,366 genes, 2,251 reactions and 1,136 different metabolites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In an animal the breakdown runs in three stages, digestion cutting large molecules into their subunits, glycolysis converting each glucose into two pyruvate, and a third stage in the mitochondria yielding most of the cell’s energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of that energy is used where it is released: ATP carries it, serving as a convenient and versatile currency, made by adding a phosphate group to ADP and spent by taking it off again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Metabolism never stops, and the rate it runs at is what a body’s food supplies, but the familiar rule that this rate scales with body mass to the three-quarter power is not a law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Across 358 studies the scaling exponent varies, mostly between 0.5 and 1.0, and the variation is real biological difference rather than noise, which is worth knowing early about any relationship that fits a graph.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.1 Energy and Metabolism,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-1-energy-and-metabolism. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Jeffrey D. Orth, Tom M. Conrad, Jessica Na, Joshua A. Lerman, Hojung Nam, Adam M. Feist, and Bernhard Ø. Palsson, “A Comprehensive Genome-Scale Reconstruction of <i>Escherichia coli</i> Metabolism—2011,” <i>Molecular Systems Biology</i> 7 (2011): 535, https://doi.org/10.1038/msb.2011.65. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “How Cells Obtain Energy from Food,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26882/. [Open access]","Douglas S. Glazier, “Variable Metabolic Scaling Breaks the Law: From ‘Newtonian’ to ‘Darwinian’ Approaches,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 289, no. 1985 (2022): 20221605, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2022.1605. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Carbon_Catabolism.png/1920px-Carbon_Catabolism.png","title":"A map of carbon catabolism","desc":"Glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, the beta-oxidation of fats and the electron transport chain drawn as one connected diagram, with every enzyme named. This is only the energy-releasing half of metabolism, and only for carbon compounds","credit":"Diagram by Mplanine, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carbon_Catabolism.png","alt":"A dense white chart covered in chemical structures joined by hundreds of curved arrows, grouped into labelled blocks headed Carbohydrate Metabolism, Citric Acid Cycle and Lipid Metabolism, with small coloured tags naming the enzyme at each step and a rainbow bar at lower right labelled Electron Transport Chain"},"why":[{"q":"Why would almost none of a cell’s chemistry happen unaided?","a":"Because these reactions would normally proceed only at temperatures far above the one inside a cell, so enzymes are what make them possible at body temperature."},{"q":"Why is metabolism better pictured as a network than a line?","a":"Because pathways are linked to one another into a maze of interconnected reactions — a reconstruction of Escherichia coli accounts for 1,366 genes, 2,251 reactions and 1,136 different metabolites."},{"q":"Why is quarter-power scaling with body mass not a law?","a":"Because across 358 studies the exponent varies, mostly between 0.5 and 1.0, and the variation is real biological difference rather than noise."}],"wiki":"Metabolism"},
    "bio-007": {"questions":["Because no two organisms are alike, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in biology rests on independent replicates rather than on one animal, however striking that animal’s result looks.","Where the subject is a lineage that died out, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> proceeds by predicting what will be found in rocks of a given age and then going to look."],"abstract":"The <b>scientific method</b> is the procedure by which a claim is made answerable to evidence, running from an observation to a question, a hypothesis, a prediction, a test and a report.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Biology uses it in two modes: descriptive science, usually inductive, which observes in order to find what is there, and hypothesis-based science, usually deductive, which starts from a proposed answer and tests it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A test holds everything constant but one thing, comparing an experimental group with a control identical to it except for the manipulation, so that any difference can be laid at the manipulation’s door.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What makes this harder than in chemistry is that no two organisms are alike, so a result rests on replicates, and the replicates have to be independent of one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They often are not: in one journal issue surveyed, 12% of papers had treated repeated measures from the same animal as independent samples, and another 36% were suspected of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Much of biology cannot be manipulated at all, since a lineage’s history is over, and there the test is a prediction about what will be found in rocks of a given age.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The tetrapod-like fish <i>Tiktaalik roseae</i> came from one Late Devonian locality on Ellesmere Island worked over six field seasons, which is what a test looks like when the subject died out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Work in a model organism rests on shared machinery, since every cell stores its information as DNA and expresses it by the same mechanism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It does not always carry across: genomic responses to burns, trauma and infection in mice correlate almost not at all with the human responses they were taken to model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The method is the one every science uses, and what changes is how much of it can be done to the subject; where manipulation is impossible, prediction and independent evidence carry the weight.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Stanley E. Lazic, “The Problem of Pseudoreplication in Neuroscientific Studies: Is It Affecting Your Analysis?” <i>BMC Neuroscience</i> 11 (2010): 5, https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2202-11-5. [Open access]","Thomas A. Stewart, Justin B. Lemberg, Emily J. Hillan, Isaac Magallanes, Edward B. Daeschler, and Neil H. Shubin, “The Axial Skeleton of <i>Tiktaalik roseae</i>,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 15 (2024): e2316106121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2316106121. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Junhee Seok, H. Shaw Warren, Alex G. Cuenca, Michael N. Mindrinos, Henry V. Baker, Weihong Xu, Daniel R. Richards, et al., “Genomic Responses in Mouse Models Poorly Mimic Human Inflammatory Diseases,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 9 (2013): 3507–12, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1222878110. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Tiktaalik_roseae_%28fish_to_amphibian_transitional_fossil%29_%28Fram_Formation%2C_Upper_Devonian%3B_Ellesmere_Island%2C_Arctic_Canada%29_1_%2849755118288%29.jpg/960px-Tiktaalik_roseae_%28fish_to_amphibian_transitional_fossil%29_%28Fram_Formation%2C_Upper_Devonian%3B_Ellesmere_Island%2C_Arctic_Canada%29_1_%2849755118288%29.jpg","title":"A cast of the Tiktaalik roseae specimens, Field Museum","desc":"A cast of two of the Tiktaalik specimens from the Fram Formation of Ellesmere Island, on display at the Field Museum in Chicago; the flat skull is at the left and the scaled body at the right","credit":"Photograph by James St. John, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiktaalik_roseae_%28fish_to_amphibian_transitional_fossil%29_%28Fram_Formation%2C_Upper_Devonian%3B_Ellesmere_Island%2C_Arctic_Canada%29_1_%2849755118288%29.jpg","alt":"A wide fossil slab photographed against a pale grey mount, with a flattened crocodile-like skull at the left and a long body of overlapping diamond scales running to the right, the bone a mottled brown and grey"},"why":[{"q":"Why is testing harder in biology than in chemistry?","a":"Because no two organisms are alike, so a result rests on replicates, and the replicates have to be independent — in one journal issue surveyed, 12% of papers treated repeated measures from one animal as independent samples."},{"q":"Why can a lineage’s history still be tested at all?","a":"Because where manipulation is impossible the test is a prediction about what will be found in rocks of a given age, which is how Tiktaalik roseae was sought over six field seasons on Ellesmere Island."},{"q":"Why does work in a model organism not always carry across?","a":"Because shared machinery is not shared response: genomic responses to burns, trauma and infection in mice correlate almost not at all with the human responses they were taken to model."}],"wiki":"Scientific method"},
    "bio-008": {"questions":["A biologist who finds nothing has not shown there is nothing to find, because failing to reject the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may only mean the data could not tell the possibilities apart.","Stated before the data are collected, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what stops a surprising result being written up afterwards as though it had been predicted from the start."],"abstract":"A hypothesis in biology is a testable proposal about how something works, and it earns its place by yielding a prediction that an experiment could contradict.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <b>null hypothesis</b> is the version a statistical test is applied to: the claim that there is no difference between the groups being compared.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The biologist’s own idea is the alternative, and a result is reported either as rejecting the null, where a difference is too large to be likely by chance, or as failing to reject it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A p-value is the probability of seeing an effect at least this large if there were no true effect, and it is not the probability that the null is true.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That misreading is the commonest one, and its partner is treating a large p-value as evidence for the null, when it usually means only that the data cannot discriminate among competing hypotheses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> In biology the design matters as much as the arithmetic, since a comparison is fair only if animals are assigned at random, the measurer does not know which group is which, and the sample size is set in advance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reporting guidelines for animal experiments exist because those items were routinely missing from published papers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The other failure is to the hypothesis rather than the experiment: of 807 ecologists and evolutionary biologists surveyed, 51% had reported an unexpected finding as though it had been hypothesised from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the same survey 64% had at least once not reported a result because it was not statistically significant, and 42% had collected more data after checking whether it was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of that is fraud, and all of it is why a hypothesis stated before the data are seen, and an experiment built so that it could refute one, are worth more than a striking result.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Jacob Shreffler and Martin R. Huecker, “Hypothesis Testing, P Values, Confidence Intervals, and Significance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557421/. [Open access]","Sander Greenland, Stephen J. Senn, Kenneth J. Rothman, John B. Carlin, Charles Poole, Steven N. Goodman, and Douglas G. Altman, “Statistical Tests, P Values, Confidence Intervals, and Power: A Guide to Misinterpretations,” <i>European Journal of Epidemiology</i> 31, no. 4 (2016): 337–50, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10654-016-0149-3. [Open access]","Nathalie Percie du Sert, Amrita Ahluwalia, Sabina Alam, Marc T. Avey, Monya Baker, et al., “Reporting Animal Research: Explanation and Elaboration for the ARRIVE Guidelines 2.0,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 18, no. 7 (2020): e3000411, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000411. [Open access]","Hannah Fraser, Tim Parker, Shinichi Nakagawa, Ashley Barnett, and Fiona Fidler, “Questionable Research Practices in Ecology and Evolution,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 13, no. 7 (2018): e0200303, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0200303. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a p-value not the probability that the null is true?","a":"Because it is the probability of seeing an effect at least this large if there were no true effect, which is a different quantity, and reading it the other way round is the commonest misreading there is."},{"q":"Why does a large p-value not support the null hypothesis?","a":"Because it usually means only that the data cannot discriminate among competing hypotheses, rather than that no difference exists."},{"q":"Why is a hypothesis stated before the data worth more than a striking result?","a":"Because of 807 ecologists and evolutionary biologists surveyed, 51% had reported an unexpected finding as though it had been hypothesised from the start, and 42% had collected more data after checking significance."}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/One_tailed_critical_value_with_significance_level_alpha.jpg/1920px-One_tailed_critical_value_with_significance_level_alpha.jpg","title":"null hypothesis","desc":"In the generated schematic, the one tailed critical value with its significance level is demonstrated in a standard normal distribution.","credit":"Hz587, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:One_tailed_critical_value_with_significance_level_alpha.jpg","alt":"A bell-shaped probability curve whose right-hand tail is shaded red beyond a dashed vertical line."}},
    "bio-009": {"questions":["Without a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a biologist has a measurement and no comparison, so any change could as easily be the season, the cage or the batch of reagent.","When cases and controls are processed on different days, the processing day tracks the disease, and a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot rescue an experiment whose groups differ in more than one way."],"abstract":"A variable is anything in an experiment that can change, and a <b>control group</b> is a group carrying every feature of the experimental one except the manipulation being tested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what licenses the inference: if the two groups differ afterwards, the difference can be laid at the manipulation rather than at some outside factor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The licence is only as good as the sameness, so a controlled biological experiment has to say how individuals were allocated to groups, who knew which group was which, and how the sample size was arrived at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also has to say what the experimental unit was, since the thing that was allocated is the thing that counts, however many measurements were taken from each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where some difference other than the manipulation tracks the groups themselves, that difference is a confound, and no amount of later analysis will separate it from the result.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> High-throughput biology gives the clearest cases, since measurements made in different batches behave differently for technical reasons unrelated to the biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In one published proteomic test for ovarian cancer the disease turned out to be almost perfectly correlated with the day each sample was processed, and the assay was blocked pending further validation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Blinding is the control on the observer rather than on the material, and its absence is measurable: across paired studies, not recording data blind inflates the mean reported effect size by about 27%.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is also rare, reported in 6.3% of 960 animal behaviour studies,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while a survey of 2,671 in vivo publications found randomisation in 24.8% and a sample size calculation in 0.7%.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this is bureaucracy: the control is the part of an experiment that decides whether its result means anything, and the figures say it is the part most often left out.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Nathalie Percie du Sert, Amrita Ahluwalia, Sabina Alam, Marc T. Avey, Monya Baker, et al., “Reporting Animal Research: Explanation and Elaboration for the ARRIVE Guidelines 2.0,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 18, no. 7 (2020): e3000411, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000411. [Open access]","Jeffrey T. Leek, Robert B. Scharpf, Héctor Corrada Bravo, David Simcha, Benjamin Langmead, W. Evan Johnson, Donald Geman, Keith Baggerly, and Rafael A. Irizarry, “Tackling the Widespread and Critical Impact of Batch Effects in High-Throughput Data,” <i>Nature Reviews Genetics</i> 11, no. 10 (2010): 733–39, https://doi.org/10.1038/nrg2825. [Open access]","Luke Holman, Megan L. Head, Robert Lanfear, and Michael D. Jennions, “Evidence of Experimental Bias in the Life Sciences: Why We Need Blind Data Recording,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 13, no. 7 (2015): e1002190, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002190. [Open access]","Malcolm R. Macleod, Aaron Lawson McLean, Aikaterini Kyriakopoulou, Stylianos Serghiou, Arno de Wilde, et al., “Risk of Bias in Reports of In Vivo Research: A Focus for Improvement,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 13, no. 10 (2015): e1002273, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1002273. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does having a control group license the inference at all?","a":"Because the control carries every feature of the experimental group except the manipulation, so if the two differ afterwards the difference can be laid at the manipulation rather than at some outside factor."},{"q":"Why can a confound not be removed by analysis afterwards?","a":"Because it is a difference that tracks the groups themselves — in one proteomic test for ovarian cancer the disease was almost perfectly correlated with the day each sample was processed, and the assay was blocked."},{"q":"Why is blinding treated as part of the control rather than a nicety?","a":"Because its absence is measurable: across paired studies, not recording data blind inflates the mean reported effect size by about 27%, and it was reported in only 6.3% of 960 animal behaviour studies."}],"wiki":"Treatment and control groups"},
    "bio-010": {"questions":["Bias and variability are the two kinds of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and only the second of them gets smaller as a biologist takes more measurements of the same thing.","Where 47% of herbarium specimens of one plant genus were identified as the wrong species, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies in naming the organism rather than in any instrument."],"abstract":"Biology begins in observation, and every observation carried into a result is a measurement with a <b>measurement error</b> attached to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Error comes in two kinds that behave differently: bias, the difference between the average of repeated measurements and the true value, and variability, the tendency of a process to give slightly different answers each time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bias does not average out with more data, which is why an instrument is calibrated against a reference rather than trusted, while variability is quantified as a standard deviation and shrinks as measurements are repeated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The variability has more than one time scale, since short-term scatter is the precision of the instrument, and day-to-day variability from small changes in conditions and handling often exceeds it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is a fact about biology as much as about instruments, since a body, a cage and a field all change between one morning and the next. <br><br> The measurement a biologist makes first is usually the identity of the thing being measured, and identification is itself error-prone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A review of European herbarium specimens of ivy found an average error rate of 47% in the original species identification, rising to 55% for one species and falling to 23% for the commonest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the field the problem is not misreading but missing, since observed counts usually represent only a subset of the community actually present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Failing to allow for that biases everything derived from the counts, species richness included, and it does so worst where the chance of detecting an animal is lowest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this makes measurement futile; it makes the error part of the result, which is why a figure in biology is reported with a range around it rather than on its own.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","“2.1.1.3. Bias and Accuracy,” in <i>NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods</i> (Gaithersburg, MD: National Institute of Standards and Technology), https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/section1/mpc113.htm. [Open access]","“2.1.1.4. Variability,” in <i>NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods</i> (Gaithersburg, MD: National Institute of Standards and Technology), https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/mpc/section1/mpc114.htm. [Open access]","Marina Coca-de-la-Iglesia, Angélica Gallego-Narbón, Alejandro Alonso, and Virginia Valcárcel, “High Rate of Species Misidentification Reduces the Taxonomic Certainty of European Biodiversity Databases of Ivies (<i>Hedera</i> L.),” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 4876, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-54735-0. [Open access]","Ciar D. Noble, Carlos A. Peres, and James J. Gilroy, “Accounting for Imperfect Detection When Estimating Species-Area Relationships and Beta-Diversity,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 14, no. 7 (2024): e70017, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.70017. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Disperis_nitida_herbarium_sheet.jpg/1920px-Disperis_nitida_herbarium_sheet.jpg","title":"A herbarium sheet, collected in Cameroon in 1975","desc":"A pressed orchid mounted with its collector’s label and a colour reference strip, held at Meise Botanic Garden. The sheet carries the plant and the name somebody gave it, and the name is a measurement like any other. Photograph by","credit":"Meise Botanic Garden, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Disperis_nitida_herbarium_sheet.jpg","alt":"A tall cream sheet of card with a slender dried orchid taped down the middle, a single flower at its top and two leaves half way, a typed collector’s label at the lower left and a printed colour and greyscale calibration strip down the right-hand edge"},"why":[{"q":"Why does bias have to be handled differently from variability?","a":"Because bias does not average out with more data, so an instrument is calibrated against a reference, while variability is quantified as a standard deviation and shrinks as measurements are repeated."},{"q":"Why is day-to-day variability a fact about biology, not just instruments?","a":"Because a body, a cage and a field all change between one morning and the next, and that variability from small changes in conditions and handling often exceeds the instrument’s own precision."},{"q":"Why is the first measurement usually the least trusted one?","a":"Because it is the identity of the thing being measured: a review of European herbarium ivy specimens found an average error rate of 47% in the original species identification."}],"wiki":"Observational error"},
    "bio-011": {"questions":["No radiation can resolve detail much smaller than its own wavelength, which is why a light <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stops at just under 0.2 micrometres however good its lenses are.","Fixing, sectioning and staining are what a specimen undergoes before a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is pointed at it, and each of those steps can alter the structure being looked at."],"abstract":"A typical animal cell is 10 to 20 micrometres across, about a fifth of the smallest particle the naked eye can make out, so a <b>microscope</b> is what puts cells within reach at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A compound light microscope magnifies up to about a thousand times, but contrast is too poor to see much that way, so cells are fixed in alcohol, acetic acid or formaldehyde, sectioned thinly and stained.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What that bought biology was the cell itself: good light microscopes in the early 19th century are what showed plant and animal tissues to be aggregates of individual cells,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and cell theory followed from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It carries a standing caution, since any treatment used for fixing and embedding may alter the structure of the cell or its molecules in undesirable ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The instrument also has a hard floor, because no radiation resolves detail much smaller than its own wavelength, which for visible light gives a limit of just under 0.2 micrometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Electrons have a far shorter wavelength, and an electron microscope reaches about 2 nanometres on biological material, roughly a hundred times better than light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The price is that electrons are scattered by air, so the specimen sits in a vacuum, in which liquid water evaporates and unprotected biomolecules collapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Freezing water fast enough that it solidifies without crystallising lets a molecule keep its shape inside that vacuum, which is what cryo-electron microscopy does and what the 2017 chemistry Nobel Prize was given for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Light was thought finished as a probe below 0.2 micrometres, a limit stated in 1873, until fluorescent labels switched on and off one at a time were used to get underneath it, work recognised by the 2014 chemistry Nobel Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A microscope therefore does not simply show what is there: what it shows is set by the radiation used, by what the specimen had to survive, and by what was labelled.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2017: Press Release,” 4 October 2017, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2017/press-release/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2014: Press Release,” 8 October 2014, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2014/press-release/. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Indian_Muntjac_fibroblast_cells_%2824271618921%29.jpg/960px-Indian_Muntjac_fibroblast_cells_%2824271618921%29.jpg","title":"Cultured muntjac fibroblasts under a fluorescence microscope","desc":"Three dyes and three targets: the nuclei stained blue, the actin filaments green and the mitochondria red, with everything unlabelled left black. The scale bar is 20 micrometres. Photograph by","credit":"ZEISS Microscopy, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Muntjac_fibroblast_cells_%2824271618921%29.jpg","alt":"Against a black ground, four flat sprawling cells glow in three colours: bright green fibres run the length of each cell, an oval blue nucleus sits at the centre of each, and small red specks are scattered through the green, with a red scale bar marked 20 micrometres at the lower right"},"why":[{"q":"Why must cells be fixed and stained rather than simply looked at?","a":"Because a compound light microscope magnifies up to about a thousand times but contrast is too poor to see much that way, so cells are fixed, sectioned thinly and stained."},{"q":"Why does that preparation carry a standing caution?","a":"Because any treatment used for fixing and embedding may alter the structure of the cell or its molecules in undesirable ways, so what is seen has been through something first."},{"q":"Why does an electron microscope need frozen specimens?","a":"Because electrons are scattered by air, so the specimen sits in a vacuum where liquid water evaporates and unprotected biomolecules collapse; freezing water without crystallising lets a molecule keep its shape."}],"wiki":"Microscope"},
    "bio-012": {"questions":["The wavelength of the light and the numerical aperture of the lens together fix the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and no refinement of the glass can get past what those two allow.","A teaching light microscope magnifies about 400 times at a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of 200 nanometres, while an electron microscope magnifies 100,000 times and reaches 50 picometres."],"abstract":"Magnification is the enlargement of an object’s appearance, while <b>resolution</b> is an instrument’s ability to show two adjacent things as two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two come apart because an image can be enlarged as much as anyone likes, by projecting it on a screen, without ever separating two points the optics has already merged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The merging has a physical cause, since light behaves as a wave: a single point seen through a microscope appears as a blurred disc, and two close points give overlapping discs that read as one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The limiting separation at which two objects stay distinct depends on the wavelength of the light and on the numerical aperture of the lens, which measures how wide the instrument opens its eye.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> With violet light and a numerical aperture of 1.4 the floor is just under 0.2 micrometres, a figure reached by microscope makers at the end of the 19th century and rarely matched by factory optics since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In a teaching laboratory a light microscope magnifies about 400 times at a resolution near 200 nanometres,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and oil immersion takes the magnification to about a thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An electron microscope magnifies 100,000 times and resolves about 50 picometres in principle,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> though lens aberrations and specimen damage hold it to a few nanometres on biological material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The wall itself was stated in 1873, as half the wavelength of the light being used, and it stood for more than a century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fluorescent labels switched on and off one at a time were used to get underneath it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while vitrified water let electron microscopy hold molecules in something near their real shape,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> so the ceiling has moved twice in living memory. The distinction is what survives all of it: magnification is cheap and resolution is not, which is why a micrograph is judged by the scale bar on it rather than by the number printed on the objective.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2014: Press Release,” 8 October 2014, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2014/press-release/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2017: Press Release,” 4 October 2017, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2017/press-release/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does more magnification not give a clearer picture?","a":"Because an image can be enlarged as much as anyone likes without ever separating two points the optics has already merged, which is resolution rather than magnification."},{"q":"Why is there a physical floor at all?","a":"Because light behaves as a wave, so a single point appears as a blurred disc and two close points give overlapping discs; with violet light and a numerical aperture of 1.4 that floor is just under 0.2 micrometres."},{"q":"Why is a micrograph judged by its scale bar?","a":"Because magnification is cheap and resolution is not, so the number printed on the objective says nothing about whether two adjacent things are actually shown as two."}]},
    "bio-013": {"questions":["Because a nematode grows into an adult of exactly 959 cells, it serves as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in which a whole course of development can be written down cell by cell.","Findings in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travel only as far as the shared machinery does, which is why mouse responses to burns and trauma turned out to match the human ones hardly at all."],"abstract":"A <b>model organism</b> is a species studied in depth on the understanding that what is found in it will hold, with adjustments, for others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The set is small and each member was picked for convenience: yeast grows almost as fast as a bacterium, <i>Arabidopsis</i> produces thousands of seeds in 8 to 10 weeks, and a fruit fly goes from egg to adult in 9 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The nematode <i>Caenorhabditis elegans</i> was established as a model on those grounds, and every cell division from the fertilised egg to the 959 cells of the adult was then followed under a microscope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That lineage proved invariant, every worm running the same programme, and the genes controlling its cell deaths were found to have counterparts in higher animals including humans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> None of it would work if organisms did not share their machinery: every cell stores its information as DNA and expresses it the same way,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and some cell-cycle genes work interchangeably in yeast and human cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The mouse is the foremost vertebrate model for the same reason, since about 85% of the amino acids in a human protein are typically identical to those in an elephant’s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inference is not guaranteed and can fail badly: genomic responses to burns, trauma and infection in mice correlate almost not at all with the human responses they were taken to model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The second cost is what the choice does to the map, since effort concentrates where the tools already are. In one enzyme database the top eight eukaryotic species account for 37% of all entries, 88% of the species present carry fewer than ten entries each, and of 111 bacterial phyla only 24 appear at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A model organism is therefore a bargain rather than a representative sample: it buys depth in a few species, and the rest of the living world is known in proportion to how easily it keeps on a bench.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Genetic Information in Eucaryotes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26909/. [Open access]","Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2002: Press Release,” 7 October 2002, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2002/press-release/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Junhee Seok, H. Shaw Warren, Alex G. Cuenca, Michael N. Mindrinos, Henry V. Baker, Weihong Xu, Daniel R. Richards, et al., “Genomic Responses in Mouse Models Poorly Mimic Human Inflammatory Diseases,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 110, no. 9 (2013): 3507–12, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1222878110. [Open access]","Chelsea J. Vickers, Dean Fraga, and Wayne M. Patrick, “Quantifying the Taxonomic Bias in Enzymology,” <i>Protein Science</i> 30, no. 4 (2021): 914–21, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.4041. [Open access]"],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Genetic_map_of_drosophila%2C_as_of_1926.png/960px-Genetic_map_of_drosophila%2C_as_of_1926.png","title":"A linkage map of the fruit fly, published in 1926","desc":"The four linkage groups of Drosophila melanogaster set out as four lines, each mutant named and placed at its map distance from the top. This is what made the fly worth keeping: a species whose genetics could be written down.","credit":"Thomas Hunt Morgan, The Theory of the Gene, 1926. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genetic_map_of_drosophila,_as_of_1926.png","alt":"A printed chart of four vertical lines headed with the Roman numerals one to four, each crossed by short tick marks with a number and a mutant name beside it, among them yellow, white, Notch, vermilion, black, purple, vestigial, ebony and eyeless"},"why":[{"q":"Why do the same few species keep being studied?","a":"Because each was picked for convenience: yeast grows almost as fast as a bacterium, Arabidopsis makes thousands of seeds in 8 to 10 weeks, and a fruit fly goes from egg to adult in 9 days."},{"q":"Why should a finding in a worm be expected to hold in a human?","a":"Because organisms share their machinery — every cell stores its information as DNA and expresses it the same way, and some cell-cycle genes work interchangeably in yeast and human cells."},{"q":"Why does the choice of models distort the map of life?","a":"Because effort concentrates where the tools already are: in one enzyme database the top eight eukaryotic species account for 37% of entries, and of 111 bacterial phyla only 24 appear at all."}],"wiki":"Model organism"},
    "bio-014": {"questions":["A sequence from a yeast, a fly, a worm or a bacterium can be lined up against the human one, which is what gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a claim on every other part of the subject.","Biochemistry, biophysics and bioinformatics are hybrid names of recent date, but <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the one that reorganised the older divisions by group and by process."],"abstract":"<b>Molecular biology</b> is the study of living things at the scale of the molecules that carry life out, and it is the field that cuts across every other way of dividing the subject.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Biology divides first by group, so that botany, zoology and microbiology take a slice of the living world each,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> with virology, mycology and entomology taking narrower slices still.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It divides again by process, so that physiology asks how an organism works, genetics how it passes on what it is, developmental biology how it is built from one cell, and ecology how it lives among others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It divides a third time by level, ecology alone running from the single organism through populations and communities to whole ecosystems, and the same ladder holding below the organism as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The fourth division is by what the work is for, which is where medicine, agriculture, conservation, biotechnology and forensic science sit, each drawing on all of the others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> One field can cut across the rest because the machinery is shared, so a result in yeast, a fly, a worm, a plant or a bacterium can be lined up against the human version by sequence similarity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is why the boundaries are administrative rather than natural, and why so many of the newer names are hybrids: biochemistry, biophysics, neurobiology, bioinformatics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The divisions are how a person gets a purchase on the subject rather than how the subject is built, and no working biologist commands more than a corner of it. The reason is arithmetic as much as intellectual, since by 2023 the PubMed database held more than 36 million citations, was adding over 1.5 million a year, and indexed some 5,294 journals for MEDLINE alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A field is therefore also a way of choosing what to read, and the choice is forced: biology now produces more literature than any one reader can hold.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Genetic Information in Eucaryotes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26909/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.1 The Science of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “44.1 The Scope of Ecology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/44-1-the-scope-of-ecology. [Open access]","National Library of Medicine, “MEDLINE PubMed Production Statistics,” accessed 5 September 2026, https://www.nlm.nih.gov/bsd/medline_pubmed_production_stats.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can one field cut across every other division of biology?","a":"Because the machinery is shared, so a result in yeast, a fly, a worm, a plant or a bacterium can be lined up against the human version by sequence similarity."},{"q":"Why are the boundaries between fields called administrative?","a":"Because the divisions are how a person gets a purchase on the subject rather than how the subject is built, which is why so many newer names are hybrids like biochemistry and bioinformatics."},{"q":"Why is choosing a field also a way of choosing what to read?","a":"Because by 2023 PubMed held more than 36 million citations and was adding over 1.5 million a year, so biology now produces more literature than any one reader can hold."}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/DNA_Structure%2BKey%2BLabelled.png/1920px-DNA_Structure%2BKey%2BLabelled.png","title":"molecular biology","desc":"The structure of DNA showing with detail showing the structure of the four bases, adenine , cytosine , guanine and thymine , and the location of the major and minor groove.","credit":"Zephyris, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DNA_Structure%2BKey%2BLabelled.png","alt":"A ball-and-stick model of the DNA double helix with the major and minor grooves marked, and the four bases drawn out separately beside it."},"wiki":"Molecular biology"},
    "bio-015": {"questions":["Oxygen, carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen make up most of a body, and <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the study of what those elements do once they are assembled into a living thing.","An organism can neither create energy nor escape the loss of some as heat, which is the sort of constraint <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exists to trace through a living system."],"abstract":"<b>Biochemistry</b> is the chemistry of living things, and it exists because the boundary between biology and chemistry is a boundary of interest rather than of law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A body is made of the same elements as everything else, four of them accounting for most of it — oxygen at about 65%, carbon 18, hydrogen 10 and nitrogen 3 — and the reactions between them obey the same rules inside a cell as outside one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Physics binds just as tightly: an organism cannot create energy, only take it in and transform it, and every transformation loses some of it as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is why a living thing is a costly arrangement, since it is highly ordered and holds that order only by spending energy continuously and pushing disorder into its surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What biology adds to the chemistry is not an exemption but a catalyst, and reactions that would otherwise need far higher temperatures are made to run at the temperature of a cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The traffic runs the other way too, since physics supplies the instruments that make molecules visible, from the microscope to the imaging of frozen biomolecules that won the 2017 chemistry Nobel Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mathematics supplies the models, and population genetics is the clearest case, an account of how allele frequencies behave that is arithmetic before it is anything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Geology supplies the timescale and the record, and the earth sciences are where the world a species evolved in has to be reconstructed from. None of this makes biology a branch of chemistry, because the questions differ: a chemist can say what a protein does, while a biologist asks why this organism has that protein and not another. The subject is therefore constrained by the sciences beneath it without being reducible to a list of their results, which is why its borders are the busiest part of it.","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “2.1 Atoms, Isotopes, Ions, and Molecules: The Building Blocks,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/2-1-atoms-isotopes-ions-and-molecules-the-building-blocks. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.3 The Laws of Thermodynamics,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-3-the-laws-of-thermodynamics. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2017: Press Release,” 4 October 2017, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2017/press-release/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “19.2 Population Genetics,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-2-population-genetics. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the line between biology and chemistry one of interest only?","a":"Because a body is made of the same elements as everything else, and the reactions between them obey the same rules inside a cell as outside one."},{"q":"Why is being alive an expensive arrangement?","a":"Because an organism is highly ordered and can create no energy of its own, so it holds that order only by spending energy continuously and pushing disorder into its surroundings."},{"q":"Why is biology not simply a branch of chemistry?","a":"Because the questions differ: a chemist can say what a protein does, while a biologist asks why this organism has that protein and not another."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Myoglobin.png/960px-Myoglobin.png","title":"Myoglobin","desc":"The folded chain of myoglobin, the first protein whose three-dimensional structure was solved, with its haem group at the centre.","credit":"→ Aza Toth, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myoglobin.png","alt":"A ribbon diagram of a small protein, coiled into helices around a flat haem group holding an iron atom."},"wiki":"Biochemistry"},
    "bio-016": {"questions":["Each starling in a flock attends to six or seven neighbours and to nothing else, so the shape of the whole flock is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that no bird in it represents.","Philosophers hold that an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must both depend on its base and be somewhat autonomous of it, so an account dropping either half is describing something else."],"abstract":"An <b>emergent property</b> is one a whole has and its parts do not, which in biology is the ordinary case rather than a curiosity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell gathers raw materials from its surroundings and builds a new cell in its own image, and nothing less than a cell can do that, so being alive appears at that level and not below it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Philosophers pair two claims deliberately here, since emergence is meant to join dependence on a base with some autonomy from it, and an account that drops either half stops being about emergence at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They also divide the notion, weak emergence treating the higher-level facts as nothing over and above the base, and strong emergence claiming a greater distinctness between the two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What emergence is not, in either version, is the merely surprising, since being hard to predict is a fact about the predictor rather than about the world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Biology supplies the clearest examples because its hierarchy is built by composition, running from atoms and molecules through cells and tissues to populations, communities and ecosystems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A flock is a case that has been measured rather than asserted, since each starling attends to a fixed number of neighbours, six or seven, rather than to every bird within a set distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> No bird holds the shape of the flock, and the rule each one follows contains no description of it, yet the flock keeps its cohesion as its density changes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same shape of explanation runs downwards, since regulation is a property of a loop rather than of any part of it, and a sensor, a set point and an effector on their own regulate nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Emergence is therefore a reason to work at more than one level rather than a licence to stop explaining, and the useful question is always which properties of a whole its parts already fix.","sources":["Timothy O’Connor, “Emergent Properties,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2021 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2021/entries/properties-emergent/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","M. Ballerini, N. Cabibbo, R. Candelier, A. Cavagna, E. Cisbani, I. Giardina, V. Lecomte, et al., “Interaction Ruling Animal Collective Behavior Depends on Topological Rather than Metric Distance: Evidence from a Field Study,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 4 (2008): 1232–37, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0711437105. [Open access]","Harold Modell, William Cliff, Joel Michael, Jenny McFarland, Mary Pat Wenderoth, and Ann Wright, “A Physiologist’s View of Homeostasis,” <i>Advances in Physiology Education</i> 39, no. 4 (2015): 259–66, https://doi.org/10.1152/advan.00107.2015. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is emergence not the same as being surprising?","a":"Because being hard to predict is a fact about the predictor rather than about the world, whereas emergence pairs dependence on a base with some autonomy from it."},{"q":"Why is a starling flock the example that has been measured?","a":"Because each bird attends to a fixed number of neighbours, six or seven, rather than to every bird within a set distance, and the rule each follows contains no description of the flock’s shape."},{"q":"Why is emergence not a licence to stop explaining?","a":"Because it is a reason to work at more than one level, and the useful question is always which properties of a whole its parts already fix."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/SnowflakesWilsonBentley.jpg/960px-SnowflakesWilsonBentley.jpg","title":"Snowflakes photographed by Wilson Bentley","desc":"Twelve snow crystals, each a six-fold pattern that no single water molecule contains.","credit":"Wilson Bentley, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SnowflakesWilsonBentley.jpg","alt":"Twelve black-and-white photographs of snow crystals, every one six-sided and none the same as another."},"wiki":"Emergence"},
    "bio-017": {"questions":["Wrasses and cichlids keep a similar jaw linkage across very different diets, a many-to-one result that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reached by measuring mechanics rather than by comparing shapes.","Bone responds to the loads placed on it and also to hormones and to age, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> treats internal architecture as evidence rather than as proof."],"abstract":"<b>Functional morphology</b> studies the relationship between the form of a biological structure and what that structure does, and its working question is how far functional demand explains a shape as against inheritance and development.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The tightest case is molecular, since the precise shape of a protein molecule determines its function in the cell, and that shape is specified by the order of amino acids along the chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The evidence there is an experiment rather than an inference, because a protein unfolded by a denaturing solvent will often refold into its original conformation once the solvent is taken away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At the level of a tissue the relationship is looser but still measurable, since bone responds to the mechanical loads placed on it, which is why the internal architecture of a bone is read as a record of how an animal moved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even there the reading is not clean, since bone is also affected by hormones, metabolism and age, so a difference in architecture need not be a difference in loading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Whole organs are studied by measuring performance rather than by inspecting a shape, and the answer is often not the one the anatomy suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In fishes that feed by suction, skeletal levers and linkages transmit muscle force into a three-dimensional expansion of the mouth cavity, but the power for that expansion comes mostly from large regions of the body muscles rather than from the head alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The relationship also runs many to one, since different morphologies can produce similar mechanical properties, so several shapes may do a job equally well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Wrasses and cichlids show that, their four-bar jaw linkages keeping much the same geometry across diets that differ in hardness and in how far the prey can flee.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A structure's use therefore has to be established rather than assumed, and a shape that looks purpose-built is a hypothesis about performance until somebody measures it.","sources":["Alexander Blanke, Helmut Schmitz, Alessandra Patera, Hugo Dutel, and Michael J. Fagan, “Form–Function Relationships in Dragonfly Mandibles under an Evolutionary Perspective,” <i>Journal of the Royal Society Interface</i> 14, no. 128 (2017): 20161038, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2016.1038. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Christine M. Harper and Biren A. Patel, “Functional Morphology of Trabecular Bone in the Calcaneus of African Apes,” <i>Journal of Anatomy</i> 249, no. 4 (2026): 883–98, https://doi.org/10.1111/joa.70141. [Open access]","Ariel L. Camp and Sam Van Wassenbergh, “A Mechanical Perspective on Suction Feeding in Fishes,” <i>Journal of Experimental Biology</i> 228, no. 18 (2025): jeb250567, https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.250567. [Open access]","H. Camarillo, E. D. Burress, and M. M. Muñoz, “Four-Bar Geometry Is Shared among Ecologically Divergent Fish Species,” <i>Integrative Organismal Biology</i> 6, no. 1 (2024): obae019, https://doi.org/10.1093/iob/obae019. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is protein shape the tightest case of form following function?","a":"Because the shape is specified by the order of amino acids, and it can be shown experimentally: a protein unfolded by a denaturing solvent will often refold into its original conformation once the solvent is removed."},{"q":"Why is bone architecture not a clean record of how an animal moved?","a":"Because bone is also affected by hormones, metabolism and age, so a difference in internal architecture need not be a difference in mechanical loading."},{"q":"Why must a structure’s use be measured rather than read off its shape?","a":"Because the relationship runs many to one and the anatomy can mislead: in suction-feeding fishes the power for expanding the mouth comes mostly from the body muscles rather than the head."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Homology_vertebrates-en.svg/1920px-Homology_vertebrates-en.svg.png","title":"Homologous forelimbs","desc":"The same bones, shaded to match, doing four different jobs in a human, a dog, a bird and a whale.","credit":"Волков Владислав Петрович, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Homology_vertebrates-en.svg","alt":"Four forelimb skeletons side by side, with corresponding bones in matching colours: an arm, a foreleg, a wing and a flipper."},"wiki":"Functional morphology"},
    "bio-018": {"questions":["The genes of the translation system are conserved across bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes, which is the usual evidence for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a formal proof of it is thought unlikely.","A single origin for every cellular organism alive is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> asserts, and the ciliates that read stop codons as amino acids are the qualification it has to carry."],"abstract":"<b>Common descent</b> is the claim that all cellular life on Earth has a single origin, and it rests on the universality of the genetic code and on the conservation of the genes that carry out translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The shared machinery is specific rather than vague, since every cell stores its hereditary information in DNA, transcribes portions of it into RNA, and translates that RNA into protein in a way that is identical, with minor exceptions, in all living things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What makes the sharing evidence is that the rules are in part arbitrary, so a code with no chemical necessity behind it is better explained as an accident frozen into the earliest organisms and inherited since than as a solution each lineage found for itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The argument does not rest on the code alone, the genes of the translation system being conserved across the whole of cellular life.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its own advocates state the limit plainly, holding that the comparative-genomic evidence is overwhelming while a formal demonstration is unlikely to be feasible in principle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The word universal has to be qualified, because ciliates reassign stop codons to amino acids, and of 24 species whose codes were inferred from marine transcriptomes, nine matched no code table then on record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One of them, <i>Condylostoma magnum</i>, reads all three standard stop codons as amino acids inside a message and as a stop only close to its far end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Against that unity the diversity is enormous and badly counted, the most cited estimate putting eukaryotic species at about 8.7 million, give or take 1.3 million, with some 1.2 million catalogued so far.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Published estimates run from 2 million to 3 trillion, about 80% of the 8.7 million are hypothetical, and the disagreement cannot be settled without a better count of bacteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Unity and diversity are therefore claims of very different quality, one inferred from machinery every cell carries and the other extrapolated from the small fraction of the planet anyone has yet examined.","sources":["Eugene V. Koonin and Yuri I. Wolf, “The Common Ancestry of Life,” <i>Biology Direct</i> 5 (2010): 64, https://doi.org/10.1186/1745-6150-5-64. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Stephen M. Heaphy, Marco Mariotti, Vadim N. Gladyshev, John F. Atkins, and Pavel V. Baranov, “Novel Ciliate Genetic Code Variants Including the Reassignment of All Three Stop Codons to Sense Codons in <i>Condylostoma magnum</i>,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 33, no. 11 (2016): 2885–89, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msw166. [Open access]","Camilo Mora, Derek P. Tittensor, Sina Adl, Alastair G. B. Simpson, and Boris Worm, “How Many Species Are There on Earth and in the Ocean?,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 9, no. 8 (2011): e1001127, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001127. [Open access]","John J. Wiens, “How Many Species Are There on Earth? Progress and Problems,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 21, no. 11 (2023): e3002388, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3002388. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a shared genetic code taken as evidence of one origin?","a":"Because the rules are in part arbitrary, so a code with no chemical necessity behind it is better explained as an accident frozen into the earliest organisms than as a solution each lineage found separately."},{"q":"Why does the word universal have to be qualified?","a":"Because ciliates reassign stop codons to amino acids, and of 24 species whose codes were inferred from marine transcriptomes, nine matched no code table then on record."},{"q":"Why are the claims of unity and of diversity of different quality?","a":"Because unity is inferred from machinery every cell carries, while the species count is extrapolated from a small fraction of the planet, with published estimates running from 2 million to 3 trillion."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Phylogenetic_Tree_of_Life.png/1920px-Phylogenetic_Tree_of_Life.png","title":"A phylogenetic tree of life","desc":"A tree drawn from gene sequences, with bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes running back to a last universal common ancestor.","credit":"User:Crion, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phylogenetic_Tree_of_Life.png","alt":"A circular branching diagram labelled Bacteria, Archaea and Eukaryota, all converging on a point marked LUCA."},"wiki":"Common descent"},
    "bio-019": {"questions":["Because the exponent linking metabolic rate to body mass turns out to vary systematically, biology's nearest thing to a law is better read as a product of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than of physical necessity.","Philosophers hold that a species is an individual rather than a kind, one reason the subject states so few laws and leans instead on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> to explain what it finds."],"abstract":"<b>Contingency</b> is the dependence of an outcome on the particular sequence of events that produced it, so that the same starting point run again need not arrive anywhere near the same place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philosophers of science once treated the absence of physics-style laws in biology as a shortcoming of the subject and now treat it as a legitimate practice of a different kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> There are hardly any universal laws of the Newtonian sort in biology, and its best-known generalisations, Mendel's among them, are described as having exceptions and as holding only other things being equal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One argument for that is that biology adds nothing to the laws of nature, and shows instead how the laws of physics and chemistry play out under particular initial and boundary conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Another is that a species is an individual rather than a kind, its organisms being parts of it rather than instances of it, so there can be no law about a species in the traditional sense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The nearest thing biology has to a law is the scaling of metabolic rate with body mass, long assumed to follow an exponent of two-thirds or three-quarters because physical constraint required it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hundreds of studies have since documented systematic variation in that exponent, which has turned the subject from a search for a constant into an inquiry into why the value moves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Contingency itself has been measured rather than argued: twelve identical populations of <i>Escherichia coli</i> founded in 1988 went more than 30,000 generations without any of them using the citrate in their medium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One population then did, and replaying its history from frozen samples produced no citrate-users among 8.4 trillion ancestral cells while later clones evolved it readily, so a potentiating mutation had arisen by 20,000 generations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Even the near-universal genetic code is a case of the same thing, its arbitrary features being read as an accident frozen into the earliest cells rather than as a necessity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Zachary D. Blount, Christina Z. Borland, and Richard E. Lenski, “Historical Contingency and the Evolution of a Key Innovation in an Experimental Population of <i>Escherichia coli</i>,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 105, no. 23 (2008): 7899–906, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0803151105. [Open access]","Alexander Reutlinger, Gerhard Schurz, Andreas Hüttemann, and Siegfried Jaag, “Ceteris Paribus Laws,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Spring 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/spr2024/entries/ceteris-paribus/. [Open access]","Jay Odenbaugh and Paul Griffiths, “Philosophy of Biology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2024 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta and Uri Nodelman, https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2024/entries/biology-philosophy/. [Open access]","Douglas S. Glazier, “Variable Metabolic Scaling Breaks the Law: From ‘Newtonian’ to ‘Darwinian’ Approaches,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 289, no. 1985 (2022): 20221605, https://doi.org/10.1098/rspb.2022.1605. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does biology have so few laws of the Newtonian sort?","a":"Because it adds nothing to the laws of nature and shows instead how physics and chemistry play out under particular initial and boundary conditions, so its generalisations hold only other things being equal."},{"q":"Why can there be no law about a species in the traditional sense?","a":"Because a species is an individual rather than a kind, its organisms being parts of it rather than instances of it, so there is nothing for a law to quantify over."},{"q":"Why is the Escherichia coli experiment evidence rather than illustration?","a":"Because replaying the citrate-user’s history from frozen samples produced none among 8.4 trillion ancestral cells while later clones evolved it readily, showing a potentiating mutation had arisen on the way."}],"wiki":"Contingency (evolutionary biology)"},
    "bio-020": {"questions":["Germ-free mice colonised with another animal's microbiota gained fat while eating less, an intervention worth running because an association can always be produced by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> instead.","Genetic variants serve as natural experiments because they are fixed at conception, though shared variation can still produce <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of its own between a risk factor and an outcome."],"abstract":"<b>Confounding</b> is what most often makes a correlation fail as evidence of cause, some third factor moving both of the things compared so that they rise and fall together with neither driving the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other standard failure is reverse causation, where the outcome alters the supposed cause: people with symptoms of heart disease may drink less alcohol, which makes drinking look protective.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One way round both is to use genetic variants as natural experiments, a variant being fixed at conception and so beyond the reach of the disease it is being tested against.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is not a clean escape either, since shared genetic variation can itself influence a supposed risk factor and an outcome at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The older biological answer is a sequence rather than a statistic: establish the association, isolate the suspected agent, then show that the agent alone reproduces the effect in something that did not have it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The gut microbiome is where that sequence is being worked through now, its composition having been associated with obesity and with insulin resistance in large cohorts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One such association did not survive sensitivity analyses using twin and sibling-matched data, which is what residual confounding looks like when somebody catches it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The causal step is an intervention, and germ-free mice colonised with the microbiota of normal animals gained adipose tissue and insulin resistance while eating less chow than the animals they were compared with.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Where the experiment can be run on people it sometimes reverses the expectation, as when beta-carotene given to prevent lung cancer raised the risk instead, at a pooled relative risk of 1.16 across eight trials and 167,141 participants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A correlation is the beginning of a biological question rather than an answer to one, and what settles it is an intervention somebody can actually perform.","sources":["Neil M. Davies, Michael V. Holmes, and George Davey Smith, “Reading Mendelian Randomisation Studies: A Guide, Glossary, and Checklist for Clinicians,” <i>BMJ</i> 362 (2018): k601, https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.k601. [Open access]","Roshni A. Patel, Joshua G. Schraiber, Matt Pennell, and Michael D. Edge, “Observational Epidemiological Studies Can Mitigate Genetic Confounding with Genetic Relatedness Matrices,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 19 (2026): e2533909123, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2533909123. [Open access]","David W. Evans, “How to Gain Evidence for Causation in Disease and Therapeutic Intervention: From Koch's Postulates to Counter-Counterfactuals,” <i>Medicine, Health Care and Philosophy</i> 25, no. 3 (2022): 509–21, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11019-022-10096-x. [Open access]","Connor Prosty, Khaled Katergi, Jesse Papenburg, Alexander Lawandi, Todd C. Lee, et al., “Causal Role of the Gut Microbiome in Certain Human Diseases: A Narrative Review,” <i>eGastroenterology</i> 2, no. 3 (2024): e100086, https://doi.org/10.1136/egastro-2024-100086. [Open access]","Jacek Kordiak, Filip Bielec, Sławomir Jabłoński, and Dorota Pastuszak-Lewandoska, “Role of Beta-Carotene in Lung Cancer Primary Chemoprevention: A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis and Meta-Regression,” <i>Nutrients</i> 14, no. 7 (2022): 1361, https://doi.org/10.3390/nu14071361. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a correlation so often fail as evidence of cause?","a":"Because some third factor may move both things compared, or the outcome may alter the supposed cause — people with symptoms of heart disease drink less, which makes drinking look protective."},{"q":"Why are genetic variants used as natural experiments?","a":"Because a variant is fixed at conception and so beyond the reach of the disease being tested against, though shared genetic variation can still influence a risk factor and an outcome at once."},{"q":"Why does an intervention settle what a cohort cannot?","a":"Because it can reverse the expectation: beta-carotene given to prevent lung cancer raised the risk instead, at a pooled relative risk of 1.16 across eight trials and 167,141 participants."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Comparison_confounder_mediator.svg/1920px-Comparison_confounder_mediator.svg.png","title":"A confounder and a mediator","desc":"The difference a diagram makes: a mediator sits on the path from cause to effect, a confounder causes both and sits on neither.","credit":"Cmglee, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comparison_confounder_mediator.svg","alt":"Two flow diagrams. In the first, exposure causes a mediator which causes the outcome; in the second a confounder causes both exposure and outcome."},"wiki":"Confounding"},
    "bio-021": {"questions":["Four of the six elements of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> account for 96.5% of an organism's weight, a composition quite unlike that of the rock the organism is standing on.","Nitrogen limits plankton growth in the subtropical gyres and iron does so in upwelling regions, so even the elements of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are not equally available everywhere."],"abstract":"<b>CHNOPS</b> is the set of six elements common to all known life — carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur — and their presence is one of the conditions of habitability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Of the 92 naturally occurring elements, organisms are built from only a small selection, four of which, carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen, make up 96.5% of an organism's weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That composition differs markedly from the abundances in the Earth's crust, which is itself evidence of a distinctive chemistry rather than of life simply using whatever lay to hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Carbon supplies the frame, having four electrons and four vacancies in its outer shell, so it forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbon atoms in chains and rings with no obvious upper limit on size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phosphorus shows how specific a job an element can hold: about 0.9 kg (2 pounds) sits in a human body, up to 90% of it as apatite in bones and teeth, the rest largely in the phosphate links of DNA and RNA and in the esters that carry energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The proportions are regular enough at sea to be assumed, and models of the carbon cycle give ocean particles a mean carbon to nitrogen to phosphorus ratio of 106 to 16 to 1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured instead, the ratio shifts from one ocean region to another with how much protein, carbohydrate and lipid the plankton are carrying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Which element runs short first shifts as well, nitrogen limiting growth in the stratified subtropical gyres, iron in upwelling regions, and the two together in the water between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Manganese can be limiting alongside iron in parts of the Southern Ocean, so an element present in traces can still be the one that stops everything.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Deciding which elements are genuinely essential is harder than a list of six suggests, since genes do not code for elements but for particular chemical species, an element in a given oxidation state with particular groups around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Karen Donaldson, Jonah Mack, Yuchen Shang, Ian Underwood, and Charles Cockell, “The Design of a Bioinspired Integrated Total Habitability Instrument for Planetary Exploration,” <i>Biomimetics</i> 10, no. 11 (2025): 742, https://doi.org/10.3390/biomimetics10110742. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Prinessa Chellan and Peter J. Sadler, “The Elements of Life and Medicines,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 373, no. 2037 (2015): 20140182, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2014.0182. [Open access]","Justin D. Liefer, Angelicque E. White, Zoe V. Finkel, Andrew J. Irwin, et al., “Latitudinal Patterns in Ocean C:N:P Reflect Phytoplankton Acclimation and Macromolecular Composition,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 46 (2024): e2404460121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2404460121. [Open access]","Thomas J. Browning and C. Mark Moore, “Global Analysis of Ocean Phytoplankton Nutrient Limitation Reveals High Prevalence of Co-limitation,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 14 (2023): 5014, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-40774-0. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is life’s composition read as evidence of a distinctive chemistry?","a":"Because organisms are built from a small selection of the 92 natural elements, four of them making up 96.5% of an organism’s weight, and that differs markedly from the abundances in the Earth’s crust."},{"q":"Why is carbon the element that supplies the frame?","a":"Because it has four electrons and four vacancies in its outer shell, so it forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbon atoms in chains and rings with no obvious upper limit on size."},{"q":"Why is a list of six elements harder to justify than it looks?","a":"Because genes do not code for elements but for particular chemical species — an element in a given oxidation state with particular groups around it — and an element present only in traces can still be the one that stops everything."}]},
    "bio-022": {"questions":["A cubic micrometre of bacterium or yeast is estimated to hold two to four million proteins, which is the order of how many of one kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a cell carries.","One carbon atom is about 0.2 nm across, and a cluster of such atoms joined by covalent bonds is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose mass is quoted in daltons."],"abstract":"A <b>molecule</b> is a cluster of atoms held together by covalent bonds, and it is where the materials a cell is built from take on their character, which depends on how the atoms are linked rather than on which elements are present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An atom is a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Atomic_nucleus\">nucleus</span> of protons and neutrons surrounded by electrons; the proton count is the atomic number and fixes the chemistry, while the neutrons may vary, giving isotopes that are physically distinguishable and chemically identical.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A carbon atom is roughly 0.2 nm across, so about 5 million of them in a line would span a millimetre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Masses are quoted in daltons, and quantities are counted in <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Mole_(unit)\">moles</span>, one mole being Avogadro's number of units, a figure that is now exact by definition at 6.02214076 × 10<sup>23</sup> per mole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The small organic molecules of a cell have molecular weights between 100 and 1000 and carry up to about 30 carbon atoms, sitting free in solution as subunits for larger molecules, as fuel, or as both at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A cell is water, inorganic ions and carbon-containing molecules, with water the most abundant of the three at 70% or more of the total mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counting the rest is harder than it sounds, and an estimate built from the protein fraction of cell mass and the average length of a protein gives 2 to 4 million protein molecules in a cubic micrometre of bacterium, yeast or mammalian cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured proteome censuses of yeast and mammalian cells often come out 3 to 10 times lower than that, a discrepancy the estimate was published to expose rather than to settle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Numbers of this kind are scattered so widely that finding even a simple one takes a long time, which is why they are gathered into a curated database with each figure's source attached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Counting is the point: a cell is a definite number of definite objects, and the chemistry becomes biology when somebody says how many.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","National Institute of Standards and Technology, “Avogadro Constant,” CODATA Internationally Recommended 2022 Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants, https://physics.nist.gov/cgi-bin/cuu/Value?na. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Ron Milo, “What Is the Total Number of Protein Molecules per Cell Volume? A Call to Rethink Some Published Values,” <i>BioEssays</i> 35, no. 12 (2013): 1050–55, https://doi.org/10.1002/bies.201300066. [Open access]","Ron Milo, Paul Jorgensen, Uri Moran, Griffin Weber, and Michael Springer, “BioNumbers—the Database of Key Numbers in Molecular and Cell Biology,” <i>Nucleic Acids Research</i> 38, suppl. 1 (2010): D750–53, https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkp889. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a molecule’s character depend on more than its elements?","a":"Because it depends on how the atoms are linked by covalent bonds rather than on which elements are present, which is why the same elements build materials of quite different kinds."},{"q":"Why can isotopes be used as labels without changing the chemistry?","a":"Because the proton count fixes an atom’s chemistry while the neutron number may vary, so isotopes are physically distinguishable and chemically identical."},{"q":"Why was an estimate of protein numbers published to be contradicted?","a":"Because measured proteome censuses of yeast and mammalian cells come out 3 to 10 times lower than the estimate built from cell mass, and the point was to expose that discrepancy rather than settle it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/PTCDA_AFM.jpg/500px-PTCDA_AFM.jpg","title":"A molecule imaged by atomic force microscopy","desc":"A single PTCDA molecule on a surface, its ring skeleton resolved by a microscope that feels rather than sees.","credit":"Kota Iwata et al., CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PTCDA_AFM.jpg","alt":"A grey microscope image in which the outline of a flat, many-ringed molecule is visible against a textured background."},"wiki":"Molecule"},
    "bio-023": {"questions":["Streptavidin holds biotin at a dissociation constant near 10 femtomolar without a single covalent link, which shows how far a crowd of weak examples of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can go.","A cell keeps its structures because one kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is about a hundred times stronger than the knocks molecules take from their neighbours."],"abstract":"A <b>chemical bond</b> is an attraction holding atoms together, and its strength is measured by the <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Energy_(biology)\">energy</span> that must be supplied to break it, conventionally quoted in kilocalories per mole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That figure is what decides how the bond behaves inside a cell, since a typical covalent bond is about a hundred times stronger than the thermal knocks molecules take from their neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Covalent bonds are therefore not undone by heat but by particular chemical reactions, which in cells are controlled by highly specific <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Catalysis\">catalysts</span>.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Noncovalent bonds are as a rule much weaker, and that weakness is the point, since a great deal of biology consists of molecules that must associate and come apart again readily.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their strength also depends on where they are: water forms hydrogen bonds with polar molecules and surrounds ions, which is why ionic compounds and polar molecules dissolve in it while covalent bond strengths are unaffected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The weak ones matter because they add up, and large numbers of hydrogen bonds between adjacent regions of a folded protein chain help to hold its three-dimensional shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A crowd of them can be formidable: streptavidin binds the small molecule biotin at a dissociation constant of about 10<sup>−14</sup> M, one of the strongest noncovalent interactions known, with no covalent link involved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In DNA the same arithmetic is predictive rather than descriptive, the stability of a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Nucleic_acid_double_helix\">double helix</span> being calculated from its sequence by adding up the contributions of neighbouring base pairs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One such set of parameters, derived from 108 short <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Nucleic_acid_double_helix\">duplexes</span>, reconciled seven laboratories whose published values had appeared to disagree.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Bond strength is not a chemical footnote but the reason a cell can be stable and quick at once: covalent where nothing should change, weak and numerous where things must.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Ian D. Wadsworth, Komal Sethi, Sara Abu Jalboush, LeAnn Rogers, Thomas Hollis, and Adam R. Hall, “Scalable Production of Monovalent Streptavidin with High Yield and Purity,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 16 (2026): 20814, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-51631-7. [Open access]","John SantaLucia, “A Unified View of Polymer, Dumbbell, and Oligonucleotide DNA Nearest-Neighbor Thermodynamics,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 95, no. 4 (1998): 1460–65, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.95.4.1460. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a covalent bond not undone by heat inside a cell?","a":"Because it is about a hundred times stronger than the thermal knocks molecules take from their neighbours, so it is broken instead by particular reactions under highly specific <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Catalysis\">catalysts</span>."},{"q":"Why is the weakness of a noncovalent bond the point of it?","a":"Because a great deal of biology consists of molecules that must associate and come apart again readily, which a bond as strong as a covalent one would prevent."},{"q":"Why can weak bonds still produce an enormously tight grip?","a":"Because they add up: streptavidin binds biotin at a dissociation constant of about 10−14 M, one of the strongest noncovalent interactions known, with no covalent link involved."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Dihydrogen-phase-3D-balls.png/1920px-Dihydrogen-phase-3D-balls.png","title":"Bonding and antibonding orbitals","desc":"Two hydrogen atoms sharing a pair of electrons, and the same pair in the arrangement that pushes them apart.","credit":"Benjah-bmm27; derivative work: MikeRun, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dihydrogen-phase-3D-balls.png","alt":"Two three-dimensional lobes: in one, a single cloud surrounds both nuclei; in the other, two clouds meet at a plane between them."},"wiki":"Chemical bond"},
    "bio-024": {"questions":["A single bond lets one part of a molecule rotate about it while a double one does not, so the rigidity of a molecule is set by which kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> it carries.","Penicillin works by leaving a permanent chemical adduct on its target, which is to say that the drug forms a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with the enzyme it disables."],"abstract":"A <b>covalent bond</b> is formed when two atoms complete their outer electron shells by sharing a pair of electrons rather than by transferring them from one to the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sharing is often unequal, one atom drawing the pair towards itself, and the result is a polar covalent bond that carries a partial charge at each end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two atoms may share more than one pair, and a double bond is shorter and stronger than a single one and, unlike a single bond, prevents the two parts of the molecule from rotating about it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carbon's four bonds point towards the corners of a regular <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Tetrahedral_molecular_geometry\">tetrahedron</span>, which is where the three-dimensional geometry of organic molecules comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a protein each amino acid is joined to the next by a covalent peptide bond, formed as a molecule of water is lost between them, and the repeating chain of atoms that results is the backbone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Some proteins carry covalent cross-links as well, and the commonest are sulfur-sulfur bonds made as a protein is prepared for export, which act as staples reinforcing a shape the chain has already found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The peptide bond is planar, and quantum-chemical work attributes the tight backbone hydrogen-bond angle of about 155 degrees to the distribution of electrons around that plane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Planarity is an approximation rather than a law, and a neutron structure at 1.2 <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Angstrom\">Å</span> resolution shows the amide protons sitting off the plane, shifted towards the atoms they hydrogen-bond to.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Covalent bonding is also how a large family of drugs works, since penicillin and the other beta-lactams disable their target by forming a covalent adduct with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same property is the risk: a reactive drug can form adducts with proteins it was never aimed at, which is why covalent medicines are watched for side effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Kuan Pern Tan, Khushboo Singh, Anirban Hazra, and M. S. Madhusudhan, “Peptide Bond Planarity Constrains Hydrogen Bond Geometry and Influences Secondary Structure Conformations,” <i>Current Research in Structural Biology</i> 3 (2021): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crstbi.2020.11.002. [Open access]","Yuya Hanazono, Yu Hirano, Kazuki Takeda, Katsuhiro Kusaka, Taro Tamada, and Kunio Miki, “Revisiting the Concept of Peptide Bond Planarity in an Iron-Sulfur Protein by Neutron Structure Analysis,” <i>Science Advances</i> 8, no. 20 (2022): eabn2276, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abn2276. [Open access]","Ghazaleh Jafari and Dustin Duncan, “Covalent Inhibitors in Antimicrobial Drug Development—Beyond β-Lactams,” <i>Molecules</i> 31, no. 12 (2026): 2186, https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules31122186. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a double bond change a molecule’s shape and not just its strength?","a":"Because it is shorter and stronger than a single bond and, unlike a single bond, prevents the two parts of the molecule from rotating about it."},{"q":"Why do organic molecules have the three-dimensional geometry they do?","a":"Because carbon’s four bonds point towards the corners of a regular tetrahedron, which sets the shapes the rest of the chemistry is built out of."},{"q":"Why is the same property that makes a covalent drug work also its risk?","a":"Because penicillin and the other beta-lactams disable their target by forming a covalent adduct with it, and a reactive drug can form adducts with proteins it was never aimed at."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Covalent_bond_hydrogen.svg/1920px-Covalent_bond_hydrogen.svg.png","title":"A covalent bond in hydrogen","desc":"Two hydrogen atoms, each with one electron, forming a shared pair that belongs to both of them.","credit":"Jacek FH, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Covalent_bond_hydrogen.svg","alt":"A diagram showing two hydrogen nuclei, first apart with one electron each, then joined by a shared pair written H:H and H-H."},"wiki":"Covalent bond"},
    "bio-025": {"questions":["Sodium chloride is called a salt rather than a molecule because its crystal is a balanced lattice of ions held by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a cluster of shared electrons.","An ion in water travels with a shell of water molecules around it, which is why an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is far weaker in a cell than in a dry crystal."],"abstract":"An <b>ionic bond</b> forms when one atom transfers electrons to another rather than sharing them, leaving two ions of opposite charge that attract one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A solid held together this way is a <span class=\"ttip\" data-k=\"Crystal\">lattice</span> rather than a molecule, which is why sodium chloride is called a salt: a crystal a millimetre across holds about 2 × 10<sup>19</sup> ions of each kind in a regular array with the charges exactly balanced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water takes such a solid apart, since ionic bonds are greatly weakened in it and each ion leaves surrounded by its own group of water molecules, while covalent bond strengths are untouched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is a fact about water rather than about the salt, water molecules being polar and able to interact with charged ions as well as with other polar molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Inside a protein the same attraction turns up as one of the three kinds of weak bond that fold the chain, each 30 to 300 times weaker than a covalent bond and useful only in numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Because an ion in water carries a shell of water with it, moving one across a membrane means stripping that shell away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ion channels do exactly that, a permeating ion shedding most or all of its water to pass the narrowest part of the channel, which is what makes the channel selective and puts a ceiling on its rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The gradients that result are a cell's electrical state, and in animal cells the membrane potential rests mainly on potassium leak channels and the potassium gradient across the plasma membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Ions also hold larger molecules in shape, magnesium being the clearest case, since it defines much of the structural and catalytic chemistry of RNA and binds either directly or through its own shell of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> An ionic bond is therefore weak, wet and everywhere, which is the opposite of the covalent bond's role in a cell and the reason both are needed.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Ion Channels and the Electrical Properties of Membranes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/. [Open access]","J. A. Cowan, “Understanding the Thermodynamics of Magnesium Binding to RNA Structural Motifs,” <i>Life</i> 14, no. 6 (2024): 765, https://doi.org/10.3390/life14060765. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is an ionic solid called a salt rather than a molecule?","a":"Because it is a lattice: a sodium chloride crystal a millimetre across holds about 2 × 10¹⁹ ions of each kind in a regular array with the charges exactly balanced."},{"q":"Why does water dissolve a salt but leave covalent molecules intact?","a":"Because ionic bonds are greatly weakened in water, each ion leaving surrounded by its own group of water molecules, while covalent bond strengths are untouched — a fact about water rather than about the salt."},{"q":"Why does moving an ion across a membrane have a speed limit?","a":"Because an ion in water carries a shell of water with it, and a permeating ion must shed most or all of that water to pass the narrowest part of a channel, which is also what makes the channel selective."}],"wiki":"Ionic bonding"},
    "bio-026": {"questions":["The alpha helix and the beta sheet form from links between the N-H and C=O groups of a protein's backbone alone, so this kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be made by almost any sequence.","A always pairs with T and G with C because a purine and a pyrimidine of matching width can be joined across the helix by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at each contact."],"abstract":"A <b>hydrogen bond</b> is the weak attraction between a hydrogen atom already bound to an electron-hungry atom and another such atom nearby, as when one of water's hydrogens is drawn towards a neighbouring molecule's oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taken singly they are feeble and are broken by ordinary thermal motion, so each lasts an exceedingly short time and liquid water is a network of them continually breaking and re-forming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The aggregate effect is not small: it is only because of these bonds between its molecules that water is a liquid at room temperature, with a high boiling point and high surface tension, rather than a gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are at their strongest when the three atoms involved lie on a straight line, and water weakens hydrogen bonds between dissolved molecules by offering competing partners of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In proteins the two commonest folding patterns, the alpha helix and the beta sheet, arise from hydrogen bonds between the N-H and C=O groups of the backbone alone, which is why many different amino acid sequences can form them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The geometry is measured rather than assumed: across a large body of solved structures the backbone-to-backbone bonds sit at a tightly restricted angle near 155 degrees, and the donor-acceptor distance varies with how deeply the pair is buried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In DNA the two chains are held to each other by hydrogen bonds between the bases, a bulky two-ring purine always facing a single-ring pyrimidine, with A opposite T and G opposite C.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is what lets the double helix be regular, since every pair is the same width and the sugar-phosphate backbones can run outside the stack.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The arrangement is quantitative as well as neat, a duplex's stability being computed from its sequence by summing neighbouring pairs, which is why one stretch of DNA comes apart before another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A bond that cannot hold anything on its own turns out to hold water together, fold proteins and carry the genetic code, entirely by being made in numbers.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Kuan Pern Tan, Khushboo Singh, Anirban Hazra, and M. S. Madhusudhan, “Peptide Bond Planarity Constrains Hydrogen Bond Geometry and Influences Secondary Structure Conformations,” <i>Current Research in Structural Biology</i> 3 (2021): 1–8, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crstbi.2020.11.002. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Structure and Function of DNA,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26821/. [Open access]","John SantaLucia, “A Unified View of Polymer, Dumbbell, and Oligonucleotide DNA Nearest-Neighbor Thermodynamics,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 95, no. 4 (1998): 1460–65, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.95.4.1460. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is water a liquid at room temperature?","a":"Because of the hydrogen bonds between its molecules, which also give it a high boiling point and high surface tension, even though each individual bond lasts an exceedingly short time."},{"q":"Why can many different amino acid sequences form the same fold?","a":"Because the alpha helix and the beta sheet arise from hydrogen bonds between the N-H and C=O groups of the backbone alone, which every sequence carries."},{"q":"Why does base pairing let the double helix stay regular?","a":"Because a bulky two-ring purine always faces a single-ring pyrimidine, A opposite T and G opposite C, so every pair is the same width and the backbones can run outside the stack."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/3D_model_hydrogen_bonds_in_water.svg/1920px-3D_model_hydrogen_bonds_in_water.svg.png","title":"Hydrogen bonds between water molecules","desc":"One water molecule held by four others, each attraction running from a hydrogen to a neighbouring oxygen.","credit":"Qwerter; translated by Michal Maňas; vectorised by Magasjukur2, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3D_model_hydrogen_bonds_in_water.svg","alt":"A ball-and-stick diagram of five water molecules, with dotted lines from the hydrogens of one to the oxygens of the others."},"wiki":"Hydrogen bond"},
    "bio-027": {"questions":["Nonpolar side chains pack into a protein's interior because they disturb the hydrogen-bonded network of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around them, which is what folds much of the chain.","A tardigrade in anhydrobiosis survives losing almost all its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and the longer it stays in that state the longer it takes to revive."],"abstract":"<b>Water</b> is the most abundant substance in a cell, accounting for about 70% of its weight, and most reactions inside a cell take place in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell is water, inorganic ions and carbon-containing molecules, and a great deal of biological chemistry is simply the dealings between the first of those and the other two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The molecule is polar, its hydrogens carrying a slight positive charge and its oxygen a slight negative one, so it hydrogen-bonds to itself and interacts with ions and other polar molecules, which is why they dissolve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Life began in the ocean, and those conditions left a permanent mark on the chemistry of living things; water is also what pushes nonpolar molecules together, since they disturb its hydrogen-bonded network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A folded protein shows the result directly, its nonpolar side chains packed away in the interior while the polar ones face outward where they can meet water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That exclusion does mechanical work as well as structural, since elastin, the protein that lets an artery or a lung spring back, recoils because of the hydrophobic effect, with the ordering of water at its surface changing as the protein is stretched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The scale of that job is the point: arterial elastin goes through more than two billion stretch-and-release cycles in a human lifetime without ever being replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because so much rests on water, losing it is normally fatal, yet some small animals survive almost complete dehydration without damage, a state called anhydrobiosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among tardigrades that capacity differs between species and appears to follow the habitat rather than the diet, and the longer an animal has spent dried out, the longer it needs to come back to activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Water is therefore not the setting biology happens in but a participant in it, which is why so much of the chemistry ahead is really chemistry in water.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Nour M. Jamhawi, Ronald L. Koder, and Richard J. Wittebort, “Elastin Recoil Is Driven by the Hydrophobic Effect,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 12 (2024): e2304009121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2304009121. [Open access]","Milena Roszkowska, Bartłomiej Gołdyn, Daria Wojciechowska, Zofia Księżkiewicz, et al., “How Long Can Tardigrades Survive in the Anhydrobiotic State? A Search for Tardigrade Anhydrobiosis Patterns,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 18, no. 1 (2023): e0270386, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0270386. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a protein bury its nonpolar side chains?","a":"Because water pushes nonpolar molecules together, since they disturb its hydrogen-bonded network, so the polar side chains face outward where they can meet water and the nonpolar ones pack inside."},{"q":"Why is the hydrophobic effect described as doing mechanical work?","a":"Because elastin recoils because of it, the ordering of water at its surface changing as the protein is stretched — arterial elastin doing that more than two billion times in a lifetime without replacement."},{"q":"Why is water called a participant rather than a setting?","a":"Because life began in the ocean and those conditions left a permanent mark on the chemistry, so much of biological chemistry is the dealings between water and everything else in the cell."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/H2O_2D_labelled.svg/1920px-H2O_2D_labelled.svg.png","title":"The water molecule","desc":"Two hydrogens set at 104.45 degrees about an oxygen, at a bond length of 95.84 picometres.","credit":"Dan Craggs, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H2O_2D_labelled.svg","alt":"A diagram of a water molecule with the angle between its two hydrogen atoms and the length of each bond marked."},"wiki":"Water"},
    "bio-028": {"questions":["A membrane lipid has a water-loving head and water-fearing tails, so half of it behaves as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and half does not, which is why a bilayer assembles itself.","Proteins find their partners by matching patterns of partial charge across a surface, and that pattern exists only because each group in it either is or is not a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> itself."],"abstract":"A <b>polar molecule</b> is one whose charge is spread unevenly, gathered positive towards one end and negative towards the other, and water is the standard case, since its oxygen attracts the shared electrons strongly while its hydrogens attract them weakly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rule is worth carrying about: an O-H or an N-H bond is polar, while a C-H bond is not, carbon and hydrogen attracting the shared pair about equally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Polar covalent bonds create permanent dipoles, so a large molecule carrying many polar groups has a pattern of partial positive and negative charges spread over its surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Two molecules whose patterns are complementary attract one another through those dipoles, by interactions that resemble ionic bonds and are weaker than them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The property sorts molecules into two kinds, since polar ones interact readily with water and dissolve in it while nonpolar ones do not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Membrane lipids are deliberately both at once, each one amphipathic, with a polar head group and two hydrophobic hydrocarbon tails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Put in water they arrange themselves, burying the tails and turning the heads outward, which is why a bilayer forms spontaneously and why about 5 million lipid molecules sit in a single square micrometre of one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Patterns of surface charge are also how proteins find each other, and electrostatic complementarity across an interface has been shown to drive the brief encounters that much of cell signalling depends on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What such complementarity is worth can be measured rather than assumed, and in one enzyme's active site it accounts for at most a modest 300-fold of the catalysis, even though the hydrogen bonds shorten as charge builds up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Polarity is a small asymmetry inside one molecule, and most of what water does to biology follows from it.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Lipid Bilayer,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/. [Open access]","Greta Grassmann, Lorenzo Di Rienzo, Giorgio Gosti, Marco Leonetti, Giancarlo Ruocco, Mattia Miotto, and Edoardo Milanetti, “Electrostatic Complementarity at the Interface Drives Transient Protein-Protein Interactions,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 13 (2023): 10207, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-023-37130-z. [Open access]","Daniel A. Kraut, Paul A. Sigala, Brandon Pybus, Corey W. Liu, Dagmar Ringe, Gregory A. Petsko, and Daniel Herschlag, “Testing Electrostatic Complementarity in Enzyme Catalysis: Hydrogen Bonding in the Ketosteroid Isomerase Oxyanion Hole,” <i>PLoS Biology</i> 4, no. 4 (2006): e99, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.0040099. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is an O-H bond polar where a C-H bond is not?","a":"Because oxygen attracts the shared electrons strongly while carbon and hydrogen attract the shared pair about equally, so no appreciable charge separation arises in the second case."},{"q":"Why does a lipid bilayer form on its own in water?","a":"Because each membrane lipid is amphipathic, with a polar head and two hydrophobic tails, so in water they arrange themselves burying the tails and turning the heads outward."},{"q":"Why is electrostatic complementarity worth measuring rather than assuming?","a":"Because its contribution can be modest: in one enzyme’s active site it accounts for at most a 300-fold of the catalysis, even though the hydrogen bonds shorten as charge builds up."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Water-elpot-transparent-3D-balls.png/960px-Water-elpot-transparent-3D-balls.png","title":"The charge on a water molecule","desc":"The electrostatic surface of water: negative where the oxygen pulls the electrons, positive at the hydrogens it pulls them from.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Water-elpot-transparent-3D-balls.png","alt":"A three-dimensional water molecule wrapped in a coloured surface, red at the oxygen end and blue at the hydrogens."}},
    "bio-029": {"questions":["A water strider stands on a film it cannot break, which exists because the hydrogen bonds between water molecules give the liquid the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that produces surface tension.","Sap in a tree is pulled rather than pushed, so the column depends on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and fails when drought raises the tension enough to admit a gas bubble."],"abstract":"<b>Cohesion</b> is the tendency of a liquid to hold to itself, and in water it comes from the hydrogen bonds between neighbouring molecules, which is why water is a liquid at room temperature with a high boiling point and a high surface tension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Adhesion is the same attraction pointed outwards, water forming hydrogen bonds with other polar molecules and interacting with ions, so it clings to surfaces built from either rather than beading away from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The pair produce capillarity, the rise of a liquid into a narrow space, though the word covers more than that, standing for all the physical consequences of the thin skin that separates water from air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those consequences dominate at small scales, governing the transport of water inside plants, the dispersal of fungal spores, the water-repellency of arthropods and the fate of bacteria and viruses in respiratory drops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A water strider lives on that film, its legs and its turning gait built around a surface it can push against without breaking.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Cohesion is also what allows a tree to lift water, since sap in the xylem is pulled rather than pushed and travels under tension.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A column held that way can fail, and under drought the rising tension lets gas emboli form and spread through the conduits, blocking them and in the worst case killing the tissue they feed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How much tension a species tolerates is measured as the water potential at which half its conduits have embolised, and across 22 eucalypts that resistance trades off against how fast the plant grows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The faster growers had wider vessels, better at conducting and worse at holding on, while the slower ones had narrower vessels with thicker walls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Two ordinary stickinesses therefore set both what small animals can walk on and how tall a tree can afford to be.","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","David L. Hu, Ho-Young Kim, and Dominic Vella, “Editorial: Capillarity and Elastocapillarity in Biology,” <i>Interface Focus</i> 15, no. 2 (2025): 20250020, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsfs.2025.0020. [Open access]","Javad Meshkani, Hamed Rajabi, Alexander Kovalev, and Stanislav N. Gorb, “Kinematics and Directionality of Body Turning in Water Striders,” <i>Insect Science</i> 33, no. 1 (2026): 298–312, https://doi.org/10.1111/1744-7917.13486. [Open access]","Tiantian Pan, Vinod Jacob, Travis G. Britton, Julian Schrader, et al., “Evidence for a Trade-off between Growth Rate and Xylem Embolism Resistance in 22 <i>Eucalyptus</i> Species,” <i>New Phytologist</i> 251, no. 5 (2026): 2407–26, https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.71345. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a tree lift water hundreds of feet without a pump?","a":"Because cohesion lets sap in the xylem be pulled rather than pushed, so the column travels under tension held together by hydrogen bonds between the water molecules."},{"q":"Why can that column fail in a drought?","a":"Because rising tension lets gas emboli form and spread through the conduits, blocking them and in the worst case killing the tissue they feed."},{"q":"Why does resistance to embolism trade off against growth?","a":"Because across 22 eucalypts the faster growers had wider vessels, better at conducting and worse at holding on, while slower ones had narrower vessels with thicker walls."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/A_water_droplet_DWR-coated_surface2.jpg/960px-A_water_droplet_DWR-coated_surface2.jpg","title":"A water droplet holding its shape","desc":"Cohesion at work: water pulled into a bead on a surface it will not wet.","credit":"Brocken Inaglory, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_water_droplet_DWR-coated_surface2.jpg","alt":"A single droplet of water sitting almost spherical on a blue woven fabric."},"wiki":"Cohesion (chemistry)"},
    "bio-030": {"questions":["Flight raised the internal temperature of a dove by only 0.6 °C (1.1 °F), which is what a body mostly made of a liquid with a high <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> does with waste heat.","The ocean has absorbed most of the planet's extra heat, and the reason it can is the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the seawater rather than anything about the water's depth."],"abstract":"The <b>specific heat capacity</b> of a substance is the energy needed to raise the temperature of a given mass of it, and water's is high, so large amounts of absorbed energy produce only small changes in how hot it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason is the hydrogen-bond network, since much of the energy goes into rearranging that before any of it appears as the motion of the molecules themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The magnitude is easy to hold onto, a kilocalorie being the energy needed to raise one litre of water by 1 °C (1.8 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The effect operates at every scale at once, buffering a cell against the heat it makes, steadying the temperature of a pond or a lake, and moderating the seasons of a planet mostly covered in ocean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A working animal shows the small version of it, since in ringed turtle-doves a bout of flight raised internal body temperature by only 0.6 °C (1.1 °F).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Buffering is not the same as escaping the problem, because the heat still has to leave in the end, and a larger animal needs longer to shed what its muscles produce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The consequence is visible in how animals move, the travel speeds of the largest species falling short of what their energy budgets alone would allow, because the capacity to dissipate heat gives out first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the far end of the scale the ocean is where most of the planet's additional heat has gone, and its heat content is now tracked from year to year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In 2022 the upper 2,000 metres (6,600 feet) held 10.9 ± 8.3 zettajoules more heat than in 2021, a record, with four basins at their highest since the 1950s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One property therefore keeps a cell from cooking itself and makes the ocean the planet's memory for heat.","sources":["Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Kristen E. Crandell, Donald R. Powers, and Bret W. Tobalske, “The Role of Plumage and Heat Dissipation Areas in Thermoregulation in Doves,” <i>Journal of Experimental Biology</i> 228, no. 4 (2025): jeb248200, https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.248200. [Open access]","Alexander Dyer, Ulrich Brose, Emilio Berti, Benjamin Rosenbaum, and Myriam R. Hirt, “The Travel Speeds of Large Animals Are Limited by Their Heat-Dissipation Capacity,” <i>PLOS Biology</i> 21, no. 4 (2023): e3001820, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3001820. [Open access]","Lijing Cheng, John Abraham, Kevin E. Trenberth, John Fasullo, Tim Boyer, Michael E. Mann, et al., “Another Year of Record Heat for the Oceans,” <i>Advances in Atmospheric Sciences</i> 40, no. 6 (2023): 963–74, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00376-023-2385-2. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does water absorb so much heat for so little change in temperature?","a":"Because much of the energy goes into rearranging its hydrogen-bond network before any of it appears as the motion of the molecules themselves."},{"q":"Why is buffering not the same as escaping the problem?","a":"Because the heat still has to leave in the end, and a larger animal needs longer to shed what its muscles produce."},{"q":"Why do the largest animals travel slower than their energy budgets allow?","a":"Because the capacity to dissipate heat gives out before the energy does, so shedding heat rather than supplying fuel is what sets their top travel speeds."}],"wiki":"Specific heat capacity"},
    "bio-031": {"questions":["A row of negatively charged phosphates could not sit along a DNA backbone at all if the surrounding <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> did not weaken the repulsion between them.","Zooplankton in an oxygen minimum zone respond to changes of 1% or less, because oxygen is a gas held in a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that carries very little of it."],"abstract":"A <b>solvent</b> is a liquid that other substances dissolve in, and water is biology's, taking up ions and polar molecules readily while leaving nonpolar ones out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A salt goes into solution because the ionic bonds holding its lattice together are greatly weakened in water, each ion departing surrounded by its own group of water molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The figure behind that is water's dielectric constant, about 80 at room temperature, which greatly attenuates the electrical attraction between charges and lets electrolytes dissociate and stay apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same screening lowers the cost of holding like charges close together, which is what allows the row of negatively charged phosphates along a nucleic acid backbone to exist at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What water will not dissolve matters as much, since nonpolar molecules disturb its hydrogen-bonded network and are pushed together rather than taken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Water is a participant as well as a medium, since it solvates nucleophiles, electrophiles and charged intermediates, lowering activation barriers and making reactions run that otherwise would not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The solution inside a cell is also nothing like dilute, an estimate built from cell composition giving 2 to 4 million protein molecules in a cubic micrometre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Oxygen is the hard case, since large regions of the middle ocean hold very little of it, and those oxygen minimum zones are expected to spread as the climate warms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Animals there live near their physiological limits, and in the eastern tropical North Pacific many zooplankton groups shift their distribution in response to changes in oxygen of 1% or less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Being a good solvent is therefore a specification rather than a virtue, and life is built around the particular one water happens to be.","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Ron Milo, “What Is the Total Number of Protein Molecules per Cell Volume? A Call to Rethink Some Published Values,” <i>BioEssays</i> 35, no. 12 (2013): 1050–55, https://doi.org/10.1002/bies.201300066. [Open access]","K. F. Wishner, B. A. Seibel, C. Roman, C. Deutsch, et al., “Ocean Deoxygenation and Zooplankton: Very Small Oxygen Differences Matter,” <i>Science Advances</i> 4, no. 12 (2018): eaau5180, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aau5180. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does water pull an ionic solid apart?","a":"Because its dielectric constant of about 80 greatly attenuates the electrical attraction between charges, letting electrolytes dissociate and each ion leave surrounded by water molecules."},{"q":"Why can a nucleic acid backbone carry a row of like charges at all?","a":"Because water’s screening lowers the cost of holding like charges close together, which is what allows the line of negatively charged phosphates to exist."},{"q":"Why is being a good solvent called a specification rather than a virtue?","a":"Because what water will not dissolve matters as much as what it will, and life is built around the particular solvent water happens to be, nonpolar exclusion included."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Na%2BH2O.svg/1920px-Na%2BH2O.svg.png","title":"Water as a solvent","desc":"A sodium ion in solution, surrounded by water molecules turned so their negative ends face it.","credit":"Original: Dr. Steven P. Berg Vector: Duncan Keall , Glrx , JoKalliauer ,, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Na%2BH2O.svg","alt":"A diagram of a sodium ion at the centre of six water molecules, each oriented with its oxygen towards the ion."},"wiki":"Solvent"},
    "bio-032": {"questions":["A lake in winter stays liquid underneath because the layer of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on top is lighter than the water below and insulates it from the air.","Fishes in polar seas carry proteins that bind <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and stop the crystals growing, which lowers the temperature at which their tissue freezes."],"abstract":"<b>Ice</b> is the solid form of water, and water is one of the few substances that expands as it freezes, the molar volume of the liquid being about 8% less than that of the solid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The liquid is densest at about 4°C (39°F) and swells again as it cools from there towards freezing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason is geometric, since each molecule can hydrogen-bond to its neighbours in a network,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and as the temperature falls from 4 to 0°C the ideal tetrahedral arrangements become more probable and longer-lived, holding molecules further apart than the less regular packing above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The solid therefore forms on top rather than at the bottom, and that layer, with water's high heat capacity, insulates what lies beneath and keeps it liquid while the air above is below freezing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life carries on underneath on that account, and the same substance is odd under pressure too, its melting point falling rather than rising, which is what lets a glacier slide over its bed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Freezing is still dangerous to a cell, and animals that meet it have evolved proteins that bind ice crystals, inhibit their growth and lower the temperature at which tissue freezes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Four kinds of antifreeze protein have evolved separately in fishes, which is what has allowed teleosts to colonise polar seas again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The trick is not theirs alone, since winter-active spiders carry an ice-binding protein that halts crystal growth at −4°C (25°F) and is folded unlike any of the fish types, so it arose on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A plant cannot move away from it, so what matters there is where the ice forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Outside the cells it can even protect them, drawing water out and concentrating what remains until the inside freezes at a lower temperature, though prolonged drying of that kind ruptures the membrane instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","S. N. Bogan, N. Surendran, S. Hotaling, T. Desvignes, et al., “Temperature and Pressure Shaped the Evolution of Antifreeze Proteins in Polar and Deep Sea Zoarcoid Fishes,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 42, no. 10 (2025): msaf219, https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msaf219. [Open access]","Laurie A. Graham, Stano Pekár, Ina M. Hainer, and Peter L. Davies, “Winter-Active Spiders (<i>Clubiona</i>) Have a Hyperactive Antifreeze Protein,” <i>The FEBS Journal</i> 293, no. 8 (2026): 2270–90, https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.70323. [Open access]","Lia Lamacque, Nicolas Dusart, Pierre Amato, Katline Charra-Vaskou, et al., “Ice Nucleation and Freezing Consequences in Perennial Plants,” <i>Physiologia Plantarum</i> 178, no. 3 (2026): e70961, https://doi.org/10.1111/ppl.70961. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does ice float, and why does that matter to life?","a":"Because water expands as it freezes, the liquid being about 8% denser, so the solid forms on top and insulates what lies beneath, keeping it liquid while the air is below freezing."},{"q":"Why does the liquid swell as it cools below 4 °C?","a":"Because the ideal tetrahedral hydrogen-bond arrangements become more probable and longer-lived as the temperature falls, holding molecules further apart than the less regular packing above."},{"q":"Why is antifreeze protein evidence of repeated invention?","a":"Because four kinds evolved separately in fishes, and winter-active spiders carry an ice-binding protein folded unlike any of them, so it arose on its own."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/1920px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg","title":"A block of ice","desc":"Ice floats because the hydrogen bonds in it hold the molecules further apart than they sit in liquid water.","credit":"Sharon Mollerus, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ice_Block,_Canal_Park,_Duluth_(32752478892).jpg","alt":"A large clear block of ice resting on a frozen shore."},"wiki":"Ice"},
    "bio-033": {"questions":["The vertebrate stomach shows how much <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a body will make on purpose, obligate scavengers holding theirs near pH 1.3 while foregut herbivores sit closer to 6.1.","Aspartate and glutamate carry carboxyl groups that hand a proton away inside the cell, which makes each of them an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and leaves its side chain negatively charged."],"abstract":"An <b>acid</b> is a substance that releases protons when it dissolves in water, each bare hydrogen nucleus attaching itself to a water molecule to make a hydronium ion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A base does the reverse, raising the concentration of hydroxyl ions, which are formed by removing a proton from a water molecule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water is faintly both, since a proton can leave one water molecule for another, so pure water carries equal traces of hydronium and hydroxyl and sits at the middle of the logarithmic pH scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The inside of a cell is held close to that neutral point, which is one of the conditions its chemistry is built around.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Acids and bases are ordinary furniture there: the carboxyl groups of aspartate and glutamate give a proton away and carry a negative charge, while the side chains of lysine and arginine take one on and carry a positive one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Away from that guarded interior, animals make acid deliberately, and the vertebrate stomach is the clearest case.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across mammals and birds the acidity of the stomach tracks diet rather than size: obligate scavengers average about pH 1.3, generalist carnivores 2.2, omnivores 2.9 and foregut herbivores 6.1, with the human stomach near 1.5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The suggestion is that the stomach works as an ecological filter, killing most of what arrives before it can settle further down the gut, so the animals eating the dirtiest food keep the fiercest acid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A few organisms live in acid rather than making it, among them <i>Oxyplasma meridianum</i>, an archaeon taken from a rock on the Italian island of Vulcano, which grows between pH 0.5 and 4.0 and does best at 1.0; relatives in its order endure below zero on the scale.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Small shifts tell as well as large ones: <i>Pocillopora</i> corals in the southern Mexican Pacific lost 28.6% of their skeletal density over 23 years, a decline reported as running alongside falling seawater pH and carbonate saturation rather than proved to be caused by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","DeAnna E. Beasley, Amanda M. Koltz, Joanna E. Lambert, Noah Fierer, and Rob R. Dunn, “The Evolution of Stomach Acidity and Its Relevance to the Human Microbiome,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 10, no. 7 (2015): e0134116, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0134116. [Open access]","Olga V. Golyshina, Evgenii A. Lunev, Marco A. Distaso, Rafael Bargiela, Matthew C. Gaines, Bertram Daum, Manuel Ferrer, et al., “Oxyplasma meridianum gen. nov., sp. nov., an Extremely Acidophilic Organotrophic Member of the Order Thermoplasmatales,” <i>International Journal of Systematic and Evolutionary Microbiology</i> 74, no. 8 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1099/ijsem.0.006499. [Open access]","Andrés López-Pérez, Omar Jiménez-Gutiérrez, Héctor Reyes-Bonilla, Rafael A. Cabral-Tena, Gerardo Leyte-Morales, Francisco Medellín-Maldonado, et al., “Two Decades of Skeletal Density Decline in Pocillopora spp. Corals in the Mexican Pacific Ocean: Insight into a Tropical Eastern Pacific Acidification Scenario?” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 21, no. 2 (2026): e0342741, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0342741. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is pure water counted as faintly acid and faintly basic at once?","a":"Because a proton can leave one water molecule for another, so pure water carries equal traces of hydronium and hydroxyl and sits at the middle of the pH scale."},{"q":"Why is stomach acidity read as an ecological filter?","a":"Because across mammals and birds it tracks diet rather than size — obligate scavengers near pH 1.3 against foregut herbivores at 6.1 — as though the dirtiest food demanded the fiercest acid."},{"q":"Why is the coral figure reported as an association rather than a cause?","a":"Because the 28.6% loss of skeletal density over 23 years is stated as running alongside falling seawater pH and carbonate saturation, which is not the same as having been shown to follow from it."}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Acetic-acid-dissociation-3D-balls.png/1920px-Acetic-acid-dissociation-3D-balls.png","title":"acid","desc":"Acetic acid and water on the left, acetate and a hydronium ion on the right — the proton transfer that makes a substance an acid.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acetic-acid-dissociation-3D-balls.png","alt":"A ball-and-stick diagram: an acetic acid molecule beside a water molecule on one side of a double arrow, an acetate ion and a hydronium ion on the other."},"wiki":"Acid"},
    "bio-034": {"questions":["A lysosome digests what a cell takes in at about <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> 5, two units below the cytosol around it, and its enzymes fall idle if they ever leak into that neutral surrounding.","<i>Sulfolobus acidocaldarius</i> grows best at a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between 2 and 3 while a soda-lake <i>Bacillus</i> prefers 8.5 to 11.5, each carrying enzymes whose optima match its own water."],"abstract":"<b>pH</b> measures how many protons a solution carries, on a logarithmic scale that fits an enormous range of concentrations into a handful of numbers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pure water sits at 7, where hydronium and hydroxyl ions are equally common; below that a solution is acidic and above it basic, and the inside of a cell is held close to that neutral point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The logarithm is the substance of the scale rather than a convenience, since each unit stands for a tenfold change in the concentration of protons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lysosome, the compartment in which a cell digests what it has taken in, runs near pH 5 against a cytosol of about 7.2, and a pump in its membrane keeps roughly a hundredfold difference across a single membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its enzymes are built to work at that acidity and go idle at the cytosol's, so a leaking lysosome does not digest the cell around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Newer work puts the lysosome lower still, near 4.5, and finds that it is not filled from the cytosol alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In yeast and in human cells the compartment also takes up protons pumped out by mitochondria across direct membrane contacts, and ageing breaks those contacts and lets the lysosome slip towards neutral, a loss an engineered linker between the two organelles can reverse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Whole organisms are built around their own values: <i>Sulfolobus acidocaldarius</i>, from a Yellowstone spring measured at 2.4, grows best between 2 and 3, while a <i>Bacillus</i> from a soda lake in Inner Mongolia grows between 8.5 and 11.5, each carrying enzymes whose optima match.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Human tissue can run the gradient backwards: in many tumours the fluid outside the cells turns acidic while the cytosol stays in a narrow alkaline band near 7.2 to 7.4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That reversal is now read as a feature of malignant progression rather than a by-product of it, one that contributes to invasion, immune evasion and resistance to treatment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Lysosomes,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9953/. [Open access]","Qingqing Liu, Seungmin Yoo, Zhixin A. Zhang, Liying Li, Hetian Su, Lingraj Vannur, Alexandra C. Wooldredge, et al., “Mitochondria-Lysosome Coupling Contributes to Lysosome Acidification and Aging,” <i>Molecular Cell</i> 86, no. 12 (2026): 2425–42, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.molcel.2026.05.004. [Open access]","Peng Zhou, Zu-Xin Zhang, Xiaoyong Pan, Xinfeng Dai, and Qiaoli Chen, “Leveraging AI-Driven Predictors of Enzyme pH Optima to Unravel Microbial Adaptation to Environmental pH,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 17 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2026.1788098. [Open access]","Fangquan Chen, Xiutao Cai, Qile Zhou, Zhen Huang, Jie Wang, Rui Kang, Daolin Tang, and Jiao Liu, “Compartmental pH Regulation in Cancer and Antitumor Immunity: Therapeutic Opportunities and Challenges,” <i>Apoptosis</i> 31, no. 8 (2026): 197, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10495-026-02408-w. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the logarithm the substance of the scale rather than a convenience?","a":"Because each unit stands for a tenfold change in the concentration of protons, so a small-looking difference in pH is an enormous difference in what the solution contains."},{"q":"Why does a leaking lysosome not digest the cell around it?","a":"Because its enzymes are built to work near pH 5 and go idle at the cytosol’s 7.2, so the hundredfold difference a pump maintains across one membrane is also a safety catch."},{"q":"Why is the acidity outside a tumour treated as a feature of it?","a":"Because the fluid outside the cells turns acidic while the cytosol stays in a narrow alkaline band, and that reversal is now read as contributing to invasion, immune evasion and resistance to treatment."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/PH_scale_3.jpg/1920px-PH_scale_3.jpg","title":"A pH indicator across the scale","desc":"The same indicator in a row of solutions, from strongly acidic at one end to strongly alkaline at the other.","credit":"Alvy16, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PH_scale_3.jpg","alt":"A rack of test tubes whose contents run through red, orange, yellow, green and blue."},"wiki":"PH"},
    "bio-035": {"questions":["Human blood is kept between 7.35 and 7.45 by a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built from carbon dioxide, carbonic acid and bicarbonate, with phosphate, proteins and haemoglobin doing the same work beside it.","The Revelle factor measures how strong a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the ocean's carbonate system makes, a high value meaning a weak one, so that the same added carbon dioxide moves the pH further."],"abstract":"A <b>buffer</b> is a mixture that resists a change in pH, holding a weak acid together with the base it turns into, so that added protons are taken up and lost ones replaced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Blood is the clearest case: carbon dioxide from respiration joins water to make carbonic acid, which dissociates into bicarbonate and a proton, and the reaction stands in equilibrium, shifting whichever way its surroundings demand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Human blood is held between 7.35 and 7.45 and averages 7.40, a value below that band being an acidemia and one above it an alkalemia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bicarbonate is not the only system at work, since phosphate, the body's proteins and haemoglobin all buffer as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Something of the kind underlies a cell's near-neutral interior, because metabolism makes acid continuously and neutrality is not a resting state that comes for free.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside muscle much of the work is done by particular molecules rather than dissolved salts, carnosine being the most abundant histidine dipeptide there and known chiefly for its pH buffering alongside antioxidant and metal-binding roles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The sea is buffered by its carbonate system, and how strongly is measured by the Revelle factor, a high value meaning weak buffering, so that a given amount of added carbon dioxide moves the pH further.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Weak buffering does not settle the outcome on its own: in the least ventilated and most poorly buffered oxygen-minimum waters acidification turns out to be negligible, little industrial carbon dioxide having reached them, while it is plain in the moderately ventilated water above.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A buffer can also be thought of as a reserve that is spent, and one recent model treats the ageing body's declining capacity to buffer acid, excrete it through the kidney and breathe it off as a hidden erosion behind frailty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Whether that model holds is not settled, but its shape is the general point: a buffer does not abolish a problem, it holds a value steady until the reserve behind it is spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Erin Hopkins, Terrence Sanvictores, and Sandeep Sharma, “Physiology, Acid Base Balance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2022), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507807/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Belisa Parmeggiani, Bruna Klippel Ferreira, Patricia Fernanda Schuck, and Gustavo Costa Ferreira, “Considerations for Carnosine Actions in Biology,” <i>Neurochemical Research</i> 51, no. 2 (2026): 126, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11064-026-04735-5. [Open access]","Liang Xue, Christopher Sabine, Jianfang Chen, Siv K. Lauvset, Qinsheng Wei, Kuiping Li, and Wei-Jun Cai, “Ventilation and Buffering Capacity Effects on Ocean Acidification in Low Oxygen Environments,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2025): 228, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-67807-0. [Open access]","Wan-Hui Liao, Wolfgang Langhans, and Maciej Henneberg, “Acid–Base Dysregulation Links Aging Metabolism to Frailty,” <i>Aging Cell</i> 25, no. 4 (2026): e70466, https://doi.org/10.1111/acel.70466. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a buffer hold a pH steady at all?","a":"Because it holds a weak acid together with the base it turns into, so added protons are taken up and lost ones replaced, and the equilibrium shifts whichever way its surroundings demand."},{"q":"Why is a cell’s neutrality not a resting state that comes for free?","a":"Because metabolism makes acid continuously, so the near-neutral interior has to be held there by buffering rather than simply settling at it."},{"q":"Why does weak buffering not settle whether a water will acidify?","a":"Because in the least ventilated, most poorly buffered oxygen-minimum waters acidification is negligible, little industrial carbon dioxide having reached them, while it is plain in the water above."}],"wiki":"Buffer solution"},
    "bio-036": {"questions":["Because carbon links to itself with no obvious limit, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> counts its possible molecules in the billions — 166.4 billion for structures of no more than seventeen heavy atoms.","All five canonical nucleobases turned up in samples of the asteroid Bennu, which is why <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is no longer taken to mean the chemistry of things that are alive."],"abstract":"<b>Organic chemistry</b> is the chemistry of carbon compounds, and its whole subject rests on one atom's habits: carbon forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbon atoms through stable carbon-carbon bonds to make chains and rings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There is no obvious upper limit to the size of what follows, so the same element frames a sugar of six carbons and a protein of many thousands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Perhaps a thousand kinds of small carbon compound are present in a typical cell, and these together with the large ones are what a biologist means by organic molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Inside a cell they fall into four families — carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids — each assembled from a small set of repeating parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That the frame is carbon rather than something else has been tested rather than assumed: a systematic assessment of silicon, the obvious alternative, finds no planetary environment in which a life built primarily on silicon chemistry is plausible, since in water its capacity is sharply limited by the ubiquitous formation of silica.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The reach of the chemistry is hard to overstate: a database enumerating every possible molecule of no more than seventeen atoms of carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, sulfur and the halogens holds 166.4 billion of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That counts what the rules of bonding allow rather than what exists, and the largest catalogue of compounds thought readily synthesisable holds 13.6 billion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The word organic no longer carries its old sense of something made by life, and returned asteroid samples are where that is plainest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> All five canonical nucleobases have been found in material brought back from the asteroid Bennu, evidence that some of life's ingredients were put together without life in that asteroid's parent body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The detail matters as much as the headline: pyrimidines outnumber purines in the Bennu sample, as they do in the Orgueil meteorite but unlike Murchison, so this chemistry differs from body to body rather than arriving everywhere in one mixture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Janusz Jurand Petkowski, William Bains, and Sara Seager, “On the Potential of Silicon as a Building Block for Life,” <i>Life</i> 10, no. 6 (2020): 84, https://doi.org/10.3390/life10060084. [Open access]","Aylin Del Moral-Morales, Francisco L. Feitosa, Carolina Horta Andrade, and José L. Medina-Franco, “The (r)evolution of Chemical Space and Molecular Modeling: A Time-Resolved Perspective,” <i>Journal of Computer-Aided Molecular Design</i> 40, no. 1 (2026): 136, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10822-026-00850-1. [Open access]","Yasuhiro Oba, Toshiki Koga, Yoshinori Takano, Hiroshi Naraoka, Yuta Hirakawa, Sako Sunami, Yoshihiro Furukawa, et al., “Distribution of Extraterrestrial Nucleobases, Other N-Heterocycles, and Their Precursors in a Sample from Asteroid Bennu,” <i>Communications Chemistry</i> 9, no. 1 (2026): 132, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42004-026-01966-z. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does one atom’s habits carry a whole branch of chemistry?","a":"Because carbon forms four covalent bonds and joins to other carbons through stable carbon-carbon bonds, so the same element frames a six-carbon sugar and a protein of many thousands."},{"q":"Why is silicon not a plausible alternative frame for life?","a":"Because a systematic assessment finds no planetary environment where it would work: in water silicon’s capacity is sharply limited by the ubiquitous formation of silica."},{"q":"Why does the word organic no longer mean made by life?","a":"Because all five canonical nucleobases have been found in material returned from the asteroid Bennu, so some of life’s ingredients were assembled without life in that asteroid’s parent body."}],"wiki":"Organic chemistry"},
    "bio-037": {"questions":["Collagen's proline is hydroxylated in a step that depends on vitamin C, so scurvy is what follows when a single <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fails to be attached where it belongs.","One survey counted 64,397 phosphorylation sites and 14,245 acetylation sites across proteins, each of them a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> added after the protein was built and altering what it does."],"abstract":"A <b>functional group</b> is one of the small clusters of atoms that occur over and over again attached to a carbon skeleton — the methyl, hydroxyl, carboxyl, carbonyl, phosphate and amino groups among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each has chemical and physical properties of its own, and it is these rather than the skeleton beneath that largely settle how the molecule containing them behaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same frame carrying a different group is therefore a different substance, which is how a short list of groups generates an enormous variety of molecules from a limited stock of shapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A carboxyl group gives a proton away inside a cell and leaves what carries it negatively charged, while an amino group takes one on and leaves it positive, which is what makes aspartate and glutamate acidic residues and lysine and arginine basic ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The groups are not fixed at the moment a molecule is built, and a cell attaches and removes them afterwards, changing what a protein does without altering the sequence it was made from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That editing has been counted: one recent survey identified 64,397 phosphorylation sites across proteins — 43,721 on serine, 8,414 on threonine and 12,262 on tyrosine — along with 14,245 acetylation and 1,461 mono-methylation sites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most such sites sit where an enzyme can reach them, but a 2025 study describes cryptic phosphosites buried inside a protein's core, exposed only while the chain is folding and more conserved than the ones on the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Modifying one of those can destabilise the folded structure and send the protein to be broken up, which makes the position of a group as consequential as its identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A single hydroxyl can decide whether a tissue holds together at all: the proline and lysine of collagen are hydroxylated inside the endoplasmic reticulum in a step that depends on vitamin C.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where the vitamin is short that hydroxylation fails, and the result is scurvy, with weak connective tissue, bleeding gums and poor wound healing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Mukesh Kumar, Anthony P. Possemato, Barry M. Zee, Srikanth Subramanian, Jian Min Ren, Alissa J. Nelson, Bin Zhang, et al., “Expanding the Global Map of Protein Post-Translational Modifications with Immunoaffinity Enrichment and nDIA Analysis on the Orbitrap Astral Mass Spectrometer,” <i>Molecular &amp; Cellular Proteomics</i> 25, no. 5 (2026): 101562, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpro.2026.101562. [Open access]","Dino Gasparotto, Annarita Zanon, Valerio Bonaldo, Elisa Marchiori, Massimo Casagranda, Erika Di Domenico, Laura Copat, et al., “Mapping Cryptic Phosphorylation Sites in the Human Proteome,” <i>The EMBO Journal</i> 44, no. 22 (2025): 6704–31, https://doi.org/10.1038/s44318-025-00567-1. [Open access]","Nader Rahimi and Marjorie V. Launico, “Biochemistry, Collagen Synthesis,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2026), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507709/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a short list of groups generate so many molecules?","a":"Because each group has chemical properties of its own and largely settles how the molecule behaves, so the same carbon skeleton carrying a different group is a different substance."},{"q":"Why can a cell change what a protein does without changing its sequence?","a":"Because groups are attached and removed after the chain is built — one survey identified 64,397 phosphorylation sites alongside 14,245 acetylation and 1,461 mono-methylation sites."},{"q":"Why does a single hydroxyl group decide whether tissue holds together?","a":"Because collagen’s proline and lysine are hydroxylated in a step depending on vitamin C, and where the vitamin is short that step fails, giving weak connective tissue, bleeding gums and poor healing."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Benzyl_acetate_-_functional_groups_and_moieties.svg/1920px-Benzyl_acetate_-_functional_groups_and_moieties.svg.png","title":"Functional groups on one molecule","desc":"Benzyl acetate, with its ester group and its benzyl group ringed: the parts that decide how a molecule behaves.","credit":"Jonatan Svensson Glad, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Benzyl_acetate_-_functional_groups_and_moieties.svg","alt":"A structural formula of a molecule with two of its parts circled in dashed lines, one green and one orange."},"wiki":"Functional group"},
    "bio-038": {"questions":["Because 11-cis-retinal flips to an all-trans <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the instant it absorbs a photon, vision begins with a change of shape rather than a change of composition.","Only the l-form of each amino acid is found in proteins, so life uses one member of every mirror-image <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pair and leaves the other very largely alone."],"abstract":"Molecules that share a chemical formula but differ in structure are called <b>isomers</b>, and the ones that come as mirror-image pairs are called optical isomers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are widespread among organic molecules and are a large part of what makes the sugars so various, since each simple sugar exists in a d-form and an l-form that are mirror images of each other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every amino acid but glycine does the same, and life uses one hand of each: only l-forms are ever found in proteins, though d-amino acids occur in bacterial cell walls and in some antibiotics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Once that choice was established it could not be changed, because too much had come to depend on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difference need not be handedness at all: the glucose units of cellulose are joined in the β configuration where those of the storage polysaccharides are α, the same sugar differently arranged, and the reason one is a structural material and the other food.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An isomerisation can be the working part of a mechanism rather than a curiosity of nomenclature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vision begins when 11-cis-retinal absorbs a photon and is reconfigured to all-trans-retinal, and the spent form has to be re-isomerised before the photoreceptor can respond again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fatty acids come in geometrical isomers, which differ in whether a double bond is cis or trans, and positional isomers, which differ in where along the chain that bond sits; industrial partial hydrogenation produces trans forms now studied as a cause of dyslipidaemia and cardiovascular disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Mirror images can even carry different histories, and both forms of glutamic acid are present in the material returned from the asteroid Bennu with nitrogen isotope values differing by 87 parts per thousand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Since the two are chemically near-identical, a difference that wide implies they were formed or altered under unlike conditions, which unsettles the assumption that a molecule's two hands share one isotopic record.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Ala Moshiri and Akrit Sodhi, “Illuminating Retinoid Flux in the Neurosensory Retina,” <i>Journal of Clinical Investigation</i> 136, no. 3 (2026): e201863, https://doi.org/10.1172/jci201863. [Open access]","Mona A. Hegazy, Bojana Vidovic, Shimaa Abobakr, Aleksandra Zeljkovic, Aleksandra Stefanovic, and Jelena Vekic, “Hidden Industrial Trans-Fatty Acids: Mechanistic Insights into Dyslipidemia, Cardiovascular Disease, and Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 26, no. 23 (2025): 11715, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms262311715. [Open access]","Allison A. Baczynski, Ophélie M. McIntosh, Danielle N. Simkus, Hannah L. McLain, Jason P. Dworkin, Daniel P. Glavin, Jamie E. Elsila, et al., “Multiple Formation Pathways for Amino Acids in the Early Solar System Based on Carbon and Nitrogen Isotopes in Asteroid Bennu Samples,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 8 (2026): e2517723123, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2517723123. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why could life’s choice of one hand not be reversed later?","a":"Because once the choice was established too much had come to depend on it, which is why only l-forms are ever found in proteins even though d-amino acids exist elsewhere."},{"q":"Why is cellulose a structural material where starch is food?","a":"Because the glucose units of cellulose are joined in the β configuration and those of the storage polysaccharides in α — the same sugar differently arranged."},{"q":"Why does the Bennu glutamic acid finding unsettle an assumption?","a":"Because its two mirror forms differ by 87 parts per thousand in nitrogen isotopes, and since the two are chemically near-identical that implies they formed or were altered under unlike conditions."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/IsomerOverview.svg/1920px-IsomerOverview.svg.png","title":"The kinds of isomer","desc":"One formula, two ways of differing: in which atoms are joined to which, and in how they are arranged in space.","credit":"Smokefoot, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsomerOverview.svg","alt":"A branching chart with isomers at the top, dividing into constitutional isomers and stereoisomers."},"wiki":"Isomer"},
    "bio-039": {"questions":["Lactose is split into glucose and galactose by lactase in the small intestine, and where that enzyme has declined the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> fails and the sugar reaches the colon undigested.","Every biological polymer is assembled by expelling water, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the name for the reverse reaction, the one that consumes a molecule of water to break each bond."],"abstract":"<b>Hydrolysis</b> is the breaking of a bond by a molecule of water, and it is the reverse of the condensation reaction that made the bond in the first place, in which a molecule of water was expelled as the two parts were joined.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same pair of reactions builds and dismantles all three great families of biological polymer, so sugars, nucleic acids and proteins are each assembled by losing one water molecule per subunit added and taken apart by consuming one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The condensation half also goes by the name of a dehydration reaction, and it is what joins two monosaccharides through a glycosidic bond between two of their carbons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hydrolysis is not only destructive: the terminal phosphate of ATP is split off by it, and the energy released is what drives reactions that would not otherwise run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nowhere in biology is a peptide, phosphodiester or glycosidic bond made by any other means than this expulsion of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> That universality sits oddly with the medium, since water is also what spontaneously takes those polymers apart, and one recent review calls the arrangement a paradox at the heart of biochemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Prebiotic chemistry answers it by drying the mixture out and wetting it again, and an experimental study of wet-dry cycling under gamma irradiation reports infrared evidence of peptide bond formation, though its authors describe the results as preliminary.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In a living body the reaction is put to work rather than waited for, and lactose is hydrolysed into glucose and galactose by lactase at the brush border of the small intestine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That enzyme peaks shortly after birth and then declines whether or not milk keeps being drunk, a pattern shared with most other mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> People of northern European and northwestern Indian ancestry more often keep it into adult life, so whether one particular bond is hydrolysed at all differs from person to person.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","E. Fonseca-González, C. A. Fuentes-Carreon, and A. Heredia-Barbero, “Experimental and Computational Simulation of the Prebiotic Peptide Bond Formation Driven by Wet-Dry Cycles and Gamma Ionizing Radiation: An Insight into Molecular Evolution,” <i>Journal of Molecular Evolution</i> 94, no. 2 (2026): 369–82, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00239-026-10309-4. [Open access]","Eric Goosenberg and Muriam Afzal, “Lactose Intolerance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532285/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the same pair of reactions enough to build and dismantle life’s polymers?","a":"Because sugars, nucleic acids and proteins are each assembled by expelling one water molecule per subunit added and taken apart by consuming one, and no other means is used anywhere in biology."},{"q":"Why is that arrangement called a paradox?","a":"Because water is both the thing expelled to make a bond and the thing that spontaneously takes those polymers apart again, so the polymers are built in the medium that destroys them."},{"q":"Why does whether one bond is hydrolysed differ from person to person?","a":"Because lactase peaks shortly after birth and then declines whether or not milk keeps being drunk, and only some ancestries more often keep it into adult life."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/ff/Acid-CatAmideHydrolMarch.png/960px-Acid-CatAmideHydrolMarch.png","title":"hydrolysis","desc":"Mechanism for the acid-catalysed hydrolysis of an amide, adapted from March's Advanced Organic Chemistry.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Acid-CatAmideHydrolMarch.png","alt":"A chemical scheme carrying an amide through a row of arrows and intermediates to a carboxylic acid and an amine."},"wiki":"Hydrolysis"},
    "bio-040": {"questions":["Glycogen, starch and cellulose are made entirely of glucose, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can differ from another built of the very same subunit in nothing but the bonds joining them.","Ribosome measurements put the addition of subunits at between 0.4 and 5 per second depending on the method, which is the rate at which a protein <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is actually assembled."],"abstract":"A <b>polymer</b> is a long molecule built by joining many small subunits, called monomers, one after another into a chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each such chain grows by adding a monomer onto its end, so the stepwise repetition of one simple reaction is enough to manufacture a molecule of enormous size and complexity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The economy of the strategy is that the set of subunits is small: proteins, nucleic acids and polysaccharides are each drawn from a limited stock, and what varies between one chain and the next is the order rather than the ingredients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Glycogen, starch and cellulose are made entirely of glucose residues, and differ in the bonds joining them and in the branching that results, one storing sugar and another serving as a structural material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two polymers can therefore share every atom of their composition and do completely unrelated jobs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The assembly can be watched, and how fast it runs depends on how you look: measurements of ribosomes putting amino acids onto a growing chain give about 0.4 per second by single-molecule fluorescence, 0.7 to 1.5 in cell extracts and 3 to 5 by ribosome profiling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The spread is a fact about the methods rather than about the cells, and any figure quoted for the speed of protein synthesis carries the technique that produced it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At the other end of the scale the strategy dominates the planet's chemistry, lignocellulosic biomass — cellulose, hemicellulose and lignin together — being the most abundant renewable source of organic matter on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What biology does that industrial chemistry still cannot is control the order exactly, and a chemist's polymer is usually a mixture of chain lengths and sequences rather than one defined molecule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Work on sequence-defined synthetic chains is explicit that the fine-tuning nature achieves through controlled structure remains beyond abiotic macromolecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Leslie Watkins, Mulin Li, and Bin Wu, “Translation Elongation: Measurements and Applications,” <i>RNA Biology</i> 22, no. 1 (2025): 1–10, https://doi.org/10.1080/15476286.2025.2504727. [Open access]","Adrian Cătălin Puițel and Mircea Teodor Nechita, “Recent Progress on Lignocellulosic-Based Polymeric Materials,” <i>Polymers</i> 18, no. 12 (2026): 1498, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym18121498. [Open access]","Sara Njoku, Ariel F. Perez Mellor, Johanna Brazard, Simone G. Giuffrida, Wojciech Dudziak, Céline Besnard, Thomas Bürgi, Takuji B. M. Adachi, and Róża Szweda, “Stereocontrol as a Tool for Shaping Abiotic, Sequence-Defined Oligourethanes,” <i>Polymer Chemistry</i> 17, no. 24 (2026): 2619–28, https://doi.org/10.1039/d6py00299d. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is building from a small stock of subunits an economy?","a":"Because the stepwise repetition of one simple reaction manufactures molecules of enormous size, and what varies from one chain to the next is the order rather than the ingredients."},{"q":"Why can two polymers of identical composition do unrelated jobs?","a":"Because glycogen, starch and cellulose are all glucose and differ only in the bonds joining the residues and in the branching that results, one storing sugar and another serving as structure."},{"q":"Why does a quoted rate of protein synthesis carry its method with it?","a":"Because measurements give about 0.4 amino acids per second by single-molecule fluorescence, 0.7 to 1.5 in cell extracts and 3 to 5 by ribosome profiling, and that spread is a fact about the methods."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Polyethylene_balls1.jpg/1920px-Polyethylene_balls1.jpg","title":"Polyethylene pellets","desc":"A polymer as it is handled in bulk, each pellet a tangle of chains built from one repeated monomer.","credit":"Lluis tgn, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polyethylene_balls1.jpg","alt":"A heap of small translucent plastic pellets on a grey surface."},"wiki":"Polymer"},
    "bio-041": {"questions":["Cells burn their food in small steps rather than in a flame, taking the energy out through a long series of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reactions that pass electrons from carrier to carrier.","Cable bacteria move electrons along conductive fibres for centimetres through sediment, which is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> chemistry stretched across a distance no single cell could otherwise span."],"abstract":"A <b>redox</b> reaction is one in which electrons move from one atom to another, and its two halves have separate names: oxidation is the removal of electrons and reduction their addition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oxidation does not require oxygen at all, so an iron atom is oxidised when it loses an electron to become Fe³⁺, and a chlorine atom reduced when it gains one to become Cl⁻.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because the number of electrons is conserved, the two always happen together: whatever one molecule gains, another has lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell does not oxidise its food in one step, the way organic material burns in a fire, but takes it apart gradually through enzyme-catalysed stages, which is how the energy is captured rather than released as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The electrons are carried between those stages: NAD⁺ accepts a pair to become NADH, and in an aerobic organism they are handed down the electron transport chain until oxygen is reduced to water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Not every organism keeps that traffic inside itself, and cable bacteria run electrons along conductive fibres in their own envelopes over centimetre-scale distances through layered sediment, so that sulphide is oxidised in one place and oxygen reduced in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> One freshwater species also passes electrons to solid material outside the cell altogether, growing under anoxic conditions at rates comparable to aerobic respiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The oxygen that most of this chemistry now depends on is itself a product of it, having accumulated in the atmosphere about 2.45 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much accumulated is still argued over, with published estimates of the level spanning orders of magnitude and several divergent readings all defensible on the present evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor is oxidation only damage: plants generate reactive oxygen species in every compartment of the cell and use them as signalling molecules, including in a long-distance cell-to-cell wave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Kartik Aiyer, Leonid Digel, Markéta Linhartová, Andreas Schramm, Ugo Marzocchi, Lars Peter Nielsen, and Ian P. G. Marshall, “Extracellular Electron Transfer in Cable Bacteria Enables Growth Rates Comparable to Aerobic Respiration,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 6622, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-73282-y. [Open access]","Peter W. Crockford, Ichiko Sugiyama, Michael A. Kipp, Jihua Hao, Lyle L. Nelson, Jordon D. Hemingway, Sabine Wimmer, and Mojtaba Fakhraee, “Revisiting the Greatness of Earth's Great Oxidation,” <i>Communications Earth &amp; Environment</i> 7, no. 1 (2026): 348, https://doi.org/10.1038/s43247-026-03518-8. [Open access]","Christine H. Foyer, “Reactive Oxygen Species and Oxidative Signalling in Plants,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 70, no. 1 (2026): 25–33, https://doi.org/10.1042/ebc20250047. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why must oxidation and reduction always happen together?","a":"Because the number of electrons is conserved, so whatever one molecule gains another has lost, and the two named halves are two views of a single transfer."},{"q":"Why does a cell take its food apart in stages rather than burning it?","a":"Because gradual enzyme-catalysed steps let the energy be captured as electrons are carried between them, where burning would release it all as heat."},{"q":"Why is oxidation not simply damage?","a":"Because plants generate reactive oxygen species in every compartment of the cell and use them as signalling molecules, including in a long-distance cell-to-cell wave."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Galvanic_cell_with_no_cation_flow.svg/1920px-Galvanic_cell_with_no_cation_flow.svg.png","title":"A galvanic cell","desc":"Oxidation at one electrode and reduction at the other, with the electrons made to travel between them.","credit":"Original: Ohiostandard Vector: AntiCompositeNumber, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galvanic_cell_with_no_cation_flow.svg","alt":"A diagram of two beakers joined by a membrane, a zinc anode in one and a copper cathode in the other, with electrons flowing through a lamp."},"wiki":"Redox"},
    "bio-042": {"questions":["A gram of fat carries about 9 kcal against 4 for a gram of carbohydrate, a difference in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> packed into each by the way its atoms are put together.","Breaking a bond always costs energy, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a cell draws from ATP comes from the bonds formed afterwards and not from the one that was broken."],"abstract":"<b>Chemical energy</b> is the energy a substance holds in the arrangement of its atoms and the bonds between them, and a reaction releases or takes up energy by rearranging them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A reaction gives energy out when the bonds formed in its products are stronger overall than the bonds broken in its reactants, so it is the balance across the whole change that decides the direction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Covalent bonds are stable enough to build a cell from because they are roughly a hundred times stronger than the thermal energies jostling the molecules around them, and so are not broken by ordinary motion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Living things release the energy in food by oxidising it gradually in enzyme-catalysed steps rather than burning it in one, and couple that release to the reactions that need paying for.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The usual shorthand for all this is wrong in a specific and well-documented way: breaking a bond requires energy rather than releasing it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The error has been watched happening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In interviews, most introductory biology students explained the energy from ATP hydrolysis by saying that a bond had been broken, and about a third corrected themselves unprompted once they were shown a reaction-coordinate diagram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The authors read that as chemistry the students already knew but did not bring into a biology setting, rather than as chemistry they had never learnt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The vocabulary makes it easier to go wrong, since calling a molecule with weak bonds energy-rich invites the misreading, and some authors would rather speak of a high group transfer potential; that replacement suits nucleoside triphosphates and thioesters, which transfer a phosphoryl or acyl group, but not dioxygen, so the two notions are kept apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Measured out per gram, the chemical energy in food differs by fuel, a gram of carbohydrate or of protein carrying about 4 kcal and a gram of fat about 9.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Lucien Bettendorff and Pierre Wins, “Energy-Rich Molecules and Group Transfer Potentials in Energetic Coupling Reactions,” <i>Molecules</i> 31, no. 2 (2026): 242, https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules31020242. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Heidi Hall and Mingyu Yang, “Making Sense of ATP Hydrolysis: How Students Reconcile Conflicting Ideas from Chemistry and Biology,” <i>Journal of Microbiology &amp; Biology Education</i> 27, no. 1 (2026): e00279-25, https://doi.org/10.1128/jmbe.00279-25. [Open access]","Alyssa L. Morris and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Nutrients,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554545/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a reaction give energy out at all?","a":"Because the bonds formed in its products are stronger overall than the bonds broken in its reactants, so it is the balance across the whole change that decides the direction."},{"q":"Why is the usual account of ATP energy wrong?","a":"Because breaking a bond requires energy rather than releasing it, so saying the energy comes from breaking ATP’s bond gets the arithmetic backwards."},{"q":"Why is that error read as chemistry not brought across rather than not learnt?","a":"Because in interviews about a third of introductory biology students corrected themselves unprompted once they were shown a reaction-coordinate diagram."}],"wiki":"Chemical energy"},
    "bio-043": {"questions":["About 99% of a body's calcium sits in the skeleton, a store as well as a structure, since the same <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is needed for clotting and for nerve and muscle.","Because it carries a charge, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> such as potassium can be concentrated on one side of a membrane, and the difference is what a nerve cell spends to keep itself ready to fire."],"abstract":"An <b>electrolyte</b> is an inorganic substance that carries an electric charge in solution, and the ones that matter in a body are sodium, potassium, chloride, magnesium, calcium, phosphate and bicarbonate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They come in with food and drink, and they maintain electrical neutrality in cells and generate and conduct the impulses of nerve and muscle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell exploits them by keeping concentrations different inside and out: ion channels are narrow selective pores that let particular ions — chiefly sodium, potassium, calcium or chloride — move rapidly down the electrochemical gradients across the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Channels open mainly to potassium are found in almost every animal cell, and by leaving the membrane far more permeable to that ion than to others they hold the membrane potential.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because the transport through a channel is always downhill, none of this works unless the difference is set up and paid for beforehand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The quantities are held between narrow bounds, and serum sodium runs from 135 to 145 mmol per litre, with low sodium the most frequent of all the electrolyte disorders.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of the supply is structural as well as functional, since calcium is the fifth most abundant element in a human body, about a kilogram of it in a person of seventy, of which roughly 99% is in the skeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same ions turn up regulating things far from nerve and bone: a 2026 study reports that high potassium inside a resting T lymphocyte keeps a signalling protein from being recruited to its receptor, and that raising potassium outside suppresses the response.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The equivalent module in the related kinase of B cells is not sensitive to potassium at all, so this is a specific arrangement rather than a general effect of charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much a diet carries matters at the scale of populations, and replacing part of the sodium in table salt with potassium is under study for its effect on blood pressure and on recurrent stroke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Isha Shrimanker and Sandeep Bhattarai, “Electrolytes,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541123/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Ion Channels and the Electrical Properties of Membranes,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26910/. [Open access]","Taylor M. Drake and Muhammad Zubair, “Calcium,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2024), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557683/. [Open access]","Swarnendu Roy, Soumee SenGupta, Kaustav Gangopadhyay, Sudipta Majumder, Jibitesh Das, Anushka Sinha, Prosad Kumar Das, Bidisha Sinha, and Rahul Das, “Potassium Suppresses Allosteric Activation of ZAP-70-Dependent T Cell Receptor Signaling,” <i>Journal of Biological Chemistry</i> 302, no. 6 (2026): 113083, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2026.113083. [Open access]","Priya Patel, Biff F. Palmer, and Richard A. Lange, “Sodium Reduction and Salt Substitutes: Impact on Blood Pressure and Recurrent Stroke,” <i>Current Opinion in Cardiology</i> 41, no. 4 (2026): 286–90, https://doi.org/10.1097/HCO.0000000000001300. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a channel need a gradient set up beforehand?","a":"Because transport through a channel is always downhill, so nothing moves unless the concentration difference across the membrane has already been established and paid for."},{"q":"Why does a cell rest at the potential it does?","a":"Because channels open mainly to potassium are found in almost every animal cell, leaving the membrane far more permeable to that ion than to others."},{"q":"Why is the potassium effect on T cells called specific rather than general?","a":"Because the equivalent module in the related kinase of B cells is not sensitive to potassium at all, so it is an arrangement rather than a consequence of charge."}],"wiki":"Electrolyte"},
    "bio-044": {"questions":["Iron limits the growth of phytoplankton across whole upwelling regions, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can decide the productivity of an ocean as readily as the health of a body.","A human body holds at least 60 detectable elements and only about 25 that take part in its functioning, most of those being needed as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than in bulk."],"abstract":"A <b>trace element</b> is one an organism requires in very small quantity, where the word trace describes the amount and not the importance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Deciding which elements belong on the list is harder than it looks, since a human body contains at least 60 detectable chemical elements and only about 25 are thought to take part in its healthy functioning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Copper is the standard illustration, about 80 mg of it in a body, needed for the development of connective tissue, bone and the coverings of nerves, while chronic copper toxicity can damage the liver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A trace element therefore has a window rather than a threshold, the same substance being a requirement at one intake and a poison at another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Iodine, selenium, manganese, molybdenum, chromium and boron have all been counted as ultratrace elements whose health benefits merit intakes of their own, along with cobalt in the form of vitamin B12.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Falling short is not a small matter: zinc supplementation is estimated to be capable of reducing global child mortality by about 4%, and in 2004 zinc deficiency was held responsible for over 260,000 deaths in Africa and 182,000 in Asia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Iodine shows how carefully such findings have to be read, because a study of 1,211 mother and child pairs found lower first-trimester iodine associated with lower vocabulary scores at fifteen but not with matrix reasoning or full-scale IQ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Within that cohort the children of severely deficient mothers scored 4.25 points lower on vocabulary and 3.3 points lower on full-scale IQ than those whose mothers had sufficient iodine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same shortages govern whole oceans, iron being the element most commonly limiting phytoplankton in upwelling regions, with manganese co-limiting alongside it in parts of the Southern Ocean and cobalt limiting in some settings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Grown at Southern Ocean concentrations, the Antarctic diatom <i>Fragilariopsis cylindrus</i> expresses its iron-stress proteins consistently under low iron, and one of its two flavodoxins responds to the shortage while the other does not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Prinessa Chellan and Peter J. Sadler, “The Elements of Life and Medicines,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 373, no. 2037 (2015): 20140182, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2014.0182. [Open access]","David Rabinovich and Yamen Smadi, “Zinc,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547698/. [Open access]","Sarai M. Keestra, Marsh Königs, Nienke van Welie, Kim Dreyer, Jaap Oosterlaan, Velja Mijatovic, Tessa J. Roseboom, and Martijn J. J. Finken, “Iodine Deficiency in the First Pregnancy Trimester and Intelligence in Adolescence,” <i>European Journal of Nutrition</i> 65, no. 3 (2026): 111, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00394-026-03955-3. [Open access]","Thomas J. Browning and C. Mark Moore, “Global Analysis of Ocean Phytoplankton Nutrient Limitation Reveals High Prevalence of Co-limitation,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 14 (2023): 5014, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-023-40774-0. [Open access]","Loay J. Jabre, Elden Rowland, J. Scott P. McCain, and Erin M. Bertrand, “Deep Proteomic Profiles of the Antarctic Diatom <i>Fragilariopsis cylindrus</i> under Varying Iron and Manganese Conditions,” <i>Proteomics</i> 26, no. 5 (2026): 112–19, https://doi.org/10.1002/pmic.70109. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a trace element have a window rather than a threshold?","a":"Because the same substance is a requirement at one intake and a poison at another: copper is needed for connective tissue, bone and nerve coverings, and chronic excess damages the liver."},{"q":"Why is deciding which elements belong on the list difficult?","a":"Because a human body contains at least 60 detectable elements and only about 25 are thought to take part in its healthy functioning, so presence is not evidence of a role."},{"q":"Why does the iodine study have to be read carefully?","a":"Because lower first-trimester iodine was associated with lower vocabulary scores at fifteen but not with matrix reasoning or full-scale IQ, so the effect found was narrower than the headline."}],"wiki":"Trace element"},
    "bio-045": {"questions":["All five canonical nucleobases have been recovered from an asteroid, which removes the premise <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rested on, that the compounds of living things could arise only inside something alive.","Chemists can now grow membrane-bounded compartments from small prebiotic molecules in one pot, so the organisation <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> attributed to a vital force is being assembled out of ordinary reagents."],"abstract":"<b>Vitalism</b> is the doctrine that living systems are animated by something immaterial, a force or principle not reducible to the matter they are made of.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is worth naming because the whole of this deck's chemistry is an answer to it, and because the position was neither foolish nor quickly overturned: the substances peculiar to organisms really did seem to arise only within them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Organisms turn out to be built from the same elements as everything else, of which a human body holds at least 60 detectable and only about 25 that take any part in its functioning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The reactions between them run on the same rules as reactions anywhere, and the properties of water — dehydration and condensation, hydrolysis, the hydrophobic effect — are described in one recent review not as obstacles that life overcame but as channels that guided chemical evolution from the start.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> On that reading nothing extra is being added to the chemistry; the chemistry is being used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The old premise has since been tested directly, and all five canonical nucleobases have been found in material returned from the asteroid Bennu, put together without life in that asteroid's parent body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The compounds are not merely present but various, and pyrimidines outnumber purines there as they do in the Orgueil meteorite and unlike Murchison.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Organisation is being approached the same way: a 2026 study starts from acetaldehyde and a prebiotically plausible catalyst and obtains lipid-like molecules up to twenty carbons long that assemble themselves into membrane-bounded compartments, with the catalyst embedded in the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of that amounts to making life, and the question vitalism was reaching for — what distinguishes a living arrangement of matter from a dead one — is not settled by any of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What has gone is the idea that the answer lies in the material, since the atoms of an organism are the atoms of everything else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Sandy C. Boucher, “What Was Really Wrong (and Right) with Vitalism? Methodological Naturalism, Organicism and Immaterialist Theories in Biology,” <i>History and Philosophy of the Life Sciences</i> 48, no. 1 (2026): 11, https://doi.org/10.1007/s40656-026-00719-4. [Open access]","Prinessa Chellan and Peter J. Sadler, “The Elements of Life and Medicines,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A</i> 373, no. 2037 (2015): 20140182, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2014.0182. [Open access]","Juliana DiGiacomo, George D. Rose, and Loren Dean Williams, “The Master Molecule That Built Biology: How Water Shaped the Chemistry of Life,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 5 (2026): e70532, https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70532. [Open access]","Yasuhiro Oba, Toshiki Koga, Yoshinori Takano, Hiroshi Naraoka, Yuta Hirakawa, Sako Sunami, Yoshihiro Furukawa, et al., “Distribution of Extraterrestrial Nucleobases, Other N-Heterocycles, and Their Precursors in a Sample from Asteroid Bennu,” <i>Communications Chemistry</i> 9, no. 1 (2026): 132, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42004-026-01966-z. [Open access]","Marian Simon Rafael Ebeling, Otto Berninghausen, Khang Hoang Nguyen, Roland Beckmann, and Oliver Trapp, “Organocatalyzed Bottom-Up Formation of Protocells,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 1983, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-69597-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why was vitalism neither foolish nor quickly overturned?","a":"Because the substances peculiar to organisms really did seem to arise only within them, so the doctrine was an inference from the evidence available rather than a superstition."},{"q":"Why does the Bennu material bear on the doctrine directly?","a":"Because all five canonical nucleobases were found in returned asteroid material, put together without life in that asteroid’s parent body, which tests the old premise rather than arguing with it."},{"q":"Why has the question vitalism was reaching for not been settled?","a":"Because none of this amounts to making life; what has gone is the idea that the answer lies in the material, the atoms of an organism being the atoms of everything else."}],"wiki":"Vitalism"},
    "bio-046": {"questions":["Lipids are grouped by what they do rather than by any repeating subunit, which makes them the family of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that a definition built on polymers cannot reach.","Macromolecules make up 80 to 90% of the dry weight of most cells, so a large <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> accounts for nearly everything a cell is built from once water is set aside."],"abstract":"A <b>biomolecule</b> is a molecule made and used by a living thing, and most of them belong to one of four classes: carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Three of those classes are macromolecules built by joining small precursors — sugars, amino acids and nucleotides — and together they make up 80 to 90% of the dry weight of most cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lipids are the exception and the other major constituent, grouped by what they do rather than by any repeating subunit, since they store energy, form the bulk of cell membranes and carry signals as steroid hormones and messengers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of this outweighs water, which accounts for about 70% of a cell's weight and is where nearly every reaction between the four takes place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest of the mass is small organic molecules, perhaps a thousand kinds of them in a typical cell, serving as the subunits the large ones are made from and the intermediates they are broken into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Each class is more varied than its name suggests, and lipidomics finds even a single cell type carrying hundreds to thousands of distinct lipid species, differing in headgroup, in chain length and in how saturated those chains are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Why cells maintain such complex lipid inventories is not settled, and the review that asks the question says plainly that the significance of the diversity remains incompletely understood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The carbohydrates behave least like the rest, because unlike nucleic acids and proteins their assembly is not template-driven but emerges from the competing activity of the enzymes that build and trim them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why the sugar structures attached to proteins are the commonest and most diverse modification in eukaryotic cells, and why they carry information that no gene spells out directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The four are measurable outside a cell as well: across a transect of the North Pacific, a falling carbon-to-nitrogen ratio in seawater particles reflected a rise in protein relative to carbohydrate and lipid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Why Do Cells Contain Thousands of Lipid Species? Toward an Integrated Framework for Lipid Diversity in Biological Membranes,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 4089, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27094089. [Open access]","Katrin Mani, “Glycobiology in Human Health and Disease,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 7 (2026): 2951, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27072951. [Open access]","Justin D. Liefer, Angelicque E. White, Zoe V. Finkel, Andrew J. Irwin, Mathilde Dugenne, Keisuke Inomura, François Ribalet, et al., “Latitudinal Patterns in Ocean C:N:P Reflect Phytoplankton Acclimation and Macromolecular Composition,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 46 (2024): e2404460121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2404460121. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are lipids grouped differently from the other three classes?","a":"Because they are grouped by what they do rather than by any repeating subunit, storing energy, forming the bulk of membranes and carrying signals as steroid hormones and messengers."},{"q":"Why do carbohydrates behave least like the other classes?","a":"Because their assembly is not template-driven but emerges from the competing activity of the enzymes that build and trim them, so they carry information no gene spells out directly."},{"q":"Why is the diversity of a cell’s lipids an open question?","a":"Because lipidomics finds a single cell type carrying hundreds to thousands of distinct species, and the review that asks why says the significance of that diversity remains incompletely understood."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/DNA_Structure%2BKey%2BLabelled.pn_NoBB.png/1920px-DNA_Structure%2BKey%2BLabelled.pn_NoBB.png","title":"The structure of DNA","desc":"The double helix labelled: two sugar-phosphate backbones with paired bases between them.","credit":"Zephyris, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DNA_Structure%2BKey%2BLabelled.pn_NoBB.png","alt":"A labelled diagram of the DNA double helix, showing the major and minor grooves and the pairing of purines with pyrimidines."},"wiki":"Biomolecule"},
    "bio-047": {"questions":["Glycogen, starch and cellulose are built only from glucose and differ in their linkages, so one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can store energy where another holds a plant upright.","Because the sugars attached to a protein are assembled without any template, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can carry information that no gene spells out directly."],"abstract":"A <b>carbohydrate</b> is a sugar or a chain of sugars, most of them having the basic formula (CH2O)n, which is where the name comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Simple sugars such as glucose are the major nutrients of cells, and they are joined into longer chains by dehydration reactions that link them through a glycosidic bond between two of their carbons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No other family of biological molecule can be arranged in so many ways from so few parts, since each simple sugar exists in mirror-image d- and l-forms and those with five or more carbons can close into rings that differ in the position of a single group.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Isomers of that kind play a major part in generating the enormous variety of sugars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Glycogen, starch and cellulose are composed entirely of glucose residues and differ in the bonds joining them, two of the three storing sugar while the third is the principal structural material of plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> That structural role makes this the most abundant kind of organic matter on the planet, lignocellulosic biomass — cellulose and hemicellulose together with lignin — being the largest renewable source of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Carbohydrates also carry information, because the sugar structures attached to proteins and lipids are not built from a template but emerge from the competing activity of the enzymes that add, trim and modify them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Glycosylation is on that account the commonest and most diverse modification of proteins in eukaryotes, and the sugar layer at a cell's surface mediates adhesion and recognition.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In nutrition the single word covers foods that behave very differently, and one measure of quality is simply the ratio of carbohydrate to fibre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Among 3,171 participants in a long-running cohort, better carbohydrate quality and a higher intake of fibre-rich foods were favourably associated with the mass and function of the left ventricle, with effect sizes between 10 and 26% of a standard deviation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Adrian Cătălin Puițel and Mircea Teodor Nechita, “Recent Progress on Lignocellulosic-Based Polymeric Materials,” <i>Polymers</i> 18, no. 12 (2026): 1498, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym18121498. [Open access]","Katrin Mani, “Glycobiology in Human Health and Disease,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 7 (2026): 2951, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27072951. [Open access]","So-Yun Yi, Lyn M. Steffen, Weihua Guan, Daniel Duprez, Kamakshi Lakshminarayan, and David R. Jacobs, “Dietary Carbohydrate Quality, Fibre-Rich Food Intake, and Left Ventricular Structure and Function: The CARDIA Study,” <i>European Heart Journal</i> 46, no. 41 (2025): 4329–37, https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehaf406. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can so few parts be arranged in so many ways?","a":"Because each simple sugar exists in mirror-image d- and l-forms, and those with five or more carbons close into rings differing in the position of a single group."},{"q":"Why is one glucose polymer food and another a structural material?","a":"Because glycogen, starch and cellulose are all glucose residues and differ only in the bonds joining them, which is enough to make two stores of sugar and one skeleton for plants."},{"q":"Why is glycosylation the most diverse protein modification in eukaryotes?","a":"Because the sugar structures are not built from a template but emerge from enzymes competing to add, trim and modify them, so the possibilities are not fixed in advance."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png/960px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png","title":"Carbohydrates from one sugar to many","desc":"The same ring unit alone, in pairs and in chains: monosaccharide, disaccharide, oligosaccharide, polysaccharide.","credit":"explorebiology, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Carbohydrate.png","alt":"An illustration showing single green sugar rings, then two joined, then a small group, then a long chain."},"wiki":"Carbohydrate"},
    "bio-048": {"questions":["Ribose carries five carbons and glucose six, so counting the carbons is the usual way of sorting one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from another before anything else is said about it.","Blood is held near 80 to 90 mg per decilitre of one particular <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> between meals, rising to 120 to 140 afterwards before the body brings it down again."],"abstract":"A <b>monosaccharide</b> is a single sugar unit, the smallest carbohydrate and the subunit from which every longer chain of sugars is built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The basic formula is (CH2O)n, and the familiar ones are sorted by how many carbons they carry: five in ribose and deoxyribose, six in glucose, fructose and galactose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A sugar with five or more carbons can close into a ring, and it does so in either of two alternative forms according to the arrangement at one particular carbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each also exists in mirror-image d- and l-forms, and isomers of these kinds are a large part of what makes the sugars so various.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Glucose is the one most of biology runs on, the primary metabolic fuel of mammals and the universal fuel of the fetus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Its concentration in blood is held within a narrow band, about 80 to 90 mg per decilitre 3 or 4 hours after eating, rising to between 120 and 140 after a meal before the body's own feedback brings it back down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Simple sugars are not confined to living things, and an analysis of the Orgueil meteorite published in 2026 detected ribose, arabinose, xylose and lyxose in it, along with the ketopentose ribulose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Several of them show near-racemic distributions, both mirror images present in nearly equal amount, which is consistent with their having formed without any life involved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same work is candid about its limits, noting that sugars remain rare and poorly characterised in meteorites beside amino acids and nucleobases, and that recovery experiments show their abundances are severely underestimated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Why life then uses only the d-forms is unsettled, and one 2026 hypothesis proposes that d-ribose crosses a lipid membrane more readily than its mirror image and is trapped inside once phosphorylated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Mihir N. Nakrani, Robert H. Wineland, and Fatima Anjum, “Physiology, Glucose Metabolism,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560599/. [Open access]","Vanessa Leyva, Manuel Robert, Raphaël Pepino, Jana Bocková, and Cornelia Meinert, “Abiotic Sugar Enantiomers in the CI Carbonaceous Chondrite Orgueil,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 2060, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-68709-5. [Open access]","Vladimir M. Subbotin and Gennady Fiksel, “Stereoselective Phosphorylation of d-Ribose as a Driver of Life's Homochirality,” <i>Life</i> 16, no. 5 (2026): 846, https://doi.org/10.3390/life16050846. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is blood glucose held in so narrow a band?","a":"Because it is the primary metabolic fuel of mammals and the universal fuel of the fetus, so the body’s own feedback returns it to about 80 to 90 mg per decilitre after each meal."},{"q":"Why do near-equal mirror images in a meteorite matter?","a":"Because a near-racemic distribution is consistent with the sugars having formed without any life involved, life using only one hand of each."},{"q":"Why is the meteorite work candid about being incomplete?","a":"Because sugars remain rare and poorly characterised in meteorites beside amino acids and nucleobases, and recovery experiments show their abundances are severely underestimated."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Alpha-D-Glucopyranose.svg/1920px-Alpha-D-Glucopyranose.svg.png","title":"Glucose","desc":"Alpha-D-glucopyranose, the ring form in which glucose mostly exists in solution.","credit":"NEUROtiker, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alpha-D-Glucopyranose.svg","alt":"A structural formula of a six-membered sugar ring with hydroxyl groups around it and a CH2OH group above."},"wiki":"Monosaccharide"},
    "bio-049": {"questions":["Maltose is two glucoses, sucrose is glucose with fructose and lactose is glucose with galactose, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is named by which pair was joined and how.","Sucrose travels out of a leaf through the phloem to wherever a plant needs it, which makes that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a transport form rather than a fuel in its own right."],"abstract":"A <b>disaccharide</b> is two simple sugars joined together, linked through a glycosidic bond between two of their carbons in a dehydration reaction that expels a molecule of water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each monosaccharide carries several free hydroxyl groups able to form that link, so the same two sugars can be joined at different positions to give different substances.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The three met most often are maltose, which is two glucoses; sucrose, which is glucose with fructose; and lactose, which is glucose with galactose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> None of them can be absorbed as it stands, and each must first be split back into its units by an enzyme that puts the water back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lactose is hydrolysed into glucose and galactose by lactase at the brush border of the small intestine, an enzyme whose activity peaks after birth and declines thereafter in most mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Sucrose is the form in which a plant moves sugar about, exported from the leaves through the phloem to wherever growth or storage requires it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How it is loaded differs between species, being driven actively into the apoplast in many herbaceous plants, trapped as larger oligosaccharides in others, or carried passively through interconnected cytoplasm in large trees, whose foliage reaches higher sugar concentrations in consequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Trehalose, two glucoses joined differently again, is neither food nor freight but protection, a non-reducing sugar that stabilises proteins and membranes against drying out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tardigrades that survive desiccation make it, and a 2026 study found predicted trehalose-synthase genes rising in their eggs and dried tuns — though those genes belonged to the animals' bacteria, and whether the microbiome contributes is still unknown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What separates these molecules is largely which hydroxyl groups were joined and in what orientation, which is how two sugars of the same size become a food, a freight and a preservative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Eric Goosenberg and Muriam Afzal, “Lactose Intolerance,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532285/. [Open access]","Pierre Vollenweider, Carlo di Bello, Nathalie Valle, Marco M. Lehmann, Matthias Saurer, Arthur Gessler, and Stanislaus J. Schymanski, “Towards Better Understanding of Assimilate Transfer and Phloem Loading in Conifer Trees,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 77, no. 10 (2026): 2805–9, https://doi.org/10.1093/jxb/erag016. [Open access]","Monika Mioduchowska, Pushpalata Kayastha, Magdalena M. Bartylak, Edyta Konecka, Bayu Brahmantio, Julita Mackiewicz, Wojciech Przybyszewski, et al., “Let's Dry up and Survive Together: Is Anhydrobiosis in Two Paramacrobiotus Species (Tardigrada) Associated with a Specific Microbiome Community?” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 12 (2026): 5256, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27125256. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can the same two sugars make different substances?","a":"Because each monosaccharide carries several free hydroxyl groups able to form the link, so the pair can be joined at different positions and in different orientations."},{"q":"Why must a disaccharide be split before it can be absorbed?","a":"Because none of them can be taken up as it stands: an enzyme has to put the water back and separate the units, as lactase does with lactose at the brush border."},{"q":"Why is the tardigrade trehalose finding not yet an answer?","a":"Because the predicted trehalose-synthase genes rising in eggs and dried tuns belonged to the animals’ bacteria, and whether the microbiome contributes is still unknown."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Saccharose2.svg/1920px-Saccharose2.svg.png","title":"Sucrose","desc":"Two monosaccharides joined: glucose and fructose, linked by the bond that a disaccharide is.","credit":"NEUROtiker, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saccharose2.svg","alt":"A structural formula of two joined sugar rings, one six-membered and one five-membered, sharing an oxygen."},"wiki":"Disaccharide"},
    "bio-050": {"questions":["Starch joins its glucose units one way and cellulose joins them the other, so the bond alone decides whether a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a store of sugar or a structural material.","Dietary fibre is largely the kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a human gut cannot break down, left instead to bacteria in the large intestine that ferment it into short-chain fatty acids."],"abstract":"A <b>polysaccharide</b> is a chain of many simple sugars joined by glycosidic bonds, and unlike a protein or a nucleic acid it has neither a fixed length nor a sequence that must be reproduced exactly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each sugar unit carries several free hydroxyl groups able to form a link, so a chain can branch as well as extend, which is something the other great polymer families cannot do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Glycogen, starch and cellulose are all built from glucose alone and differ in the bond that joins the units and in how often the chain divides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The glucose residues of starch and glycogen are joined in the α configuration and those of cellulose in the β, and that difference alone separates a store of sugar from a structural material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Glycogen is the storage form in animals and starch its counterpart in plants, while cellulose is the principal structural component of the plant cell wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The structural kind dominates the planet: lignocellulosic biomass, largely cellulose and hemicellulose, is the most abundant renewable source of organic matter on Earth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Human digestion cannot touch most of it, and dietary fibre is defined as the carbohydrates and lignin that resist digestion in the small intestine and are then partly or wholly fermented in the large one, yielding short-chain fatty acids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Between 2017 and 2020 American adults averaged 17 g of fibre a day against recommended intakes of 25 to 38 g, so this is a dietary role that most people undersupply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Bacteria build with these molecules too, and <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> defends itself in the cystic fibrosis lung with exopolysaccharides called Psl and Pel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Enzymes that cut those chains improve the reach of antibiotics, though unevenly: one targeting Psl disrupted nine of fourteen clinical isolates and helped in 24 of 42 strain-and-drug pairings, where the Pel-cutting enzyme managed three and five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Chemical Components of a Cell,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/. [Open access]","Adrian Cătălin Puițel and Mircea Teodor Nechita, “Recent Progress on Lignocellulosic-Based Polymeric Materials,” <i>Polymers</i> 18, no. 12 (2026): 1498, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym18121498. [Open access]","Sharon F. Daley and Aparna P. Shreenath, “The Role of Dietary Fiber in Health Promotion and Disease Prevention: A Practical Guide for Clinicians,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559033/. [Open access]","Isaac Martin, Jonathan Chung, Deepa Raju, Yvonne Yau, Amanda J. Morris, Shafinaz Eisha, P. Lynne Howell, and Valerie Waters, “Glycoside Hydrolases Enhance Antibiotic Activity against <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i> Biofilms from Cystic Fibrosis Airways,” <i>Microbiology Spectrum</i> 14, no. 4 (2026): e01354-25, https://doi.org/10.1128/spectrum.01354-25. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a polysaccharide branch where a protein cannot?","a":"Because each sugar unit carries several free hydroxyl groups able to form a link, so a chain can divide as well as extend, and it has no sequence that must be reproduced exactly."},{"q":"Why does one bond configuration separate food from structure?","a":"Because starch and glycogen join their glucose residues in the α configuration and cellulose in the β, and that difference alone makes one a store of sugar and the other a structural material."},{"q":"Why is fibre defined by what digestion cannot do to it?","a":"Because it is the carbohydrate and lignin that resist digestion in the small intestine and are then fermented in the large one, yielding short-chain fatty acids."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Amylose_3Dprojection.svg/1920px-Amylose_3Dprojection.svg.png","title":"Amylose","desc":"A run of glucose units joined end to end, the chain a polysaccharide is made of.","credit":"Hbf878, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amylose_3Dprojection.svg","alt":"A structural formula of four sugar rings joined in a line, with brackets and an n marking the repeat."},"wiki":"Polysaccharide"},
    "bio-051": {"questions":["Amylose is all but unbranched and amylopectin is branched, yet both sit in the same granule of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which makes up about 70% of a wheat grain.","Copy number of the salivary amylase gene runs highest in Indigenous Andean populations, where selection for digesting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> dates to roughly the time the potato was domesticated."],"abstract":"<b>Starch</b> is the storage polysaccharide of plants, a chain of glucose units joined in the α configuration and laid down wherever a plant has more sugar than it presently needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It exists as two molecules within the same granule: amylose, which is essentially unbranched, and amylopectin, which divides repeatedly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The proportions are not fixed, and in one 2026 comparison of wheat cultivars amylose ran between 27 and 31% of the starch, with the grain itself about 70% starch by weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The granules differ too, medium ones measuring roughly 44 to 47 micrometres across and about a quarter of the material being crystalline, and those differences change how a flour behaves in the hand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An animal reaches the glucose inside by hydrolysing those α bonds with amylase, secreted chiefly by the salivary glands and the pancreas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Amylase was among the earliest enzymes ever described, in the first decades of the 19th century, and falls into three classes that differ in where along a chain they cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not all of the starch is reached: the fraction that resists digestion in the small intestine passes on to be fermented in the large one, which is why resistant starch is counted as dietary fibre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much of it a person can digest varies genetically, since the salivary amylase gene AMY1 differs in copy number between individuals and that variation is linked to starch-rich diets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A study of 3,723 people from 85 populations found the highest copy numbers anywhere in Indigenous Peruvian Andean populations, with evidence of positive selection on a haplotype carrying at least five copies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That expansion is dated to about 10,000 years ago, which coincides with the domestication of the potato in the same region.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Zhenbing Ma, Yuanyuan Tian, Rong Xiong, Haibo Lan, Jia Zheng, Shunchuan Sun, Jian Su, Chenyang Wang, and Yi Ma, “Comparative Analysis of the Fine Molecular Structures and Pasting Properties of Zhiqu and Bread Wheat Starch,” <i>Food Chemistry: X</i> 37 (2026): 104080, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fochx.2026.104080. [Open access]","Muhammad Zubair and Thiruvengadam Muniraj, “Analytical and Clinical Perspectives on Amylase,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2026), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557738/. [Open access]","Sharon F. Daley and Aparna P. Shreenath, “The Role of Dietary Fiber in Health Promotion and Disease Prevention: A Practical Guide for Clinicians,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559033/. [Open access]","Kendra Scheer, Luane J. B. Landau, Kelsey Jorgensen, Charikleia Karageorgiou, Lindsey Siao, Can Alkan, Angelis M. Morales Rivera, et al., “Rapid Adaptive Increase of Amylase Gene Copy Number in Indigenous Andeans,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 3822, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-71450-8. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does starch behave differently from one flour to the next?","a":"Because the proportions are not fixed — amylose ran between 27 and 31% across wheat cultivars — and the granules differ in size and in how much of the material is crystalline."},{"q":"Why is some starch counted as dietary fibre?","a":"Because the fraction that resists digestion in the small intestine passes on to be fermented in the large one rather than being absorbed as glucose."},{"q":"Why is the Andean amylase result read as selection rather than chance?","a":"Because the highest copy numbers anywhere were found there with evidence of positive selection on a haplotype of at least five copies, dated to about 10,000 years ago, coinciding with potato domestication."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornstarch_mixed_with_water.jpg/960px-Cornstarch_mixed_with_water.jpg","title":"Cornstarch and water","desc":"Starch grains stirred into water, where they thicken it without dissolving.","credit":"Picasa author kalaya, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cornstarch_mixed_with_water.jpg","alt":"A hand spooning white cornstarch into a bowl of water."},"wiki":"Starch"},
    "bio-052": {"questions":["After a meal the liver lays down <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> under the influence of insulin, giving it back over the following hours as the concentration of glucose in the blood falls.","A faulty enzyme in one storage disease leaves <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> piling up in the liver and kidneys while the patient suffers severe hypoglycaemia between meals."],"abstract":"<b>Glycogen</b> is the storage carbohydrate of animals, composed entirely of glucose molecules in the α configuration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its principal linkage joins carbon 1 of one glucose to carbon 4 of the next, while occasional α(1→6) links attach a new chain to the side of an existing one, so that the molecule divides again and again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Starch is built the same way, its amylopectin branched and its amylose not, which makes the animal store and the plant store a difference of degree rather than of kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each particle is begun on a protein rather than from nothing: glycogenin attaches the first glucose units to itself, and the chains grow outward from that core.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Humans carry two glycogenins, and a 2025 study reports that GYG2 has minimal self-glucosylating activity and acts as a suppressor of glycogen formation while still driving particle assembly, which GYG1 does not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The liver's store serves the whole body: after a meal, insulin has glucose deposited there as glycogen, and over the following hours it is handed back as the concentration in the blood falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> When the handing back fails the consequences are severe, and in glycogen storage disease type I a defective glucose-6-phosphatase leaves glycogen accumulating in the liver and kidneys with severe fasting hypoglycaemia, high blood lipids and an enlarged liver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That condition occurs in about 1 in 100,000 live births, four fifths of cases being the type Ia form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Muscle keeps a separate store, and how much of it a trained athlete needs is genuinely disputed, a 2026 nutrition journal setting the cases for and against low-carbohydrate diets side by side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their agreed statement holds that promoting carbohydrate availability remains the best approach, while allowing that a ketogenic diet raises the capacity to oxidise fat and that even then carbohydrate taken during long events improves performance by preventing hypoglycaemia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Tzu-Han Weng, Yu-Chung Pien, Ching-Jou Chen, Po-Pang Chen, Yu-Ting Tseng, Ying-Chen Chen, Wen-Po Hsiao, et al., “Human Glycogenins Maintain Glucose Homeostasis by Regulating Glycogen Metabolism,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 6556, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-61862-3. [Open access]","Mihir N. Nakrani, Robert H. Wineland, and Fatima Anjum, “Physiology, Glucose Metabolism,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560599/. [Open access]","Mitan Nana, Catherine Anastasopoulou, Nirzar S. Parikh, and Rajni Ahlawat, “Glycogen Storage Disease Type I,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2025), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK534196/. [Open access]","Louise M. Burke and Timothy D. Noakes, “Does a Low-Carbohydrate Diet Impede Endurance Sports Performance? Debate Consensus,” <i>The American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</i> 123, no. 5 (2026): 101271, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajcnut.2026.101271. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the animal and plant stores a difference of degree?","a":"Because starch is built the same way, its amylopectin branched and its amylose not, so glycogen differs from it in how often the chain divides rather than in kind."},{"q":"Why does a glycogen particle need a protein at its centre?","a":"Because it is not begun from nothing: glycogenin attaches the first glucose units to itself, and the chains grow outward from that core."},{"q":"Why is failing to hand the store back so serious?","a":"Because in glycogen storage disease type I a defective glucose-6-phosphatase leaves glycogen accumulating in liver and kidneys, with severe fasting hypoglycaemia, high blood lipids and an enlarged liver."}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Glycogen_phosphorylase_stereo.png/1920px-Glycogen_phosphorylase_stereo.png","title":"glycogen","desc":"Action of glycogen phosphorylase on glycogen (source: D. Voet and J. G. Voet. Biochemistry , Wiley, 2011, p. 641).","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glycogen_phosphorylase_stereo.png","alt":"Glycogen phosphorylase stereo"},"wiki":"Glycogen"},
    "bio-053": {"questions":["Termites live on wood by proxy, depending on gut microbes and flagellated protists to break down the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in it that no animal enzyme of their own can touch.","Even though it is the most abundant renewable organic matter on Earth, the packing of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> microfibrils at the nanoscale is still being argued over."],"abstract":"<b>Cellulose</b> is the principal structural material of the plant cell wall, composed entirely of glucose as starch and glycogen are, but with its units joined in the β rather than the α configuration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That single difference straightens the chain, so that many of them lie alongside one another and bind into fibres, and it puts the linkage out of reach of the enzymes that open a store of starch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a plant it is a building material rather than a reserve, which is how the same sugar that fuels an animal can also hold a tree upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The result is the most abundant renewable source of organic matter on the planet, lignocellulosic biomass being largely cellulose together with hemicellulose and lignin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its fine structure is nonetheless still being worked out, and a 2025 study opens by calling the structure of plant cellulose microfibrils elusive despite the abundance of the material and its industrial use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Solid-state NMR of never-dried plants resolved six distinct glucose environments common to softwood, hardwood and grasses, of which only two lie within the core of a microfibril.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A second study of wheat straw published in 2026 notes that no explicit structural model had yet satisfied X-ray scattering and NMR observations at the same time, and proposes one that does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> So the commonest structural polymer in the living world is one whose arrangement at the nanometre scale is still being settled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Animals that live on it do so at second hand: the gut microbiome of termites is what degrades the lignocellulose they eat, and flagellated protists are essential symbionts of the lower termites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A 2026 metagenomic comparison of two <i>Anacanthotermes</i> species found bacteria making up about 53% of the gut community in both, while the diversity of that community differed between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Adrian Cătălin Puițel and Mircea Teodor Nechita, “Recent Progress on Lignocellulosic-Based Polymeric Materials,” <i>Polymers</i> 18, no. 12 (2026): 1498, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym18121498. [Open access]","Rosalie Cresswell, Parveen Kumar Deralia, Yoshihisa Yoshimi, Tomohiro Kuga, Alberto Echevarría-Poza, W. Trent Franks, Steven P. Brown, Ray Dupree, and Paul Dupree, “Using Solid-State NMR to Understand the Structure of Plant Cellulose,” <i>Journal of the American Chemical Society</i> 147, no. 51 (2025): 47223–36, https://doi.org/10.1021/jacs.5c14452. [Open access]","Yucheng Hu, Pan Chen, Peng Xiao, Shixu Yu, Lingfeng Zhou, Zhe Ling, Yutong Zhu, et al., “Native Architecture of Wheat Straw Cell Walls: A Unified Model from X-ray Scattering and Solid-State NMR,” <i>Journal of the American Chemical Society</i> 148, no. 20 (2026): 20448–63, https://doi.org/10.1021/jacs.5c23116. [Open access]","Ulugbek Togaev, Vartika Mathur, Aziza Rakhmonkulova, Surbhi Agarwal, Aarav Mathur, Shuhrat Turageldiyev, Rasul Ruzmetov, et al., “Comparative Metagenomic Analysis of Gut Microbiota in Anacanthotermes turkestanicus and A. ahngerianus Reveals Diet- and Habitat-Driven Functional Divergence,” <i>Frontiers in Insect Science</i> 6 (2026): 1807673, https://doi.org/10.3389/finsc.2026.1807673. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does one bond configuration turn a food into a building material?","a":"Because the β linkage straightens the chain, so many lie alongside one another and bind into fibres, and it puts the bond out of reach of the enzymes that open a store of starch."},{"q":"Why is the commonest structural polymer still not fully described?","a":"Because its arrangement at the nanometre scale is still being settled: a 2025 study calls plant cellulose microfibril structure elusive, and no model had satisfied X-ray scattering and NMR at once until 2026."},{"q":"Why do animals that live on cellulose do so at second hand?","a":"Because it is the gut microbiome that degrades the lignocellulose — in termites, with flagellated protists as essential symbionts of the lower species."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Cellulose_Struktur_in_Sesselform.svg/1920px-Cellulose_Struktur_in_Sesselform.svg.png","title":"The cellulose repeat unit","desc":"Glucose joined by the beta linkage that makes cellulose straight, strong and indigestible to most animals.","credit":"Chembro-lignin, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cellulose_Struktur_in_Sesselform.svg","alt":"A structural formula of a glucose ring in brackets with an n outside, marking the unit that repeats along the chain."},"wiki":"Cellulose"},
    "bio-054": {"questions":["An insect can only grow by shedding its old cuticle, so it must carry enzymes that digest its own <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and silencing one of them is lethal.","Second only to cellulose in abundance, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> barely accumulates in marine sediments, because bacteria in the sea carry the enzymes to break it apart again."],"abstract":"<b>Chitin</b> is a polysaccharide built from N-acetylglucosamine, a glucose ring carrying an acetylamino group, with the sugars joined end to end by &beta;-1,4 glycosidic bonds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That &beta; linkage is what makes cellulose a fibre rather than a store, and it does the same work here, holding the chains straight so they pack side by side instead of coiling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is the structural material of fungal cell walls and of the cuticle of crustaceans and insects, and some algae build with it as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Only cellulose is more abundant: the living world is estimated to make something on the order of a hundred billion tons of chitin a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost none of it accumulates in marine sediments, because heterotrophic bacteria in the sea carry the enzymes to take it apart and return the carbon and nitrogen locked in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because the material is rigid it cannot stretch, so an arthropod grows only by shedding it, which means digesting part of its own cuticle with chitinases it makes itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the pine sawyer beetle <i>Monochamus alternatus</i>, silencing one such enzyme, MaCht-2, caused molting failure, disordered cuticle ultrastructure, impaired movement and heavy mortality in larvae, pupae and adults alike.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Animals that eat arthropods meet the same polymer from the other side, and mammals break it down with an enzyme called acidic mammalian chitinase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Across 37 rodent species the gene for it, CHIA, is often deleted or turned into a pseudogene in obligate herbivores whose food holds almost no chitin, and the strength of selection on it tracks how much of the diet is invertebrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chitin is also a signal the immune system watches for, and fungi such as <i>Cryptococcus neoformans</i> carry deacetylases that convert wall chitin into chitosan, which blunts the response that exposed chitin would provoke.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Xuebing Ren, Xin Song, Hang Zhang, Guangqiang Wang, Fan Xie, Haerlihashi Muhetaerhan, Lianzhong Ai, and Yanjun Tian, “Genomic and Transcriptomic Analysis Reveals Chitin Metabolic Pathways in the Marine Bacterium <i>Microbulbifer harenosus</i> CGMCC 1.13584T,” <i>BMC Microbiology</i> 26, no. 1 (2026): 562, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12866-026-05115-3. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Siming Fang, Xiaoxiao Chang, Han Chen, and Juan Shi, “RNA Interference Targeting MaCht-2 Induces Severe Molting Defects and Lethality in <i>Monochamus alternatus</i>,” <i>Insects</i> 17, no. 5 (2026): 530, https://doi.org/10.3390/insects17050530. [Open access]","Yuntao Tian, Zhenyu Xiong, Chengyao Yang, Chaoyang Luo, Xionghui Xu, Yangsong Wu, Qiurong Li, et al., “Evolution of Acidic Mammalian Chitinase Gene (<i>CHIA</i>) Is Related to Insectivory Feeding in Rodents,” <i>Ecology and Evolution</i> 16, no. 7 (2026): e74062, https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.74062. [Open access]","Margareta J. Hellmann, Rajendra Upadhya, Evelyn Tchoub, Bruno M. Moerschbacher, Jennifer K. Lodge, and Stefan Cord-Landwehr, “Acetylation and Accessibility of <i>Cryptococcus neoformans</i> Cell Wall Chitosans Influence the Strength of Host Immune Responses,” <i>The Cell Surface</i> 15 (2026): 100175, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tcsw.2026.100175. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does chitin behave like cellulose rather than like a sugar store?","a":"Because its sugars are joined by the same β-1,4 bonds, which hold the chains straight so they pack side by side instead of coiling."},{"q":"Why must an arthropod digest part of itself in order to grow?","a":"Because the material is rigid and cannot stretch, so growth means shedding the cuticle, which it does with chitinases of its own making."},{"q":"Why does the CHIA gene’s fate track diet across rodents?","a":"Because it is often deleted or turned into a pseudogene in obligate herbivores whose food holds almost no chitin, with the strength of selection tracking how much of the diet is invertebrate."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Chitin.svg/1920px-Chitin.svg.png","title":"The chitin repeat unit","desc":"Glucose with a nitrogen-bearing group added, repeated into the material of an insect's cuticle and a fungal wall.","credit":"Dschanz, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chitin.svg","alt":"A structural formula of two joined sugar rings each carrying an acetamido group, bracketed with an n."},"wiki":"Chitin"},
    "bio-055": {"questions":["A gram of fat yields more than twice the energy a gram of carbohydrate does, which is why an animal that must carry its reserves banks them in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> form rather than sugar.","Mass-spectrometry surveys find more than a thousand distinct <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> species in a single mammalian cell, and nobody yet knows why a cell should need so many."],"abstract":"<b>Lipids</b> are a diverse group of small molecules, hydrophobic or amphipathic, whose chemical structures vary widely, so the category is held together by how its members behave in water rather than by a common backbone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The simplest are the fatty acids, long hydrocarbon chains ending in a carboxyl group, whose nonpolar carbon-hydrogen bonds cannot interact with water; that hydrophobicity governs much of how the more complex lipids behave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cells put them to three uses: storing energy, building membranes and carrying signals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fats, three fatty acids joined to a glycerol, are the storage form, and they yield more than twice as much energy per unit weight as carbohydrates, which lets an animal hold the same reserve in less than half the body weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Phospholipids, two fatty acids attached to a polar head group, are the principal components of cell membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That tidy list understates the category, because a single cell type can hold hundreds to thousands of distinct lipid species, and mammalian cells more than a thousand, differing in head group, chain length and degree of unsaturation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Why a cell should maintain so many is unsettled, and the explanations on offer run from the physics of membranes through specific binding to proteins to signalling and evolutionary history.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Part of the answer is that the mixture is tuned rather than fixed: cold packs membrane lipids more tightly, so organisms vary the proportion carrying unsaturated chains to hold fluidity steady, a response called homeoviscous adaptation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <i>Escherichia coli</i> completes it within a single generation of a temperature shock, using a valve that shunts synthesis between the saturated and unsaturated branches and a feedback loop that deliberately overshoots before settling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How much of the variety is strictly necessary is another open question, since a synthetic compartment can be made from a single lipid species and how complex a lipidome a self-reproducing cell needs is not known.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Maria Fedorova, Anne K. Bendt, Justine Bertrand-Michel, Oliver Fiehn, Joanna Godzien, Laura Goracci, Florian Gruber, et al., “A Lipidomics Roadmap: From Basic Research to Societal Challenges,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 4778, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-73797-4. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Why Do Cells Contain Thousands of Lipid Species? Toward an Integrated Framework for Lipid Diversity in Biological Membranes,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 4089, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27094089. [Open access]","Loles Hoogerland, Stefan Pieter Hendrik van den Berg, Yixing Suo, Yuta W. Moriuchi, Adja Zoumaro-Djayoon, Esther Geurken, Flora Yang, et al., “A Temperature-Sensitive Metabolic Valve and a Transcriptional Feedback Loop Drive Rapid Homeoviscous Adaptation in <i>Escherichia coli</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15, no. 1 (2024): 9386, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-53677-5. [Open access]","Sergiy Gan, Victoria Scarpelli, and Marten Exterkate, “Exploring Lipid Diversity and Minimalism to Define Membrane Requirements for Synthetic Cells,” <i>FEBS Letters</i> 599, no. 24 (2025): 3569–90, https://doi.org/10.1002/1873-3468.70131. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is this category defined by behaviour rather than by structure?","a":"Because its members' chemical structures vary widely and what they share is being hydrophobic or amphipathic — how they behave in water rather than any common backbone."},{"q":"Why is fat the storage form rather than carbohydrate?","a":"Because fats yield more than twice as much energy per unit weight, which lets an animal hold the same reserve in less than half the body weight."},{"q":"Why do organisms change their lipid mixture with temperature?","a":"Because cold packs membrane lipids more tightly, so they vary the proportion carrying unsaturated chains to hold fluidity steady — homeoviscous adaptation, done within a generation in E. coli."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Common_lipid_types.svg/1920px-Common_lipid_types.svg.png","title":"Four kinds of lipid","desc":"Cholesterol, a free fatty acid, a triglyceride and a phospholipid, drawn to the same scale.","credit":"User:Innerstream, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Common_lipid_types.svg","alt":"Four structural formulae stacked and labelled: a four-ringed steroid, a single long chain, three chains on a glycerol, and two chains with a phosphate head."},"wiki":"Lipid"},
    "bio-056": {"questions":["Adrenaline does not reach the fat itself but unlocks it, phosphorylating the protein that coats the droplet so the lipases can begin cutting a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> apart.","Because it cannot dissolve in blood, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> travels in a lipoprotein particle, and the level of those particles is what a routine blood test reports."],"abstract":"A <b>triglyceride</b>, or triacylglycerol, is three fatty acids joined to a single glycerol molecule, and it is the form in which fatty acids are stored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With all three of the glycerol's hydroxyls taken up in ester bonds the molecule keeps no polar group, so it is insoluble in water and gathers as fat droplets in the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is what makes it worth using as a fuel store, since fats yield more than twice as much energy per unit weight as carbohydrates and let an animal carry a given reserve in less than half the body weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Building one ends with diacylglycerol acyltransferase, which esterifies the third fatty acid onto a diacylglycerol and catalyses the committed, rate-limiting step of the pathway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plants run the same chemistry into their seeds, which is where seed oil comes from; oats hold more of it than any other cereal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Getting the energy back out is lipolysis, and three enzymes take the fatty acids off in turn: adipose triglyceride lipase, hormone-sensitive lipase and monoacylglycerol lipase, leaving free fatty acids and glycerol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The store is locked rather than merely kept, because perilipin-1 coats the droplet and holds the first lipase's coactivator away from it until adrenaline, acting through protein kinase A, phosphorylates the perilipin and lets go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is not the only switch, and work on adipose tissue in the bone marrow, which resists the usual cues, describes a route driven by low glucose and low insulin that needs no adrenaline at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because a triglyceride will not dissolve in blood either, it travels inside lipoprotein particles, chylomicrons from the gut after a fatty meal and very-low-density lipoprotein from the liver, whose cargo lipoprotein lipase cuts up at the tissue that needs it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those particles and the remnants they leave are what a blood triglyceride reading measures, and their disordered handling raises the risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Yan Sun, Jinzhou Yang, Ruirui Hu, Chen Li, Xiping Sun, Zhiwei Zhang, Runzhi Li, and Jinai Xue, “Systematic Analysis of the Diacylglycerol Acyltransferase (DGAT) Genes in Oat and Functional Characterization of AsDGAT2-1 Involved in Oil Accumulation and Cold Stress Tolerance,” <i>BMC Plant Biology</i> 26, no. 1 (2026): 398, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12870-026-08254-x. [Open access]","Courtney L. Bordelon and Jacqueline M. Stephens, “Lipolysis in Health and Disease: Pathways, Regulation, and Metabolic Consequences,” <i>Current Diabetes Reports</i> 26, no. 1 (2026): 8, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11892-026-01624-7. [Open access]","Xiao Zhang, Sreejith S. Panicker, Jordan M. Bollinger, Anurag Majumdar, Rami Kheireddine, Lila F. Dabill, Clara Kim, et al., “A Catecholamine-Independent Pathway Controlling Adaptive Adipocyte Lipolysis,” <i>Nature Metabolism</i> 8, no. 1 (2026): 96–115, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42255-025-01424-5. [Open access]","Henry N. Ginsberg and Karin E. Bornfeldt, “Triglyceride-Rich Lipoprotein Metabolism and Cardiovascular Risk in Diabetes: Bedside to Bench to Bedside,” <i>Circulation Research</i> 138, no. 12 (2026): e327267, https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCRESAHA.125.327267. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a triglyceride gather as droplets rather than dissolving?","a":"Because all three of the glycerol’s hydroxyls are taken up in ester bonds, so the molecule keeps no polar group and is insoluble in water."},{"q":"Why is the store described as locked rather than merely kept?","a":"Because perilipin-1 coats the droplet and holds the first lipase’s coactivator away until adrenaline, acting through protein kinase A, phosphorylates the perilipin and lets go."},{"q":"Why must fat travel inside particles in the blood?","a":"Because it will not dissolve there either, so it moves as chylomicrons from the gut and very-low-density lipoprotein from the liver, cut up by lipoprotein lipase at the tissue that needs it."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Triglyceride.png/960px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Triglyceride.png","title":"A triglyceride","desc":"Three fatty acid tails on a glycerol head, joined by ester bonds.","credit":"explorebiology, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Triglyceride.png","alt":"An illustration of a glycerol backbone with three long fatty acid chains attached, the ester bonds marked."},"wiki":"Triglyceride"},
    "bio-057": {"questions":["Mitochondria dismantle one by clipping two carbons off the end at a time, and the chain has to be carried in on a carnitine shuttle before any <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be burnt.","Two of them, linoleic and alpha-linolenic, are called essential because no human enzyme can make either, so this kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to come from food."],"abstract":"A <b>fatty acid</b> is the simplest kind of lipid: a long hydrocarbon chain with a carboxyl group at one end, most often sixteen or eighteen carbons long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chain carries only nonpolar carbon-hydrogen bonds and cannot interact with water, and that hydrophobicity accounts for much of how the larger lipids built from fatty acids behave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Chains are written as a pair of numbers giving length and the count of double bonds, so palmitate is 16:0, stearate 18:0 and oleate 18:1.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Making one is the work of fatty acid synthase, which assembles saturated long chains, predominantly palmitate, from acetyl-CoA and malonyl-CoA using NADPH.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Taking one apart runs the other way, and mitochondrial beta-oxidation clips off acetyl-CoA to leave a chain two carbons shorter, over and over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A long chain cannot simply drift into a mitochondrion, so it is attached to carnitine and carried across, a transfer that is the rate-limiting step of the whole oxidation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each round yields one acetyl-CoA along with a reduced flavin adenine dinucleotide and a reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, which is why fat is such a productive fuel during fasting and hard exercise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two fatty acids are exceptions to all this, because humans lack the enzymes to build linoleic acid or alpha-linolenic acid and must eat them instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> George Burr showed as much in 1929, when rats on a fat-free diet fell ill and a little linoleic acid put them right again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those chains have to be made by something, and much of the world's supply begins in marine diatoms and other microalgae at the base of the ocean food web, whose fatty acid profiles shift measurably under warming and acidification.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Jacob T. Talley and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Fatty Acid Oxidation,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556002/. [Open access]","Tongchun Lan, Xinru Zhang, Jiarui Xie, Xu Xing, Chunyan Wang, and Xiaofeng Ma, “Inhibitory Effects and the Potential Mechanism of Phloretin on Animal Fatty Acid Synthase,” <i>ACS Omega</i> 10, no. 35 (2025): 40392–401, https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.5c05729. [Open access]","Jodi Wolff, Mary Petrea Cober, and Katie A. Huff, “Essential Fatty Acid Deficiency in Parenteral Nutrition: Historical Perspective and Modern Solutions, a Narrative Review,” <i>Nutrition in Clinical Practice</i> 40, no. 2 (2025): 350–67, https://doi.org/10.1002/ncp.11278. [Open access]","Maria Nicoară, Charlotte L. Briddon, Alexandra Mortu, Adriana Hegedűs, Emese Gal, Horia Leonard Banciu, and Bogdan Drugă, “Warming and Acidification Alter Essential Fatty Acid Profiles in Marine Diatom <i>Skeletonema marinoi</i>,” <i>Journal of Plankton Research</i> 48, no. 4 (2026): fbag057, https://doi.org/10.1093/plankt/fbag057. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why must a long fatty acid be carried into a mitochondrion?","a":"Because it cannot simply drift in, so it is attached to carnitine and ferried across, a transfer that is the rate-limiting step of the whole oxidation."},{"q":"Why is fat so productive a fuel during fasting and hard exercise?","a":"Because each round of beta-oxidation clips off an acetyl-CoA along with a reduced flavin adenine dinucleotide and a reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, over and over down the chain."},{"q":"Why are two fatty acids exceptions that must be eaten?","a":"Because humans lack the enzymes to build linoleic acid or alpha-linolenic acid, which Burr showed in 1929 when rats on a fat-free diet fell ill and a little linoleic acid put them right."}],"wiki":"Fatty acid"},
    "bio-058": {"questions":["Nine randomised trials covering 13,532 people found no significant fall in mortality from cutting <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> intake, which is not the answer the food labels imply.","Invented in the early 1900s as a cheap stand-in for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> from butter and animals, partially hydrogenated oil kept longer and survived repeated deep frying."],"abstract":"A <b>saturated fat</b> is one whose fatty acid chains carry no carbon-carbon double bonds, every carbon being bonded to as many hydrogen atoms as it can hold, where an unsaturated fat has one or more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That single difference decides how the chains pack against one another, and unsaturated lipids tend to raise membrane fluidity while saturated ones promote a more ordered state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cold reduces fluidity by pressing membrane lipids closer together, so organisms counteract it by varying the proportion of chains that disrupt the packing, unsaturated ones among them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Saturation and chain length are the two variables a cell routinely adjusts to keep membrane fluidity inside the narrow range it has to stay in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The difference tells in a kitchen as well as a membrane, and partially hydrogenated oils were developed in the early 1900s as a cheap replacement for butter and other animal fats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Industry liked them because they are inexpensive, they extend the shelf life of processed food and they need changing less often than natural oils during deep frying.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The process also yields industrially produced trans fat, estimated to cause up to 500,000 deaths a year and entirely replaceable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Since 2018 the World Health Organization has run a global elimination programme against it, and 43 countries have adopted best-practice regulations covering a further 3.2 billion people.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Saturated fat itself is a harder case than the labels suggest: a meta-analysis of nine randomised trials with 13,532 participants found no significant difference in cardiovascular or all-cause mortality between restricting it and not, and concluded that restriction cannot be recommended at present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That is a finding about the trials rather than about food, and commentary on it argues the question turns on what the saturated fat is replaced by and which foods it arrives in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Why Do Cells Contain Thousands of Lipid Species? Toward an Integrated Framework for Lipid Diversity in Biological Membranes,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 4089, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27094089. [Open access]","Loles Hoogerland, Stefan Pieter Hendrik van den Berg, Yixing Suo, Yuta W. Moriuchi, Adja Zoumaro-Djayoon, Esther Geurken, Flora Yang, et al., “A Temperature-Sensitive Metabolic Valve and a Transcriptional Feedback Loop Drive Rapid Homeoviscous Adaptation in <i>Escherichia coli</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 15, no. 1 (2024): 9386, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-53677-5. [Open access]","Lindsay Steele, Elizabeth Drummond, Chizuru Nishida, Rain Yamamoto, Francesco Branca, Cristina Parsons Perez, Lorena Allemandi, et al., “Ending Trans Fat: The First-Ever Global Elimination Program for a Noncommunicable Disease Risk Factor,” <i>Journal of the American College of Cardiology</i> 84, no. 7 (2024): 663–74, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2024.04.067. [Open access]","Satoru Yamada, Tomomi Shirai, Sakiko Inaba, Gaku Inoue, Minami Torigoe, and Naoto Fukuyama, “Saturated Fat Restriction for Cardiovascular Disease Prevention: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Randomized Controlled Trials,” <i>JMA Journal</i> 8, no. 2 (2025): 395–407, https://doi.org/10.31662/jmaj.2024-0324. [Open access]","Atsushi Mizuno, “There Is More Than Meets the Label: Rethinking Saturated Fat and Cardiovascular Health,” <i>JMA Journal</i> 8, no. 2 (2025): 408–10, https://doi.org/10.31662/jmaj.2025-0120. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does one difference in the chain change a membrane?","a":"Because carbon-carbon double bonds decide how chains pack against one another, so unsaturated lipids tend to raise membrane fluidity while saturated ones promote a more ordered state."},{"q":"Why did industry adopt partially hydrogenated oils?","a":"Because they are inexpensive, extend the shelf life of processed food and need changing less often than natural oils during deep frying, which is why they replaced butter and animal fats."},{"q":"Why is saturated fat a harder case than the labels suggest?","a":"Because a meta-analysis of nine randomised trials with 13,532 participants found no significant difference in cardiovascular or all-cause mortality between restricting it and not."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Saturated_Fatty_Acid.png/960px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Saturated_Fatty_Acid.png","title":"saturated fat","desc":"A lipid which has no double bonds in its hydrocarbon chain.","credit":"explorebiology, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Saturated_Fatty_Acid.png","alt":"XBio illustration – Saturated Fatty Acid"},"wiki":"Saturated fat"},
    "bio-059": {"questions":["Flippases and scramblases spend energy keeping each kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on the face of the membrane where it belongs, and a dying cell stops paying.","Some enveloped viruses carry a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> normally hidden inside a living cell on their outer surface instead, so a scavenging cell swallows them as apoptotic debris."],"abstract":"A <b>phospholipid</b> is two fatty acids joined to a polar head group, and in the glycerol phospholipids those chains are bound to a glycerol whose third carbon carries a phosphate instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That phosphate usually has a small polar molecule attached to it, choline or ethanolamine or serine or inositol, and which one it carries is what names the lipid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sphingomyelin is the exception, the only non-glycerol phospholipid in cell membranes, its two chains hanging from a head group built on serine rather than glycerol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> All of them therefore have two hydrophobic tails and a hydrophilic head, which makes the molecule amphipathic, part water-soluble and part not, and that property is the basis on which biological membranes form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a strong enough property to work alone, since a synthetic compartment can be assembled out of a single lipid species with nothing else present.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The head group is not decoration, and phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate is cut by phospholipase C into diacylglycerol and inositol trisphosphate, which in turn releases calcium inside the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The two faces of a membrane are not alike either, since phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin sit mostly in the outer leaflet while phosphatidylserine, phosphatidylethanolamine and phosphatidylinositol are enriched in the inner one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That arrangement is held rather than settled, maintained by ATP-driven flippases, floppases and scramblases that go on moving lipids across the bilayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When a cell dies the arrangement collapses and phosphatidylserine appears on the outside, where it acts as an eat-me signal for scavenging cells, although on its own it is not enough to make one engulf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Several enveloped viruses exploit exactly that, carrying phosphatidylserine on their own membranes so that a cell takes them in as though they were the debris of a dead one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Sergiy Gan, Victoria Scarpelli, and Marten Exterkate, “Exploring Lipid Diversity and Minimalism to Define Membrane Requirements for Synthetic Cells,” <i>FEBS Letters</i> 599, no. 24 (2025): 3569–90, https://doi.org/10.1002/1873-3468.70131. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Why Do Cells Contain Thousands of Lipid Species? Toward an Integrated Framework for Lipid Diversity in Biological Membranes,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 4089, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27094089. [Open access]","Kyung-Hee Kim and Byong Chul Yoo, “Metabolic Control of Membrane Lipid Asymmetry in Cancer,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 3846, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27093846. [Open access]","Melinda A. Brindley and Michael A. Pisciotta, “Viral Apoptotic Mimicry and the Role of Phosphatidylserine Receptors,” <i>mSphere</i> 10, no. 10 (2025): e00089-25, https://doi.org/10.1128/msphere.00089-25. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a membrane form from these molecules alone?","a":"Because each has two hydrophobic tails and a hydrophilic head, and that amphipathic property is strong enough on its own that a compartment can be assembled from a single lipid species."},{"q":"Why is the difference between a membrane’s two faces maintained rather than settled?","a":"Because ATP-driven flippases, floppases and scramblases go on moving lipids across the bilayer to keep phosphatidylserine and its neighbours on the inner leaflet."},{"q":"Why does that arrangement matter when a cell dies?","a":"Because it collapses and phosphatidylserine appears on the outside as an eat-me signal — which several enveloped viruses exploit by carrying it on their own membranes."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Phospholipid.png/960px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Phospholipid.png","title":"A phospholipid","desc":"One head that water is drawn to and two tails that it is not, which is why these molecules make membranes.","credit":"explorebiology, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_Phospholipid.png","alt":"An illustration of a phospholipid with a labelled choline, phosphate and glycerol head and two hydrocarbon tails, one of them kinked."},"wiki":"Phospholipid"},
    "bio-060": {"questions":["Being a lipid, one of these crosses the membrane rather than stopping at it, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is met by a receptor inside the cell that switches genes on directly.","Fungi build ergosterol where animals build cholesterol, and an antifungal azole exploits that gap in the pathway to this kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without harming its patient."],"abstract":"A <b>steroid</b> is a lipid built on four fused hydrocarbon rings rather than on the long straight chains of a fat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cholesterol is the familiar one, and its rings are strongly hydrophobic while the single hydroxyl group at one end is weakly hydrophilic, which makes the whole molecule amphipathic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also the precursor of the steroid hormones, a diverse set of chemical messengers that all carry those same four rings and differ in the functional groups attached to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Making one begins with the steroidogenic acute regulatory protein, which moves cholesterol to the inner mitochondrial membrane, where the enzyme CYP11A1 converts it to pregnenolone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The rest follows from there, pregnenolone giving progesterone and the corticosteroids while dehydroepiandrosterone gives the androgens and oestrogens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Because a steroid is a lipid it crosses a membrane rather than stopping at it, and its receptor waits inside the cell and works as a ligand-activated transcription factor, so the hormone reaches the genes directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vitamin D belongs to the family as well, a secosteroid made in skin from 7-dehydrocholesterol, an intermediate of cholesterol synthesis, under ultraviolet B light, and acting through a receptor of exactly that kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plants build their own, and the brassinosteroids, the sixth class of plant hormone to be identified, govern growth and environmental adaptation in rice and are now a target for breeding.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fungi build ergosterol where animals build cholesterol, which is why irradiating a mushroom yields vitamin D2 rather than D3.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That same gap is what the azole antifungals exploit, blocking lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase in the ergosterol pathway, and point mutations in the gene for it now turn up in <i>Aspergillus fumigatus</i> worldwide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Kha Chin Long and Elena Aisha Azizan, “The Adrenal Gland and Primary Aldosteronism: Anatomy, Steroidogenesis, Regulation, and Genetic Insights,” <i>Frontiers in Endocrinology</i> 16 (2026): 1736534, https://doi.org/10.3389/fendo.2025.1736534. [Open access]","Marianna Raczyk and Carsten Carlberg, “From Sunlight to Signaling: Evolutionary Integration of Vitamin D and Sterol Metabolism,” <i>Metabolites</i> 16, no. 1 (2026): 74, https://doi.org/10.3390/metabo16010074. [Open access]","Shuai Li, HanJing Sha, JinHui Zhang, ZhongHua Wei, LiChao Liu, LongNan Men, ZhongHua Sun, et al., “Harnessing Brassinosteroid Signaling in Rice: From Molecular Pathways to Environmentally Adaptive Breeding,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 17 (2026): 1781641, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2026.1781641. [Open access]","Anneke Fengler, Oliver Bader, Oliver Cornely, Oliver Kurzai, Jacques F. Meis, Florent Morio, and Axel Hamprecht, “Global Epidemiology of Azole Resistance in <i>Aspergillus fumigatus</i>,” <i>JAC-Antimicrobial Resistance</i> 8, no. 1 (2026): dlaf219, https://doi.org/10.1093/jacamr/dlaf219. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a steroid hormone reach the genes directly?","a":"Because it is a lipid and crosses a membrane rather than stopping at it, so its receptor waits inside the cell and works as a ligand-activated transcription factor."},{"q":"Why do so many different messengers come from one molecule?","a":"Because the steroid hormones all carry cholesterol’s four fused rings and differ only in the functional groups attached to them, branching from pregnenolone onwards."},{"q":"Why can an antifungal drug attack a pathway animals also have?","a":"Because fungi build ergosterol where animals build cholesterol, so the azoles block lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase in a step that is the fungus’s alone."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Trimethyl_steroid-nomenclature.svg/1920px-Trimethyl_steroid-nomenclature.svg.png","title":"The steroid skeleton","desc":"Four fused rings, lettered A to D, with every carbon numbered: the frame every steroid is built on.","credit":"NEUROtiker ( talk ), Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trimethyl_steroid-nomenclature.svg","alt":"A structural formula of four joined carbon rings labelled A, B, C and D, with the positions numbered."},"wiki":"Steroid"},
    "bio-061": {"questions":["Deep learning now predicts the folded shape of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> straight from its sequence, though it still does poorly on disordered regions and large assemblies.","Enamel and collagen sequences read from fossils 1 million years old show that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outlasts the DNA that specified it, which is why palaeontologists now look for them."],"abstract":"A <b>protein</b> is a polymer of amino acids, of which twenty kinds are used, and proteins are the most diverse of all the macromolecules, a single cell holding several thousand different ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They serve as structural components of cells and tissues, they transport and store small molecules as haemoglobin carries oxygen, they carry information between cells as protein hormones, and they defend against infection as antibodies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The most fundamental role is catalysis, since as enzymes they speed nearly every chemical reaction in a living system, so that proteins direct virtually all of a cell's activities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The name records that judgement, coming from the Greek <i>proteios</i>, meaning ‘of the first rank’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Which protein a chain becomes depends on the order of its amino acids, each of which carries a distinctive side chain whose chemistry decides the part it plays.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Working out what shape a given sequence folds into was for decades a problem solved one protein at a time, by measurement rather than calculation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Deep learning changed that, and millions of structure models have been generated in a very short space of time, led by AlphaFold, which predicts to near-experimental accuracy directly from a sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has not closed the problem, because prediction remains weakest for intrinsically disordered regions, for ligand-bound states, for post-translational modifications and for large multi-component assemblies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Counting proteins is harder than counting genes, since one gene no longer means one protein: alternative translation start sites alone yield several proteoforms from a single transcript, differing in activity, location and dynamics.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Proteins also outlast the DNA that specified them, and enamel and collagen sequences have been recovered from fossil material at least 1 million years old and used to test how extinct hominids are related.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Martin Luke Rennie and Michael R. Oliver, “Emerging Frontiers in Protein Structure Prediction Following the AlphaFold Revolution,” <i>Journal of the Royal Society Interface</i> 22, no. 225 (2025): 20240886, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2024.0886. [Open access]","Mandarina Qing Cheng Li, Sihan Wang, Shi-Ruei Lin, Li Eric Ngok Ting, Zhi-Hong Wan, Guodong Xie, and Jane Zhang, “Advantages and Limitations of AlphaFold in Structural Biology: Insights from Recent Studies,” <i>The Protein Journal</i> 45, no. 1 (2026): 22–38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10930-025-10310-8. [Open access]","Evan J. Morrison and Olivia S. Rissland, “The Generation and Consequences of N-terminal Proteoform Diversity,” <i>Cell Reports</i> 44, no. 9 (2025): 116275, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2025.116275. [Open access]","Ioannis Patramanis, Laurits Skov, Enrico Cappellini, and Fernando Racimo, “Assessing the Potential of Ancient Protein Sequences in the Study of Hominid Evolution,” <i>Genome Biology and Evolution</i> 18, no. 3 (2026): evag035, https://doi.org/10.1093/gbe/evag035. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is catalysis called the most fundamental of the roles?","a":"Because as enzymes proteins speed nearly every chemical reaction in a living system, so they direct virtually all of a cell’s activities rather than merely taking part in them."},{"q":"Why has structure prediction not closed the folding problem?","a":"Because prediction remains weakest for intrinsically disordered regions, for ligand-bound states, for post-translational modifications and for large multi-component assemblies."},{"q":"Why is counting proteins harder than counting genes?","a":"Because one gene no longer means one protein: alternative translation start sites alone yield several proteoforms from a single transcript, differing in activity, location and dynamics."}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/CHOP_protein_structure.png/1920px-CHOP_protein_structure.png","title":"protein","desc":"CHOP protein displays a classic example of a transcription factor with a leucine zipper (bZIP) domain.","credit":"Mashaalx, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHOP_protein_structure.png","alt":"Ribbon diagram of a protein, the CHOP transcription factor, showing its coiled leucine zipper"},"wiki":"Protein"},
    "bio-062": {"questions":["Nine of them cannot be built by any human cell, so this kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has to come from food and is called essential for that reason alone.","Every living thing builds proteins from the left-handed mirror form only, and why an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> should have that handedness at all is still an unsettled question."],"abstract":"An <b>amino acid</b> is a carbon atom bonded to a carboxyl group, an amino group, a hydrogen atom and a distinctive side chain, and the chemistry of that side chain decides what part the molecule plays in a protein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The twenty used in proteins fall into four groups by side chain: ten nonpolar, five polar but uncharged, three basic and two acidic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nonpolar side chains do not interact with water and tend to end up in the interior of a folded protein, while the polar ones form hydrogen bonds with water and sit on the outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cysteine is worth singling out, because the sulfhydryl group on its side chain lets disulfide bonds form between two cysteines and hold a structure together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nine of the twenty cannot be made by human or other mammalian cells and must come from food: histidine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan and valine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A further group counts as semi-essential, made by the body ordinarily but needed from the diet during growth, pregnancy and other periods of stress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The twenty are not a hard limit either, and bacteria have been engineered to make non-canonical amino acids and read them into proteins through an expanded genetic code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> What is universal is the handedness: every organism builds its proteins from the left-handed mirror form alone, and how that single-handedness arose is still argued over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> After an organism dies the handedness slowly decays towards a mixture, so the ratio of right-handed to left-handed forms measures how far the protein has broken down and, where the rate is predictable, how long ago that was.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Dating the Schöningen hunting spears that way moved them from roughly 300,000 years old to about 200,000, and with them the record of when hunting became a communal business.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Michael J. Lopez and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Essential Amino Acids,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2024), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557845/. [Open access]","Jingxuan Zhang, Keying Yu, Yali Xu, Wushuang Zhao, Yulian Li, Ying Wang, Florian P. Seebeck, et al., “A Robust Platform Streamlining Aromatic Noncanonical Amino Acid Biosynthesis and Genetic Code Expansion in <i>Escherichia coli</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 8605, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63679-6. [Open access]","S. Furkan Ozturk and Dimitar D. Sasselov, “Life's Homochirality: Across a Prebiotic Network,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 34 (2025): e2505126122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2505126122. [Open access]","Jarod M. Hutson, Felix Bittmann, Peter Fischer, Alejandro García-Moreno, Sabine Gaudzinski-Windheuser, Ellie Nelson, José E. Ortiz, et al., “Revised Age for Schöningen Hunting Spears Indicates Intensification of Neanderthal Cooperative Behavior around 200,000 Years Ago,” <i>Science Advances</i> 11, no. 19 (2025): eadv0752, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adv0752. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a side chain decide where a residue ends up in a fold?","a":"Because nonpolar side chains do not interact with water and tend to end up in the interior, while polar ones form hydrogen bonds with water and sit on the outside."},{"q":"Why must nine amino acids come from food?","a":"Because human and other mammalian cells cannot make them — histidine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan and valine."},{"q":"Why can handedness be used to date a fossil protein?","a":"Because after death the single left-handed form slowly decays towards a mixture, so the ratio measures how far the protein has broken down and, where the rate is predictable, how long ago."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Nonproteinogenic_AAs.svg/1920px-Nonproteinogenic_AAs.svg.png","title":"What counts as an amino acid","desc":"The twenty encoded during translation, the two special ones, and the many added afterwards or made outside protein.","credit":"Matteo Ferla (aka. Squidonius in Wikipedia), CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nonproteinogenic_AAs.svg","alt":"A diagram of overlapping circles dividing amino acids into stereoisomers, the twenty proteinogenic ones, and those added after translation."},"wiki":"Amino acid"},
    "bio-063": {"questions":["The ribosome makes each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at a centre built of RNA rather than protein, so the central reaction of protein synthesis is catalysed by a ribozyme.","Chains of amino acids must have existed before ribosomes did, so how the first <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was made without one is still an open question about life's origin."],"abstract":"A <b>peptide bond</b> joins the amino group of one amino acid to the carboxyl group of another, and a chain of them is a polypeptide, usually hundreds or thousands of residues long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such a chain has two unlike ends, an amino or N terminus and a carboxyl or C terminus, and it is both built and written from the N end to the C end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bond itself is planar and permits no rotation, while the other two backbone bonds each residue contributes do rotate, so the chain behaves as a run of rigid plates hinged between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That constraint, together with the requirement that no two atoms overlap, severely restricts the conformations a chain can take, although a long one can still fold in an enormous number of ways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What a protein is, then, is a polypeptide with one particular sequence, a point settled in 1953 when Frederick Sanger worked out the first complete one, for insulin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In a living cell the bond is made by the ribosome, at the peptidyl transferase centre in its large subunit, which also carries out the hydrolysis that releases a finished chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That centre is built from ribosomal RNA rather than protein, so the reaction at the heart of protein synthesis is catalysed by a ribozyme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The ribosome is not the only route, and a class of enzymes called peptide aminoacyl-tRNA ligases extends a peptide at its C terminus without any template, adding amino acids the genetic code does not encode.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bond will also form with no enzyme at all, and simulations of an air-water interface have it assembling rapidly there by an isomerisation and elimination route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That last point matters because chains of amino acids had to exist before ribosomes did, and how the bond was first made in a prebiotic world remains unclear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Letian Bao and Anthony C. Forster, “Fast Peptide Bond Formation and Release by the Ribosomal Large Subunit,” <i>Journal of Biological Chemistry</i> 301, no. 7 (2025): 110336, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2025.110336. [Open access]","Amy M. Weeks, “PEARLs of Wisdom for Ribosome-Independent Peptide Bond Synthesis,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 17 (2025): e2504930122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2504930122. [Open access]","Deming Xia, Fanqi Zeng, Wanting Chen, Hui Zhao, Hong-bin Xie, Jingwen Chen, and Joseph S. Francisco, “Accelerated Peptide Bond Formation at Air-Water Interfaces,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 12 (2025): e2501323122, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2501323122. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a chain behave as rigid plates on hinges?","a":"Because the peptide bond itself is planar and permits no rotation, while the other two backbone bonds each residue contributes do rotate."},{"q":"Why does that constraint matter for folding?","a":"Because it, together with the requirement that no two atoms overlap, severely restricts the conformations a chain can take, though a long one can still fold in an enormous number of ways."},{"q":"Why is the reaction at the heart of protein synthesis unusual?","a":"Because the peptidyl transferase centre is built from ribosomal RNA rather than protein, so the bond is made by a ribozyme."}],"wiki":"Peptide bond"},
    "bio-064": {"questions":["Swapping one glutamate for a valine at the sixth position of the beta-globin chain is a change to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that produces haemoglobin S and, inherited twice, sickle cell anaemia.","Deep mutational scanning measures thousands of variants of one protein at once, which is a way of asking which changes to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> actually matter."],"abstract":"The <b>primary structure</b> of a protein is the order of the amino acids along its chain, and that order is the defining characteristic of a protein: not a class of similar molecules but one particular sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is written from the amino terminus to the carboxyl terminus, which is the order the chain is built in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor is it the protein's own choice, since the sequence is determined by the order of nucleotides in the gene that encodes it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A single position in that order can matter enormously, and in the sickle cell mutation a negatively charged glutamate is replaced by a neutral valine at the sixth position of the beta-globin chain, a substitution Vernon Ingram identified in 1956 by a fingerprinting technique.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The result is haemoglobin S, and inheriting the variant from both parents gives sickle cell anaemia, the commonest and most severe form of the disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It does not follow that every substitution behaves that way, and the globin genes, HBB above all, carry a wide spread of variants whose relations to the clinical picture are complicated enough to make diagnosis genuinely difficult.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which changes matter is now measured rather than argued about, since deep mutational scanning assays many thousands of variants of a single protein in one pooled experiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those results are used to classify clinical variants and to test how well the predictive models actually do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Reading a sequence has changed as much, and single-molecule instruments now probe individual peptides in real time, at single amino acid resolution, rather than working on bulk samples.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They do not replace mass spectrometry, because neither approach on its own achieves full coverage of a sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Ankit Mangla, Nikki Agarwal, and Smita Maruvada, “Sickle Cell Anemia,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482164/. [Open access]","Emelie Foord, Darius Sairafi, Monika Berg, Sofia Westerling, Toheeb Adigun, Mehmet Uzunel, Thessalia Papasavva, and Michael Uhlin, “Decoding Thalassemia and Sickle Cell Disease: Advances in Molecular Technologies for Comprehensive Variant Detection,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 17 (2026): 1810737, https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2026.1810737. [Open access]","Hasan Çubuk, Xinyi Jin, Belinda Phipson, Joseph A. Marsh, and Alan F. Rubin, “Variant Scoring Tools for Deep Mutational Scanning,” <i>Molecular Systems Biology</i> 21, no. 10 (2025): 1293–305, https://doi.org/10.1038/s44320-025-00137-x. [Open access]","Tomasz A. Leski, Sean M. Brown, Zachary T. Johnson, Scott N. Dean, Ellen R. Goldman, and David A. Stenger, “Optimization of the Quantum-Si Platinum Single-Molecule Protein Sequencing Platform toward Improved Complex-Matrix Protein Identification,” <i>ACS Omega</i> (2026), https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.6c01140. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the sequence not the protein’s own choice?","a":"Because it is determined by the order of nucleotides in the gene that encodes it, so the defining characteristic of a protein is set before the chain exists."},{"q":"Why can one position out of hundreds matter so much?","a":"Because in the sickle cell mutation a negatively charged glutamate is replaced by a neutral valine at the sixth position of the beta-globin chain, giving haemoglobin S."},{"q":"Why is which changes matter now measured rather than argued about?","a":"Because deep mutational scanning assays many thousands of variants of one protein in a single pooled experiment, and those results are used to classify clinical variants and test the predictive models."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Protein_structure_%281%29-en.svg/1920px-Protein_structure_%281%29-en.svg.png","title":"Primary structure","desc":"The order of the amino acids, which is the first of the four levels a protein is described at.","credit":"Jmarchn , from Thomas Shafee, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protein_structure_(1)-en.svg","alt":"A four-band diagram of protein structure with the primary level highlighted, showing a labelled run of amino acids."},"wiki":"Protein primary structure"},
    "bio-065": {"questions":["Nearly half of human protein-coding genes contain long segments that never settle into any <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all, and those segments do real work.","In amyloid the sheets stack and pack against one another through their side chains, so a change of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is what turns a soluble protein into a fibril."],"abstract":"The <b>secondary structure</b> of a protein is the regular arrangement its chain takes up over short stretches, and two forms are common: the alpha helix and the beta sheet, first proposed by Linus Pauling and Robert Corey in 1951.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both are held together by hydrogen bonds between the CO and NH groups of peptide bonds, in a helix between groups four residues apart, and in a sheet between two stretches of chain lying side by side, either parallel or running in opposite directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decisive point is that those bonds involve the backbone and not the side chains, so the same pattern can be formed by many unrelated sequences.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A helix makes a complete turn every 3.6 amino acids, and both patterns were first found in ordinary material, the helix in the keratin of hair and the sheet in the fibroin of silk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Helices also wrap around one another into coiled-coils, which frame elongated proteins such as keratin and myosin, and a membrane protein usually crosses the lipid bilayer as a helix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Not every part of a protein has any regular structure, and nearly half of human protein-coding genes contain long disordered segments that exist as shifting ensembles rather than fixed shapes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those regions are not failures of folding, since they carry out signalling, transcriptional regulation, chromatin organisation and the assembly of organelles that have no membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Regular structure can also be built in the wrong way, and in an amyloid fibril beta sheets pack against one another through the side chains projecting from them, an arrangement called cross-beta packing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A single protein can take up several different amyloid folds, and those adopted by prion protein, tau, alpha-synuclein and TDP-43 differ from one neurodegenerative disease to another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Disordered regions remain among the hardest cases for structure prediction, whose methods principally return a single static conformation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Sami N. Al Harake, Said Btadini, Abrar H. Qadri, Frederic Harb, and Iqball Faheem, “Beyond the Structure-Function Paradigm: A Comprehensive Review of Intrinsically Disordered Proteins,” <i>Biochemistry and Biophysics Reports</i> 47 (2026): 102706, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bbrep.2026.102706. [Open access]","Sjors H. W. Scheres, “The Amyloid Packing Difference: A Pairwise Comparison Metric for Amyloid Structures,” <i>Structure</i> 34, no. 7 (2026): 1061–71, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.str.2026.04.014. [Open access]","Mandarina Qing Cheng Li, Sihan Wang, Shi-Ruei Lin, Li Eric Ngok Ting, Zhi-Hong Wan, Guodong Xie, and Jane Zhang, “Advantages and Limitations of AlphaFold in Structural Biology: Insights from Recent Studies,” <i>The Protein Journal</i> 45, no. 1 (2026): 22–38, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10930-025-10310-8. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can unrelated sequences form the same regular structure?","a":"Because the hydrogen bonds holding a helix or a sheet involve the CO and NH groups of the backbone rather than the side chains, and every sequence carries the same backbone."},{"q":"Why are disordered regions not failures of folding?","a":"Because nearly half of human protein-coding genes contain them, and they carry out signalling, transcriptional regulation, chromatin organisation and the assembly of membraneless organelles."},{"q":"Why can regular structure be built the wrong way?","a":"Because in an amyloid fibril beta sheets pack against one another through their side chains, and the folds adopted by tau, alpha-synuclein and TDP-43 differ from one neurodegenerative disease to another."}],"wiki":"Protein secondary structure"},
    "bio-066": {"questions":["Individually the bonds holding it are thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent one, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is stable only because so many of them act together.","Deep-learning methods can now generate sequences meant to fold into a chosen <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, including shapes that no organism has ever used."],"abstract":"The <b>tertiary structure</b> of a protein is the folding of one polypeptide chain into its three-dimensional shape, produced by interactions between side chains that lie in quite different regions of the sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The decisive one is the sorting of hydrophobic side chains into the interior and hydrophilic ones onto the surface, where they meet water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Helices and sheets can sit in that hydrophobic core because backbone hydrogen bonding has already neutralised the polar character of their CO and NH groups, while the loops joining them lie on the outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hydrogen bonds and ionic bonds between polar side chains at the surface matter as well, and covalent disulfide bonds between cysteines stabilise many secreted and cell-surface proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Taken singly the noncovalent bonds are thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent bond, so a fold holds only because a great many of them act in parallel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Helices and sheets joined by loops fold into compact globular units called domains, the basic units of tertiary structure; a small protein such as myoglobin has one, and a large protein several, often with distinct functions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because a domain is a unit it can be reused, and gene duplication has produced families whose members share sequence and fold alike, among them the serine proteases: chymotrypsin, trypsin, elastase and several enzymes of blood clotting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Comparing genomes shows the same process at scale, and a survey of 446 plant genomes found land plants carrying more domains and more domain combinations than algae, with shuffling generating the new combinations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A fold is not fixed once made, and non-reducing proteomics finds oxidative stress remodelling disulfide bonding across more than a thousand proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Folds no organism has used can now be designed as well, since deep learning has shifted protein design from optimising energy functions to generating sequence and structure together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Jia-Yu Xue, Cheng-Ao Yang, Shuaiya Hu, Hai-Yun Fan, Yan-Mei Zhang, Zhu-Qing Shao, Linzhou Li, et al., “Evolution of Protein Domains and Protein Domain Combinations Provides Insights into the Origin and Diversification of Land Plants,” <i>Cell Genomics</i> 6, no. 4 (2026): 101107, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xgen.2025.101107. [Open access]","Yeonjoo Lee, Tae-Kyung Kim, Seungjin Na, Kong-Joo Lee, Jaeho Jeong, and Eun Joo Song, “Non-Reducing Proteomics Reveals Disulfide-Dependent Proteoform Remodeling under Oxidative Stress,” <i>Molecular and Cellular Proteomics</i> 25, no. 4 (2026): 101548, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpro.2026.101548. [Open access]","Shaotong Luo and Bo Zhou, “Generative Protein Design: From Deep Learning Algorithms to Translational Applications,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 9 (2026): 3917, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27093917. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a helix sit inside a hydrophobic core at all?","a":"Because backbone hydrogen bonding has already neutralised the polar character of its CO and NH groups, while the loops joining helices and sheets lie on the outside."},{"q":"Why does a fold need so many weak bonds?","a":"Because taken singly the noncovalent bonds are thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent bond, so a shape holds only because a great many of them act in parallel."},{"q":"Why does a domain being a unit matter evolutionarily?","a":"Because it can be reused: gene duplication has produced families sharing sequence and fold, such as the serine proteases, and domain shuffling generates new combinations across genomes."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Protein_structure_%283%29-en.svg/1920px-Protein_structure_%283%29-en.svg.png","title":"Tertiary structure","desc":"The whole chain folded into one shape, the third of the four levels a protein is described at.","credit":"Jmarchn , from Thomas Shafee, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protein_structure_(3)-en.svg","alt":"A four-band diagram of protein structure with the tertiary level highlighted, showing sheets and helices packed into a single body."},"wiki":"Protein tertiary structure"},
    "bio-067": {"questions":["Structure prediction stalls until you know how many copies of each subunit are present, so a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> needs that count established first.","Ferritin shells and virus-like particles are being used as vaccine scaffolds because their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> displays many copies of an antigen at once."],"abstract":"The <b>quaternary structure</b> of a protein is the set of interactions between the separate polypeptide chains of a molecule built from more than one, and they are the same interactions that hold a single chain in its fold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each chain in such a molecule is called a subunit, and the assembly happens because a binding site on one folded chain recognises the surface of another, which gives the pair a precisely defined geometry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Where two identical chains bind head to head through two identical sites the result is a symmetric dimer, as in the Cro repressor that switches off genes in a bacterium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Repeating one binding interaction can close a ring instead, and the enzyme neuraminidase is four identical chains joined head to tail.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many assemblies mix chain types, haemoglobin being two identical alpha-globin subunits and two identical beta-globin subunits arranged symmetrically, and such multisubunit proteins are both common and often very large.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Knowing that a complex exists is not the same as knowing its composition, and predicting a structure requires the stoichiometry first, meaning how many copies of each subunit it contains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That information is often simply unavailable, and few computational methods address it, so recent work generates plausible subunit combinations, models each and ranks them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Predicting the interface between two chains is not settled either, and current models have been assessed against experimental structures rather than assumed to replace them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Assembly can also be put to work, since ferritin shells, encapsulins and virus-like particles display many copies of an antigen at once and are being developed as vaccine scaffolds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Designed particles go further, allowing the geometry itself to be optimised for how an antigen is shown to the immune system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Jian Liu, Pawan Neupane, and Jianlin Cheng, “PreStoi Allows Accurate Prediction of Protein Complex Stoichiometry by Integrating AlphaFold3 and Template Information,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 9, no. 1 (2026): 891, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-026-10126-x. [Open access]","Xiao Liu, Kaiwen Pang, Hangfei Wu, Bin Chong, Zhixiang Yin, Zhaoxi Sun, and Xiaohui Wang, “AlphaFold3 Prediction of Protein-Protein Complex: Is It Ready for Thermodynamic Analysis?,” <i>Computational and Structural Biotechnology Journal</i> 27 (2025): 4373–83, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.csbj.2025.10.015. [Open access]","Yue Zhang, Yi Ru, Xiuping Li, Guanghua Wang, Yong Hu, Yingna Jian, and Liqing Ma, “Application and Research Progress of Self-Assembling Protein Nanoparticles in Vaccine Development,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 10 (2026): 4503, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27104503. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do separate chains assemble into a precise shape?","a":"Because a binding site on one folded chain recognises the surface of another, which gives the pair a precisely defined geometry rather than an arbitrary contact."},{"q":"Why is knowing a complex exists not enough to model it?","a":"Because predicting a structure requires the stoichiometry first — how many copies of each subunit it contains — and that information is often simply unavailable."},{"q":"Why are such assemblies useful as vaccine scaffolds?","a":"Because ferritin shells, encapsulins and virus-like particles display many copies of an antigen at once, and designed particles let the geometry itself be optimised."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Protein_structure_%284%29-en.svg/1920px-Protein_structure_%284%29-en.svg.png","title":"Quaternary structure","desc":"Several folded chains assembled into one working molecule, the fourth level a protein is described at.","credit":"Jmarchn , from Thomas Shafee, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protein_structure_(4)-en.svg","alt":"A four-band diagram of protein structure with the quaternary level highlighted, showing three folded subunits fitted together."},"wiki":"Protein quaternary structure"},
    "bio-068": {"questions":["A 1969 paradox pointed out that a chain cannot search its conformations at random, and the funnel-shaped energy landscape is the answer to how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> happens so fast.","Chaperones stop a new chain's exposed hydrophobic patches from sticking together in the crowded cytoplasm, making <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> more reliable without changing where it ends up."],"abstract":"<b>Protein folding</b> is the process by which a newly made polypeptide reaches the single compact conformation it works in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That the conformation is set by the sequence was shown by Christian Anfinsen, who disrupted proteins by treatments such as heating, which break noncovalent bonds, and found that under milder conditions they often returned to their native shape unaided.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> How a chain finds that shape is a separate question, and Levinthal's paradox of 1969 pointed out that sampling conformations at random is implausible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Energy landscape theory answered it in the 1990s by treating folding as a funnel-guided process, with the native state sitting at an energy minimum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A native-like nucleus forms first, which holds down the entropic cost of organising the chain, and the funnel then guides the rest into place rapidly and cooperatively in spite of kinetic traps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inside a cell the process is usually assisted, by proteins called molecular chaperones that bind partly folded chains and keep them on the most energetically favourable pathway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They earn their place because the cytoplasm is crowded: they stop the hydrophobic regions temporarily exposed on a new chain from associating with one another into aggregates, and they change nothing about the destination, only make reaching it more reliable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Folding also begins well before a chain is finished, since a cytosolic protein starts to fold as soon as it emerges from the ribosome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Those first steps are hard to observe, and measuring them in live cells shows that a domain's topology shapes the pathway it takes and that different chaperones engage the growing chain at different points.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A textbook of 2000 could still state that predicting a structure directly from its sequence was impossible, and deep learning has since produced millions of structure models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Omer Faruk Kuzu, Lars Jørgen Tvenge Granerud, and Fahri Saatcioglu, “Navigating the Landscape of Protein Folding and Proteostasis: From Molecular Chaperones to Therapeutic Innovations,” <i>Signal Transduction and Targeted Therapy</i> 10, no. 1 (2025): 358, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41392-025-02439-w. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Xiuqi Chen, Vincent J. Hilser, and Christian M. Kaiser, “Arrest Peptide Profiling Resolves Co-translational Folding Pathways and Chaperone Interactions in Vivo,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025): 6833, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-61398-6. [Open access]","Martin Luke Rennie and Michael R. Oliver, “Emerging Frontiers in Protein Structure Prediction Following the AlphaFold Revolution,” <i>Journal of the Royal Society Interface</i> 22, no. 225 (2025): 20240886, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2024.0886. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the working shape said to be set by the sequence?","a":"Because Anfinsen disrupted proteins by treatments such as heating that break noncovalent bonds, and under milder conditions they often returned to their native shape unaided."},{"q":"Why can a chain not simply search for its shape at random?","a":"Because Levinthal’s paradox showed that sampling conformations at random is implausible, which energy landscape theory answered by treating folding as a funnel-guided process."},{"q":"Why does a crowded cytoplasm need chaperones?","a":"Because they stop the hydrophobic regions temporarily exposed on a new chain from associating into aggregates; they change nothing about the destination, only make reaching it reliable."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Protein_structure.png/960px-Protein_structure.png","title":"The four levels of protein structure","desc":"Sequence, local pattern, whole fold and assembly, each drawn from the same protein.","credit":"Holger87, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Protein_structure.png","alt":"Four panels on a dark ground showing a run of amino acids, a helix and a sheet, a folded chain, and several chains joined."},"wiki":"Protein folding"},
    "bio-069": {"questions":["Heat, molar urea and hydrostatic pressure all produce <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but put on one thermodynamic footing they turn out not to do it the same way.","A drug bound to its target shifts that protein's melting temperature, so heating a whole proteome and watching for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> reveals what the drug binds."],"abstract":"<b>Denaturation</b> is the disruption of a protein's three-dimensional structure by treatments that break the noncovalent bonds holding it together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What is left is a flexible polypeptide chain that has lost its natural shape while remaining one continuous chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Take the denaturing agent away and the protein often refolds, or renatures, spontaneously into its original conformation, which is how the shape was shown to be specified by the sequence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Several quite different treatments will do it, among them heating, hydrostatic pressure, and molar concentrations of a chaotrope such as urea, which disrupts noncovalent interactions directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They are not interchangeable, and when the three are put on a single thermodynamic footing heat-induced unfolding turns out to be the most cooperative of the three, a result obtained on apolipoprotein A-1 and lysozyme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Unfolding need not be a single step from folded to not, and mass spectrometry, which captures the states actually populated rather than an average over them, finds intermediates appearing as the urea concentration rises.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Optical methods report an ensemble average and can struggle to resolve those intermediates at all, so how simple the process looks depends partly on the instrument watching it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The sensitivity of a fold to its conditions has been turned into an instrument in its own right, since a small molecule bound to a protein shifts that protein's melting temperature, stabilising or destabilising it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Heating cells across a range of temperatures and measuring which proteins are still soluble therefore identifies what a drug binds, unbiased and across a whole proteome at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Both of the classic accounts are careful to say that a denatured protein often refolds rather than that it always does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Joachim Seelig and Anna Seelig, “Pressure Protein Denaturation Compared to Thermal and Chemical Unfolding: Analyses with Cooperative Models,” <i>The Journal of Physical Chemistry B</i> 129, no. 4 (2025): 1229–36, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jpcb.4c07703. [Open access]","Nicklas Österlund, Jacob S. Jordan, Eleonora Renzi, Gergo Peter Szekeres, and Kevin Pagel, “Mass Spectrometry Detects Folding Intermediates Populated during Urea-Induced Protein Denaturation,” <i>Chemical Science</i> 16, no. 47 (2025): 22407–16, https://doi.org/10.1039/d5sc05773f. [Open access]","Camilla Johansson, Martin Johansson, Phanindra Babu Kasi, Mårten Larsson, Per-Johan Jakobsson, Ulf Göransson, Jordi Carreras Puigvert, et al., “Cell Painting and Thermal Proteome Profiling for Inference of Drug Targets and Mechanism of Action,” <i>Molecular Systems Biology</i> 22, no. 8 (2026): 1270–91, https://doi.org/10.1038/s44320-026-00214-9. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is denaturation the experiment that proved sequence sets shape?","a":"Because taking the denaturing agent away often lets the flexible chain refold spontaneously into its original conformation, with nothing but the sequence left to specify it."},{"q":"Why are the different denaturing treatments not interchangeable?","a":"Because put on a single thermodynamic footing they differ, heat-induced unfolding proving the most cooperative of the three on apolipoprotein A-1 and lysozyme."},{"q":"Why does how simple unfolding looks depend on the instrument?","a":"Because mass spectrometry captures the states actually populated and finds intermediates as urea rises, where optical methods report an ensemble average and can struggle to resolve them."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/DNA_Denaturation_by_Formamide.png/960px-DNA_Denaturation_by_Formamide.png","title":"denaturation","desc":"DNA being denatured by formamide.","credit":"Jerlynn5, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DNA_Denaturation_by_Formamide.png","alt":"A diagram of a DNA molecule drawn as a long loop, pulled apart across the middle so that the paired bases stand separated."},"wiki":"Denaturation (biochemistry)"},
    "bio-070": {"questions":["Four drugs approved for Duchenne muscular dystrophy work by making the splicing machinery skip an exon, which is a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> used as a medicine rather than as information.","At up to 455 exabytes per gram and stable for millennia, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is being developed as a storage medium for ordinary computer files."],"abstract":"The <b>nucleic acids</b>, DNA and RNA, are the principal informational molecules of the cell, deoxyribonucleic acid having the unique role of genetic material and the several kinds of ribonucleic acid taking part in a range of cellular activities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> RNA is not only informational, since in present-day cells it also catalyses reactions, among them steps of protein synthesis and of RNA processing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both are polymers of nucleotides, each a purine or pyrimidine base linked to a phosphorylated sugar, ribose in RNA and deoxyribose in DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The chain is built by phosphodiester bonds between the 5' phosphate of one nucleotide and the 3' hydroxyl of another, so it has a direction as well as a sequence, and it is always synthesised and written from the 5' end towards the 3'.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A short chain is an oligonucleotide; the polynucleotides making up cellular RNA and DNA run to thousands and to millions of units respectively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Short chains made deliberately are now medicines, and antisense oligonucleotides work by binding a target transcript and redirecting how it is spliced or how much of it survives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Four such drugs are approved in the United States for Duchenne muscular dystrophy, eteplirsen, golodirsen, viltolarsen and casimersen, each making the splicing machinery skip an exon so that some dystrophin is made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small interfering RNAs work differently again, intercepting genetic information in transit rather than altering the genome, which has taken them from a niche technique to the routine management of chronic conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The molecule is also being taken out of biology altogether, since DNA offers an information density of up to 455 exabytes per gram and stability over thousands of years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What has held that back is not writing the data but reading a chosen part of it back, and electric fields are one way now being used to address stored strands individually.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Liping Xu, Huaqun Zhang, Bingchen Jiang, Yuanying Jiang, and Hui Lu, “Antisense Oligonucleotides: Technological Advances, Clinical Progress, and Expanding Therapeutic Frontiers,” <i>Pharmaceutics</i> 18, no. 4 (2026): 446, https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics18040446. [Open access]","Hidenori Moriyama, Shiori Moriyama, and Toshifumi Yokota, “FDA-Approved Antisense Oligonucleotide Therapies for Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy: Current Status and Future Outlook,” <i>RNA Biology</i> 23, no. 1 (2026): 1–17, https://doi.org/10.1080/15476286.2026.2689117. [Open access]","Quoc-Viet Le and Gayong Shim, “Evolution of siRNA Therapeutics: From Mechanistic Foundations to Clinical Expansion,” <i>Pharmaceutics</i> 18, no. 5 (2026): 593, https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics18050593. [Open access]","Doyeon Lim, Taeseok Kang, Wonjin Lee, Zeeshan Haider, Seunghwan Noh, Minsang Yu, Xiaohua Huang, Hyuk Soo Eun, and Youngjun Song, “Electric Field-Guided Random-Access DNA Data Storage,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 23 (2026): eaee4328, https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aee4328. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a nucleic acid chain have a direction as well as a sequence?","a":"Because phosphodiester bonds join the 5' phosphate of one nucleotide to the 3' hydroxyl of another, so the chain is always synthesised and written 5' to 3'."},{"q":"Why is RNA not simply an informational molecule?","a":"Because in present-day cells it also catalyses reactions, among them steps of protein synthesis and of RNA processing."},{"q":"Why has DNA data storage not arrived despite its density?","a":"Because what has held it back is not writing the data but reading a chosen part of it back, which is why electric fields are being used to address stored strands individually."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Difference_DNA_RNA-EN.svg/1920px-Difference_DNA_RNA-EN.svg.png","title":"DNA against RNA","desc":"The two nucleic acids compared: one sugar and one base differ, and one is a double strand where the other is single.","credit":"File:Difference DNA RNA-DE.svg : Sponk / *translation: Sponk, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Difference_DNA_RNA-EN.svg","alt":"A diagram of an RNA strand beside a DNA double helix, with the bases and the sugar-phosphate backbones labelled."},"wiki":"Nucleic acid"},
    "bio-071": {"questions":["Blocking the first enzyme that builds purines from scratch kills tuberculosis bacteria, which makes the manufacture of every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a target for a new class of antibiotic.","An antiviral drug that mimics a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> closely enough to be incorporated can be cut out again by a coronavirus that carries a proofreading enzyme."],"abstract":"A <b>nucleotide</b> is a nitrogen-containing base linked to a sugar, with one or more phosphate groups attached to that sugar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bases come in two chemical families, the purines and the pyrimidines: DNA uses the purines adenine and guanine with the pyrimidines cytosine and thymine, and RNA replaces thymine with uracil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The distinction worth holding is that a base joined to a sugar alone is a nucleoside, and it becomes a nucleotide only once a phosphate is attached to the 5' carbon of that sugar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sugar itself is ribose in RNA and 2'-deoxyribose in DNA, and that one missing oxygen is what separates the two families of nucleic acid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A nucleotide may carry one, two or three phosphates in a row, and joining nucleotides into a chain is only one of the things a cell does with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Cells both build purines from scratch and salvage them from breakdown products, and the first route matters enough that a small-molecule inhibitor of PurF, the first enzyme of mycobacterial de novo purine synthesis, kills tuberculosis bacteria at nanomolar concentrations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Supply matters in less obvious places too, and enterocytes lean on purine synthesis and salvage to raise the ATP that absorbing dietary fat demands.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A close enough imitation can be slipped into a growing chain to stop it, which is how nucleotide analogue antivirals work, and SARS-CoV-2 resists two of them because its proofreading exoribonuclease excises the intruder again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nucleotides serve as signals as well as parts, cyclic AMP being the long-established second messenger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Where it operates is still being revised, and the plant auxin receptor TIR1 turns out to make cyclic AMP itself, which is required for the transcriptional response to the hormone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Dirk A. Lamprecht, Richard J. Wall, Annelies Leemans, Barry Truebody, Joke Sprangers, Patricia Fiogbe, Cadi Davies, et al., “Targeting De Novo Purine Biosynthesis for Tuberculosis Treatment,” <i>Nature</i> 644, no. 8075 (2025): 214–20, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09177-7. [Open access]","Yu Wang, Li Chen, Yingze Ma, Mingqi Zhou, Aleksander Geske, Marcus Seldin, Jiangjiang Zhu, et al., “Enterocytes Rely on Purine Biosynthesis/Salvage Pathway to Facilitate Dietary Fat Absorption,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 3888, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-70332-3. [Open access]","Chang Liu, Yu Li, Xiaocong Cao, Ryan J. Gleason, Bin Liu, and Yang Yang, “Mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 Resistance to Nucleotide Analog-Based Antivirals,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 1601, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-68304-8. [Open access]","Huihuang Chen, Linlin Qi, Minxia Zou, Mengting Lu, Mateusz Kwiatkowski, Yuanrong Pei, Krzysztof Jaworski, and Jiří Friml, “TIR1-Produced cAMP as a Second Messenger in Transcriptional Auxin Signalling,” <i>Nature</i> 640, no. 8060 (2025): 1011–16, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08669-w. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a nucleoside not yet a nucleotide?","a":"Because a base joined to a sugar alone is a nucleoside, and it becomes a nucleotide only once a phosphate is attached to the 5' carbon of that sugar."},{"q":"Why does one missing oxygen separate two families of nucleic acid?","a":"Because the sugar is ribose in RNA and 2'-deoxyribose in DNA, and that difference at the 2' position is what distinguishes the two polymers."},{"q":"Why does SARS-CoV-2 resist some nucleotide analogue drugs?","a":"Because the drug works by being slipped into a growing chain to stop it, and the virus’s proofreading exoribonuclease excises the intruder again."}],"image":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Cyclic-AMPchemdraw.png/1920px-Cyclic-AMPchemdraw.png","title":"nucleotide","desc":"Anionic structure of c-AMP.","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cyclic-AMPchemdraw.png","alt":"Chemical structure diagram of cyclic AMP, showing its base, sugar ring and phosphate group"},"wiki":"Nucleotide"},
    "bio-072": {"questions":["A human body hydrolyses between 100 and 150 moles of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a day, which is far more than it contains at any moment and shows how fast the molecule is remade.","Restoring the ion balance after one nerve impulse costs a neuron close to a billion molecules of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is where most of the brain's energy goes."],"abstract":"<b>ATP</b>, adenosine triphosphate, is the principal form of chemical energy inside cells, and it is a nucleotide: an adenine base on a ribose sugar carrying three phosphate groups bonded one after another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Removing the outermost phosphate gives ADP, and that hydrolysis is energetically favourable, which is what lets a cell couple it to reactions that would not otherwise run.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The metaphor of a currency is apt because the molecule is spent and remade rather than stored, and a human body hydrolyses somewhere between 100 and 150 moles of it in a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The bill falls hardest on tissues that pump ions, and a neuron hydrolyses close to a billion molecules to reset its sodium and potassium after a single depolarisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Other nucleotides do similar work, carrying energy or reactive chemical groups through a wide range of metabolic reactions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Energy is not the whole of what ATP does, and it is co-stored and co-released with neurotransmitters, which makes it a mediator of purinergic signalling in the nervous system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Released from damaged cells it becomes a warning: in the liver, extracellular ATP acts on neighbouring and on its own cells as a signal in the progression of fibrosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A third role has nothing to do with either, since at the concentrations found inside a cell ATP alters how proteins interact with one another and how stable they are.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That effect is being examined on proteins which cluster into droplets, where ATP and other small amphiphilic molecules tune how those clusters form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A molecule known for a century as the cell's fuel is turning out also to be part of how its interior stays soluble.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Jacob Dunn and Michael H. Grider, “Physiology, Adenosine Triphosphate,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553175/. [Open access]","Blanca Verónica Ramos-Rosillo, Esperanza Mata-Martínez, Mauricio Díaz-Muñoz, and Francisco G. Vázquez-Cuevas, “Liver Fibrosis and Purinergic Signaling: Autocrine-Paracrine Role of ATP in Liver Damage,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 13 (2026): 6030, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27136030. [Open access]","Shuyuan Tan and Robin Curtis, “Adenosine Triphosphate as a Modulator of Protein Interactions and Stability,” <i>FEBS Open Bio</i> (2026), https://doi.org/10.1002/2211-5463.70312. [Open access]","Mrityunjoy Kar, “ATP and Small Amphiphilic Molecules Act as Molecular Matchmakers to Fine-Tune FET Protein Clusters,” <i>Communications Chemistry</i> 8, no. 1 (2025): 386, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42004-025-01807-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is ATP called a currency rather than a store?","a":"Because the molecule is spent and remade rather than kept: a human body hydrolyses somewhere between 100 and 150 moles of it in a day."},{"q":"Why does hydrolysing it drive reactions that would not otherwise run?","a":"Because removing the outermost phosphate to give ADP is energetically favourable, and a cell couples that favourable change to the unfavourable one it needs."},{"q":"Why is the fuel account of ATP incomplete?","a":"Because it is co-released with neurotransmitters as a purinergic signal, warns of damage in liver fibrosis, and at cellular concentrations alters how proteins interact and how stable they are."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Adenosintriphosphat_protoniert.svg/1920px-Adenosintriphosphat_protoniert.svg.png","title":"Adenosine triphosphate","desc":"Adenine on a ribose, with three phosphates in a row: the cell's usable energy is in the bonds between them.","credit":"NEUROtiker, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adenosintriphosphat_protoniert.svg","alt":"A structural formula of ATP showing three linked phosphate groups, a five-membered sugar ring and an adenine base."}},
    "bio-073": {"questions":["Because it reports the peptide bond rather than any particular molecule, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> answers to anything carrying enough of them, which is a limit as well as a convenience.","Clinical laboratories measure serum total protein with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, though a refractometer is quicker and cheaper and agrees with it closely enough to substitute."],"abstract":"Cells are built from four classes of molecule, carbohydrates, lipids, nucleic acids and proteins, and the oldest way of asking which of them is in a sample is a reagent that changes colour when it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The <b>biuret test</b> is the one for protein: at alkaline pH copper forms chelates with the peptide linkages of a protein, and the solution turns violet.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What it reports is therefore the peptide bond rather than any particular protein, so anything carrying enough of them will answer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is no classroom relic, and the biuret method is used routinely in reference and in-clinic laboratories to measure serum total protein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor is it irreplaceable, and a refractometer, quicker and cheaper, correlates strongly enough with it to be used instead in cattle and goats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Sugars have their own colour reactions, and reducing sugars have been determined by the Somogyi-Nelson, phenol-sulfuric, anthrone, Fehling and dinitrosalicylic acid methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some of the older ones are laborious, Fehling's involving precipitation and titration, and newer routes are designed to report not only that the sugar is there but how much of it is.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That is the direction of travel: a colour change answers whether, and a calibrated assay answers how much.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The descendant of these tests that a reader is most likely to meet is the urine dipstick, whose reagent pads report protein, glucose, blood and several other things by colour, each read against a printed chart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every pad has its documented false positives and negatives, the protein pad reading positive in alkaline or concentrated urine and negative when the protein present is not albumin, and reference ranges themselves shift with the substrates and reagents an assay uses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Sileshi Mesele Mesa and Yoseph Cherinet Megerssa, “Comparison of Biuret and Refractometery Method for Serum Total Protein Measurements in Cattle and Goat,” <i>BMC Research Notes</i> 17, no. 1 (2024): 234, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13104-024-06906-5. [Open access]","Reda M. El-Shishtawy, Yasser M. Al Angari, Maha M. Alotaibi, and Yaaser Q. Almulaiky, “Novel and Facile Colorimetric Detection of Reducing Sugars in Foods via In Situ Formed Gelatin-Capped Silver Nanoparticles,” <i>Polymers</i> 15, no. 5 (2023): 1086, https://doi.org/10.3390/polym15051086. [Open access]","Daniel A. Queremel Milani and Ishwarlal Jialal, “Urinalysis,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557685/. [Open access]","Muhammad Zubair and Thiruvengadam Muniraj, “Analytical and Clinical Perspectives on Amylase,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2026), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557738/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why will the biuret test answer for almost any protein?","a":"Because what it reports is the peptide linkage itself, copper forming chelates with those bonds at alkaline pH, so anything carrying enough of them turns the solution violet."},{"q":"Why is a colour change not the same as a measurement?","a":"Because a colour reaction answers whether a substance is there while a calibrated assay answers how much, which is why newer routes for sugars were designed to quantify rather than detect."},{"q":"Why must a dipstick result be read with its known failures in mind?","a":"Because every pad has documented false positives and negatives — the protein pad reads positive in alkaline or concentrated urine and negative when the protein present is not albumin."}],"wiki":"Biuret test"},
    "bio-074": {"questions":["Nearly half of human protein-coding genes carry long segments with no stable fold, which is the standing exception to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a failure of folding.","One protein taking several different amyloid folds, each tied to a different disease, shows that one sequence does not always fix one shape, which the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> quietly assumes."],"abstract":"The <b>structure-function relationship</b> is the organising assumption of molecular biology, so settled that the papers questioning it are titled as going beyond a paradigm: what a molecule does follows from its shape, and its shape follows from how it is built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A protein is the standard demonstration, since its conformation is determined by its amino acid sequence, with hydrophobic side chains sorting into the interior and hydrophilic ones onto the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The chain has preferred shapes at all only because the peptide bond is planar and permits no rotation, while the other backbone bonds do, which sharply restricts what conformations are available.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The bonds that then hold a fold are individually thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent bond, so a structure is stable without being rigid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Where the reasoning bites hardest is at a single position: replacing a glutamate with a valine at the sixth place in the beta-globin chain gives haemoglobin S.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The relationship is not universal, and nearly half of human protein-coding genes contain long disordered segments that never settle into a stable three-dimensional fold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those regions are not failures of folding but work as shifting ensembles, carrying out signalling, transcriptional regulation, chromatin organisation and the assembly of organelles without membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The other half of the assumption fails too, since a single protein can take up several different amyloid folds, and the ones adopted by prion protein, tau, alpha-synuclein and TDP-43 differ between neurodegenerative diseases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> So one sequence does not always specify one shape, and the flexibility comes from the packing interactions themselves rather than from any change in the chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> What survives is a rule of thumb of enormous power rather than a law, and knowing where it does not hold is part of knowing how to use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Sami N. Al Harake, Said Btadini, Abrar H. Qadri, Frederic Harb, and Iqball Faheem, “Beyond the Structure-Function Paradigm: A Comprehensive Review of Intrinsically Disordered Proteins,” <i>Biochemistry and Biophysics Reports</i> 47 (2026): 102706, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bbrep.2026.102706. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Molecular Composition of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “The Shape and Structure of Proteins,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/. [Open access]","Ankit Mangla, Nikki Agarwal, and Smita Maruvada, “Sickle Cell Anemia,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK482164/. [Open access]","Sjors H. W. Scheres, “The Amyloid Packing Difference: A Pairwise Comparison Metric for Amyloid Structures,” <i>Structure</i> 34, no. 7 (2026): 1061–71, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.str.2026.04.014. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a chain have preferred shapes at all?","a":"Because the peptide bond is planar and permits no rotation while the other backbone bonds do, which sharply restricts what conformations are available to it."},{"q":"Why is a fold stable without being rigid?","a":"Because the bonds holding it are individually thirty to three hundred times weaker than a covalent bond, so the structure holds in numbers rather than by strength."},{"q":"Why is the relationship a rule of thumb rather than a law?","a":"Because nearly half of human protein-coding genes carry long disordered segments, and one protein can take several amyloid folds that differ between neurodegenerative diseases."}]},
    "bio-075": {"questions":["In the gel filtration form of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> the small molecules linger inside the beads while the large ones flow past, so the biggest leave the column first.","Measuring a mixture measures an average, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now the front half of nearly every method that reports on individual molecules."],"abstract":"<b>Chromatography</b> separates a mixture by passing it through a column packed with a porous solid matrix, in which the different components are retarded to different extents and can be collected separately as they flow out of the bottom.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What the matrix is made of decides what the column sorts by: charge in ion-exchange chromatography, exposed hydrophobic regions in hydrophobic chromatography, size in gel filtration, and the ability to bind a particular molecule in affinity chromatography.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Gel filtration is the one worth thinking through, since its beads are porous and small molecules linger inside them while larger ones stay in the flowing solvent between, so the biggest leave the column first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Charge can also be used without a column at all, and in electrophoresis an electric field moves a protein at a rate set by its net charge, its size and its shape, which SDS polyacrylamide-gel electrophoresis turned into routine analysis in the mid-1960s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before any of that, whole organelles are sorted by differential centrifugation, a method developed by Albert Claude, Christian de Duve and their colleagues in the 1940s and 1950s that separates components by size and density.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Separation is now almost always the front half of a measurement rather than an end in itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lipidomics is the clear case, resting on advances in mass spectrometry, chromatography and computational analysis together, which is what has allowed lipid networks to be mapped at high resolution across molecular, cellular and organismal scales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A working study now combines several such methods at once, one recent example pairing liquid chromatography and mass spectrometry for metabolites with non-reducing tandem mass tag proteomics in the same cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What that buys is not only sensitivity, since electrospray mass spectrometry captures the states actually present in a sample rather than an average over them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Optical methods that report an ensemble average can struggle to resolve those states at all, which is the whole argument for separating a mixture before asking anything of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts, Alexander Johnson, Julian Lewis, Martin Raff, Keith Roberts, and Peter Walter, “Fractionation of Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26936/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Maria Fedorova, Anne K. Bendt, Justine Bertrand-Michel, Oliver Fiehn, Joanna Godzien, Laura Goracci, Florian Gruber, et al., “A Lipidomics Roadmap: From Basic Research to Societal Challenges,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026): 4778, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-73797-4. [Open access]","Yeonjoo Lee, Tae-Kyung Kim, Seungjin Na, Kong-Joo Lee, Jaeho Jeong, and Eun Joo Song, “Non-Reducing Proteomics Reveals Disulfide-Dependent Proteoform Remodeling under Oxidative Stress,” <i>Molecular and Cellular Proteomics</i> 25, no. 4 (2026): 101548, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpro.2026.101548. [Open access]","Nicklas Österlund, Jacob S. Jordan, Eleonora Renzi, Gergo Peter Szekeres, and Kevin Pagel, “Mass Spectrometry Detects Folding Intermediates Populated during Urea-Induced Protein Denaturation,” <i>Chemical Science</i> 16, no. 47 (2025): 22407–16, https://doi.org/10.1039/d5sc05773f. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do the largest molecules leave a gel filtration column first?","a":"Because the beads are porous, so small molecules linger inside them while larger ones stay in the flowing solvent between and are carried straight through."},{"q":"Why does the packing material decide what a column sorts by?","a":"Because the matrix is what retards the components: charge in ion exchange, exposed hydrophobic regions in hydrophobic chromatography, size in gel filtration, binding in affinity."},{"q":"Why separate a mixture before asking anything of it?","a":"Because mass spectrometry captures the states actually present in a sample, where optical methods report an ensemble average and can struggle to resolve those states at all."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/FDA_History_-_Column_Chromatography.jpg/1920px-FDA_History_-_Column_Chromatography.jpg","title":"Column chromatography in a laboratory of the mid-1950s","desc":"A chemist pours a sample into the top of one of a bank of tall glass columns used to separate the constituents of a coal-tar colour, the separated fractions being collected as they run out of the bottom.","credit":"The U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FDA_History_-_Column_Chromatography.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a narrow laboratory. A man in shirtsleeves stands on a step stool pouring liquid from a flask into the top of a tall glass column; more columns of the same kind stand in wooden racks around him, each packed with a pale material banded in different shades, with glass funnels above them and a cluttered bench along the left wall."},"wiki":"Chromatography"},
    "bio-076": {"questions":["Sunlight, photosynthesis, the oxidation of sugars and the turnover of ATP are all accounted for within <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the study of how energy moves through living systems.","Sulfur-oxidising bacteria that fix carbon in total darkness are the standing exception, in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, to the rule that sunlight is the ultimate source of the energy living things use."],"abstract":"<b>Bioenergetics</b> is the study of how energy is captured, stored, transformed and spent by living things, and is the thread tying photosynthesis, respiration and every act of cellular work together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cell is never idle: it builds macromolecules, pumps ions against concentration gradients, hauls cargo along its own filaments and, in many organisms, moves the whole body about. All of that runs against the grain of physics, since cells create and maintain order in a universe tending always to greater disorder, so a constant input of energy is needed simply to stay alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Energy is never created, only converted from one form into another, and biologists keep the accounts in free energy, a quantity combining the heat content of a system with its degree of disorder.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For virtually all metabolic energy in the living world the ultimate source is sunlight, turned into usable chemical energy by photosynthesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Autotrophs assemble their organic molecules from inorganic starting materials, plants drawing their carbon from atmospheric carbon dioxide, their hydrogen and oxygen from water and their nitrogen from ammonia and nitrates, while heterotrophs obtain theirs by eating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those molecules hold energy in their chemical bonds, and a cell releases it by oxidising them through a long series of small enzyme-catalysed steps to carbon dioxide and water, so that free energy arrives in conveniently sized packets instead of the single destructive flare of ordinary burning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Much of what is captured is held briefly in adenosine triphosphate, of which an adult turns over some 100 to 150 moles a day,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while a large share of the total is dissipated as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not everything runs on the sun, and sulfur-oxidising bacteria in coastal sediments fix carbon in complete darkness on energy stripped from inorganic chemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> None of this is mere bookkeeping: crippling the mitochondrial bioenergetics of one supporting cell type in the mouse brain weakened the neurons around it and impaired memory, a deficit reversed by supplying lactate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “How Cells Obtain Energy from Food,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26882/. [Open access]","Jacob Dunn and Michael H. Grider, “Physiology, Adenosine Triphosphate,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2023), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK553175/. [Open access]","Xiao-Lan Yue et al., “13C-Labeled Single-Cell Raman Sorting Reveals Sulfur-Driven Dark Carbon Fixation in Coastal Sediments,” <i>ISME Communications</i> 6, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1093/ismeco/ycag073. [Open access]","Kateryna Murlanova et al., “NPAS3-Regulated Astrocyte Mitochondrial Bioenergetics Is Required for Cognition,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 25 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adt2527. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does staying alive require a constant input of energy?","a":"Because cells create and maintain order in a universe tending always to greater disorder, and they are never idle, building molecules, pumping ions and hauling cargo."},{"q":"Why oxidise food in many small steps rather than burning it?","a":"Because a long series of enzyme-catalysed steps delivers free energy in conveniently sized packets instead of the single destructive flare of ordinary burning."},{"q":"Why is bioenergetics not merely bookkeeping?","a":"Because crippling the mitochondrial bioenergetics of one supporting cell type in the mouse brain weakened the neurons around it and impaired memory, a deficit reversed by supplying lactate."}],"wiki":"Bioenergetics"},
    "bio-077": {"questions":["The first and second laws of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> together say that energy is never created and that disorder can only increase, which sets the terms every organism must live on.","An organism counts as an open system in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, exchanging both energy and matter with its surroundings, which is precisely why building order inside itself is allowed."],"abstract":"<b>Thermodynamics</b> is the study of energy and its transfer, and its laws bind a bacterium and a blue whale as tightly as a steam engine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It divides the world into a system and the surroundings outside it, and living organisms count as open systems, exchanging both energy and matter with the world around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first law states that the total energy of the universe is constant, so energy may be moved or changed in form but never created or destroyed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A plant therefore makes no energy at all, and instead converts the energy of sunlight into chemical energy held in the bonds of organic molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second law states that in the universe, or in any isolated system, the degree of disorder can only increase, and the measure of that disorder is entropy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Living things grow and assemble elaborate structures and so appear to defy it, but a cell is no isolated system: it takes in energy as food or as sunlight, spends it generating order within itself, and discharges part of it as heat, disordering its surroundings by more than it orders itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No energy transfer is ever completely efficient, so some is always lost in a form that cannot be used, usually heat, which is why a mammal is warm to the touch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two laws are folded into one quantity, free energy, which sets the heat content of a system against its disorder and so predicts which reactions will run without being pushed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That waste heat can itself become an adaptation, and two honeybee species have independently evolved mitochondria whose membranes leak protons more freely, generating extra warmth and letting both spread from the tropics into temperate country.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Heat production and heat loss can now be tracked in a person in real time, and the resulting estimates of entropy production and export are being explored, as a hypothesis rather than a clinical tool, as a measure of health.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.3 The Laws of Thermodynamics,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-3-the-laws-of-thermodynamics. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Ye Xiao et al., “Convergent Elevation of Mitochondrial Proton Leakage Improves Thermogenesis in Honeybees,” <i>Molecular Biology and Evolution</i> 43, no. 8 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msag174. [Open access]","Nicolas Brodeur, André Longtin, Glen P. Kenny, and Andrew J. E. Seely, “Real-Time Measurement of Entropy Production and Export in Humans: A Step toward Entropically Informed Medicine,” <i>Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences</i> 1550, no. 1 (2025): 283–95, https://doi.org/10.1111/nyas.15385. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a growing organism not break the second law?","a":"Because a cell is no isolated system: it takes in energy, spends it generating order within itself, and discharges part of it as heat, disordering its surroundings by more than it orders itself."},{"q":"Why is a mammal warm to the touch?","a":"Because no energy transfer is ever completely efficient, so some is always lost in a form that cannot be used, which is usually heat."},{"q":"Why can wasted heat become an adaptation?","a":"Because two honeybee species have independently evolved mitochondria whose membranes leak protons more freely, generating extra warmth and letting both spread from the tropics into temperate country."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Carnot_heat_engine_2.svg/1920px-Carnot_heat_engine_2.svg.png","title":"A heat engine","desc":"Heat taken from a hot reservoir, work done, and the remainder given up to a cold one, which is what the second law forbids doing without.","credit":"Eric Gaba ( Sting - fr:Sting ), Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carnot_heat_engine_2.svg","alt":"A diagram of a circle between two labelled reservoirs, with arrows for heat in, heat out and work done."},"wiki":"Thermodynamics"},
    "bio-078": {"questions":["Subtracting the energy lost to entropy from a reaction's total energy change gives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the part that is actually available to do work in a cell.","Rust forms without being pushed and still takes years, which is why a fall in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tells you a reaction can happen but says nothing whatever about how fast."],"abstract":"<b>Gibbs free energy</b>, written G and named for Josiah Willard Gibbs, is the part of a system's energy that remains available to do work once the price paid in entropy has been deducted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Every reaction involves a change in it, written as delta G, and that change is the reaction's total energy change, its enthalpy, less the energy surrendered to disorder multiplied by the absolute temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A reaction that releases free energy has a negative value and is called exergonic, its products holding less free energy than its reactants, and it can proceed without being pushed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A reaction that must be supplied with energy has a positive value and is called endergonic, its products storing more than went into them, and it will not happen on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Spontaneous here means unforced rather than immediate: iron rusts spontaneously over years, and a book does not burst into flames because its molecules must first be kicked over a barrier of activation energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The figure depends not only on the molecules involved but on their concentrations and conditions, so biochemists quote a standard change measured in water at pH 7, roughly the conditions inside a cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At equilibrium the change is zero and the reaction runs no further in either direction, and the standard value fixes where that equilibrium sits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many of the reactions life depends on, the assembly of large molecules above all, are unfavourable under cellular conditions and can run only if something else drives them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The sign is a hard physical constraint rather than a bookkeeping convenience, and metabolic models are now written to exclude loops of reactions that would circulate for ever with nothing going in, which no real cell can do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Landscapes of it can also be measured for a structure rather than a reaction, and one mapped for compacted chromatin separated three ways in which linker histones tighten the coil.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.2 Potential, Kinetic, Free, and Activation Energy,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Nicolás Améstica-Toledo, Maximiliano Farías-Miño, Raúl Conejeros, David Tourigny, and Marcelo Rivas-Astroza, “Thermodynamically Enabled and Reaction Attuned Estimation of Metabolic Fluxes,” <i>iScience</i> 29, no. 6 (2026): 115822, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.isci.2026.115822. [Open access]","Kalven Bonin et al., “Free Energy Spectroscopy Reveals the Mechanistic Landscape of Chromatin Compaction,” <i>Nucleic Acids Research</i> 54, no. 14 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkag723. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a favourable reaction not always happen quickly?","a":"Because spontaneous means unforced rather than immediate: iron rusts over years, and a book does not burst into flames because its molecules must first be kicked over a barrier of activation energy."},{"q":"Why do biochemists quote a standard change at pH 7 in water?","a":"Because the figure depends not only on the molecules involved but on their concentrations and conditions, so the standard is set at roughly the conditions inside a cell."},{"q":"Why must a metabolic model exclude looping reactions?","a":"Because the sign of the free energy change is a hard physical constraint, and a loop circulating for ever with nothing going in is something no real cell can do."}],"wiki":"Gibbs free energy"},
    "bio-079": {"questions":["Hydrolysing one ATP to shift three sodium ions out of a cell and two potassium ions in is a textbook case of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, done by transferring a phosphate.","Because the free energy changes of two reactions run together simply add up, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lets a cell pay for building work out of the energy released by breaking food down."],"abstract":"<b>Energy coupling</b> is the trick by which a cell drives a reaction that would never happen on its own, running it together with a second reaction that releases more energy than the first one needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The free energy change of the combined reaction is simply the sum of the changes of its parts, so the pair will run as long as that total is negative, and enzymes are what hold the two together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Without it almost all the energy released by oxidising food would escape as heat, and what coupling does is catch that release in a chemically useful form instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The catch is held in a small set of activated carrier molecules, of which ATP is the chief, and these diffuse through the cell carrying energy from where it is made to where it is spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Hydrolysing ATP to ADP releases about 30 kJ per mole under standard conditions,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and almost double that inside a cell, where the concentrations are nothing like standard.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Nothing is chemically remarkable about the bonds between its phosphates, which are called high-energy only because breaking them in a cell releases so much free energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The commonest way of spending that release is phosphorylation, in which the outermost phosphate is handed to another molecule rather than drifting off, leaving it unstable and making it change shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The sodium-potassium pump works exactly so, hydrolysing one ATP for each cycle that carries three sodium ions out of the cell and two potassium ions in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That ratio has consequences, because exporting a net positive charge every cycle makes the pump electrogenic, which helps prevent runaway firing but also shifts a busy neuron's baseline and can disturb what it computes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Coupling is a drug target too, and the bacterial transporters that spend ATP to pull vitamins in, having no counterpart in us, are being pursued as an antibiotic route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.4 ATP: Adenosine Triphosphate,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Metabolic Energy,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9903/. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Liz Weerdmeester, Jan-Hendrik Schleimer, and Susanne Schreiber, “The Electrogenicity of the Na+/K+-ATPase Poses Challenges for Computation in Highly Active Spiking Cells,” <i>eLife</i> 14 (2025), https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.103781. [Open access]","Eleonora Diamanti et al., “Targeting the Energy-Coupling Factor Transporters: A Novel Antibiotic Drug Target in <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>,” <i>Journal of Medicinal Chemistry</i> 69, no. 12 (2026): 14054–68, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jmedchem.5c03638. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can coupling drive a reaction that would never run alone?","a":"Because the free energy change of the combined reaction is the sum of its parts, so the pair runs as long as that total is negative, with enzymes holding the two together."},{"q":"Why is nothing chemically remarkable about ATP’s so-called high-energy bonds?","a":"Because they are called that only because breaking them in a cell releases so much free energy, not because the bonds themselves are unusual."},{"q":"Why does the sodium-potassium pump’s ratio have consequences?","a":"Because carrying three sodium out for two potassium in exports a net positive charge each cycle, making the pump electrogenic, which shifts a busy neuron’s baseline as well as restraining firing."}]},
    "bio-080": {"questions":["Reactions that would take years at body temperature run in a fraction of a second when the right <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> holds the reacting molecules in position for each other.","Ribonuclease P is unusual because it is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> made of RNA rather than protein, which almost every other one in the cell is built from."],"abstract":"An <b>enzyme</b> is a biological catalyst: a molecule that makes a chemical reaction go faster without itself being consumed or permanently altered by it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Almost all of them are proteins, chains of amino acids folded so that a pocket on the surface, the active site, can hold the reacting molecules in the positions the reaction needs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The molecules an enzyme works on are its substrates, and how closely they fit the active site is what makes enzymes specific, most handling one reaction or a narrow family.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Without them almost nothing in a cell would happen fast enough to matter, because a molecule needs a kick over an energy barrier before it will react, and at the temperatures life runs at those kicks are rare.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> They speed reactions up by well over a millionfold, so a change that would otherwise take years is done in a fraction of a second, and a single cell carries thousands of different ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What an enzyme changes is the rate and nothing else: it leaves the free energy of reactants and products as it found them, so it cannot make an unfavourable reaction favourable or shift where the equilibrium sits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also returns to its original state once the reaction is finished, which is why one molecule of it can work on substrate after substrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cells do not run every enzyme they can make at full tilt, and controlling which are present and how active each is remains a chief way metabolism is governed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Not all enzymes are proteins, since RNA catalyses reactions too, and ribonuclease P, which is found in all three domains of life, has now been imaged working through its RNA component alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They are also designed now rather than only discovered, and one workflow produced an enzyme differing from any natural protein at more than 140 positions that matches natural enzymes for efficiency on a reaction nature never invented.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Dina Listov et al., “Complete Computational Design of High-Efficiency Kemp Elimination Enzymes,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8074 (2025): 1421–27, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09136-2. [Open access]","Yun-Tzai Lee et al., “Structural Basis for Protein-Free Catalysis by Ribonuclease P Ribozyme,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 17, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-71597-4. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why would a cell’s chemistry not happen without enzymes?","a":"Because a molecule needs a kick over an energy barrier before it will react, and at the temperatures life runs at those kicks are rare, so reactions would take years rather than moments."},{"q":"Why can an enzyme not make an unfavourable reaction favourable?","a":"Because it changes the rate and nothing else, leaving the free energy of reactants and products as it found them, so it cannot shift where the equilibrium sits."},{"q":"Why can one enzyme molecule work on substrate after substrate?","a":"Because it returns to its original state once the reaction is finished, being neither consumed nor permanently altered by the change it speeds up."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Glucosidase_enzyme.png/960px-Glucosidase_enzyme.png","title":"An enzyme at work","desc":"A glucosidase and the reaction it catalyses: maltose in, two glucose molecules out.","credit":"Thomas Shafee, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glucosidase_enzyme.png","alt":"A ribbon diagram of an enzyme beside an arrow, with a maltose molecule entering and two glucose molecules leaving."},"wiki":"Enzyme"},
    "bio-081": {"questions":["An enzyme speeds a reaction up by lowering its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and by nothing else, which is why it cannot move an equilibrium or make an unfavourable reaction favourable.","Because forward and reverse reactions pass through the same transition state, a catalyst that lowers the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must speed both directions up by exactly the same factor."],"abstract":"The <b>activation energy</b> is what a molecule must first acquire before it can react at all, since even a reaction that releases energy overall has to pass through a higher-energy arrangement on the way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That arrangement is called the transition state, and the height of the barrier leading up to it, rather than the energy of the products, is what decides how fast the reaction goes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An enzyme catalyses precisely by lowering that barrier, so that at any moment far more molecules carry enough energy to cross it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It leaves the energies of the reactants and the products where they were, and therefore cannot make an unfavourable reaction favourable or move the equilibrium an inch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also lowers the barrier equally in both directions, because the forward and the reverse reaction must pass through the very same transition state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The work is done by binding, the substrate settling into the active site where the surrounding chemistry suits the shape of the transition state better than it suits the substrate itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An enzyme may also strain or contort what it holds, or lend chemical groups of its own that take part in the reaction directly, and it comes back to its original state once the product leaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The gains are enormous, since a serine protease runs something like a million million times faster than the same reaction unhelped, and the textbook picture of a catalytic triad and an oxyanion hole does not on its own account for that.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A quantitative reckoning instead finds features that are destabilising while the enzyme sits at rest, among them strained bond rotations, cramped distances and poor hydrogen bonds, each of them relieved as the transition state forms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured barriers want reading with care, because fitting reaction rate against temperature can give activation energies well away from the real ones when the underlying quantities themselves shift with temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Catalysis and the Use of Energy by Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/. [Open access]","Daniel Herschlag and Siyuan Du, “Understanding How Enzymes Work: The Journey to Ensemble–Function Studies,” <i>The FEBS Journal</i> 293, no. 14 (2026): 4106–19, https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.70456. [Open access]","Matthew J. McLeod and Robert E. Thorne, “Activation Parameters, Enthalpy–Entropy Compensation and the Temperature-Dependent Activity of Enzymes,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 8 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70722. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the barrier’s height rather than the products’ energy what sets the rate?","a":"Because even a reaction that releases energy overall must pass through a higher-energy transition state, and how many molecules carry enough energy to cross that decides how fast it goes."},{"q":"Why does lowering the barrier speed both directions equally?","a":"Because the forward and reverse reactions must pass through the very same transition state, so the catalysis cannot favour one over the other or move the equilibrium."},{"q":"Why does a catalytic triad not explain the speed on its own?","a":"Because a quantitative reckoning finds features that are destabilising while the enzyme rests — strained bond rotations, cramped distances, poor hydrogen bonds — each relieved as the transition state forms."}],"wiki":"Activation energy"},
    "bio-082": {"questions":["Hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and hydrophobic contacts hold a substrate in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> weakly enough that the product can leave once the reaction is done.","Mutations far from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can still improve catalysis by widening its entrance, which shows that a well-organised one is necessary but not sufficient."],"abstract":"The <b>active site</b> is the small part of an enzyme where the reaction actually takes place, a cleft or a groove in the surface into which the substrate settles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a place rather than a stretch of the chain, since the amino acids lining it usually come from parts of the sequence lying far apart, and are brought into company only when the protein folds into its final shape.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Those residues differ in size, in charge and in whether they welcome water or shun it, and their particular combination builds a chemical environment that suits one substrate or a narrow family of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The substrate is held by noncovalent interactions, among them hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and hydrophobic contacts, each weak enough on its own that the product can leave again afterwards.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where a reaction needs two substrates the site holds both at once, in the position and orientation that let them meet, acting as a template on which they are assembled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> It is also where the enzyme's own chemistry does its work, and acidic and basic side chains within the site may react with the substrate directly and form bonds with the intermediates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the product is released the site is left exactly as it was, which is what allows one enzyme molecule to work through substrate after substrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A site is not a bare pocket in a solid lump, and in the heme peroxidases conserved water-filled channels lead into it while a continuous electrostatic funnel draws protons along them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rest of the protein is not scaffolding either, since engineering three designed enzymes showed that mutations far from the site improved catalysis by widening its entrance and reorganising the surface loops, so a well-built site is necessary and not sufficient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Enzyme design now starts from the site: a generative model given only the geometry of the catalytic groups built scaffolds for all 41 sites in one benchmark, where earlier methods managed 16.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Reynier Suardíaz et al., “Solvent Channels and Electric Fields Guide Proton Delivery to the Active Site of Heme Peroxidases,” <i>Angewandte Chemie International Edition</i> 64, no. 50 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1002/anie.202515743. [Open access]","Niayesh Zarifi et al., “Distal Mutations Enhance Catalysis in Designed Enzymes by Facilitating Substrate Binding and Product Release,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63802-7. [Open access]","Woody Ahern et al., “Atom-Level Enzyme Active Site Scaffolding Using RFdiffusion2,” <i>Nature Methods</i> 23, no. 1 (2026): 96–105, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-025-02975-x. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the active site a place rather than a stretch of the chain?","a":"Because the amino acids lining it usually come from parts of the sequence lying far apart, and are brought into company only when the protein folds into its final shape."},{"q":"Why must the substrate be held by weak interactions?","a":"Because hydrogen bonds, ionic bonds and hydrophobic contacts are each weak enough on their own that the product can leave again afterwards, freeing the site for the next molecule."},{"q":"Why is a well-built active site necessary but not sufficient?","a":"Because engineering three designed enzymes showed mutations far from the site improving catalysis by widening its entrance and reorganising the surface loops."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Enzyme_structure.svg/1920px-Enzyme_structure.svg.png","title":"The active site","desc":"The binding sites that hold and orient the substrate, and the catalytic site that lowers the energy the reaction needs.","credit":"Thomas Shafee, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enzyme_structure.svg","alt":"A protein surface with a pocket marked, the binding sites coloured blue and the catalytic site red."},"wiki":"Active site"},
    "bio-083": {"questions":["Bending a substrate towards the shape it must take in the transition state is how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> explains catalysis, where the lock-and-key picture explained only specificity.","Stopped-flow mixing and NMR can tell <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, in which the ligand binds first, from conformational selection, in which the protein changes shape before anything binds."],"abstract":"<b>Induced fit</b> is the idea that an enzyme and its substrate each change shape as they come together, moulding one another rather than meeting like a key in a lock already cut to receive it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That older picture is the lock-and-key model, in which the substrate fits precisely into a site that is the right shape for it before it arrives, and which accounts neatly for specificity and for nothing else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Under induced fit the binding alters the conformation of both partners, and the substrate is bent towards the shape it will have to take in the transition state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The strain of that distortion weakens the very bonds that must break, and the transition state, once reached, is held tightly by the enzyme, which is where the drop in activation energy comes from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The enzyme takes no lasting harm from any of it and settles back to its resting shape as soon as the product has gone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Neither model belongs to enzymes alone, and both are used to describe any protein taking up a small molecule, a drug included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A third description, conformational selection, has the protein already flickering between shapes of its own accord, with the ligand binding whichever suits it, so that the change comes before the binding rather than after.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The two can be told apart by how fast things happen, and for the neurotensin receptor 1 both stopped-flow mixing and nuclear magnetic resonance point to induced fit, the ligand binding the inactive shape first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> None of the three is a full account, and one complaint is that they describe how a ligand arrives while saying nothing about how it leaves, although how tightly it is held depends on both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The subject has moved past single shapes altogether, towards treating a protein as a whole population of conformations, since the textbook drawings do not by themselves explain the rates enzymes reach.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Danislav S. Spassov, “Binding Affinity Determination in Drug Design: Insights from Lock and Key, Induced Fit, Conformational Selection, and Inhibitor Trapping Models,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 25, no. 13 (2024): 7124, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms25137124. [Open access]","Kazem Asadollahi, Paul R. Gooley, and Thomas R. Weikl, “The Allosteric Mechanism of G-Protein-Coupled Receptors Is Induced Fit, Not Conformational Selection,” <i>BioEssays</i> 47, no. 12 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1002/bies.70082. [Open access]","Daniel Herschlag and Siyuan Du, “Understanding How Enzymes Work: The Journey to Ensemble–Function Studies,” <i>The FEBS Journal</i> 293, no. 14 (2026): 4106–19, https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.70456. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the lock-and-key picture not enough?","a":"Because it accounts neatly for specificity and for nothing else, saying nothing about where the drop in activation energy comes from."},{"q":"Why does moulding the substrate help the reaction along?","a":"Because the substrate is bent towards the shape it must take in the transition state, and the strain of that distortion weakens the very bonds that have to break."},{"q":"Why can conformational selection be told apart from induced fit?","a":"Because the two differ in how fast things happen: for neurotensin receptor 1, stopped-flow mixing and nuclear magnetic resonance both point to the ligand binding the inactive shape first."}]},
    "bio-084": {"questions":["The Michaelis constant, meaning the substrate concentration at which a reaction runs at half its maximum rate, is the quantity <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> exists to measure alongside the maximum itself.","Piling on more substrate stops helping once every active site is refilled the moment it empties, which is the saturation <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was built to describe."],"abstract":"<b>Enzyme kinetics</b> is the study of how fast an enzyme-catalysed reaction runs and of what that speed depends on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The mechanism is easily stated: an enzyme binds its substrate reversibly, and the complex either goes forward irreversibly to product or falls apart again with nothing made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Measure the rate at a series of substrate concentrations and two numbers fall out: the maximal rate the reaction can reach, and the concentration at which it reaches half of that, the Michaelis constant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The relation they belong to is the Michaelis-Menten equation, proposed by Henri and by Michaelis and Menten and put into its familiar form by Briggs and Haldane, on the assumption that the enzyme-substrate complex holds a steady concentration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Michaelis constant is not a measure of binding strength but a ratio of rate constants, setting how fast the complex forms against how fast it either breaks up or turns over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> At low substrate concentrations most enzyme molecules are standing idle and the rate climbs almost in proportion to what is on offer, a regime with conditions of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Piling on more cannot raise it for ever, since every active site is refilled the moment it empties, and the rate levels off at a ceiling set by how fast the enzyme itself can work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The catalytic constant and the Michaelis constant are how an enzyme's efficiency and its specificity are put into numbers, and they are now being tabulated against the three-dimensional structures of the complexes they describe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The equation rests on two idealisations, that the mixture is uniform in space and that the complex holds steady in time, and neither is guaranteed outside a stirred flask, as a wearable enzyme sensor worn under the skin has shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Two constants also cannot hold everything a turnover contains, and higher-order forms of the equation now recover the lifetime of the complex and the odds that a binding yields any product at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Divya Singh, Tal Robin, Michael Urbakh, and Shlomi Reuveni, “High-Order Michaelis-Menten Equations Allow Inference of Hidden Kinetic Parameters in Enzyme Catalysis,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16, no. 1 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-57327-2. [Open access]","Seolah Shin, Seok Joo Chae, Seunggyu Lee, and Jae Kyoung Kim, “Beyond Homogeneity: Assessing the Validity of the Michaelis-Menten Rate Law in Spatially Heterogeneous Environments,” <i>PLOS Computational Biology</i> 20, no. 6 (2024): e1012205, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1012205. [Open access]","Marco Fratus and Muhammad A. Alam, “Can We Still Use the Michaelis-Menten Model for Enzymatic Microneedle Sensors?,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 30 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2418168122. [Open access]","Justin Eilertsen, Santiago Schnell, and Sebastian Walcher, “The Michaelis-Menten Reaction at Low Substrate Concentrations: Pseudo-First-Order Kinetics and Conditions for Timescale Separation,” <i>Bulletin of Mathematical Biology</i> 86, no. 6 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1007/s11538-024-01295-z. [Open access]","Sowmya Ramaswamy Krishnan, Nishtha Pandey, Rajgopal Srinivasan, and Arijit Roy, “A Structure-Oriented Kinetics Dataset of Enzyme-Substrate Interactions,” <i>Scientific Data</i> 12, no. 1 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-025-05829-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the rate level off as substrate is piled on?","a":"Because every active site is refilled the moment it empties, so the ceiling is set by how fast the enzyme itself can work rather than by what is on offer."},{"q":"Why is the Michaelis constant not a measure of binding strength?","a":"Because it is a ratio of rate constants, setting how fast the complex forms against how fast it either breaks up or turns over."},{"q":"Why do the equation’s idealisations matter outside a flask?","a":"Because it assumes the mixture is uniform in space and the complex holds steady in time, neither of which is guaranteed — as a wearable enzyme sensor worn under the skin has shown."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/MM-curve.jpg/1920px-MM-curve.jpg","title":"The Michaelis-Menten curve","desc":"Reaction rate against substrate concentration: rising steeply, then flattening as the enzyme runs out of capacity.","credit":"Athel cb, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MM-curve.jpg","alt":"A graph curving up from the origin and levelling off, with the half-maximal rate and the Michaelis constant marked."},"wiki":"Enzyme kinetics"},
    "bio-085": {"questions":["An enzyme from a hot spring holds its shape with extensive salt bridges, which is why its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lies far above that of the same enzyme from a cold-water organism.","Many enzymes reach their fastest rate well below the temperature at which they unfold, so unfolding cannot be the whole reason the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sits where it does."],"abstract":"An enzyme's <b>optimum temperature</b> is the temperature at which it works fastest, the peak of a curve that climbs steadily and then falls away sharply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The climb is ordinary chemistry, since warmth makes molecules move faster and collide harder, so more of them arrive carrying enough energy to clear the barrier, and reaction rates in general rise with temperature whether an enzyme is present or not.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Move the temperature outside a fairly narrow range in either direction and the bonds within the active site are altered, so that it no longer holds its substrate as well as it did.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Push it far enough upward and the enzyme denatures outright, its folded shape coming apart, after which it can bind nothing at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Different organisms work at very different temperatures, and an organism's own is written into its proteins clearly enough that it can be predicted from genome sequence alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The molecular differences are known in outline: enzymes from hot environments hold their shape with extensive salt bridges and hydrophobic networks, while cold-adapted ones rely instead on a few carefully placed interactions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The textbook curve is nonetheless too simple, because many enzymes reach their fastest rate well below the temperature at which they unfold, so denaturation cannot be the whole reason the rate falls away.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The favoured explanation has the enthalpy and entropy of activation themselves shifting with temperature, and fitting it to those enzymes yields physically implausible numbers, so something else is contributing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Adaptation itself can be startlingly economical, and in one bacterial enzyme the shift to a different working temperature came down largely to a single change of residue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That same change turns up in diverse backgrounds, and residues associated with an organism's growth temperature have been identified across 1,005 bacterial enzyme families, which suggests the adjustment has been made over and over independently.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Mensur Dlakić and William P. Inskeep, “Improved Prediction of Microbial Optimal Growth Temperatures with Neural Networks and Protein Language Models,” <i>Frontiers in Genetics</i> 17 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2026.1874451. [Open access]","Dulitha P. Kulathunga and Davit A. Potoyan, “Thermal Adaptation of Extremozymes: Temperature-Sensitive Contact Analysis of Serine Proteases,” <i>Biophysical Journal</i> 124, no. 14 (2025): 2291–2302, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpj.2025.06.001. [Open access]","Matthew J. McLeod and Robert E. Thorne, “Activation Parameters, Enthalpy–Entropy Compensation and the Temperature-Dependent Activity of Enzymes,” <i>Protein Science</i> 35, no. 8 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70722. [Open access]","Margaux M. Pinney et al., “Parallel Molecular Mechanisms for Enzyme Temperature Adaptation,” <i>Science</i> 371, no. 6533 (2021), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13050526/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the rate climb with temperature at first?","a":"Because warmth makes molecules move faster and collide harder, so more of them arrive carrying enough energy to clear the barrier — ordinary chemistry, enzyme or no enzyme."},{"q":"Why is denaturation not the whole reason the curve falls away?","a":"Because many enzymes reach their fastest rate well below the temperature at which they unfold, so something else must be pulling the rate down first."},{"q":"Why does adapting to a new working temperature look economical?","a":"Because in one bacterial enzyme the shift came down largely to a single change of residue, and such residues have been identified across 1,005 bacterial enzyme families."}]},
    "bio-086": {"questions":["Because pepsin works in the stomach's strong acid and the pancreatic proteases in an alkaline intestine, their values of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> lie far apart, which is why the body separates them.","For the AlkB repair enzymes a shift in acidity changes not just the rate but which DNA strand they prefer, so their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not the whole story."],"abstract":"An enzyme's <b>optimum pH</b> is the acidity at which it works fastest, and every enzyme is suited to a certain range on either side of it rather than to any acidity at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reason lies in the active site, whose amino acid residues carry acidic and basic groups of their own that have to be in the right state for catalysis to happen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Changing the acidity adds protons to those groups or takes them away, so the site binds its substrate less well than it did, and a large enough change unfolds the enzyme altogether.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What sets the figure can be traced to particular residues: in two bacterial enzymes it turned on a single pair beside the cofactor, and altering the charges there moved both towards working in mildly acidic conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One catalytic residue's state can be worth a study of its own, as it has been for the cysteine that does the cutting in the protease of the parasite behind Chagas disease.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The body puts the same principle to work by holding different compartments at different acidities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Chief cells release pepsinogen into the stomach, where hydrochloric acid both cleaves it into the working enzyme pepsin and unfolds the dietary protein it is about to cut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Further along, bicarbonate from the pancreas raises the acidity of the intestinal contents, and it is that rise which wakes the pancreatic proteases from the inactive forms they are secreted in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Measured across a wide range, enzymes generally give bell-shaped activity curves with peaks of their own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Acidity can also change more than the rate, and for the AlkB family of DNA repair enzymes it shifts which strand they prefer to work on, one of them favouring single-stranded DNA in acid and double-stranded DNA nearer neutral.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Yuanxiang Liu et al., “Electrostatic Tuning of the Pyridoxal-5′-Phosphate Cofactor Site Defines pH Dependence in Type I Cystathionine β-Lyases,” <i>Journal of Biological Chemistry</i> 302, no. 6 (2026): 111469, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2026.111469. [Open access]","Clauber H. S. da Costa et al., “Evaluating the Protonation State of the Catalytic Cys25 in Cruzain Cysteine Protease: A Target for Chagas Disease,” <i>Protein Science</i> 34, no. 10 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1002/pro.70283. [Open access]","Tatyana S. Gurina and Shamim S. Mohiuddin, “Biochemistry, Protein Catabolism,” in <i>StatPearls</i> (Treasure Island, FL: StatPearls Publishing, 2022), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK556047/. [Open access]","Samuel D. Howarth et al., “pH Tunes the DNA Repair Efficiency and Strand Preference of the AlkB Family Enzymes,” <i>Chemical Research in Toxicology</i> 39, no. 7 (2026): 1418–30, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.chemrestox.6c00202. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does acidity change what an enzyme can do?","a":"Because the active site’s residues carry acidic and basic groups that must be in the right state, and changing the pH adds protons to them or takes them away."},{"q":"Why does the body hold its compartments at different acidities?","a":"Because it uses pH as a switch: stomach acid both cleaves pepsinogen into pepsin and unfolds the dietary protein, while pancreatic bicarbonate wakes the proteases further along."},{"q":"Why can acidity change more than the rate?","a":"Because for the AlkB family of DNA repair enzymes it shifts which strand they prefer, one favouring single-stranded DNA in acid and double-stranded nearer neutral."}]},
    "bio-087": {"questions":["Isoleucine shutting down the first enzyme of the pathway that makes it is feedback inhibition, which means the amino acid is acting as an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on its own supply.","A reactive cysteine that LIMK1 has and its near-twin LIMK2 has not lets a covalent <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> pick one kinase out of more than a hundred."],"abstract":"An <b>enzyme inhibitor</b> is a molecule that binds an enzyme and reduces or stops what it does, and cells and pharmacists alike depend on them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some are similar enough to the substrate to settle into the active site itself and simply occupy the place the substrate would have taken.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Others fasten somewhere else entirely, at what is called an allosteric site, and alter the enzyme's shape so that the active site holds its substrate less willingly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same trick runs in reverse, since allosteric activators bind away from the active site and change the shape the other way, raising rather than lowering the enzyme's appetite for its substrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cells govern themselves with inhibition: in feedback inhibition the end product of a pathway shuts down the first enzyme in it, so that isoleucine switches off threonine deaminase and no more is made than is wanted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Inhibition may be reversible or permanent, and a covalent inhibitor forms an actual bond with the enzyme rather than merely resting in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That permanence can buy precision: LIM domain kinase 1 carries a reactive cysteine near its active site which its near-twin LIM domain kinase 2 lacks, and an inhibitor built to react with it proved selective over that twin and over a panel of more than a hundred other kinases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which kind of inhibitor a molecule is cannot be assumed and has to be measured, and one recent method tells rapid reversible, slow-binding and covalent inhibition apart from the heat a single reaction gives off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It was tested on the main protease of the coronavirus behind the pandemic, against four of its inhibitors including the one in the antiviral pill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most drugs work by inhibiting an enzyme, and fresh targets are still being opened, among them bacterial transporters with no counterpart in us whose inhibitors cut pneumococcal load in mice.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Jon B. Patteson et al., “A Covalent Inhibitor Targeting Cys-349 of LIMK1 Confers Selectivity over LIMK2,” <i>Journal of Biological Chemistry</i> 302, no. 9 (2026): 113322, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2026.113322. [Open access]","Luca Mazzei, Sofia Ranieri, Davide Silvestri, Gaetano T. Montelione, and Stefano Ciurli, “Decoding Enzyme-Inhibitor Kinetic Mechanisms by Isothermal Titration Calorimetry: The Case of SARS-CoV-2 3CLpro,” <i>Analytical Chemistry</i> 98, no. 26 (2026): 19520–32, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.analchem.6c00471. [Open access]","Eleonora Diamanti et al., “Targeting the Energy-Coupling Factor Transporters: A Novel Antibiotic Drug Target in <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i>,” <i>Journal of Medicinal Chemistry</i> 69, no. 12 (2026): 14054–68, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jmedchem.5c03638. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can an inhibitor work without touching the active site?","a":"Because it can fasten at an allosteric site and alter the enzyme’s shape, so that the active site holds its substrate less willingly."},{"q":"Why is feedback inhibition a sensible way to govern a pathway?","a":"Because the end product shuts down the first enzyme in it — isoleucine switching off threonine deaminase — so no more is made than is wanted."},{"q":"Why can permanence buy precision in a drug?","a":"Because LIM domain kinase 1 carries a reactive cysteine near its active site that its near-twin lacks, so an inhibitor built to react with it was selective over that twin and a hundred other kinases."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Enzyme_inhibitors_2.svg/1920px-Enzyme_inhibitors_2.svg.png","title":"How an inhibitor stops an enzyme","desc":"Catalysis above, inhibition below: the inhibitor occupies the site the substrate needs.","credit":"Thomas Shafee, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enzyme_inhibitors_2.svg","alt":"Two rows of diagrams. In the first an enzyme binds a substrate and releases product; in the second it binds an inhibitor and the substrate is blocked."},"wiki":"Enzyme inhibitor"},
    "bio-088": {"questions":["Because the two molecules are contending for the same pocket, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> slows the early part of a reaction while leaving its top speed exactly where it was.","Where an inhibitor binds away from the active site the maximum rate itself drops, which is not what happens under <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where the pocket is contested."],"abstract":"<b>Competitive inhibition</b> is what happens when an inhibitor resembles an enzyme's substrate closely enough to occupy the active site, so that the two contend for the same pocket and whichever arrives first is served.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In non-competitive inhibition the inhibitor binds somewhere else on the enzyme altogether, at an allosteric site, and still manages to stop the substrate being turned over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two leave different marks on the rate: a competitive inhibitor slows the early part of the reaction and leaves its maximum untouched, while a non-competitive one pulls the maximum itself down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That difference follows from where each one sits, because a rival for the pocket can always be outnumbered by piling on more substrate whereas a molecule bound elsewhere cannot.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An allosteric site is a real place on the protein, distinct from the catalytic one, and the cell's own regulators use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Both patterns are found in real enzymes and both are pursued as drugs, and a non-competitive inhibitor of the DNA repair polymerase beta, built from unnatural nucleotides, is among the most potent known for that enzyme, which is overexpressed in some cancers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which pattern an inhibitor follows is settled by measurement rather than by looking at its shape, and calorimetry can now sort rapid reversible inhibition from slow-binding and covalent kinds within a single experiment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The textbook pair is also a simplification, since an inhibitor may show a mixed pattern with a share of each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Acetic acid released during the pretreatment of plant biomass inhibits two families of xylanase differently, one in a mixed fashion that is mostly non-competitive and the other in a third way again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Knowing which is not academic there, since it decides how a biorefinery should be run: both enzymes lose only a tenth or so of their activity up to 0.8% acetic acid and almost all of it at 1.0.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","A. Hasan Howlader, Xuanhe Jiang, Zehui Zhou, and Marc M. Greenberg, “Noncompetitive Inhibition of DNA Polymerase β by a Nonnative Nucleotide,” <i>The Journal of Organic Chemistry</i> 90, no. 37 (2025): 12849–59, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.joc.5c01529. [Open access]","Luca Mazzei, Sofia Ranieri, Davide Silvestri, Gaetano T. Montelione, and Stefano Ciurli, “Decoding Enzyme-Inhibitor Kinetic Mechanisms by Isothermal Titration Calorimetry: The Case of SARS-CoV-2 3CLpro,” <i>Analytical Chemistry</i> 98, no. 26 (2026): 19520–32, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.analchem.6c00471. [Open access]","Dan-Gyeong Han, Beom Soo Kim, and In Jung Kim, “Distinct Mechanisms of Acetic Acid Inhibition in Thermophilic GH10 and GH11 Xylanases,” <i>Journal of Microbiology and Biotechnology</i> 36 (2026), https://doi.org/10.4014/jmb.2602.02022. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can more substrate overcome one kind of inhibitor and not the other?","a":"Because a rival for the same pocket can always be outnumbered, whereas a molecule bound elsewhere on the enzyme cannot be crowded out of a place it does not occupy."},{"q":"Why do the two kinds leave different marks on the rate?","a":"Because a competitive inhibitor slows the early part of the reaction and leaves its maximum untouched, while a non-competitive one pulls the maximum itself down."},{"q":"Why does knowing which pattern applies matter industrially?","a":"Because it decides how a biorefinery is run: the two xylanase families lose only a tenth of their activity up to 0.8% acetic acid and almost all of it at 1.0."}],"wiki":"Competitive inhibition"},
    "bio-089": {"questions":["Because DNA polymerase will not build anything without a bound zinc ion, that ion counts as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than as a substrate, and it leaves the reaction unchanged.","Pyruvate dehydrogenase needs one magnesium ion and five different organic helpers, so a single reaction can turn on more than half a dozen kinds of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at once."],"abstract":"A <b>cofactor</b> is a non-protein helper that many enzymes will not work without, bound to them either loosely by weak forces or permanently by covalent ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commonest are metal ions, and the enzyme that builds DNA needs a zinc ion in place before it will assemble anything at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Organic helpers are called coenzymes, most of them made from vitamins in the diet, so that vitamin C is a coenzyme for the enzymes that build collagen and a deficiency shows in the connective tissue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The words are not used identically everywhere, since some texts keep cofactor for the inorganic ions and coenzyme for the organic helpers,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> while others treat cofactor as the wider word covering both, along with the prosthetic groups that stay bound for good, as haem does in haemoglobin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Coenzymes differ from substrates in not being used up, since they are recycled and go round again, and NAD carries electrons from one reaction to another in exactly that way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A single reaction can call on several at once, and pyruvate dehydrogenase, which opens the aerobic breakdown of sugar, needs one magnesium ion and five different organic coenzymes together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because most coenzymes are built from vitamins, an animal that has lost the ability to make one must eat it, where a bacterium such as <i>E. coli</i> needs none from outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The protein does not simply hold its helper but tunes it, and across the flavoenzymes the same cofactor is made to work over an extremely wide range of redox conditions, its range set by which of its nitrogens the surrounding protein keeps protonated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Which metal is needed is a real distinction rather than a detail, and two newly described enzymes from bacteriophages attacking a urinary pathogen proved to need different metals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Some cofactors are elaborate enough to need enzymes of their own to build them, and the nickel-containing one assembled by a hexameric enzyme with a histidine-rich pocket has now been imaged mid-assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “6.5 Enzymes,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-5-enzymes. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/. [Open access]","Wolfgang Nitschke, Simon Duval, Kilian Zuchan, Jostin Monge-Ruiz, Frauke Baymann, and Barbara Schoepp-Cothenet, “pK Values of the Cofactor Tune the Redox Regime of Flavoenzymes,” <i>Life</i> 16, no. 8 (2026): 1277, https://doi.org/10.3390/life16081277. [Open access]","Akash Shambharkar et al., “First Experimentally Characterised Proteus Phage Endolysins Have Distinct Metal Cofactor Dependencies,” <i>Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology</i> 110, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1007/s00253-026-13891-1. [Open access]","Robert Wolfe et al., “Structural and Functional Analysis of LarC, a CTP-Dependent Cyclometallase Required for Nickel-Pincer Nucleotide Cofactor Biosynthesis,” <i>Science Advances</i> 12, no. 35 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.aeh6867. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a coenzyme not simply another substrate?","a":"Because it is not used up: it is recycled and goes round again, as NAD does in carrying electrons from one reaction to another."},{"q":"Why must an animal eat vitamins where a bacterium need not?","a":"Because most coenzymes are built from vitamins, so an animal that has lost the ability to make one has to take it in, while E. coli needs none from outside."},{"q":"Why does the protein around a cofactor matter as much as the cofactor?","a":"Because it tunes it: across the flavoenzymes the same cofactor works over an extremely wide range of redox conditions, its range set by which nitrogens the protein keeps protonated."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Succinate_Dehydrogenase_1YQ3_Electron_Carriers_Labeled.png/960px-Succinate_Dehydrogenase_1YQ3_Electron_Carriers_Labeled.png","title":"Cofactors in succinate dehydrogenase","desc":"The carriers an enzyme cannot work without, drawn in place along the path the electrons take.","credit":"Richard Wheeler ( Zephyris ), CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Succinate_Dehydrogenase_1YQ3_Electron_Carriers_Labeled.png","alt":"A coloured structure of a membrane-bound enzyme with FAD and iron-sulphur centres labelled along a chain of arrows."},"wiki":"Cofactor (biochemistry)"},
    "bio-090": {"questions":["Fixing an enzyme to a solid support so that it can be filtered out and used again is the standard economy of industrial <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since the enzyme is the expensive part.","An asparagine synthetase evolved in the laboratory to make an anti-epileptic intermediate in one step shows how <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can be given reactions that no organism performs."],"abstract":"<b>Biocatalysis</b> is the use of enzymes to run chemical processes on purpose, and its attractions over ordinary industrial chemistry are mild conditions, high efficiency and products that break down in the environment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difficulty is that a factory is a hard place for a protein, and the conditions of real manufacturing destabilise enzymes and shorten their working lives.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The usual answer is immobilisation, fixing the enzyme to a solid support so that it can be recovered and used over and over, which is what makes the economics work when the enzyme is the expensive part of the process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Immobilisation is not merely a way of holding an enzyme still, since the method chosen alters how the enzyme behaves and so changes the product as well as the cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Where the enzymes come from is a question in itself, and the guts of insects are one reservoir being searched for cellulases, amylases, proteases and lipases that are already stable because their owners live in hard places.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> An enzyme need not be taken as found, and directed evolution builds better ones by making many variants and keeping the best.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Screening is the bottleneck there, so one group rebuilt a bacterial transcription factor into a sensor that lights up for the product it wanted and used it to search mutant libraries, ending with an asparagine synthetase that makes an anti-epileptic intermediate in a single step.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That reaction is one no organism performs, which is the direction the field is going.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Enzymes are also now designed outright rather than evolved, and a computational workflow has produced one differing from any natural protein at more than 140 positions that matches natural enzymes for efficiency.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The thermal end is understood well enough to engineer, since enzymes from hot environments hold together through extensive salt bridges and hydrophobic networks that a designer can copy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Marina G. Holyavka and Valeriy G. Artyukhov, “Prospects and Problems in Enzyme Immobilization Methodology: Comprehensive Review,” <i>Biophysical Reviews</i> 17, no. 5 (2025): 1471–1509, https://doi.org/10.1007/s12551-025-01360-4. [Open access]","Aswin Viswan and Neenu Augustine, “Insect Gut Microbiota as a Reservoir of Industrially Relevant Enzymes: A Comprehensive Review,” <i>Microbial Ecology</i> 89, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1007/s00248-026-02726-1. [Open access]","Qianpeng Lai et al., “Creating a Non-Native Bio-Amidation Activity in Asparagine Synthetase via Biosensor-Guided Directed Evolution for the Synthesis of (S)-2-Aminobutyramide,” <i>Chemical Science</i> (2026), https://doi.org/10.1039/D6SC04510C. [Open access]","Dina Listov et al., “Complete Computational Design of High-Efficiency Kemp Elimination Enzymes,” <i>Nature</i> 643, no. 8074 (2025): 1421–27, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09136-2. [Open access]","Dulitha P. Kulathunga and Davit A. Potoyan, “Thermal Adaptation of Extremozymes: Temperature-Sensitive Contact Analysis of Serine Proteases,” <i>Biophysical Journal</i> 124, no. 14 (2025): 2291–2302, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bpj.2025.06.001. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a factory a hard place for an enzyme?","a":"Because the conditions of real manufacturing destabilise proteins and shorten their working lives, which is what immobilisation on a solid support is meant to answer."},{"q":"Why is immobilisation more than a way of holding an enzyme still?","a":"Because the method chosen alters how the enzyme behaves, so it changes the product as well as the cost of recovering and reusing it."},{"q":"Why is screening the bottleneck in directed evolution?","a":"Because the method makes many variants and keeps the best, so the limit is telling which is best — answered in one case by rebuilding a transcription factor into a sensor that lights up for the product."}],"wiki":"Biocatalysis"},
    "bio-092": {"questions":["Two men studying plant and animal tissues in the late 1830s arrived at the same conclusion from opposite directions, and the statement they arrived at is <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, still the frame of the subject.","The claim in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that every cell descends from an earlier one is what makes the ancestry of everything alive an unbroken chain running back to a single population."],"abstract":"<b>Cell theory</b> holds three things at once: that one or more cells make up every living thing, that the cell is the basic unit of life, and that new cells arise only from cells that already exist.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was put together in the late 1830s by a botanist and a zoologist working on plant and animal tissue respectively, who found they were describing the same unit, and it was sharpened afterwards by others.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first claim is a statement about scope: an organism is either one cell, as a bacterium is, or an assembly of them organised into tissues, organs and systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second is a statement about limits, because nothing smaller than a cell can gather materials and build a fresh copy of itself complete with its own instructions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The third is the one that does the most work, since it turns every organism alive into the end of an unbroken chain of divisions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> That chain has a direction and a length, and reconstruction places its origin at about 4.2 billion years ago in a population already carrying some 2,600 proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The theory is also what makes the origin of life a hard problem rather than a vague one, because the first cell is precisely the thing the third claim forbids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its evidence was optical from the start, and it could not have been framed before lenses good enough to show that tissue is made of separate units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A single ground lens in the hands of a Delft draper had already resolved bacteria, red blood cells and the flow in a capillary, a century and a half before the theory was stated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the theory added was not a new sight but a claim about everything, which is why it counts as the frame the subject has been built in ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Edmund R. R. Moody et al., “The Nature of the Last Universal Common Ancestor and Its Impact on the Early Earth System,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 8, no. 9 (2024): 1654–66, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-024-02461-1. [Open access]","Hyunjeong Lee and Im Joo Rhyu, “Antonie van Leeuwenhoek and the Dawn of Microscopic Observation: A Narrative Review from Delft's Lens to the Modern Microscope,” <i>Applied Microscopy</i> 56, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1186/s42649-026-00123-z. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Origin and Evolution of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9841/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the third claim do the most work?","a":"Because holding that new cells arise only from existing ones turns every organism alive into the end of an unbroken chain of divisions running back to one population."},{"q":"Why does the theory make the origin of life a sharp problem?","a":"Because the first cell is precisely the thing the third claim forbids, so the theory names what has to be explained rather than leaving it vague."},{"q":"Why could the theory not have been stated earlier?","a":"Because its evidence was optical from the start and needed lenses good enough to show that tissue is made of separate units."}],"wiki":"Cell theory"},
    "bio-093": {"questions":["Rows of cavities in cork were read in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as narrow pipes divided at intervals by valves, and it was a segment of one such pipe that got the name we still use.","The tale that the boxes were named after the rooms of a monastery is a myth, since no monk and no monastery appears anywhere in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or in its author's other writings."],"abstract":"<b>Micrographia</b> is the folio of 1665 in which the word cell entered biology, a set of engravings of what a compound microscope showed of very small things.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its author, the Royal Society's Curator of Experiments, was not idly squinting at a slice of cork: he lit specimens indirectly and cut sections in several planes to reconstruct plant material in three dimensions, and found the same texture in the pith of eight further species he names.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What he called a cell was a segment of pipe, since he read the rows of cavities as narrow tubes divided by valves that regulated the flow of fluid, which is what the biology of his day expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> He did not see only empty walls either, recording that cells in fresh material were filled with juices and sweated their contents out as the specimen dried.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The familiar story that he named them after a monastery's rooms is a myth, repeated in textbooks and on educational sites: no monk and no monastery appears anywhere in his writings on cells, nor the Latin word they supposedly come from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A better instrument was at work within a decade, and it was far simpler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A draper in Delft ground single lenses that magnified 270 times and sometimes 500, where the microscopes before them managed about 20, the gain coming from fewer optical surfaces rather than more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Looking at pond water in 1674 he described green globules and, among them, very many little animalcules, and he was probably the first person to see a protozoan.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The claim was doubted, so the Royal Society sent a party to Delft in 1680 and confirmed it, and his letters ran for 50 years, reporting red blood cells, capillary flow and the animalcules in a healthy mouth's plaque.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Neither man had a theory of the cell, and what neither could guess is that the box named here would prove to be the smallest thing able to gather materials and build a copy of itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Winfried S. Peters, “Will the Real Robert Hooke Please Stand Up?,” <i>The Plant Cell</i> 36, no. 11 (2024): 4680–82, https://doi.org/10.1093/plcell/koae244. [Open access]","Hyunjeong Lee and Im Joo Rhyu, “Antonie van Leeuwenhoek and the Dawn of Microscopic Observation: A Narrative Review from Delft's Lens to the Modern Microscope,” <i>Applied Microscopy</i> 56, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1186/s42649-026-00123-z. [Open access]","Robert Gaynes, “Antony van Leeuwenhoek, Pioneer of the Microscopic World,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 32, no. 6 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3206.260531. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why did Hooke read his cavities as pipes rather than as cells?","a":"Because he took the rows of cavities for narrow tubes divided by valves regulating the flow of fluid, which is what the biology of his day expected to find in a plant."},{"q":"Why is the monastery story about the word’s origin a myth?","a":"Because no monk, no monastery and not even the Latin word they supposedly come from appears anywhere in his writings on cells, though textbooks repeat it."},{"q":"Why did a single-lens instrument beat the compound microscopes?","a":"Because the gain came from fewer optical surfaces rather than more: a Delft draper’s ground lenses magnified 270 times and sometimes 500 where the compound instruments managed about 20."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Micrographia_Schem_11.jpg/960px-Micrographia_Schem_11.jpg","title":"Hooke's cork, Micrographia plate XI","desc":"The plate on which the word cell entered biology: two cuts of cork under a compound microscope, one across the grain showing rounded pores end-on and one along it showing rows of oblong boxes, with a branch and sprig drawn at natural size beneath.","credit":"Robert Hooke, Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Micrographia_Schem_11.jpg","alt":"An engraved plate from a 17th-century folio. A large circle, the microscope's field of view, is filled against a black ground with two pieces of cork: on the right a mass of small rounded pores seen end-on, on the left a lattice of oblong boxes seen along the grain, each marked with a letter. Beneath the circle a thin branch carries a feathery sprig of leaves, drawn at natural size."},"wiki":"Micrographia"},
    "bio-094": {"questions":["A cell of this kind carries its chromosome in a nucleoid, makes its proteins on ribosomes lying loose in the cytoplasm and usually wears a wall, which is what being a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> amounts to.","At a tenth of a micrometre to five micrometres across, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is small enough that anything entering it diffuses across the whole cell quickly, which is one reason such cells work."],"abstract":"A <b>prokaryote</b> is a cell that keeps no nucleus, and the name says so: the two domains it covers, the bacteria and the archaea, hold their DNA in an open region of the cytoplasm called the nucleoid rather than behind a membrane of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They share with every other cell a plasma membrane, a jelly-like cytoplasm, DNA and ribosomes on which to build proteins, and what they lack is the set of membrane-walled compartments a eukaryote is organised into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most bacteria wear a wall of peptidoglycan that keeps their shape and stops them drying out, and many add a polysaccharide capsule that lets them stick to surfaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some carry flagella to swim with, pili through which one cell passes genetic material directly to another, or fimbriae for gripping a host.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are small, a tenth of a micrometre to about five, which matters because anything entering such a cell diffuses across the whole of it quickly and any waste made inside it leaves as fast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Small does not mean primitive, and the same universal machinery runs in them as in us: DNA copied by templating, transcribed to RNA and translated by nearly the same code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ancestor of everything alive is reconstructed as a cell of roughly this kind, at about 4.2 billion years ago with a genome of 2.5 million bases and some 2,600 proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The category has a ragged edge, and one sulphur bacterium from a Caribbean mangrove grows to a centimetre in length, visible without a lens, with its DNA held inside membrane-bound compartments that are metabolically active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even the nucleoid is not a loose heap, since small RNAs and their partner proteins take part in folding the chromosome into it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The word therefore names an absence rather than a group with a common ancestor of its own, which is worth remembering whenever it is used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.2 Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Edmund R. R. Moody et al., “The Nature of the Last Universal Common Ancestor and Its Impact on the Early Earth System,” <i>Nature Ecology & Evolution</i> 8, no. 9 (2024): 1654–66, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-024-02461-1. [Open access]","Jean-Marie Volland et al., “A Centimeter-Long Bacterium with DNA Contained in Metabolically Active, Membrane-Bound Organelles,” <i>Science</i> 376, no. 6600 (2022): 1453–58, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abb3634. [Open access]","Gabriela Mistygacz et al., “Dual Role of Small Noncoding RNA and Hfq in Bacterial DNA Compaction: A New Perspective on Nucleoid Architecture,” <i>ACS Omega</i> 11, no. 21 (2026): 31559–69, https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.6c02202. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does being small suit a cell with no internal compartments?","a":"Because anything entering such a cell diffuses across the whole of it quickly and any waste made inside it leaves as fast, so no transport machinery is needed."},{"q":"Why is small not the same as primitive?","a":"Because the same universal machinery runs in them as in us — DNA copied by templating, transcribed to RNA and translated by nearly the same code."},{"q":"Why does the word name an absence rather than a group?","a":"Because it covers two domains defined by lacking a nucleus rather than by descent from a common ancestor of their own."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Prokaryote_cell.svg/1920px-Prokaryote_cell.svg.png","title":"A prokaryotic cell","desc":"No nucleus: the DNA lies loose in the cytoplasm, inside a cell wall and a plasma membrane.","credit":"This vector image is completely made by Ali Zifan, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prokaryote_cell.svg","alt":"A labelled diagram of a rod-shaped bacterium with a flagellum, pili, a cell wall, ribosomes and a coiled nucleoid."},"wiki":"Prokaryote"},
    "bio-095": {"questions":["Compartmenting different jobs into organelles walled off by membranes is what a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> does and a bacterium does not, and it is why such a cell can afford to be large.","Ten to a hundred micrometres across, with rod-shaped chromosomes and an endoplasmic reticulum, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is an order of magnitude bigger than the cells it evolved from."],"abstract":"A <b>eukaryote</b> is a cell with a true nucleus, which is what the name says: its DNA is enclosed in a membrane of its own rather than lying loose in the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Animals, plants, fungi and protists are all built this way, and beside the nucleus such a cell carries numerous other membrane-walled organelles, among them the endoplasmic reticulum, the Golgi apparatus and the mitochondria, with chloroplasts in plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its chromosomes are several and rod-shaped where a bacterium's genome is usually one closed loop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The organelles are the point rather than an ornament, since walling a job off in its own compartment lets incompatible chemistries run at once in one cell, which is the principle of form following function applied inside a membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also what makes size affordable, and a eukaryotic cell runs ten to a hundred micrometres across where a prokaryote manages a tenth of a micrometre to five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Being bigger costs something, because a large cell cannot rely on diffusion to move materials about and needs structural arrangements for transport instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Underneath all of it the machinery is the same as any other cell's, with information in DNA, transcribed to RNA and translated by nearly the same code.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Size within the group varies enormously and is not fixed by the plan, and yeast selected in the laboratory for smallness shrank steadily through mutations in nutrient-sensing and cell-cycle pathways at very little cost to fitness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The boundary with the prokaryotes is real but not perfectly sharp, and a bacterium has been described that is a centimetre long and keeps its DNA in membrane-bound compartments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> How a cell of this kind arose at all is a separate question and a hard one, since the nucleus and the mitochondria both had to be acquired.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Ana Garoña, Morgane V. Lemos, Andrea Giometto, and Marco Fumasoni, “Experimental Evolution of Cellular Miniaturization Reveals a Putative Mechanism for Cell Size Evolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 24 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2531280123. [Open access]","Jean-Marie Volland et al., “A Centimeter-Long Bacterium with DNA Contained in Metabolically Active, Membrane-Bound Organelles,” <i>Science</i> 376, no. 6600 (2022): 1453–58, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abb3634. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “The Origin and Evolution of Cells,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9841/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are the organelles the point rather than an ornament?","a":"Because walling a job off in its own compartment lets incompatible chemistries run at once in one cell, which is form following function applied inside a membrane."},{"q":"Why does compartmentalisation make size affordable?","a":"Because a cell of ten to a hundred micrometres cannot rely on diffusion to move materials about, and the compartments come with the structural arrangements for transport."},{"q":"Why is the origin of such a cell a separate and hard question?","a":"Because the nucleus and the mitochondria both had to be acquired, so the plan cannot be reached by gradual enlargement of a prokaryote."}],"wiki":"Eukaryote"},
    "bio-096": {"questions":["No membrane surrounds the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so transcription and translation happen in the same space, where a eukaryote separates the two by a wall.","Small RNAs and their partner proteins help fold a bacterial chromosome into the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is therefore an organised structure rather than a loose heap of DNA."],"abstract":"The <b>nucleoid</b> is the region of a prokaryotic cell in which its chromosome sits, and it is the one structure that most sharply marks such a cell off from a eukaryotic one, because no membrane surrounds it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A eukaryote encloses its DNA in a nucleus of its own and fills the rest of the cell with further membrane-walled organelles; a prokaryote has neither, and its ribosomes lie loose in the same cytoplasm as its genes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> One consequence is that a bacterium transcribes and translates in the same space, with no wall between the two operations.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The rest of the differences follow from that division of labour rather than standing beside it: several rod-shaped chromosomes against one loop, ten to a hundred micrometres across against a tenth of a micrometre to five, and structural machinery for transport against plain diffusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> What the two kinds share is more fundamental still, since every cell has a plasma membrane, a cytoplasm, DNA and ribosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The absence of a membrane is not an absence of organisation, and the chromosome is folded into its region rather than left in a heap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Small non-coding RNAs and the protein that partners them take part in that compaction, which makes the nucleoid a structure with an architecture to study.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The dividing line is also not quite absolute, and a centimetre-long sulphur bacterium has been found holding its DNA inside membrane-bound compartments that are metabolically active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That single organism breaks both halves of the textbook contrast at once, being neither small nor free of internal membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The contrast is a good working rule all the same, and the exception is what makes it worth stating as a rule rather than a law.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.2 Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Gabriela Mistygacz et al., “Dual Role of Small Noncoding RNA and Hfq in Bacterial DNA Compaction: A New Perspective on Nucleoid Architecture,” <i>ACS Omega</i> 11, no. 21 (2026): 31559–69, https://doi.org/10.1021/acsomega.6c02202. [Open access]","Jean-Marie Volland et al., “A Centimeter-Long Bacterium with DNA Contained in Metabolically Active, Membrane-Bound Organelles,” <i>Science</i> 376, no. 6600 (2022): 1453–58, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abb3634. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can a bacterium transcribe and translate in one space?","a":"Because no membrane surrounds its chromosome, so its ribosomes lie loose in the same cytoplasm as its genes with no wall between the two operations."},{"q":"Why is the lack of a membrane not a lack of organisation?","a":"Because the chromosome is folded into its region rather than left in a heap, with small non-coding RNAs and their partner protein taking part in that compaction."},{"q":"Why is the textbook contrast a rule rather than a law?","a":"Because a centimetre-long sulphur bacterium holds its DNA inside metabolically active membrane-bound compartments, breaking both halves of the contrast at once."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Subhash_nucleoid_01.png/1920px-Subhash_nucleoid_01.png","title":"The nucleoid","desc":"A bacterial genome a thousand times longer than its cell, folded into a body that occupies part of it.","credit":"Subhash C. Verma, Zhong Qian, Sankar L. Adhya, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Subhash_nucleoid_01.png","alt":"Three panels showing a circular bacterial genome, the same DNA as a random coil, and the compacted nucleoid inside a cell."},"wiki":"Nucleoid"},
    "bio-097": {"questions":["A membrane can only carry so much traffic per square micrometre, so once a growing cell's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has fallen far enough it must either divide or die.","Flattening, elongating and folding the membrane are all ways of raising a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without shrinking, which is what lets some cells grow far past the usual limit."],"abstract":"The <b>surface-area-to-volume ratio</b> is the amount of boundary a cell has for each unit of interior, and it is the arithmetic that keeps cells small.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most cells approximate a sphere, whose surface goes as the square of the radius and whose volume goes as the cube, so doubling the radius multiplies the surface by four and the contents by eight and the ratio falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A cube behaves the same way: one of a millimetre on a side has 6 square millimetres of face to one cubic millimetre of body, and doubling its edge takes that from six to one down to three to one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Everything a cell takes in or throws out crosses that boundary, which is a selective barrier letting it concentrate what it gathers and expel what it does not want,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> so once the interior outgrows the membrane serving it the cell becomes less efficient the larger it gets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At that point it either divides or dies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Dividing is not the only answer, and the alternatives are all ways of buying back boundary: folding the membrane, flattening, drawing out into a long thin shape, or walling the interior into organelles that do their own work where they stand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The last of those is what a eukaryotic cell is, which is how it manages ten to a hundred micrometres where a bacterium is held to a tenth of a micrometre to five.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The rule bites hardest on the small end, where diffusion alone carries material across the whole cell almost at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is a constraint rather than a fixed setting, and yeast selected in the laboratory for smallness shrank steadily through mutations in nutrient-sensing and cell-cycle pathways with very little loss of fitness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The other end is stretched by a sulphur bacterium a centimetre long, which is possible only because almost all of that length is fluid-filled sac rather than working cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.2 Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “The Universal Features of Cells on Earth,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Ana Garoña, Morgane V. Lemos, Andrea Giometto, and Marco Fumasoni, “Experimental Evolution of Cellular Miniaturization Reveals a Putative Mechanism for Cell Size Evolution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 123, no. 24 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2531280123. [Open access]","Jean-Marie Volland et al., “A Centimeter-Long Bacterium with DNA Contained in Metabolically Active, Membrane-Bound Organelles,” <i>Science</i> 376, no. 6600 (2022): 1453–58, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abb3634. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does growing bigger make a cell less efficient?","a":"Because surface goes as the square of the radius and volume as the cube, so doubling the radius multiplies the boundary by four and the interior by eight, and everything must cross that boundary."},{"q":"Why is dividing not the only answer to the arithmetic?","a":"Because the alternatives all buy boundary back: folding the membrane, flattening, drawing out into a long thin shape, or walling the interior into organelles that work where they stand."},{"q":"Why can a bacterium be a centimetre long at all?","a":"Because almost all of that length is fluid-filled sac rather than working cytoplasm, so the interior actually served by the membrane stays small."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Comparison_of_surface_area_vs_volume_of_shapes.svg/1920px-Comparison_of_surface_area_vs_volume_of_shapes.svg.png","title":"Surface area against volume","desc":"As a shape grows, its volume outruns its surface: the reason cells stay small.","credit":"cmglee, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comparison_of_surface_area_vs_volume_of_shapes.svg","alt":"A graph of surface area against volume for a series of solids, with cubes, spheres and other shapes plotted along curves."},"wiki":"Surface-area-to-volume ratio"},
    "bio-098": {"questions":["Living cells can be watched under a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and are usually too transparent to show their parts, so they are stained, which generally kills them.","Enlarging an image beyond the point where a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can separate two adjacent structures adds size without adding detail, which is why resolution matters more than magnification."],"abstract":"A <b>light microscope</b> passes visible light through a specimen and two sets of lenses, and it is the instrument almost all biology was done with before the 20th century.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The compound form is the older idea, though a single well-ground lens long beat it, having fewer surfaces to spoil the image.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two quantities describe it and only one matters much: magnification is how far the image is enlarged, resolving power the ability to show two adjacent structures as two rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No radiation can probe details much smaller than its own wavelength, and visible light runs from about 0.4 micrometres in the violet to 0.7 in the deep red, which sets a hard floor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> At best, with violet light and a wide aperture, that floor is just under 0.2 micrometres, a figure the instrument makers reached at the end of the 19th century and few factory microscopes match today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> A bacterium or a mitochondrion is about 0.5 micrometres across, so those are roughly the smallest things whose shape can be made out at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Enlarging past that point adds size and no information, which is why a teaching microscope stops around 400 times and an oil-immersion lens around 1,000,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> where a single 17th-century lens already reached 270 and sometimes 500 against the 20 of the magnifying glasses before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its great advantage is that it can be pointed at something alive, and its difficulty is that cells are largely transparent, so their parts have to be coloured with stains, which generally kills them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A permanent preparation is made by fixing the tissue first, usually with an aldehyde that cross-links proteins and locks everything in place, then cutting sections thin enough for light to pass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The floor has since been got round rather than removed, and fluorescence methods that switch emission on and off now resolve structures far finer than 0.2 micrometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Hyunjeong Lee and Im Joo Rhyu, “Antonie van Leeuwenhoek and the Dawn of Microscopic Observation: A Narrative Review from Delft's Lens to the Modern Microscope,” <i>Applied Microscopy</i> 56, no. 1 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1186/s42649-026-00123-z. [Open access]","Bruce Alberts et al., “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Robert Gaynes, “Antony van Leeuwenhoek, Pioneer of the Microscopic World,” <i>Emerging Infectious Diseases</i> 32, no. 6 (2026), https://doi.org/10.3201/eid3206.260531. [Open access]","Wenxin Zhu et al., “Luminescent-Reaction-Enabled Super-Resolution Imaging,” <i>Nature</i> 657, no. 8130 (2026): 127–34, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10889-7. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does enlarging past a certain point add nothing?","a":"Because no radiation probes detail much smaller than its own wavelength, so beyond about 0.2 micrometres more magnification adds size and no information."},{"q":"Why must cells usually be killed to be looked at?","a":"Because they are largely transparent, so their parts have to be coloured with stains, which generally kills them, and a permanent preparation is fixed and sectioned first."},{"q":"Why is the wavelength floor no longer the end of the matter?","a":"Because it has been got round rather than removed: fluorescence methods that switch emission on and off resolve structures far finer than 0.2 micrometres."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Compound_Monocular_Microscope_-_DPLA_-_034c5810cbd1b1a6d364e3066e1deb24.jpg/1920px-Compound_Monocular_Microscope_-_DPLA_-_034c5810cbd1b1a6d364e3066e1deb24.jpg","title":"A compound monocular microscope by Nachet of Paris, 1880s","desc":"A brass compound microscope of the kind most biology was done with before the 20th century: an eyepiece at the top of a draw tube, an objective above the stage, and a mirror below to throw light up through the specimen.","credit":"Maison Nachet & Fils, Paris; Science History Institute, via the Digital Public Library of America. Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Compound_Monocular_Microscope_-_DPLA_-_034c5810cbd1b1a6d364e3066e1deb24.jpg","alt":"A brass compound microscope photographed against a plain pale ground: a cylindrical eyepiece at the top of a draw tube, a conical objective pointing down at a flat black stage with two clips, a focusing knob on the pillar behind, and a small round mirror on a swivel below the stage, all mounted on a black horseshoe foot."},"wiki":"Optical microscope"},
    "bio-099": {"questions":["Electrons travel properly only in a vacuum and the preparation kills the specimen, so nothing living has ever been watched through an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whatever it shows of the dead.","Sweeping a beam across a surface gives one kind of picture and driving it through a thin slice gives another, which are the two ways an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is ordinarily used."],"abstract":"An <b>electron microscope</b> illuminates a specimen with a beam of electrons instead of light, and the reason for doing so is the same rule that limits every microscope: no radiation resolves detail much finer than its own wavelength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> An electron's wavelength shortens as it speeds up, and one accelerated through 100,000 volts has a wavelength of 0.004 nanometres, which in theory would allow a resolution ten thousand times better than visible light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Practice falls well short of theory, because the aberrations of an electron lens are harder to correct than those of glass and only the very centre of the lens can be used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best modern instruments reach about 0.1 nanometres, and specimen preparation, contrast and radiation damage hold biological work to roughly 2 nanometres, which is still a hundred times better than a light microscope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There are two common arrangements: a beam swept back and forth across a surface, which gives the shape of the outside, and a beam driven through a thin section, which shows what is inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The price is that nothing alive has ever been seen this way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Electrons move properly only in a vacuum and the preparation kills the specimen, so every image is of something dead and treated, which is a standing caution when reading one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Freezing rather than fixing has changed that considerably, and cryo-electron tomography now images cells and tissue in something close to their native state, tilting the specimen through a series of angles and reconstructing the volume from the views.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> How finely to space those tilts is a real trade, since more views mean more information and also more radiation damage to the very thing being looked at.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The method has moved out of structural biology and into drug discovery, where proteins are seen at work inside cells rather than purified out of them,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> though a tomogram shows shapes and not names, so labelling strategies are needed to say which molecule is which.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts et al., “Looking at the Structure of Cells in the Microscope,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26880/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Maarten Willem Tuijtel, Tomáš Majtner, Beata Turoňová, and Martin Beck, “Optimising the Tilt Increment for In Situ Cryo-Electron Tomography,” <i>eLife</i> 15 (2026), https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.111639. [Open access]","Camila M. Clemente, Juan-Carlos Mobarec, and Tanmay A. M. Bharat, “Applications and Prospects of Cryo-Electron Tomography in Drug Discovery and Understanding Disease,” <i>Current Opinion in Structural Biology</i> 98 (2026): 103283, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sbi.2026.103283. [Open access]","Yoon Ho Park, Rana Kim, Kun-Ho Song, and Hyun Suk Jung, “Labeling and Localization Strategies for In Situ Cryo-Electron Tomography across the Viral Life Cycle,” <i>Viruses</i> 18, no. 7 (2026): 790, https://doi.org/10.3390/v18070790. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a beam of electrons resolve more than light?","a":"Because an electron’s wavelength shortens as it speeds up, one accelerated through 100,000 volts having a wavelength of 0.004 nanometres."},{"q":"Why does practice fall so far short of that theory?","a":"Because the aberrations of an electron lens are harder to correct than those of glass and only the very centre can be used, with preparation, contrast and radiation damage holding biology to about 2 nanometres."},{"q":"Why is every electron micrograph a picture of something dead?","a":"Because electrons move properly only in a vacuum and the preparation kills the specimen, which is a standing caution when reading one."}],"image":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/TITAN.jpg/1920px-TITAN.jpg","title":"A transmission electron microscope","desc":"The instrument that made the inside of a cell visible, resolving detail far beyond the reach of light.","credit":"Alice im Miniland, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons. https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TITAN.jpg","alt":"A tall grey electron microscope column standing in a laboratory beside a control screen."},"wiki":"Electron microscope"},
    "bio-100": {"questions":["Because a nucleus is heavier than a mitochondrion and both outweigh a ribosome, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can separate them by spinning the same homogenate faster and faster in turn.","Studying what an organelle does chemically means having a great many of one kind and nothing else, which is what <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was invented to supply."],"abstract":"<b>Cell fractionation</b> is the business of taking a cell apart into its working components without stopping them working, so that a single kind of organelle can be studied on its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is needed because microscopy alone cannot say what a component actually does, and a eukaryotic cell walls its different jobs into separate membrane-bound compartments.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cells are broken open first, by osmotic shock, ultrasound, forcing through a narrow opening or grinding, which shatters the plasma membrane and the endoplasmic reticulum into fragments that reseal at once into small closed vesicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Done carefully the same treatment leaves nuclei, mitochondria, lysosomes and the rest largely whole, and what results is a thick slurry holding organelles of many sizes and densities.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The medium the cells are broken in has to be found by trial and error for each organelle, and getting it right is what keeps the parts biochemically like themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The slurry is separated by spinning, which became possible only when the preparative ultracentrifuge was built commercially in the early 1940s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Larger and denser things feel more force and settle faster, so a slow spin drops the nuclei into a pellet, a faster one the mitochondria, and a longer, faster one the sealed vesicles and then the ribosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> No fraction comes out pure, and the usual remedy is to resuspend a pellet and spin it again several times over.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The method was worked out largely in the 1940s and 1950s and is what made biochemistry of the cell possible, since asking what a mitochondrion does chemically means having a great many mitochondria and very little else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its descendants ask the same question without a centrifuge, and mass spectrometry applied to one cell type at a time has found differences between astrocytes from different regions of the brain that a whole-tissue measurement would have averaged away,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> while cryo-electron tomography looks at organelles where they stand, in a cell frozen rather than taken apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Bruce Alberts et al., “Fractionation of Cells,” in <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i>, 4th ed. (New York: Garland Science, 2002), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26936/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Geoffrey M. Cooper, “Tools of Cell Biology,” in <i>The Cell: A Molecular Approach</i>, 2nd ed. (Sunderland, MA: Sinauer Associates, 2000), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9941/. [Open access]","Chien-Chang Huang, Chiung-Yun Chang, Po-Chao Chan, Weng Man Chong, Hsiao-Jen Chang, and Jung-Chi Liao, “Single Cell-Type Spatial Proteomics Uncovers Regional Heterogeneity of Astrocytes,” <i>Journal of Proteome Research</i> 25, no. 8 (2026): 3826–34, https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.jproteome.6c00321. [Open access]","Maarten Willem Tuijtel, Tomáš Majtner, Beata Turoňová, and Martin Beck, “Optimising the Tilt Increment for In Situ Cryo-Electron Tomography,” <i>eLife</i> 15 (2026), https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.111639. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is taking a cell apart necessary at all?","a":"Because microscopy alone cannot say what a component does, and asking what a mitochondrion does chemically means having a great many mitochondria and very little else."},{"q":"Why does spinning separate the organelles?","a":"Because larger and denser things feel more force and settle faster, so a slow spin drops the nuclei, a faster one the mitochondria, and a longer, faster one the vesicles and ribosomes."},{"q":"Why must the breaking medium be found by trial and error?","a":"Because getting it right for each organelle is what keeps the isolated parts biochemically like themselves rather than merely intact."}],"wiki":"Cell fractionation"},
    "bio-101": {"questions":["Crystal violet, iodine, an alcohol rinse and a pink counterstain together make a differential <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> method that sorts bacteria into two groups by the thickness of their walls.","Trypan blue cannot cross an intact membrane, so its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of only the dead or damaged cells in a sample is a standard test of how many cells are alive."],"abstract":"<b>Staining</b> is the colouring of cells or tissues with dyes so that structures which are otherwise transparent stand out under the light microscope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most cells are nearly colourless, so the dye is usually applied after the specimen has been fixed by heat or by chemicals such as formaldehyde, which kills the cells and holds their structures in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A dye is a salt, and whichever of its two ions carries the colour decides how it behaves: a basic dye has a coloured positive ion and an acidic dye a coloured negative one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because cell walls usually carry a negative charge, basic dyes such as crystal violet cling to the cells, while acidic dyes such as eosin are repelled and colour the background instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A simple stain colours every cell alike, whereas a differential stain uses several dyes so that different kinds of cell come out in different colours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The best known differential stain was devised in 1884 by the Danish microbiologist Hans Christian Gram to tell bacteria apart by their walls, and it is still a first step in identifying a bacterial infection.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Crystal violet is locked in with iodine and the slide is rinsed with alcohol, so cells with a wall of peptidoglycan 30 to 100 nanometres thick stay purple, while those with a layer of about 5 nanometres lose the dye and take up a pink counterstain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A stain can reveal shape too: the silver 'black reaction' of 1873 filled a few nerve cells at random but each one completely, showing neurons with all their branches, and work built on it won the 1906 Nobel Prize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Textbooks often say that staining kills cells, but it is fixation that kills, and some dyes are read on living cells precisely because a living membrane keeps them out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Trypan blue enters only cells with a damaged membrane, so a blue cell counts as dead, and fluorescent dyes attached to antibodies can pick out a single kind of microbe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “2.4 Staining Microscopic Specimens,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/2-4-staining-microscopic-specimens. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.1 Studying Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells. [Open access]","Shirly Berezin, Yaron Aviv, Hagit Aviv, Elad Goldberg, and Yaakov R. Tischler, “Replacing a Century Old Technique – Modern Spectroscopy Can Supplant Gram Staining,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7 (2017): 3810, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-017-02212-2. [Open access]","Sara Mariotto et al., “Clinical Neuropathology Image 1-2018: Golgi Silver Staining, the Black Reaction,” <i>Clinical Neuropathology</i> 37, no. 1 (2018): 4–5, https://doi.org/10.5414/np301087. [Open access]","Nobel Prize Outreach, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1906,” NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1906/summary/. [Open access]","Filippo Piccinini, Anna Tesei, Chiara Arienti, and Alessandro Bevilacqua, “Cell Counting and Viability Assessment of 2D and 3D Cell Cultures: Expected Reliability of the Trypan Blue Assay,” <i>Biological Procedures Online</i> 19 (2017): 8, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12575-017-0056-3. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “2.3 Instruments of Microscopy,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/2-3-instruments-of-microscopy. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do basic dyes colour cells while acidic dyes colour the background?","a":"Because a basic dye carries its colour on its positive ion and an acidic dye on its negative one, and cell walls usually carry a negative charge, so the first is drawn to the cells and the second is pushed away from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the Gram stain split bacteria into two groups?","a":"Because the wall differs: a thick layer of peptidoglycan, 30 to 100 nanometres deep, holds the crystal violet and iodine complex through the alcohol rinse, while a thin layer of about 5 nanometres lets it wash out, so those cells take the pink counterstain instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a stain be used to count living cells?","a":"Because trypan blue cannot cross an intact membrane, so only dead or damaged cells turn blue; the test reads the membrane rather than life itself, and a cell that has begun to die with its membrane still whole is counted as alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Staining"},
    "bio-102": {"questions":["Cancer has been described as cells cheating on the cooperation that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> depends on, dividing, surviving and taking resources for themselves at the expense of the whole body.","Fission yeast selected for settling fast evolved lasting clusters of cells that failed to separate after division, a laboratory view of the first step towards <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a lineage."],"abstract":"<b>Multicellularity</b> is the condition of a living thing built from many cells that cooperate in its growth, upkeep and reproduction, so that they act as one organism rather than as a crowd of independent individuals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has evolved more than 50 times independently, the oldest case being in the cyanobacteria at least 3 billion years ago, so it is not one step on a ladder towards animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A group can form in two ways: clonally, when the products of cell division fail to separate, or by aggregation, when independent cells come together into one body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Animals and their closest single-celled relatives, the choanoflagellates, were thought to be clonal only, until one choanoflagellate was shown to form its sheets clonally, by aggregation or by both, in splash pools that dry and refill.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Living together requires cells to stick, and a crucial step in many lineages was an adherent extracellular matrix, the network of material between cells that gives tissues both rigidity and flexibility.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> Most multicellular lineages stayed simple, and complex multicellularity, a large body of several specialised cell types built by a program of development, arose only five to seven times, depending on how the lineages are counted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Those few are animals, land plants, fungi and the red, brown and green seaweeds, and plants and animals are not two versions of one invention, since they arose in separate branches of the eukaryotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first step can be quick: fission yeast selected for settling fast evolved lasting clusters, in some lines through mutations that stop daughter cells from separating after division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nor is the traffic one way, since the cyanobacteria lost multicellularity at least five times and regained it at least once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because most cells in a body no longer reproduce for themselves, multicellularity depends on keeping cell-level selfishness in check, and cancer can be read as cells cheating on that cooperation by dividing, surviving and taking resources at the body's expense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Emma P. Bingham and William C. Ratcliff, “A Nonadaptive Explanation for Macroevolutionary Patterns in the Evolution of Complex Multicellularity,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 121, no. 7 (2024): e2319840121, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2319840121. [Open access]","C. Athena Aktipis et al., “Cancer across the Tree of Life: Cooperation and Cheating in Multicellularity,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 370, no. 1673 (2015): 20140219, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2014.0219. [Open access]","Núria Ros-Rocher et al., “Clonal-Aggregative Multicellularity Tuned by Salinity in a Choanoflagellate,” <i>Nature</i> 651, no. 8107 (2026): 974–85, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10137-y. [Open access]","Bettina E. Schirrmeister, Alexandre Antonelli, and Homayoun C. Bagheri, “The Origin of Multicellularity in Cyanobacteria,” <i>BMC Evolutionary Biology</i> 11 (2011): 45, https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2148-11-45. [Open access]","Rozenn M. Pineau et al., “Experimental Evolution of Multicellularity via Cuboidal Cell Packing in Fission Yeast,” <i>Evolution Letters</i> 8, no. 5 (2024): 695–704, https://doi.org/10.1093/evlett/qrae024. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “27.1 Features of the Animal Kingdom,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/27-1-features-of-the-animal-kingdom. [Open access]","Bernard Kloareg, Yacine Badis, J. Mark Cock, and Gurvan Michel, “Role and Evolution of the Extracellular Matrix in the Acquisition of Complex Multicellularity in Eukaryotes: A Macroalgal Perspective,” <i>Genes</i> 12, no. 7 (2021): 1059, https://doi.org/10.3390/genes12071059. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is multicellularity not a single step on the road to animals?","a":"Because it has evolved more than 50 times independently, first in cyanobacteria at least 3 billion years ago, and even complex multicellularity arose in several separate lineages, with plants arising in one branch of the eukaryotes and animals and fungi in another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do biologists connect multicellularity with cancer?","a":"Because a body works only if cell-level selfishness is held down, and cancer is a breakdown of that cooperation: cells cheat on the limits to division, on programmed death, on the division of labour and on the sharing of resources.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does a single-celled lineage first become multicellular?","a":"Either clonally, when daughter cells stay attached after division, or by aggregation, when separate cells gather into one body; in laboratory fission yeast, mutations that stopped daughter cells separating were enough to make lasting clusters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Multicellular organism"},
    "bio-103": {"questions":["A survey of the adult human body counts about 400 major kinds of cell, each a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sorted by its shape and function, among some 36 trillion cells in all.","Whether the beige fat cells that appear in the cold are a separate <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> or only a passing state between white and brown fat is still argued among biologists."],"abstract":"A <b>cell type</b> is a kind of cell with a recognisable and lasting identity, such as a nerve cell, a muscle cell or a fat cell, set apart from others by its shape, its work and the molecules it makes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Specialising, or differentiating, can change a cell's size, shape, metabolism and overall function, yet the cells of a body all carry the same DNA as the fertilised egg they came from.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What differs is which genes are used: each type expresses only a subset of them, reading the part of the shared script that bears on its own work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That specialisation leaves the genome intact was shown in 1962, when the nucleus of a tadpole's intestinal cell, placed in a frog's egg in place of its own, directed the growth of a normal tadpole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A survey of the adult human body counts about 400 major cell types, sorted by shape and function, among some 36 trillion cells in a man of 70 kilograms (154 pounds).<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Cell types were first defined by how they looked under the microscope, and single-cell sequencing, which reads the RNA of individual cells, has since split populations once thought uniform into new subtypes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That has made the term harder to pin down, and the planners of a census of every human cell chose not to propose a precise definition, preferring to let it follow the evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One working distinction is that a type implies persistence, like being a Purkinje cell of the cerebellum, while a state is passing, like a phase of the cell cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fat shows the difficulty: cold can bring out heat-producing brown fat cells, and it is still argued whether the beige cells in between are a separate type or a passing state between white and brown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cell types have a history too: comparing the active genes of single cells from sponges to mice points to families of contractile cells and stem cells that may date from early in animal evolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.6 Cellular Differentiation,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-6-cellular-differentiation. [Open access]","Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2012,” press release, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2012/press-release/. [Open access]","Ian A. Hatton et al., “The Human Cell Count and Size Distribution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 39 (2023): e2303077120, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2303077120. [Open access]","Aviv Regev et al., “The Human Cell Atlas,” <i>eLife</i> 6 (2017): e27041, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.27041. [Open access]","Enzo Nisoli and Saverio Cinti, “What Defines a Cell Type? Perspectives from Adipocyte Biology,” <i>International Journal of Obesity</i> 49, no. 5 (2025): 751–54, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41366-024-01696-z. [Open access]","Alexander J. Tarashansky et al., “Mapping Single-Cell Atlases throughout Metazoa Unravels Cell Type Evolution,” <i>eLife</i> 10 (2021): e66747, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.66747. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “16.1 Regulation of Gene Expression,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-1-regulation-of-gene-expression. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How can cells carrying the same DNA be so different?","a":"Because each type reads only the part of the shared DNA that bears on its own work, expressing a subset of its genes, much as actors in one film all hold the same script but each reads only their own part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was it shown that a specialised cell still holds the whole genome?","a":"Because in 1962 the nucleus of a tadpole's intestinal cell, put into a frog's egg in place of its own nucleus, directed the growth of a normal tadpole, and repeats of the experiment produced adult frogs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it hard to say exactly how many cell types there are?","a":"Because there is no agreed definition: a count of about 400 major types rests on shape and function, while single-cell sequencing keeps splitting familiar types into subtypes, and some cells may be passing states rather than types at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cell type"},
    "bio-104": {"questions":["Adding just four genes in 2006 turned a mature mouse connective-tissue cell into a pluripotent <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, able like an embryonic one to give rise to every type of body cell.","Bone marrow and skin each hold an adult <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> population, but such cells are multipotent, limited to making the cell types of their own tissue rather than anything at all."],"abstract":"A <b>stem cell</b> is a cell that can divide to make more cells like itself and can also give rise to cells that do specialised work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When one divides, the two daughters may both remain stem cells, may split into one stem cell and one more specialised cell, or may both move towards specialisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Potency, the range of cell types a stem cell can produce, runs from totipotent cells, which can form every cell an organism needs, through pluripotent and multipotent cells to unipotent cells that make only their own kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Embryonic stem cells are pluripotent and are taken from the inner cell mass of an embryo about 5 days old, a cluster of 30 to 34 cells whose removal ends the embryo's development, which is why their use is ethically contested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In 2006 mature mouse connective-tissue cells were turned into pluripotent cells by adding just four genes, and these induced pluripotent stem cells need no embryo at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The common picture of a stem cell able to become anything fits only the pluripotent kinds, since the adult stem cells of organs such as bone marrow and skin are multipotent, limited to the cell types of their own tissue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They act as an internal repair system, replacing cells lost to wear, injury or disease, but each tissue holds very few of them and their capacity to divide outside the body is limited.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the United States the only approved stem cell products are blood-forming cells from cord blood, and the bone marrow transplant, which can repopulate a patient's marrow and cure leukaemia, was recognised by the Nobel Prize of 1990.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Clinics around the world nonetheless sell unproven stem cell treatments for many diseases, an industry that researchers describe as a global public health problem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Reported harms include tumours: a boy whose brain was injected with fetal neural stem cells at a clinic in Moscow later developed growths that had arisen from the donor cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.6 Cellular Differentiation,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-6-cellular-differentiation. [Open access]","National Institutes of Health, “Stem Cell Basics,” Stem Cell Information, https://stemcells.nih.gov/info/basics/stc-basics. [Open access]","National Human Genome Research Institute, “Stem Cell,” Talking Glossary of Genomic and Genetic Terms, https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Stem-Cell. [Open access]","Andrew Siegel, “Ethics of Stem Cell Research,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, Winter 2018 ed., ed. Edward N. Zalta, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stem-cells/. [Open access]","Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2012,” press release, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2012/press-release/. [Open access]","Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1990,” press release, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1990/press-release/. [Open access]","Zubin Master, Kirstin R. W. Matthews, and Mohamed Abou-El-Enein, “Unproven Stem Cell Interventions: A Global Public Health Problem Requiring Global Deliberation,” <i>Stem Cell Reports</i> 16, no. 6 (2021): 1435–45, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.stemcr.2021.05.004. [Open access]","Gerhard Bauer, Magdi Elsallab, and Mohamed Abou-El-Enein, “Concise Review: A Comprehensive Analysis of Reported Adverse Events in Patients Receiving Unproven Stem Cell-Based Interventions,” <i>Stem Cells Translational Medicine</i> 7, no. 9 (2018): 676–85, https://doi.org/10.1002/sctm.17-0282. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can an adult stem cell not become any cell at all?","a":"Because stem cells differ in potency: only the pluripotent kinds can form every body cell, while the adult stem cells of tissues such as bone marrow and skin are multipotent and make only the cell types of their own tissue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are embryonic stem cells contested, and what do induced ones change?","a":"Because harvesting them from the inner cell mass of an embryo about 5 days old ends its development; induced pluripotent stem cells, made from mature cells by adding four genes, offer pluripotent cells without an embryo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are commercial stem cell clinics a worry?","a":"Because most of what they sell is unproven: in the United States the approved products are blood-forming cells from cord blood, yet clinics worldwide offer treatments for many diseases, and reported harms include tumours grown from injected donor cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Stem cell"},
    "bio-105": {"questions":["Working without a microscope around 1800, a French anatomist divided the human body into 21 kinds of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, units simpler than the organs they combined to build.","Plants grow from meristems whose dividing cells become dermal, vascular or ground <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which in turn cover the plant, carry water and sugars, or photosynthesise and store food."],"abstract":"A <b>tissue</b> is a group of cells found together in a body, sharing an origin and arranged in an orderly pattern that carries out a common function.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The animal body is built from four main kinds: epithelial tissue covering surfaces and lining cavities, connective tissue binding and supporting, and muscle and nervous tissue, which are both excitable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each traces back to one or more of the embryo's three germ layers, the ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm, and its structure and disorders are studied under the microscope in histology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plants have tissues too, divided differently: meristems keep dividing at the tips of shoots and roots, and their products become dermal tissue for covering, vascular tissue for transport and ground tissue for photosynthesis, storage and support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the usual ladder of organisation tissues sit between cells and organs, and organs in turn group into organ systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The textbook phrase 'a group of similar cells' is a simplification, because most tissues mix several cell types and many are largely made of material that is not alive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Connective tissue consists of living cells, chiefly fibroblasts, set in a matrix of protein fibres, which the fibroblasts make, and a ground substance, and its fibres of collagen are what give tendons their strength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Fat tissue is more than 90 per cent fat cells by volume, yet fewer than half of its cells are fat cells, the rest being blood-vessel, muscle, nerve and immune cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Blood counts as a tissue, yet part of it is made directly of molecules such as water and vitamins with no cells in between, one reason the neat layer-cake picture of biological levels has been called a caricature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The idea is old: around 1800 the French anatomist Xavier Bichat, working without one, divided the human body into 21 kinds of tissue, and a recent survey partitions it into 60 tissue systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["J. Gordon Betts et al., “4.1 Types of Tissues,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-1-types-of-tissues. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “33.2 Animal Primary Tissues,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/33-2-animal-primary-tissues. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “30.1 The Plant Body,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/30-1-the-plant-body. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “1.1 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/1-1-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Daniel Stephen Brooks, “Levels of Organization in Biology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, substantive revision 9 November 2023, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/levels-org-biology/. [Open access]","Hélène Perdicoyianni-Paleologou, “Xavier Bichat and the Renovation of the Pathological Anatomy,” <i>Journal of Medical Biography</i> 32, no. 1 (2024): 89–96, https://doi.org/10.1177/09677720221097795. [Open access]","Ian A. Hatton et al., “The Human Cell Count and Size Distribution,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 120, no. 39 (2023): e2303077120, https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2303077120. [Open access]","Enzo Nisoli and Saverio Cinti, “What Defines a Cell Type? Perspectives from Adipocyte Biology,” <i>International Journal of Obesity</i> 49, no. 5 (2025): 751–54, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41366-024-01696-z. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is 'a group of similar cells' not quite right as a definition?","a":"Because most tissues mix cell types and many are largely matrix: connective tissue is cells set in fibres and ground substance, and fat tissue, though over 90 per cent fat cells by volume, has fewer fat cells than other cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"},{"q":"How are a plant's tissues organised?","a":"Into meristems, regions of continuing division at the tips and in the girth, and permanent tissue whose cells have stopped dividing: dermal tissue covers, vascular tissue of xylem and phloem transports, and ground tissue photosynthesises, stores and supports.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How old is the idea that bodies are built of tissues?","a":"More than 2 centuries old: around 1800 Xavier Bichat, working by dissection without a microscope, resolved the human body into 21 kinds of tissue, simpler than the organs they were assembled into.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Tissue (biology)"},
    "bio-106": {"questions":["Leaves, stems, flowers and fruits make up a plant's shoot system, and each is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because several kinds of tissue work together in it to carry out a particular function.","In 2017 the mesentery, the fold that attaches the intestine to the back wall of the abdomen, was reclassified as a single continuous <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> rather than a set of separate fragments."],"abstract":"An <b>organ</b> is an anatomically distinct structure of the body built from two or more kinds of tissue, which together carry out one or more particular functions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The skin is one, although it is rarely thought of that way, because its tissues work together as a single structure with critical tasks of its own.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Organs that work together form an organ system, as the heart and blood vessels form the circulatory system, and a standard anatomy textbook divides the human body into eleven of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plants have organs too: in a vascular plant the leaves, stems, flowers and fruits make up the shoot system, and the roots, which anchor the plant and take up water and minerals, make up the root system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The picture of a body as simple tissues assembled into organs, and organs into larger working units, goes back at least to Xavier Bichat around 1800.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The tidy ladder from tissue to organ to organ system is less strict than it looks, since assigning an organ to a single system is imprecise and most organs serve more than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Philosophers of biology make the same point about levels in general: a whale is made of organs, tissues and cells but also of gut bacteria that are organisms in their own right, so the neat layer-cake picture breaks down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Even the list of organs can change, and the mesentery, the double fold of peritoneum that attaches the intestine to the back wall of the abdomen, was reclassified in 2017 as a single continuous organ.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Leonardo da Vinci had drawn it as continuous in 1508, while a description of 1885 presented it as fragments lying between the small and large intestines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Because an organ is a distinct working unit it can be replaced whole, and a kidney transplanted between identical twins opened the way to transplants of the liver, pancreas and heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup>","sources":["J. Gordon Betts et al., “1.2 Structural Organization of the Human Body,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-2-structural-organization-of-the-human-body. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “5.1 Layers of the Skin,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/5-1-layers-of-the-skin. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “30.1 The Plant Body,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/30-1-the-plant-body. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “1.2 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “1.1 Themes and Concepts of Biology,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/1-1-themes-and-concepts-of-biology. [Open access]","Markus I. Eronen and Daniel Stephen Brooks, “Levels of Organization in Biology,” in <i>The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</i>, substantive revision 9 November 2023, https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/levels-org-biology/. [Open access]","Hélène Perdicoyianni-Paleologou, “Xavier Bichat and the Renovation of the Pathological Anatomy,” <i>Journal of Medical Biography</i> 32, no. 1 (2024): 89–96, https://doi.org/10.1177/09677720221097795. [Open access]","Monjur Ahmed, “A Clinician's Perspective on the New Organ Mesentery and Non-Vascular Mesenteropathies,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 15 (2024): 1336908, https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2024.1336908. [Open access]","Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1990,” press release, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1990/press-release/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"What makes a structure an organ rather than a tissue?","a":"Being an anatomically distinct structure built from two or more kinds of tissue that work together on one or more particular functions; that is why the skin counts as an organ, its tissues acting as a single structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the ladder from tissue to organ to organ system less tidy than diagrams suggest?","a":"Because most organs serve more than one system, so assigning each to one is imprecise, and a body is not made only of its own parts: a whale includes gut bacteria that are organisms in their own right.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the mesentery reclassified as an organ in 2017?","a":"Because it was described as one continuous structure running from the duodenojejunal flexure to the anorectal junction, with its own anatomy, physiology and diseases, rather than as fragments between the small and large intestines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Organ (biology)"},
    "bio-107": {"questions":["The last common ancestor of all eukaryotes already had mitochondria, a nucleus and an advanced cytoskeleton, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> must have involved a long climb in complexity before it.","Grown from deep-sea mud after a decade of effort, an archaeon with long branching protrusions inspired an engulfment model of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the origin of cells with nuclei."],"abstract":"<b>Eukaryogenesis</b> is the series of evolutionary events by which the eukaryotic cell, the kind with a nucleus that makes up animals, plants, fungi and many single-celled organisms, emerged from prokaryotic ancestors.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> As far as can be told it happened once, for all living eukaryotes descend from a single last common ancestor, and the defining feature they share is a nucleus enclosed by an envelope with pores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That ancestor was no primitive cell, since comparing genomes shows it already had mitochondria, an internal system of membranes including the nucleus, an advanced cytoskeleton and many duplicated genes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fossils are sparse, but structures of the 10 micrometre size typical of eukaryotic cells, which might be early eukaryotes, appear in rocks about 2.1 billion years old.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because the record is so thin, the events leading to that ancestor may never be known in detail, and most of what is known comes from comparing the genomes of living organisms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The host was an archaeon: eukaryotes sit, with high confidence, inside the Asgard archaea, as the sister group of an order called the Hodarchaeales.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The genes of the last common ancestor point the same way, with Asgard archaea supplying most of its core systems and the bacterium that became the mitochondrion supplying mainly energy conversion and the making of iron–sulphur clusters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> That supports a sequence in which much of the eukaryotic cell's organisation evolved in the Asgard line first and the mitochondrial partner was captured afterwards, though the order of events is still argued.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Archaea already carry relatives of actin and tubulin, the proteins at the core of the eukaryotic cytoskeleton, which suggests the host was a fairly complex cell before the merger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The first Asgard archaeon grown in the laboratory took about a decade to isolate from deep-sea mud, and it proved to be a tiny cell some 550 nanometres across, with no organelles but long, branching protrusions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Laura Eme et al., “Inference and Reconstruction of the Heimdallarchaeial Ancestry of Eukaryotes,” <i>Nature</i> 618, no. 7967 (2023): 992–99, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-023-06186-2. [Open access]","Hiroyuki Imachi et al., “Isolation of an Archaeon at the Prokaryote–Eukaryote Interface,” <i>Nature</i> 577, no. 7791 (2020): 519–25, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1916-6. [Open access]","Victor Tobiasson, Jacob Luo, Yuri I. Wolf, and Eugene V. Koonin, “Dominant Contribution of Asgard Archaea to Eukaryogenesis,” <i>Nature</i> 650, no. 8100 (2026): 141–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09960-6. [Open access]","Eugene V. Koonin, “Origin of Eukaryotes from within Archaea, Archaeal Eukaryome and Bursts of Gene Gain: Eukaryogenesis Just Made Easier?,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 370, no. 1678 (2015): 20140333, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2014.0333. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “23.1 Eukaryotic Origins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the host of the first eukaryote thought to have been an archaeon?","a":"Because analyses of many marker genes place eukaryotes, with high confidence, inside the Asgard archaea, and most of the core genes of the last eukaryotic common ancestor trace back to that archaeal line rather than to bacteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the last eukaryotic common ancestor not thought of as primitive?","a":"Because comparing genomes shows it already had mitochondria, a nucleus within an internal membrane system, an advanced cytoskeleton, the ubiquitin network and many duplicated genes, so a great deal of evolution must have come before it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is eukaryogenesis so hard to study?","a":"Because the fossil record of early cells is very sparse, so the events may never be known in detail, and the closest living relatives are hard to grow: the first Asgard archaeon took about a decade to isolate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Eukaryogenesis"},
    "bio-108": {"questions":["First argued for chloroplasts in 1905 and revived in 1967 once organelle DNA had been found, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now the accepted account of where mitochondria and chloroplasts came from.","According to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a mitochondrion cannot live on its own because most of its ancestral bacterium's genes have moved into the host cell's nucleus over evolutionary time."],"abstract":"The <b>endosymbiotic theory</b> holds that mitochondria and chloroplasts descend from bacteria that once lived inside a host cell and became, over many generations, permanent parts of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mitochondria trace back to an alphaproteobacterium, and the chloroplasts of plants and algae to a cyanobacterium, one of the photosynthetic bacteria that raised the oxygen of the early atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The organelles still show their origin: each has a circular chromosome of its own, ribosomes resembling bacterial ones and two surrounding membranes, and mitochondria arise only from existing mitochondria by a division resembling bacterial fission.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> They cannot live alone, because most of their genes have moved into the host's nucleus: even the largest mitochondrial genome encodes only about sixty proteins, while a mitochondrion may hold a thousand or more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That transfer has not stopped, for copies of mitochondrial DNA still escape from the organelle and become built into our own nuclear DNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The idea is older than the evidence: a Russian botanist argued in 1905 that chloroplasts came from symbiotic photosynthetic bacteria, and claims in the 1920s to have grown mitochondria outside cells were dismissed as contamination.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It revived after organelle DNA was found in the 1960s, and Lynn Margulis's article of 1967, reportedly rejected by more than a dozen journals, made the case from microscopy, genetics, fossils and geology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not all of her scheme survived, and her proposal that the eukaryotic flagellum came from a symbiotic spirochete has not been accepted for want of compelling evidence.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The textbook picture of a primitive nucleated cell swallowing a bacterium is itself contested, since the host now appears to have been an archaeon and no free-living prokaryote is known to engulf prey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Gene histories also show what the partner brought, for the alphaproteobacterial share of the first eukaryote's genes lies mainly in energy conversion and the building of iron–sulphur clusters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “23.1 Eukaryotic Origins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.2 Foundations of Modern Cell Theory,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-2-foundations-of-modern-cell-theory. [Open access]","Michael W. Gray, “Lynn Margulis and the Endosymbiont Hypothesis: 50 Years Later,” <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i> 28, no. 10 (2017): 1285–87, https://doi.org/10.1091/mbc.E16-07-0509. [Open access]","William F. Martin, Sriram Garg, and Verena Zimorski, “Endosymbiotic Theories for Eukaryote Origin,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 370, no. 1678 (2015): 20140330, https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.2014.0330. [Open access]","William Martin and Marek Mentel, “The Origin of Mitochondria,” <i>Nature Education</i> 3, no. 9 (2010): 58, Scitable, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/the-origin-of-mitochondria-14232356/. [Open access]","Victor Tobiasson, Jacob Luo, Yuri I. Wolf, and Eugene V. Koonin, “Dominant Contribution of Asgard Archaea to Eukaryogenesis,” <i>Nature</i> 650, no. 8100 (2026): 141–49, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-09960-6. [Open access]","Bettina E. Schirrmeister, Alexandre Antonelli, and Homayoun C. Bagheri, “The Origin of Multicellularity in Cyanobacteria,” <i>BMC Evolutionary Biology</i> 11 (2011): 45, https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2148-11-45. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"What evidence links mitochondria and chloroplasts to bacteria?","a":"Each has its own circular chromosome, ribosomes like those of bacteria and two membranes; their DNA is closely related to that of bacteria in sequence and structure, and they divide in a way that resembles bacterial fission rather than mitosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a mitochondrion not survive outside its cell?","a":"Because most of its ancestor's genes have been transferred to the host's nucleus: even the largest mitochondrial genome encodes about sixty proteins, while a mitochondrion uses a thousand or more, nearly all made from nuclear genes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the theory neglected for so long after 1905?","a":"Because no one knew the organelles carried DNA of their own until the 1960s, claims to have cultured mitochondria were put down to contamination, and even the 1967 revival was reportedly rejected by more than a dozen journals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Symbiogenesis"},
    "bio-109": {"questions":["Cervical tumour cells taken in 1951 grew without stopping and became HeLa, the first immortal human line and the cells that made <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a reliable laboratory technology.","Normal human fibroblasts stop dividing after a limited number of passages in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, chiefly because the telomeres capping their chromosomes shorten every time a cell divides."],"abstract":"<b>Cell culture</b> is the growing of cells outside the body, in a vessel of liquid medium under controlled conditions, so that they can be studied or used to grow viruses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A primary culture is made fresh from a tissue that is minced or digested with enzymes such as trypsin to free single cells, and most such cells must attach to glass or plastic before they will grow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the dish fills, contact between neighbours halts division, so the cells, counted in most laboratories with a dye test and a counting chamber, are thinned and moved into fresh medium as a secondary culture.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Normal human cells will not go on for ever, since fibroblasts stop dividing after a long but limited number of passages, chiefly because the telomeres that cap their chromosomes shorten with each division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lines derived from tumours or transformed cells may lose contact inhibition and divide indefinitely, and such immortal lines can pile up in lumps like small tumours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The method dates from 1907, when frog embryo cells were kept alive in lymph to settle a dispute about how nerve fibres grow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Human cell culture became reliable only after 1951, when cells from the cervical tumour of Henrietta Lacks, a patient at Johns Hopkins Hospital in Baltimore, grew without stopping and became HeLa, the first immortal human cell line.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Culture opened up virology, as the 1954 Nobel Prize for growing poliovirus in tissue cultures showed, and organoids, three-dimensional cultures grown from stem cells, have since grown human noroviruses that no earlier method could.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> No professional guideline then required consent for such use of a patient's tissue, and the ethics of taking her cells is still argued, one historian holding that calls for consent arose only in the 1970s and 1980s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cultures can also mislead, and since HeLa cells were found contaminating other lines in the 1960s, research on misidentified cells has appeared in 32,755 articles, themselves cited by some half a million papers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “6.3 Isolation, Culture, and Identification of Viruses,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/6-3-isolation-culture-and-identification-of-viruses. [Open access]","Duncan Wilson, “A Troubled Past? Reassessing Ethics in the History of Tissue Culture,” <i>Health Care Analysis</i> 24, no. 3 (2016): 246–59, https://doi.org/10.1007/s10728-015-0304-0. [Open access]","Michelle Chan et al., “Novel Insights from a Multiomics Dissection of the Hayflick Limit,” <i>eLife</i> 11 (2022): e70283, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.70283. [Open access]","Nobel Prize Outreach, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1954,” NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1954/summary/. [Open access]","Serge P. J. M. Horbach and Willem Halffman, “The Ghosts of HeLa: How Cell Line Misidentification Contaminates the Scientific Literature,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 12, no. 10 (2017): e0186281, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0186281. [Open access]","Jihoon Kim, Bon-Kyoung Koo, and Juergen A. Knoblich, “Human Organoids: Model Systems for Human Biology and Medicine,” <i>Nature Reviews Molecular Cell Biology</i> 21, no. 10 (2020): 571–84, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41580-020-0259-3. [Open access]","Filippo Piccinini, Anna Tesei, Chiara Arienti, and Alessandro Bevilacqua, “Cell Counting and Viability Assessment of 2D and 3D Cell Cultures: Expected Reliability of the Trypan Blue Assay,” <i>Biological Procedures Online</i> 19 (2017): 8, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12575-017-0056-3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do normal cells in a dish eventually stop dividing?","a":"For two reasons: when they become crowded, contact with their neighbours halts division, and over many passages their telomeres shorten until the cells stop for good, because normal body cells lack active telomerase to rebuild them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the HeLa line matter so much?","a":"Because before it no one could keep human tissue cultures going reliably; HeLa was the first continuous human line, and it established cell culture as a working technology for cell biology, virology and medicine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why should a laboratory check which cells it is really growing?","a":"Because lines get contaminated or confused, a problem known since HeLa cells were found in other cultures in the 1960s; work on misidentified cells fills 32,755 articles, and in some cases the error makes the results meaningless.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cell culture"},
    "bio-110": {"questions":["Joining a jellyfish's green glowing protein to otherwise invisible proteins lets <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> follow their positions and movements inside living cells, work honoured by a Nobel Prize in 2008.","By switching single glowing molecules on and off across many images, super-resolution forms of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> resolve details far finer than the 0.2 micrometre limit stated in 1873."],"abstract":"<b>Fluorescence microscopy</b> is light microscopy in which the image is made by light that the specimen itself gives off after absorbing light of a shorter wavelength.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Molecules called fluorochromes absorb the exciting light, generally ultraviolet or blue, and emit visible light of a longer wavelength, and filters remove the exciting light so that only the glow passes on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some fluorochromes occur naturally, as chlorophyll does, while others are dyes added to the specimen, such as DAPI, which binds to nucleic acids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Attached to antibodies, a fluorochrome lights up one chosen target, so that a single kind of pathogen, such as the bacterium of tuberculosis, can be picked out in a patient's sample.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Fluorescent dyes can also tell living cells from dead ones, since propidium iodide cannot cross an intact membrane, and such dyes have proved more accurate than the older trypan blue test.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The method became central to cell biology once a protein could be made to glow from inside the cell: green fluorescent protein, first seen in the jellyfish <i>Aequorea victoria</i> in 1962, can be joined by genetic engineering to otherwise invisible proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That lets the positions, movements and interactions of the tagged proteins be watched in living cells, and the work behind it was recognised by the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2008.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Light microscopy was long held to a limit, stated in 1873, of about half the wavelength of light, so that only structures some 200 to 350 nanometres apart could be told apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fluorescence itself got round that limit, for by switching single fluorescent molecules on and off and imaging the same area many times, super-resolution methods reach details of a few tens of nanometres, work honoured by the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2014.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The light has a cost, because exciting the dyes generates reactive oxygen species, and even low doses of illumination disturb the activity of genes in living cells, so a live image can record damage the microscope itself has caused.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “2.3 Instruments of Microscopy,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/2-3-instruments-of-microscopy. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2008,” press release, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2008/press-release/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2014,” press release, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2014/press-release/. [Open access]","Lothar Schermelleh, Rainer Heintzmann, and Heinrich Leonhardt, “A Guide to Super-Resolution Fluorescence Microscopy,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 190, no. 2 (2010): 165–75, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.201002018. [Open access]","Yuki Yokoi et al., “Potential Consequences of Phototoxicity on Cell Function during Live Imaging of Intestinal Organoids,” <i>PLOS ONE</i> 19, no. 11 (2024): e0313213, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0313213. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “2.4 Staining Microscopic Specimens,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/2-4-staining-microscopic-specimens. [Open access]","Filippo Piccinini, Anna Tesei, Chiara Arienti, and Alessandro Bevilacqua, “Cell Counting and Viability Assessment of 2D and 3D Cell Cultures: Expected Reliability of the Trypan Blue Assay,” <i>Biological Procedures Online</i> 19 (2017): 8, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12575-017-0056-3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a fluorescence image show glowing parts on a dark background?","a":"Because the exciting ultraviolet or blue light is filtered out after it strikes the specimen, so only the longer-wavelength light given off by the fluorochromes gets through, and only the labelled structures shine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was green fluorescent protein such a breakthrough?","a":"Because it can be joined by genetic engineering to proteins that are otherwise invisible, so researchers can watch where those proteins go, how they move and what they meet inside living cells, and follow the fate of whole cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can imaging living cells with fluorescence distort an experiment?","a":"Because there is a trade-off between resolution and damage: the illumination excites dyes that generate reactive oxygen species, and even low doses alter gene activity, while high doses push cells towards death.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fluorescence microscope"},
    "bio-111": {"questions":["Nucleoli and stress granules have no lipid boundary at all, yet each counts as an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> because phase separation concentrates its proteins and RNA apart from the surrounding fluid.","Bacteria were long described as lacking any <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, but magnetosomes and protein-shelled microcompartments holding RuBisCO show that prokaryotic cells divide their interiors too."],"abstract":"An <b>organelle</b> is a structure inside a cell that carries out a particular job, and the word means ‘little organ’, a comparison with the organs of a body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In a eukaryotic cell the best-known organelles are wrapped in membranes, among them the nucleus, the mitochondria, the endoplasmic reticulum, the Golgi apparatus, lysosomes and peroxisomes, all held in place by the cytoskeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Walling chemistry into compartments lets incompatible reactions run side by side, so that enzymes working only at a low pH can be kept in one compartment while reactions that would fail at that pH carry on in the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Several of the membrane-bound organelles form one connected traffic system, the endomembrane system, which modifies, packages and moves lipids and proteins, while mitochondria and chloroplasts stand outside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Eukaryotic cells, being larger, run much of their chemistry in such membrane-bound compartments, whereas prokaryotic cells generally lack them and divide their cytoplasm with simpler inclusions instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The school rule that an organelle must have a membrane does not hold even inside one textbook, which defines organelles as membrane-enclosed bodies and then calls the ribosome, a complex of RNA and protein, an organelle too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A whole class of membraneless organelles is now recognised, among them the nucleolus, Cajal bodies, stress granules and processing bodies, compartments that concentrate proteins and nucleic acids with no delimiting membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> They form by liquid–liquid phase separation, in which molecules held together by many weak interactions gather into a dense, distinct phase within the surrounding fluid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Prokaryotes, long pictured as simple bags of chemistry, have organelles of their own, such as magnetosomes, crystals of magnetic iron mineral in a lipid layer that let a cell align with a magnetic field, and gas vesicles that adjust buoyancy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Bacterial microcompartments go further, enclosing enzymes such as RuBisCO inside a selectively permeable shell made entirely of protein, which is why the word is now applied to compartments walled by protein, or by nothing at all, as well as by lipid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.4 The Endomembrane System and Proteins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “3.3 Unique Characteristics of Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-3-unique-characteristics-of-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","National Human Genome Research Institute, “Organelle,” <i>Talking Glossary of Genomic and Genetic Terms</i> (Bethesda: National Institutes of Health), https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Organelle. [Open access]","Weihan Li, Chenwei Jiang, and Erhao Zhang, “Advances in the Phase Separation-Organized Membraneless Organelles in Cells: A Narrative Review,” <i>Translational Cancer Research</i> 10, no. 11 (2021): 4929–46, https://doi.org/10.21037/tcr-21-1111. [Open access]","Henning Kirst and Cheryl A. Kerfeld, “Bacterial Microcompartments: Catalysis-Enhancing Metabolic Modules for Next Generation Metabolic and Biomedical Engineering,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 17 (2019): 79, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-019-0691-z. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does one textbook's definition of an organelle contradict itself?","a":"Because it defines an organelle as a membrane-enclosed body with a unique function, and a few paragraphs later calls the ribosome, which builds proteins, an organelle as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A ribosome is a complex of RNA and protein,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and the national genome institute's glossary also lists ribosomes among the important organelles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What holds a membraneless organelle together?","a":"Its own molecules. Membraneless organelles such as the nucleolus and stress granules form by liquid–liquid phase separation, driven by many weak, multivalent interactions between proteins and nucleic acids, which concentrate those molecules in a distinct compartment without any delimiting membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it useful to keep some enzymes in a separate compartment?","a":"Because some reactions need conditions that would stop others. The digestive enzymes of lysosomes work only at a much lower pH than the cytoplasm's, and many cytoplasmic reactions could not run at that pH, so separating them lets both proceed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The endomembrane system extends the idea, passing proteins and lipids from one compartment to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Organelle"},
    "bio-112": {"questions":["Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> separates making RNA from making protein, a eukaryotic cell can regulate a gene at steps that a bacterium, doing both almost at once, does not have.","A skeletal muscle fibre holds many copies of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, left from the cells that fused to form it, while <i>Paramecium</i> keeps two of different sizes and jobs."],"abstract":"The <b>nucleus</b> is the membrane-bound compartment that holds a eukaryotic cell's chromosomes, and it is usually the largest and most prominent organelle in the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its boundary, the nuclear envelope, is made of two lipid bilayers, and the gel-like nucleoplasm inside holds the chromatin and the nucleolus, where ribosomes begin to be built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Eukaryotic cells are defined by having a nucleus, while in bacteria and archaea the DNA lies in the cytoplasm with no membrane around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The DNA inside is kept as chromatin, wrapped around proteins called histones and condensed, which is how a whole genome is packed to fit within it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> One nucleus per cell is the usual rule but not a law: a skeletal muscle fibre carries many, left from the smaller cells that fused to form it, and the single-celled <i>Paramecium</i> keeps a small one for reproduction and a large one that directs its metabolism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Textbooks often call the nucleus the cell's control centre, a picture that the cells which manage without one put under strain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A mammalian red blood cell pushes out its nucleus as it matures, making room for more haemoglobin, and then goes on working in the circulation for up to 120 days.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> What the nucleus does without exception is keep the genes behind a membrane, since RNA is made from DNA inside it and carried out to the cytoplasm, where ribosomes translate it into protein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In bacteria the two steps run almost at once in the same space, whereas the separation in a eukaryote adds points at which a gene's output can be controlled, from the processing of its RNA to the RNA's passage out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That passage runs through nuclear pore complexes, protein assemblies of about 110 megadaltons each in humans that let molecules below about 40 kilodaltons diffuse freely and make larger ones cross a selective barrier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “16.1 Regulation of Gene Expression,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-1-regulation-of-gene-expression. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.3 The Nucleus and DNA Replication,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-3-the-nucleus-and-dna-replication. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “18.3 Erythrocytes,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/18-3-erythrocytes. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","National Human Genome Research Institute, “Nucleus,” <i>Talking Glossary of Genomic and Genetic Terms</i> (Bethesda: National Institutes of Health), https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nucleus. [Open access]","Coby Rush, Zecheng Jiang, Mark Tingey, Fiona Feng, and Weidong Yang, “Unveiling the Complexity: Assessing Models Describing the Structure and Function of the Nuclear Pore Complex,” <i>Frontiers in Cell and Developmental Biology</i> 11 (2023): 1245939, https://doi.org/10.3389/fcell.2023.1245939. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Can a cell keep working without a nucleus?","a":"For a while, yes. A mammalian red blood cell pushes its nucleus out as it matures, which leaves more room for haemoglobin, and it then circulates for up to 120 days before macrophages remove it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Without a nucleus its life is short, so the body has to keep producing new ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does it matter that transcription and translation happen in different places?","a":"Because it gives a eukaryote more ways to control a gene. RNA is made and processed inside the nucleus and translated only outside it, so expression can be regulated at the DNA, at transcription, during RNA processing and as the RNA leaves the nucleus, rather than mainly at transcription as in bacteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How do molecules get into and out of the nucleus?","a":"Through pores in the nuclear envelope, which control the passage of ions, molecules and RNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each pore complex is a huge protein assembly; molecules below about 40 kilodaltons diffuse through freely, while larger ones must cross a selective barrier formed by disordered nucleoporins in its central channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cell nucleus"},
    "bio-113": {"questions":["With no membrane to hold it together, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> disperses as a cell enters mitosis and reassembles around its marked active genes once division is over.","A fibrillar centre, a dense fibrillar component and a granular component make up the three layers of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, each handling a different stage of ribosome building."],"abstract":"The <b>nucleolus</b> is the dense body inside the nucleus where a cell builds its ribosomes, and at about 5 micrometres across it is large enough to show as a dark-staining mass under an ordinary light microscope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It forms around the genes for ribosomal RNA, which sit in tandem repeats called nucleolar organiser regions, with hundreds to thousands of copies spread across several chromosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In humans these regions lie on the short arms of five chromosomes, numbers 13, 14, 15, 21 and 22, and a diploid cell carries between about 100 and 600 copies in all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Ribosomal RNA is transcribed there, cut into the 18S, 5.8S and 28S molecules and combined with proteins into small and large subunits, which leave through the nuclear pores and are finished in the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Not every copy of the genes is in use at once, and the silent ones are packed into a shell of heterochromatin around the outside of the nucleolus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> One textbook calls the nucleolus a condensed region of chromatin, but it is better understood as a compartment with no membrane, held together by the molecules it concentrates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the electron microscope it shows three layers, a fibrillar centre, a dense fibrillar component and a granular component, which match successive steps of transcription, processing and assembly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those layers behave like separate liquid phases, and the proteins that build them are unusually disordered: about 20% of 848 nucleolar proteins examined carry a high share of unstructured stretches, against 14% of proteins in the cytosol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Because nothing walls it in, the nucleolus can come apart, dispersing as the cell enters mitosis and reassembling at the end of division around active genes that stay marked throughout.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its output rises with growth signals such as the protein Myc, and when ribosome production is disturbed a free pool of ribosomal components can stabilise the protein p53 and halt the cell cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “10.2 The Cell Cycle,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/10-2-the-cell-cycle. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.3 The Nucleus and DNA Replication,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-3-the-nucleus-and-dna-replication. [Open access]","Bryony J. Leeke, Imke Staffhorst, and Michelle Percharde, “Emerging Roles for the Nucleolus in Development and Stem Cells,” <i>Development</i> 152, no. 9 (2025): dev204696, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12091869/. [Open access]","Susan J. Baserga, “Making Order from Disorder in the Nucleolus,” <i>Genes &amp; Development</i> 40, no. 5–6 (2026): 285–86, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12905727/. [Open access]","Donatella Ponti, “The Nucleolus: A Central Hub for Ribosome Biogenesis and Cellular Regulatory Signals,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 26, no. 9 (2025): 4174, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12071546/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"What holds the nucleolus together without a membrane?","a":"Its own molecules. The nucleolus is compartmentalised by liquid–liquid phase separation, and its proteins are unusually rich in disordered regions that make many weak contacts with one another.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Removing the disordered region of one RNA polymerase I subunit erased the three layers seen in the electron microscope, although the cells still made ribosomes normally.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a cell carry hundreds of copies of the ribosomal RNA genes?","a":"Because the demand for ribosomal RNA is very high, and hundreds to thousands of copies spread over several chromosomes help to meet it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Humans carry about 100 to 600 copies on five chromosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Not all are used at once: some are active and others are silent, packed into heterochromatin at the edge of the nucleolus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happens to the nucleolus during cell division?","a":"It disperses in prophase, as the nuclear envelope breaks down and the chromosomes condense.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Active ribosomal RNA genes keep the factor UBF bound through mitosis, which marks them so that the nucleolus can reassemble around them at telophase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nucleolus"},
    "bio-114": {"questions":["Textbooks fold <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a regular 30-nanometre fibre, yet in most living nuclei the beaded strings form irregular, liquid-like domains with no such fibre.","Staining divides interphase <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> into a dense heterochromatin holding mostly silent genes and a looser euchromatin where most of the genes being transcribed are found."],"abstract":"<b>Chromatin</b> is the material of eukaryotic chromosomes, DNA bound up with histones and other proteins, and it packs a very long molecule into a small nucleus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The scale is severe, for each human cell holds about 2 metres (6.5 feet) of DNA inside a nucleus that typically measures about 10 micrometres across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The first level of packing wraps 147 base pairs of DNA around a core of eight histone proteins, two each of four kinds, to make a nucleosome, and linker DNA joins one nucleosome to the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The resulting string, about 10 nanometres thick, looks like beads on a string in the electron microscope, and it condenses much further into the compact chromosomes seen when a cell divides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a cell that is not dividing, densely packed heterochromatin, which mostly holds silent genes, can be told apart by staining from looser euchromatin, which holds most of the genes being read.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The textbook drawing then folds the beaded string into a regular fibre 30 nanometres wide and coils that into ever larger helices, a hierarchy that introductory books still show.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The 30-nanometre fibre is real in a test tube, where it forms at low salt concentrations, but cryo-electron microscopy, X-ray scattering and other methods have found it only in a few exceptional cells such as starfish sperm and chicken red blood cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Inside an ordinary nucleus, about 100 millimolar potassium and other ions let nucleosomes reach distant partners freely, so chromatin forms irregular, condensed but liquid-like domains of 10-nanometre fibres instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That changes how genes are reached, because access depends on nucleosomes shifting locally rather than on whole regions unfolding from a 30-nanometre fibre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Nucleosomes can slide along the DNA to expose a stretch for reading, and chemical changes to histones and DNA, a form of epigenetic control, help decide which genes stay open in each kind of cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “14.2 DNA Structure and Sequencing,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-2-dna-structure-and-sequencing. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “16.3 Eukaryotic Epigenetic Gene Regulation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-3-eukaryotic-epigenetic-gene-regulation. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.3 The Nucleus and DNA Replication,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-3-the-nucleus-and-dna-replication. [Open access]","National Human Genome Research Institute, “Chromatin,” <i>Talking Glossary of Genomic and Genetic Terms</i> (Bethesda: National Institutes of Health), https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Chromatin. [Open access]","Kazuhiro Maeshima, “The Shifting Paradigm of Chromatin Structure: From the 30-nm Chromatin Fiber to Liquid-Like Organization,” <i>Proceedings of the Japan Academy, Series B</i> 101, no. 6 (2025): 339–56, https://doi.org/10.2183/pjab.101.020. [Open access]","Sergey V. Razin and Alexey A. Gavrilov, “Chromatin without the 30-nm Fiber: Constrained Disorder Instead of Hierarchical Folding,” <i>Epigenetics</i> 9, no. 5 (2014): 653–57, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4063823/. [Open access]","National Human Genome Research Institute, “Histone,” <i>Talking Glossary of Genomic and Genetic Terms</i> (Bethesda: National Institutes of Health), https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Histone. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the 30-nanometre fibre missing from most living cells?","a":"Because the inside of a cell is salty. The fibre forms in a test tube at low concentrations of ions, but at the roughly 100 millimolar potassium and 1 millimolar magnesium found in cells, nucleosomes stop repelling one another and bind distant partners, so chromatin gathers into irregular condensates instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Such fibres turn up only in exceptional cells like starfish sperm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How is DNA wrapped at the first level of packing?","a":"Around histones. About 147 base pairs of DNA wind round an octamer made of two each of four histones, forming a nucleosome, and linker DNA of about 50 base pairs joins it to the next, giving a beads-on-a-string fibre about 10 nanometres wide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can a gene be read if its DNA is packed away?","a":"Because the packing is mobile. Nucleosomes can move along the DNA to expose a stretch for transcription,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the accessibility of DNA appears to depend on that local mobility rather than on unfolding large regions of chromosome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Histones also help regulate which genes are expressed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chromatin"},
    "bio-115": {"questions":["Two phospholipid bilayers with a thin space between them make up the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose outer membrane runs on into the endoplasmic reticulum.","Pore complexes of about 110 megadaltons pierce the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, letting small molecules diffuse freely while larger ones must pass a barrier of disordered nucleoporins."],"abstract":"The <b>nuclear envelope</b> is the double membrane that encloses the nucleus of a eukaryotic cell and keeps the chromosomes apart from the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Each of its two layers is a phospholipid bilayer, with a thin fluid space between them, and the membrane is continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Although the inner and outer membranes join one another at the pores, each carries its own distinct lipids and proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Just inside lies the nuclear lamina, a meshwork of intermediate filaments built from A-type and B-type lamins that sets the shape of the nucleus and gives it mechanical support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Stretches of chromatin are tethered to the lamina, and mutations in the lamin genes cause a group of inherited diseases called laminopathies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The envelope is pierced by nuclear pore complexes, very large protein assemblies of about 110 megadaltons each in humans, built from about 30 different proteins called nucleoporins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Molecules smaller than about 40 to 60 kilodaltons diffuse through a pore freely, but larger ones must cross a selective barrier formed by disordered nucleoporin chains rich in phenylalanine–glycine repeats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The envelope is not a permanent wall, for in animal cells it breaks down at the start of mitosis, as the chromosomes condense, and reassembles around each new set of chromosomes at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Breaking it down is not the only way to divide, and in the closed mitosis of the malaria parasite <i>Plasmodium falciparum</i> the envelope stays intact through several rounds of nuclear division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the open mitosis of animals, by contrast, the breakdown comes before the spindle forms and attaches to the chromosomes, so the barrier between genes and cytoplasm is lifted at every division of a human cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “10.2 The Cell Cycle,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/10-2-the-cell-cycle. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.3 The Nucleus and DNA Replication,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-3-the-nucleus-and-dna-replication. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Coby Rush, Zecheng Jiang, Mark Tingey, Fiona Feng, and Weidong Yang, “Unveiling the Complexity: Assessing Models Describing the Structure and Function of the Nuclear Pore Complex,” <i>Frontiers in Cell and Developmental Biology</i> 11 (2023): 1245939, https://doi.org/10.3389/fcell.2023.1245939. [Open access]","Julia Madsen-Østerbye, Aurélie Bellanger, Natalia M. Galigniana, and Philippe Collas, “Biology and Model Predictions of the Dynamics and Heterogeneity of Chromatin-Nuclear Lamina Interactions,” <i>Frontiers in Cell and Developmental Biology</i> 10 (2022): 913458, https://doi.org/10.3389/fcell.2022.913458. [Open access]","Sabrina Absalon, “A Dynamic Barrier: Remodeling of the Nuclear Envelope during Closed Mitosis in Malaria Parasites,” <i>mSphere</i> 10, no. 7 (2025): e00999-24, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12306155/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"What gives the nucleus its shape?","a":"The nuclear lamina, a meshwork of intermediate filaments just inside the envelope's membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It is built from A-type and B-type lamins, gives the nucleus mechanical support and anchors stretches of chromatin, and mutations in the lamin genes cause diseases called laminopathies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"How large a molecule can pass through a nuclear pore unaided?","a":"Molecules below about 40 to 60 kilodaltons diffuse through freely, in either direction, down their concentration gradient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Larger cargo has to cross a selective barrier in the central channel, made of more than 200 copies of disordered nucleoporins carrying phenylalanine–glycine repeats.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Does every cell break down its nuclear envelope to divide?","a":"No. Animal cells do, in open mitosis, where the envelope breaks down before the spindle forms and attaches to the chromosomes and reassembles at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The malaria parasite <i>Plasmodium falciparum</i> instead divides by closed mitosis, keeping its envelope intact through several rounds of nuclear division.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nuclear envelope"},
    "bio-116": {"questions":["Tetracyclines bind the small subunit and macrolides the large subunit of the bacterial <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is why these antibiotics can stop bacteria from making proteins while sparing human cells.","A bacterial <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is 70S and a cytoplasmic eukaryotic one 80S, yet those inside mitochondria and chloroplasts resemble the bacterial kind in size and drug sensitivity."],"abstract":"A <b>ribosome</b> is the particle of RNA and protein on which every cell builds its proteins, reading a messenger RNA and joining amino acids in the order it specifies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It has two subunits, a small one that binds the messenger RNA and a large one that binds the transfer RNAs, which come together at the start of translation and part again at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A bacterial ribosome is 70S, made of a 30S and a 50S subunit, while those in the cytoplasm of eukaryotes are 80S, made of 40S and 60S subunits, and the numbers do not add because Svedberg units are not additive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ribosomes inside mitochondria and chloroplasts resemble bacterial ones in size and in the drugs that affect them, more than they resemble the ribosomes in the cytoplasm around them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A ribosome is about 25 nanometres across, a single <i>Escherichia coli</i> cell holds between 10,000 and 70,000 of them, and in a human ribosome the small subunit carries one large RNA and about 32 proteins, the large subunit three RNAs and about 46 proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Many scientists were at first sceptical that RNA could catalyse a reaction at all, and convincing them took a long and determined effort.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Atomic structures of the ribosome's subunits, published in 2000, settled the matter for the ribosome, whose peptide bond is made in the large subunit by peptidyl transferase, an enzyme built of RNA.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ribosome is therefore an ancient ribozyme working on an enormous scale, a finding whose impact reached from chemistry to biology.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It is also precise, checking each codon against its transfer RNA so that a wrong amino acid enters only about once in 100,000, while forming about 20 peptide bonds every second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Many antibiotics work by binding the bacterial ribosome, aminoglycosides and tetracyclines at the 30S subunit and macrolides and chloramphenicol at the 50S, while leaving human ribosomes alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “15.5 Ribosomes and Protein Synthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.4 Protein Synthesis,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-4-protein-synthesis. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “14.3 Mechanisms of Antibacterial Drugs,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/14-3-mechanisms-of-antibacterial-drugs. [Open access]","National Human Genome Research Institute, “Ribosome,” <i>Talking Glossary of Genomic and Genetic Terms</i> (Bethesda: National Institutes of Health), https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Ribosome. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Key to Life at the Atomic Level,” popular information on the Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2009, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2009/popular-information/. [Open access]","Jane E. Jackman, “An Origin Story: Ribozyme Catalysis by the Ribosome,” <i>RNA</i> 21, no. 4 (2015): 650–51, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4371318/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the ribosome called a ribozyme?","a":"Because the reaction at its heart is catalysed by RNA. The peptide bond between amino acids is formed by peptidyl transferase, an RNA-based enzyme in the large subunit,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the atomic structures of 2000 revealed the ribosome as an ancient ribozyme of enormous size.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do so many antibiotics target the ribosome?","a":"Because no cell can survive without ribosomes, and bacterial ones differ from ours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Aminoglycosides and tetracyclines bind the bacterial 30S subunit, and macrolides and chloramphenicol the 50S, blocking protein synthesis in bacteria while leaving human ribosomes alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the S values of the two subunits not add up?","a":"Because Svedberg units are not additive. A 30S and a 50S subunit make a 70S ribosome in <i>Escherichia coli</i>, and a 40S and a 60S subunit make an 80S ribosome in mammals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Ribosome"},
    "bio-117": {"questions":["Pancreatic cells that secrete digestive enzymes are packed with the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose ribosome-covered sheets fold and glycosylate proteins before vesicles carry them on to the Golgi.","Stripping the bound ribosomes from the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns its flat sheets into tubules, which suggests the ribosomes themselves help hold that part of the network in shape."],"abstract":"The <b>rough endoplasmic reticulum</b> is the part of the endoplasmic reticulum whose cytoplasmic surface is studded with ribosomes, which give it a rough look in the electron microscope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It makes and modifies proteins bound for membranes or for export, threading each new chain into its lumen, the space inside, where the protein folds and may gain sugar chains by glycosylation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Finished proteins leave in transport vesicles that bud from its membrane and travel to the Golgi apparatus, and the rough ER also makes phospholipids for the cell's membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is abundant in cells that secrete protein, and pancreatic secretory cells carry a larger share of flat, ribosome-covered sheets of ER than neurons or epithelial cells, which secrete little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The route was traced in secretory cells by electron microscopy and cell fractionation, which showed proteins made on the outer face of the reticulum entering the space between its membranes and moving on to the Golgi complex.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> A ribosome is not a permanent fitting of the rough ER, and whether one sits there depends on the protein it happens to be making.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A protein bound for the secretory pathway begins with a short hydrophobic signal sequence, and as that emerges from the ribosome a ribonucleoprotein called the signal recognition particle binds it and pauses translation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The particle docks the whole ribosome on its receptor in the ER membrane and hands it to the Sec61 channel, through which the growing chain passes into the lumen as translation resumes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the protein is complete the ribosome's two subunits come apart and are soon drawn into new rounds of translation, so the same pool of ribosomes serves both the cytosol and the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The ribosomes in turn help to shape the organelle, since removing them from the membrane converts its flat sheets into tubules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.4 The Endomembrane System and Proteins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “15.5 Ribosomes and Protein Synthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Camilla Faoro and Sandro F. Ataide, “Noncanonical Functions and Cellular Dynamics of the Mammalian Signal Recognition Particle Components,” <i>Frontiers in Molecular Biosciences</i> 8 (2021): 679584, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmolb.2021.679584. [Open access]","Hannah T. Perkins and Viki Allan, “Intertwined and Finely Balanced: Endoplasmic Reticulum Morphology, Dynamics, Function, and Diseases,” <i>Cells</i> 10, no. 9 (2021): 2341, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8472773/. [Open access]","Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1974,” press release, October 1974, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1974/press-release/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How does a ribosome end up on the rough ER?","a":"Through the protein it is making. A secretory protein starts with a hydrophobic signal sequence; as it emerges, the signal recognition particle binds it, pauses translation and docks the ribosome on its receptor in the ER membrane, before handing it to the Sec61 channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happens to a protein inside the rough ER?","a":"It is folded and modified. Sugars can be attached to it by glycosylation,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the protein then leaves in a transport vesicle for the Golgi apparatus, a membrane or the outside of the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do secretory cells have so much rough ER?","a":"Because the rough ER makes the proteins they export. Cells that secrete proteins, such as liver cells, have abundant rough ER,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and pancreatic secretory cells have a higher proportion of ribosome-covered ER sheets than neurons or epithelial cells that secrete little.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}]},
    "bio-118": {"questions":["Cells of the ovaries and testes that make steroid hormones are rich in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the ribosome-free part of the network that synthesises lipids and stores calcium ions.","The sarcoplasmic reticulum of a muscle fibre is a specialised <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that stores, releases and takes back the calcium ions that trigger each contraction."],"abstract":"The <b>smooth endoplasmic reticulum</b> is the part of the endoplasmic reticulum with few or no ribosomes on its surface, which is why it looks smooth in the electron microscope.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One of its main jobs is making lipids, including the phospholipids of cell membranes and steroid hormones, so the hormone-making cells of the ovaries and testes contain a great deal of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It also stores calcium ions and regulates their concentration in the cell, which matters in nerve cells, where calcium triggers the release of neurotransmitters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In muscle fibres a specialised form called the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores, releases and takes back the calcium ions that set off each contraction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The smooth ER also metabolises some carbohydrates and breaks down drugs and toxins, work that is concentrated in the liver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Rough and smooth ER are not separate organelles but regions of one continuous membrane, which runs from the nuclear envelope to the edge of the cell and fills about 35% of the cytoplasm's volume.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Since the 1950s the ribosome-studded parts have been linked with flat sheets and the smooth parts with a network of narrow tubules about 50 to 100 nanometres wide, and cells that make steroids carry proportionally more of the smooth kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Many of the liver's drug-handling enzymes are cytochromes P450, anchored in the membrane of the endoplasmic reticulum, which transform a vast range of foreign compounds as well as the body's own molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The amount of smooth ER can change, and in rats given the drug phenobarbital the smooth ER membranes of the liver cells proliferate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its shape is not fixed either, since proteins such as reticulons and atlastins curve and fuse the tubules continuously, and the balance between them sets how much of the network is sheet and how much is tube.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.4 The Endomembrane System and Proteins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “10.2 Skeletal Muscle,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-2-skeletal-muscle. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Hannah T. Perkins and Viki Allan, “Intertwined and Finely Balanced: Endoplasmic Reticulum Morphology, Dynamics, Function, and Diseases,” <i>Cells</i> 10, no. 9 (2021): 2341, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8472773/. [Open access]","J. A. Higgins and R. J. Barrnett, “Studies on the Biogenesis of Smooth Endoplasmic Reticulum Membranes in Livers of Phenobarbital-Treated Rats: I. The Site of Activity of Acyltransferases Involved in Synthesis of the Membrane Phospholipid,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 55, no. 2 (1972): 282–98, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2108796/. [Open access]","Doyoung Kwon, Sung-Mi Kim, and Maria Almira Correia, “Cytochrome P450 Endoplasmic Reticulum-Associated Degradation (ERAD): Therapeutic and Pathophysiological Implications,” <i>Acta Pharmaceutica Sinica B</i> 10, no. 1 (2020): 42–60, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apsb.2019.11.002. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Are rough and smooth ER separate organelles?","a":"No. The endoplasmic reticulum is one continuous membrane running from the nuclear envelope to the edge of the cell, and the smooth ER is continuous with the rough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The difference lies in ribosomes and shape: ribosome-studded regions tend to be flat sheets and smooth regions narrow tubules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the liver have so much smooth ER?","a":"Because liver cells detoxify much of what reaches the blood, and the smooth ER helps break down drugs and toxins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The liver's cytochromes P450, anchored in the ER membrane, transform a wide range of foreign compounds,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> and in rats given phenobarbital the smooth ER membranes of liver cells proliferate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What does the smooth ER do in a muscle cell?","a":"It forms the sarcoplasmic reticulum, which stores, releases and takes back calcium ions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Those ions trigger the muscle cell's coordinated contraction, and when the reticulum of heart muscle works poorly, too few calcium ions are available for a strong contraction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}]},
    "bio-119": {"questions":["First seen in 1898 in nerve cells stained with silver, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was doubted for half a century until electron microscopy showed its stacked membranes.","Vesicles from the endoplasmic reticulum fuse with the cis face of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and modified, sugar-tagged proteins leave from the trans face for their destinations."],"abstract":"The <b>Golgi apparatus</b> is a stack of flattened, membrane-bound sacs called cisternae that receives proteins and lipids from the endoplasmic reticulum, modifies them, sorts them and sends them on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It has two faces, for vesicles from the ER fuse with the cis face nearest to it and finished products leave from the trans face on the far side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The commonest change made on the way through is the addition of short chains of sugars, and molecules are then tagged with phosphate groups or other small groups that decide where they go.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some products are secreted when their vesicles fuse with the cell membrane, and the Golgi is abundant in busy secretory cells such as those of the salivary glands or the antibody-making cells of the immune system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In plants it also makes polysaccharides, some of which become part of the cell wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Its best-studied tag is mannose 6-phosphate, which an enzyme in the cis cisternae adds to the digestive enzymes bound for lysosomes, and which receptors in the trans-Golgi network then recognise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When that marking enzyme fails, as in the inherited disease mucolipidosis type II, also called I-cell disease, the enzymes are secreted instead of reaching the lysosomes and undigested material builds up inside them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> How cargo moves through the stack is explained by the cisternal maturation model, in which each cisterna itself changes from cis to trans character while resident proteins are recycled backwards in vesicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The structure was first described in 1898, as an internal reticular apparatus seen in nerve cells of the spinal ganglia with a silver impregnation method, and it was later named after its discoverer, Camillo Golgi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For half a century scientists were divided over whether it was real at all, and only electron microscopy in the 1950s settled that it is a genuine organelle with a layered structure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.4 The Endomembrane System and Proteins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","National Human Genome Research Institute, “Golgi Body,” <i>Talking Glossary of Genomic and Genetic Terms</i> (Bethesda: National Institutes of Health), https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Golgi-Body. [Open access]","Somya Mani and Mukund Thattai, “Stacking the Odds for Golgi Cisternal Maturation,” <i>eLife</i> 5 (2016): e16231, https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.16231. [Open access]","Qincheng Liu, Weiqi Wang, Liwei Xu, Qisheng Zhang, and Hongna Wang, “The Host Mannose-6-Phosphate Pathway and Viral Infection,” <i>Frontiers in Cellular and Infection Microbiology</i> 14 (2024): 1349221, https://doi.org/10.3389/fcimb.2024.1349221. [Open access]","Hong-Mei Han, Cedric Bouchet-Marquis, Jan Huebinger, and Markus Grabenbauer, “Golgi Apparatus Analyzed by Cryo-Electron Microscopy,” <i>Histochemistry and Cell Biology</i> 140, no. 4 (2013): 369–81, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00418-013-1136-3. [Open access]","Marina Bentivoglio et al., “The Original Histological Slides of Camillo Golgi and His Discoveries on Neuronal Structure,” <i>Frontiers in Neuroanatomy</i> 13 (2019): 3, https://doi.org/10.3389/fnana.2019.00003. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How does the Golgi send enzymes to lysosomes rather than out of the cell?","a":"By tagging them. In the cis cisternae an enzyme adds mannose 6-phosphate to sugar chains on lysosomal enzymes, and receptors in the trans-Golgi network recognise the tag and divert them towards lysosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Without the tag, as in I-cell disease, the enzymes are secreted instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the Golgi apparatus doubted for so long?","a":"Because for decades it could be seen only with light microscopy after staining.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It was described in 1898 in nerve cells treated with a silver impregnation method,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> and scientists were divided over whether it was real until electron microscopy in the 1950s revealed its layered membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"How do proteins move through the stack of cisternae?","a":"In the cisternal maturation model, cargo stays inside a cisterna while the cisterna itself changes, maturing from cis to trans composition as resident Golgi proteins are recycled backwards in vesicles from older to younger cisternae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Such maturation lets cells process and secrete very large cargo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Golgi apparatus"},
    "bio-120": {"questions":["Kept at a pH of about 4 to 5, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> digests material brought in by phagocytosis and the cell's own worn-out parts delivered by autophagosomes.","Far from simply bursting and killing its cell, a damaged <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may be repaired, removed by lysophagy or expelled through the cell membrane, and its damage spurs the making of new ones."],"abstract":"A <b>lysosome</b> is a membrane-bound organelle filled with digestive enzymes that break down proteins, polysaccharides, lipids, nucleic acids and even worn-out organelles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its enzymes are active at a much lower pH than the cytoplasm's, and a mature lysosome keeps its interior at a pH of about 4 to 5.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Material reaches it from outside the cell by endocytosis and phagocytosis, as when a macrophage engulfs a bacterium and the resulting vesicle fuses with a lysosome, and from inside the cell by autophagy, the digestion of the cell's own parts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In autophagy a membrane encloses damaged proteins or organelles to form a vesicle called an autophagosome, which then fuses with the lysosome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Animal cells have lysosomes but most plant cells do not, and in plants the same digestion takes place in vacuoles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The organelle was predicted before it was seen, when enzymes that attack cell components turned up in an unexpected fraction of centrifuged cells and were proposed to sit inside membrane-bound particles, which agents that dissolve membranes duly broke open.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its enzymes arrive from the Golgi apparatus tagged with mannose 6-phosphate, and when the tag is missing they are secreted instead, one of a family of inherited lysosomal storage diseases in which undigested material swells the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The old image of the lysosome as a suicide bag that bursts to kill its cell tells only part of the story, for although leaking enzymes can destroy a cell, the cell has several ways of dealing with a damaged lysosome first.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Mildly damaged lysosomes are repaired, severely damaged ones are removed by a form of autophagy called lysophagy, and some are expelled by fusing with the cell membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Damage also triggers the making of new lysosomes through the transcription factor TFEB, which is regulated by the protein complex mTORC1, by calcium signalling and by other pathways.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.4 The Endomembrane System and Proteins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1974,” press release, October 1974, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1974/press-release/. [Open access]","The Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2016,” press release, 3 October 2016, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2016/press-release/. [Open access]","Qincheng Liu, Weiqi Wang, Liwei Xu, Qisheng Zhang, and Hongna Wang, “The Host Mannose-6-Phosphate Pathway and Viral Infection,” <i>Frontiers in Cellular and Infection Microbiology</i> 14 (2024): 1349221, https://doi.org/10.3389/fcimb.2024.1349221. [Open access]","Quan Wang, Ruolin Wang, Haihui Hu, Xiaoqing Huo, and Fulong Wang, “Lysosomes’ Fallback Strategies: More Than Just Survival or Death,” <i>Frontiers in Cell and Developmental Biology</i> 13 (2025): 1559504, https://doi.org/10.3389/fcell.2025.1559504. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why must lysosomal enzymes be kept behind a membrane?","a":"Because they attack the cell's own components. The enzymes found in the new fraction all broke down cellular material, so it was proposed that they were confined in membrane-bound particles, and agents that dissolve membranes released them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They also work at an acid pH that many reactions in the cytoplasm could not tolerate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How do digestive enzymes find their way into lysosomes?","a":"They are tagged in the Golgi apparatus with mannose 6-phosphate, recognised by receptors in the trans-Golgi network and sent on to lysosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Without the tag, as in mucolipidosis type II, they are secreted, and undigested material accumulates inside the lysosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happens when a lysosome is damaged?","a":"Mild damage is repaired, severe damage leads to the lysosome's removal by lysophagy, and some damaged lysosomes are expelled by fusing with the plasma membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> These responses matter because leaking lysosomal enzymes can kill a cell,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and damage also prompts the making of new lysosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lysosome"},
    "bio-121": {"questions":["Radioactive leucine fed to pancreas slices showed up first in the rough endoplasmic reticulum, then in Golgi vesicles, then in zymogen granules, tracing the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stage by stage.","Roughly a third of a eukaryote's proteins mature along the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, where misfolded ones are held back in the endoplasmic reticulum or caught and returned from the Golgi."],"abstract":"The <b>secretory pathway</b> is the route by which a eukaryotic cell sends new proteins out of the cell or into its membranes, from the rough endoplasmic reticulum through the Golgi apparatus, carried in small membrane sacs called vesicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About a third of a eukaryote's proteins are made this way, entering the endoplasmic reticulum unfolded through a protein channel and folding inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Sorting starts on the ribosome, where a signal recognition particle binds ribosomes making a secretory protein about 6,000 times more tightly than those making a protein for the cytoplasm, and is needed for the chain to cross the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Vesicles bud from the endoplasmic reticulum, fuse with the cis face of the Golgi and leave from its trans face, their cargo now carrying sugar chains and tags that decide where it goes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some of the final vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents outside the cell, while others carry digestive enzymes and become lysosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The route was first traced in slices of guinea-pig pancreas, which were given a radioactive amino acid for 3 minutes and then followed through a chase in unlabelled medium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The labelled protein appeared first in the rough endoplasmic reticulum, peaked in the small vesicles at the edge of the Golgi 7 minutes later, and then moved into the zymogen granules that store digestive enzymes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The endoplasmic reticulum is also a checkpoint, holding back misfolded proteins and sending them for destruction, while any that slip through to the Golgi are returned or sent to the lysosome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The older picture of fixed Golgi compartments handing cargo forward in vesicles has largely given way to cisternal maturation, in which each flattened sac forms at the cis face, matures through the stack and breaks up at the trans face.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Yeast sacs tagged with fluorescent markers were seen to turn from an early colour to a late one, as that model predicts, though whether every cargo in every cell travels this way is unsettled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.4 The Endomembrane System and Proteins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","J. D. Jamieson and G. E. Palade, “Intracellular Transport of Secretory Proteins in the Pancreatic Exocrine Cell. I. Role of the Peripheral Elements of the Golgi Complex,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 34, no. 2 (1967): 577–96, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.34.2.577. [Open access]","P. Walter, I. Ibrahimi, and G. Blobel, “Translocation of Proteins across the Endoplasmic Reticulum. I. Signal Recognition Protein (SRP) Binds to In-Vitro-Assembled Polysomes Synthesizing Secretory Protein,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 91, no. 2 (1981): 545–50, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.91.2.545. [Open access]","Zhihao Sun and Jeffrey L. Brodsky, “Protein Quality Control in the Secretory Pathway,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 218, no. 10 (2019): 3171–87, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.201906047. [Open access]","Scott Emr et al., “Journeys through the Golgi: Taking Stock in a New Era,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 187, no. 4 (2009): 449–53, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.200909011. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does sorting a secretory protein begin on the ribosome?","a":"Because a signal recognition particle binds ribosomes that are making a secretory protein about 6,000 times more tightly than those making a cytoplasmic one, and it is needed for the chain to cross the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The protein then enters the endoplasmic reticulum unfolded through a channel and folds inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was the order of the stations first worked out?","a":"By a pulse and chase: pancreas slices were given a radioactive amino acid for 3 minutes and then unlabelled medium, and the label moved from the rough endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi's edge vesicles and then into the storage granules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the picture of vesicles shuttling between fixed Golgi compartments now thought incomplete?","a":"Because the favoured model is cisternal maturation, in which the sacs themselves form at the cis face, mature and break up at the trans face, carrying cargo with them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Fluorescent tags in yeast showed single sacs changing from an early to a late identity, though the evidence is not complete for every cell type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Secretion"},
    "bio-122": {"questions":["A circular genome of its own and bacterial-sized ribosomes mark the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> as the descendant of an engulfed aerobic bacterium, and it still multiplies only by dividing in two.","Live imaging shows that a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not a fixed bean but part of a tubular network that splits and fuses constantly as the cell's energy needs change."],"abstract":"A <b>mitochondrion</b> is a double-membraned organelle of eukaryotic cells in which most of the ATP made from food is produced, a process that uses oxygen and gives off carbon dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its inner membrane is folded into ridges called cristae around a central matrix, and the folds give a large surface for the proteins of cellular respiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Electron carriers set in that inner membrane pass electrons from food to oxygen and pump hydrogen ions out of the matrix, and the ions flowing back through the enzyme ATP synthase turn its parts like a tiny generator to make ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cells that use the most energy are packed with mitochondria, muscle and nerve cells among them, and repeatedly stimulating a muscle cell makes it produce more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A mitochondrion carries its own small, circular genome and bacterial-sized ribosomes, relics of an aerobic bacterium that a host cell took in and did not destroy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The popular label 'powerhouse of the cell' is true but incomplete, since mitochondria also take part in programmed cell death, immune defence, calcium balance and chemical signalling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Nor are they the separate beans of textbook diagrams, for live imaging shows them forming a tubular network that constantly splits and fuses, changing the number, size and position of its parts as the cell's needs change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A cell cannot build one from scratch, because most mitochondrial proteins are now encoded in the nucleus, made in the cytoplasm and imported, so new mitochondria arise only by the division of old ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Plant cells, which also have chloroplasts, still have mitochondria, and with only a few exceptions every eukaryotic cell does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The exceptions are telling, for the gut parasite <i>Giardia lamblia</i> keeps only tiny remnants called mitosomes, and some other anaerobic eukaryotes carry hydrogenosomes, which make hydrogen instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Julianne Zedalis and John Eggebrecht, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology for AP Courses</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-ap-courses/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nature Education, “Mitochondria,” in <i>Cell Biology for Seminars</i>, unit 3.4, Scitable by Nature Education, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/mitochondria-14053590/. [Open access]","Lisa Tilokani, Shun Nagashima, Vincent Paupe, and Julien Prudent, “Mitochondrial Dynamics: Overview of Molecular Mechanisms,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 62, no. 3 (2018): 341–60, https://doi.org/10.1042/EBC20170104. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can an antibiotic aimed at bacteria sometimes harm human cells?","a":"Because mitochondria still carry 70S ribosomes, the same size as those of bacteria, so a drug that attacks bacterial ribosomes can also reach the ones inside a human cell's mitochondria and cause side effects.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Those ribosomes, with the organelle's circular genome, are relics of its bacterial ancestor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"If plant cells have chloroplasts, why do they also need mitochondria?","a":"Because chloroplasts make sugar by photosynthesis, but the plant still has to break that sugar down to make ATP, and that cellular respiration happens in its mitochondria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> With only a few exceptions, every eukaryotic cell contains mitochondria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a cell not make a new mitochondrion from nothing?","a":"Because the organelle needs products of both its own small genome and the nuclear genome, most of its proteins being made in the cytoplasm and imported, so mitochondria multiply only by dividing in two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Fission and fusion between them are going on all the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Mitochondria"},
    "bio-123": {"questions":["Only about a hundred protein genes remain in the small circular genome of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so most of its 2,000 to 3,000 proteins are made outside it and imported.","Because the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is passed on through the mother in most crop plants, genes engineered into its small genome are not carried away from the field in pollen."],"abstract":"A <b>chloroplast</b> is the organelle in which plants and algae carry out photosynthesis, using light energy, carbon dioxide and water to make sugar and release oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In a leaf, the cells that hold chloroplasts lie mostly in the mesophyll, the middle layer, which takes in carbon dioxide through small pores called stomata.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Each chloroplast has an outer and an inner membrane around a gel-like stroma, in which floats a third system of flattened membrane sacs called thylakoids, stacked into grana in most plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The thylakoid membranes hold chlorophyll and run the light-dependent reactions, while the Calvin cycle, which builds sugar from carbon dioxide, works in the stroma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Like a mitochondrion it carries its own DNA and bacterial-sized ribosomes, and the evidence points to descent from a cyanobacterium taken in by an early eukaryotic cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Most of that ancestor's genes have since moved to the nucleus, leaving a land plant's chloroplast with one circular genome of 120 to 130 genes, between 107 and 218 kilobases long depending on the species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Most of the 2,000 to 3,000 proteins a chloroplast needs are therefore encoded in the nucleus, made on ribosomes in the cytoplasm and pulled in through protein channels in its two envelope membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The stroma holds many copies of that small genome, which is why a foreign gene placed in it can reach up to 10,000 copies in each plant cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In most cultivated crops the chloroplast genome is passed on only through the mother, so genes engineered into it are not spread by pollen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Animals have no chloroplasts and must eat their food, while plants make their own sugars and then burn them for ATP in their mitochondria, the basic difference between autotrophs and heterotrophs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nature Education, “Plant Cells, Chloroplasts, and Cell Walls,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, unit 3.5, Scitable by Nature Education, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/plant-cells-chloroplasts-and-cell-walls-14053956/. [Open access]","Jean-David Rochaix, “Chloroplast Protein Import Machinery and Quality Control,” <i>FEBS Journal</i> 289, no. 22 (2022): 6908–18, https://doi.org/10.1111/febs.16464. [Open access]","Henry Daniell, Choun-Sea Lin, Ming Yu, and Wan-Jung Chang, “Chloroplast Genomes: Diversity, Evolution, and Applications in Genetic Engineering,” <i>Genome Biology</i> 17 (2016): 134, https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-016-1004-2. [Open access]","Julianne Zedalis and John Eggebrecht, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology for AP Courses</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-ap-courses/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a chloroplast have DNA and ribosomes of its own?","a":"Because it descends from a photosynthetic bacterium that an early eukaryotic cell took in without destroying, and its ribosomes are still the 70S size of a prokaryote's.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Over time most of that bacterium's genes moved to the host's nucleus, leaving close to 100 protein genes in the chloroplast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must most chloroplast proteins be imported from outside?","a":"Because their genes now sit in the nucleus, so the proteins are made on ribosomes in the cytoplasm as precursors and then passed through the translocons of the outer and inner envelope membranes into the stroma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do genetic engineers like to put genes into the chloroplast?","a":"Because each plant cell holds many copies of the chloroplast genome, so a foreign gene can reach up to 10,000 copies per cell, and because in most crops chloroplasts are inherited only through the mother, which keeps the gene out of pollen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chloroplast"},
    "bio-124": {"questions":["Named from the Latin for ‘empty’, the plant <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> breaks down waste, stores proteins, ions, rubber and opium, and helps to defend against pathogens.","Storage and lytic forms of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can sit side by side in one plant cell and later fuse into the single large compartment that fills most of it."],"abstract":"A <b>vacuole</b> is a membrane-bound sac inside a cell, larger than a vesicle, used for storage and, in plants, for breaking down large molecules with the enzymes it holds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The best known is the central vacuole of a plant cell, which typically takes up about 30 per cent of the cell's volume and can fill as much as 90 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its fluid presses outwards against the cell wall as turgor pressure, and when the soil dries and water leaves the vacuole the walls are left unsupported and the plant wilts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Animal cells have no central vacuole, but many eukaryotic cells make smaller ones when they engulf a particle, wrapping it in a pocket of membrane that pinches off around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Plant vacuoles are related to lysosomes and carry digestive enzymes, which is why the digestion done by lysosomes in an animal cell happens in the vacuoles of a plant cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The name comes from the Latin 'vacuus', meaning ‘empty’, and the picture of a passive bag of water is still the one most readers carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In fact vacuoles break down cellular waste, store ions and proteins, defend the plant against pathogens and help to hold its pH steady, and both rubber and opium are stored in them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One cell may hold storage vacuoles and lytic, digesting vacuoles at the same time, and these can fuse to form a single large central vacuole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That also corrects the textbook rule that a vacuole's membrane never fuses with other membranes, since material bound for the vacuole arrives in compartments that fuse with the vacuole already there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Turgor pressure of up to 5 bar has long been taken as the force behind plant cell growth, and although the vacuole's exact part is still being worked out, restricting its size stops cells and roots from lengthening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nature Education, “Plant Cells, Chloroplasts, and Cell Walls,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, unit 3.5, Scitable by Nature Education, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/plant-cells-chloroplasts-and-cell-walls-14053956/. [Open access]","Sabrina Kaiser and David Scheuring, “To Lead or to Follow: Contribution of the Plant Vacuole to Cell Growth,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 11 (2020): 553, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2020.00553. [Open access]","Chunhua Zhang, Glenn R. Hicks, and Natasha V. Raikhel, “Plant Vacuole Morphology and Vacuolar Trafficking,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 5 (2014): 476, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2014.00476. [Open access]","Julianne Zedalis and John Eggebrecht, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology for AP Courses</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-ap-courses/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a plant wilt when it is not watered?","a":"Because as the soil dries, water moves out of the central vacuoles and cytoplasm, the vacuole shrinks and stops pressing against the cell wall, and the unsupported walls give the plant its wilted look.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does the central vacuole let a plant cell grow cheaply?","a":"When the vacuole takes up more water the cell becomes larger without spending much energy on making new cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Vacuoles usually account for changes in cell size while the cytoplasm's volume stays the same, and restricting a vacuole's size has been shown to hold back cell lengthening.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do most plant cells manage without lysosomes?","a":"Because their vacuoles are related to lysosomes and hold degradative enzymes of their own, so the breaking down of large molecules that lysosomes do in animal cells takes place in the plant cell's vacuoles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Vacuole"},
    "bio-125": {"questions":["Faults in the PEX genes that build the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cause Zellweger syndrome and related disorders, in which this single-membraned organelle of oxidation reactions fails to form properly.","Once thought to multiply only by growth and division, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was found to form anew from the endoplasmic reticulum, and it imports its proteins already folded."],"abstract":"A <b>peroxisome</b> is a small, round organelle bounded by a single membrane that carries out oxidation reactions, breaking down fatty acids and amino acids and detoxifying many of the poisons that enter the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is named after hydrogen peroxide, which many of its enzymes produce by passing hydrogen atoms from other molecules to oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Hydrogen peroxide is a highly reactive molecule and would damage the rest of the cell, but inside the organelle the enzyme catalase breaks it down into water and oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Liver cells, which detoxify the blood, hold an exceptionally large number of peroxisomes, and alcohol is among the poisons they break down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peroxisomes also share in making lipids alongside the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, and the organelle varies with its owner, for glyoxysomes in plants and yeasts turn stored fat into sugar, and some trypanosome parasites make modified peroxisomes called glycosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Peroxisomes take in their proteins in a way that sets them apart from most other organelles, importing them from the cytoplasm already folded and sometimes already joined together in groups.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> For decades they were thought to be autonomous like mitochondria and chloroplasts, receiving their proteins from free ribosomes and multiplying only by growth and division, never forming anew.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That picture was overturned when yeast mutants that seemed to lack any peroxisomal membrane built new peroxisomes from the endoplasmic reticulum once the missing gene was restored.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some textbooks still describe peroxisomes as forming independently of the endomembrane system, and how much each route contributes in different cells remains a matter of competing models.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In humans, faults in the PEX genes that build peroxisomes cause the peroxisome biogenesis disorders, Zellweger syndrome among them, and the genes behind all 14 known groups of these disorders have been identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Ewald H. Hettema, Ralf Erdmann, Ida van der Klei, and Marten Veenhuis, “Evolving Models for Peroxisome Biogenesis,” <i>Current Opinion in Cell Biology</i> 29 (2014): 25–30, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ceb.2014.02.002. [Open access]","Yukio Fujiki, Kanji Okumoto, Satoru Mukai, Masanori Honsho, and Shigehiko Tamura, “Peroxisome Biogenesis in Mammalian Cells,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 5 (2014): 307, https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2014.00307. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are hydrogen-peroxide reactions kept inside an organelle?","a":"Because hydrogen peroxide is reactive enough to damage the cell's other components, so it is held within the peroxisome and broken down there by peroxisomal enzymes into water and oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Shutting such degradative chemistry inside an organelle protects the cytoplasm from unwanted damage.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are liver cells so rich in peroxisomes?","a":"Because the liver is the organ chiefly responsible for detoxifying the blood, and peroxisomes neutralise poisons such as alcohol by transferring hydrogen from them to oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Alcohol is detoxified by the peroxisomes of liver cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the idea that peroxisomes never form anew abandoned?","a":"Because mutants that appeared to have no peroxisomal membrane at all made new peroxisomes from the endoplasmic reticulum once the missing gene was supplied, which challenged the growth-and-division model.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The endoplasmic reticulum has been central to the study of peroxisome formation ever since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Peroxisome"},
    "bio-126": {"questions":["Far from being a fixed frame, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of a crawling amoeba is assembled at the cell's leading edge and taken apart at its rear as the cell moves forward.","Even bacteria have a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of a kind, with a tubulin-like protein marking where they will divide and actin-like filaments that set the width of rod-shaped cells."],"abstract":"The <b>cytoskeleton</b> is the network of protein fibres inside a cell that keeps its shape, holds organelles in place, moves vesicles and cytoplasm about and lets some cells crawl or swim.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a eukaryotic cell it is built from three kinds of fibre, the narrowest being microfilaments of the protein actin, about 7 nanometres across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Intermediate filaments, 8 to 10 nanometres across, are rope-like and mainly mechanical, and their proteins vary with the cell, with keratins in epithelial cells and neurofilaments in nerve cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Microtubules, hollow tubes of the protein tubulin about 25 nanometres wide, resist compression, carry vesicles along their length and pull the chromosomes apart when a cell divides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Exocytosis, for example, depends on a vesicle being moved along this network to the plasma membrane before it releases its contents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The name suggests a fixed frame of bones, but actin filaments and microtubules are built up and taken apart all the time, and a crawling amoeba adds actin at its leading edge while dismantling it at its rear.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Intermediate filaments are the steadiest of the three, and one class of them, the lamins, forms a meshwork that reinforces the inside of the nuclear membrane in every cell type.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The other common simplification is that only eukaryotes have a cytoskeleton, whereas all cells, from bacteria to complex eukaryotes, have protein filaments of this kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Bacteria have relatives of all three families, for a tubulin-like protein called FtsZ forms the ring that marks where a cell will divide, and actin-like MreB proteins help to set the width of rod-shaped cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the curved bacterium <i>Caulobacter crescentus</i>, a protein called crescentin that resembles intermediate filament proteins lines the inner side of the curve, and cells that lose it grow straight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.5 The Cytoskeleton,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-5-the-cytoskeleton. [Open access]","Julianne Zedalis and John Eggebrecht, “4.5 The Cytoskeleton,” in <i>Biology for AP Courses</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-ap-courses/pages/4-5-cytoskeleton. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nature Education, “Microtubules and Filaments,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, unit 3.2, Scitable by Nature Education, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/microtubules-and-filaments-14052932/. [Open access]","Matthew T. Cabeen and Christine Jacobs-Wagner, “Skin and Bones: The Bacterial Cytoskeleton, Cell Wall, and Cell Morphogenesis,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 179, no. 3 (2007): 381–87, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.200708001. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a cell need three kinds of fibre rather than one?","a":"Because they bear different loads: microfilaments thicken the cortex at the cell's edge and resist tension like rubber bands, microtubules resist compression from the interior, and intermediate filaments hold organelles in place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> No single component makes up the cytoskeleton; the three work together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does the cytoskeleton let a cell crawl?","a":"Actin filaments assemble and disassemble very quickly, and at the leading edge of a moving cell they are rapidly polymerising while at the rear they are quickly breaking down, which is what drives the crawling of an amoeba.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happens to bacteria that lose part of their cytoskeleton?","a":"A curved <i>Caulobacter crescentus</i> cell that loses crescentin grows as a straight rod, and without the tubulin-like FtsZ, the ring that organises cell division cannot form, so division fails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cytoskeleton"},
    "bio-127": {"questions":["A <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grows while a cap of GTP-bound tubulin protects its tip, then collapses abruptly once the cap is lost, a switching behaviour known as dynamic instability.","In a dividing cell each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the spindle reaches out from a centrosome to attach to a chromosome, and together they pull the sister chromatids apart."],"abstract":"A <b>microtubule</b> is a hollow tube of the protein tubulin, about 25 nanometres wide, and the thickest of the fibres that make up the cytoskeleton.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its wall is built from paired units of alpha- and beta-tubulin strung end to end into strands called protofilaments, thirteen of which lie side by side to form the tube.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Microtubules help a cell resist being squashed, serve as tracks along which vesicles travel, and hold organelles in position in the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two ends differ, the faster-growing plus end reaching outwards while the minus end is usually anchored in a microtubule organising centre, chiefly the centrosome beside the nucleus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They also form the core of cilia and flagella, and when a cell divides they make up the spindle that attaches to each chromosome and pulls the sister chromatids apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> What sets microtubules apart from most polymers is that each one switches abruptly between growing and collapsing, a behaviour called dynamic instability.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Tubulin carries the energy-rich molecule GTP when it joins the end, and because that GTP is broken down only after a delay, a cap of GTP-tubulin builds up at the growing tip.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Behind the cap the tubulin holds GDP and is held straight against its natural curve, so when the cap is lost the strands curl outwards, the bonds between them break and the tube falls apart in a catastrophe.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A population of microtubules can therefore lose members even while its average length increases, which is how the switching was first deduced before it was ever watched.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> This constant turnover underlies how dividing cells separate their chromosomes, how cells move into a healing wound and how nerve cells extend their branches, and many proteins act on it by speeding growth or making collapse more frequent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.5 The Cytoskeleton,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-5-the-cytoskeleton. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.5 Cell Growth and Division,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-5-cell-growth-and-division. [Open access]","Nature Education, “Microtubules and Filaments,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, unit 3.2, Scitable by Nature Education, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/microtubules-and-filaments-14052932/. [Open access]","Gary J. Brouhard, “Dynamic Instability 30 Years Later: Complexities in Microtubule Growth and Catastrophe,” <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i> 26, no. 7 (2015): 1207–10, https://doi.org/10.1091/mbc.E13-10-0594. [Open access]","Julianne Zedalis and John Eggebrecht, “4.5 The Cytoskeleton,” in <i>Biology for AP Courses</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-ap-courses/pages/4-5-cytoskeleton. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a microtubule collapse so suddenly?","a":"Because the tubulin in its body holds GDP and is kept straight only by the cap of GTP-tubulin at its tip; once that cap is lost the strands curl outwards, the bonds between them break and the GDP-tubulin falls away rapidly, which actin filaments do not do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was dynamic instability first recognised?","a":"Electron microscopy showed that a population of microtubules could shrink in number even while its average length went up, so some must be vanishing while others grew; combined with what was known about GTP, this led to the idea that each tube switches when it loses its cap.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a dividing cell depend on microtubules?","a":"Because the spindle is made of them: microtubules extend from the two centrosomes, attach to each chromosome and pull the sister chromatids to opposite ends of the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their growth and shrinkage is what makes that separation possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Microtubule"},
    "bio-128": {"questions":["A fully mature <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> becomes the basal body from which a cilium grows, perhaps its clearest job, since cells whose centrosome is removed can still divide.","The protist <i>Naegleria</i> divides without a single <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> while it lives as an amoeba, then builds them from nothing, along with two flagella, when conditions turn harsh."],"abstract":"A <b>centriole</b> is a small barrel built of microtubules, and in an animal cell two of them lying at right angles form the core of the centrosome, the main microtubule-organising centre beside the nucleus.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most centrioles have a wall of nine triplets of microtubules held together by other proteins, and every centriole shares that ninefold symmetry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They are about 200 to 220 nanometres across, while their length runs from about 150 nanometres in a fruit-fly embryo to 500 in a human body cell and more than 1,000 in some sperm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each mother centriole duplicates only once per cell cycle, a single daughter growing at right angles to its wall, so that a dividing line of cells keeps two centrosomes generation after generation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> As a cell enters mitosis its two centrosomes move apart, and microtubules growing from each help to form the two poles of the spindle that separates the chromosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> That role makes it tempting to say that centrioles divide the cell, but their exact part in division is unclear, since cells whose centrosome has been removed can still divide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Plant cells, which have no centrosomes, divide too, and centrosomes are not essential for cell division in every kind of cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Centrioles have a second job that is easier to pin down, for a fully mature mother centriole becomes the basal body from which a cilium grows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In a resting cell the centrosome moves to the cell surface, where the mother centriole forms the base of a primary cilium, an antenna-like organelle used in signalling and sensing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Centrioles can even form without a parent, as in the freshwater protist <i>Naegleria</i>, which divides without them as an amoeba but builds them anew, along with two flagella, when conditions turn against it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.5 The Cytoskeleton,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-5-the-cytoskeleton. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.5 Cell Growth and Division,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-5-cell-growth-and-division. [Open access]","Jadranka Loncarek and Mónica Bettencourt-Dias, “Building the Right Centriole for Each Cell Type,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 217, no. 3 (2018): 823–35, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.201704093. [Open access]","Tetsuo Kobayashi and Brian D. Dynlacht, “Regulating the Transition from Centriole to Basal Body,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 193, no. 3 (2011): 435–44, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.201101005. [Open access]","Julianne Zedalis and John Eggebrecht, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology for AP Courses</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-ap-courses/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are centrioles not thought essential for cell division?","a":"Because cells that have had their centrosome removed can still divide, and plant cells, which lack centrosomes altogether, divide as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Centrosomes organise the spindle in animal cells but are not essential for division in all cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must a mother centriole duplicate only once per cycle?","a":"Because a cycling cell keeps two centrosomes over the generations only if each mother forms a single new centriole per cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When resident centrioles are removed and new ones form from nothing, control over their number is usually lost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How are centrioles connected to cilia?","a":"In a resting cell the centrosome migrates to the cell surface and the mother centriole becomes a basal body, which builds the primary cilium, an antenna-like organelle for signalling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> A fully mature centriole nucleates the cilium's microtubule core at its far end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Centriole"},
    "bio-129": {"questions":["Nine pairs of microtubules around a central pair form the core of a motile <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and dynein motors sliding the pairs past one another make it bend.","A single non-moving <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> with a 9+0 core stands on many kidney cells, fibroblasts and neurons, where it works as a sensor rather than as a paddle."],"abstract":"A <b>cilium</b> is a slender projection from the surface of a eukaryotic cell, built around a core of microtubules called the axoneme and sheathed in an extension of the cell membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Motile cilia are usually short and numerous, beating to drive a cell such as a <i>Paramecium</i> through water or to sweep fluid past a fixed cell, as in the lining of the airways and the Fallopian tubes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the airways their constant rhythmic beat carries mucus, dust and bacteria upwards, away from the lungs and towards the mouth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their core is a 9+2 array of nine pairs of microtubules around a central pair, and dynein motor proteins make neighbouring pairs slide past one another, which bends the cilium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A eukaryotic flagellum, such as the tail of a sperm, is the same structure made longer and fewer in number, and both grow from a basal body built like a centriole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The word flagellum is also used for the propeller of a bacterium, but that is a different machine altogether, a stiff spiral filament of the protein flagellin that spins in the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its base is a rotary motor set in the cell membrane, driven in <i>Escherichia coli</i> by a flow of protons across the membrane, and it can turn either clockwise or anticlockwise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A eukaryotic flagellum, by contrast, does not rotate at all but bends like a flexible whip as its microtubules slide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Many cells of the body also carry a single non-moving primary cilium with a 9+0 core lacking the central pair, found on kidney cells, fibroblasts and neurons and long dismissed as functionless before it was recognised as a sensor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Faults in the dynein arms that power beating cause primary ciliary dyskinesia, whose effects include infertility and a higher incidence of hydrocephalus, a measure of how much the body depends on motile cilia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.5 The Cytoskeleton,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-5-the-cytoskeleton. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.3 Unique Characteristics of Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-3-unique-characteristics-of-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Peter Satir and Søren T. Christensen, “Structure and Function of Mammalian Cilia,” <i>Histochemistry and Cell Biology</i> 129, no. 6 (2008): 687–93, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00418-008-0416-9. [Open access]","Yusuke V. Morimoto and Tohru Minamino, “Structure and Function of the Bi-Directional Bacterial Flagellar Motor,” <i>Biomolecules</i> 4, no. 1 (2014): 217–34, https://doi.org/10.3390/biom4010217. [Open access]","Nature Education, “Microtubules and Filaments,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, unit 3.2, Scitable by Nature Education, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/microtubules-and-filaments-14052932/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is a bacterial flagellum not the same thing as a sperm's tail?","a":"Because the bacterial one is a stiff spiral filament of flagellin turned by a rotary motor in the cell membrane, while a sperm's flagellum is a flexible whip of microtubules in a 9+2 array that bends as dynein makes the pairs slide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The bacterial motor is powered by protons crossing the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happens when motile cilia fail?","a":"Mutations that disable the dynein arms, most often in a dynein heavy-chain gene, cause primary ciliary dyskinesia, in which infertility and a raised incidence of hydrocephalus point to the processes that depend on beating cilia.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are cilia and eukaryotic flagella treated as one structure?","a":"Because despite their differences in length and number they share the same 9+2 arrangement of microtubules and the same bending mechanism, and each grows from a basal body of triplet microtubules like a centriole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cilium"},
    "bio-130": {"questions":["Many antibiotics block the making of peptidoglycan, the meshwork of the bacterial <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so that weakened cells are exposed to osmotic pressure and fail.","A plant's growing <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> resists strong tension yet can expand its surface more than a thousandfold, loosened by proteins such as expansins as water enters."],"abstract":"A <b>cell wall</b> is a rigid layer outside the plasma membrane that protects a cell, supports it and gives it its shape, and it is found in plants, fungi, algae, bacteria and some protists but not in animal cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its material depends on the organism, being cellulose in plants, chitin in fungi, silica, calcium carbonate, agar or carrageenan in various algae and protists, and in bacteria a meshwork called peptidoglycan found nowhere else.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A plant's wall is built mainly of cellulose, the most abundant large molecule on Earth, whose long chains of glucose gather in bundles of about forty called microfibrils, set in a hydrated network of other polysaccharides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Young cells have a flexible primary wall, and many later lay down a thicker, more durable secondary wall inside it once they stop growing, an adaptation that lets land plants grow upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Because peptidoglycan exists only in bacteria, many antibiotics work by blocking its synthesis, leaving the weakened cell exposed to osmotic pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The familiar picture of the wall as a rigid box misses how it lets a plant grow, for a growing wall has to be both strong and able to stretch.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It resists tensions in its plane of about 10 megapascals or more, generated by a turgor pressure inside the cell of about 0.5 to 1 megapascal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Yet between its formation at cell division and the end of growth a wall can expand its surface area from ten to more than a thousand times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That expansion depends on selective loosening by proteins such as expansins, which let the wall extend slowly and irreversibly, a process called creep, as water flows into the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> With a healthy supply of water turgor keeps a plant from wilting, and in drought the walls still hold its stems and leaves in shape around a shrinking vacuole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.3 Unique Characteristics of Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-3-unique-characteristics-of-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “3.4 Unique Characteristics of Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Nature Education, “Plant Cells, Chloroplasts, and Cell Walls,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, unit 3.5, Scitable by Nature Education, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/plant-cells-chloroplasts-and-cell-walls-14053956/. [Open access]","Daniel J. Cosgrove, “Catalysts of Plant Cell Wall Loosening,” <i>F1000Research</i> 5 (2016): F1000 Faculty Rev-119, https://doi.org/10.12688/f1000research.7180.1. [Open access]","Kelly Houston, Matthew R. Tucker, Jamil Chowdhury, Neil Shirley, and Alan Little, “The Plant Cell Wall: A Complex and Dynamic Structure as Revealed by the Responses of Genes under Stress Conditions,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 7 (2016): 984, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2016.00984. [Open access]","Julianne Zedalis and John Eggebrecht, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology for AP Courses</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-ap-courses/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does amoxicillin fail against some bacteria?","a":"Because it works by interfering with the making of peptidoglycan, and a bacterium such as <i>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</i> has no cell wall and makes no peptidoglycan, so an antibiotic that targets ribosomes is needed instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Drugs of the first kind leave walled bacteria weakened against osmotic pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does turgor not burst a plant cell?","a":"Because the wall's strength lets it resist the tensions in its plane, of about 10 megapascals or more, that the cell's internal pressure of 0.5 to 1 megapascal produces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The wall gives tensile strength and protection against mechanical and osmotic stress.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can a rigid wall let a plant cell grow?","a":"Because growing walls are loosened selectively by proteins such as expansins, which allow slow, irreversible extension as water enters, so a wall can expand from ten to more than a thousand times in area before growth stops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Thicker secondary walls are laid down only after growth ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cell wall"},
    "bio-131": {"questions":["Plant viruses spread through a leaf by making movement proteins that widen the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the narrow channels through which neighbouring plant cells share their cytoplasm across the wall.","A plant under attack can isolate its cells from one another by depositing callose around the necks of its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, closing the cytoplasmic routes that run through the cell walls."],"abstract":"<b>Plasmodesmata</b> are narrow channels that cross the walls between neighbouring plant cells and join their cytoplasm, so that materials can pass from one cell to the next and onwards through the plant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They are one of the structures that plant cells have and animal cells lack, alongside the cell wall, the large central vacuole and the chloroplasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Each channel is lined by plasma membrane that runs unbroken from one cell into the other, and down its centre runs a tightly squeezed tube of endoplasmic reticulum called the desmotubule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most traffic moves through the cytoplasmic sleeve between those two membranes, carrying RNAs, proteins, hormones and metabolites, and the channels also help load sugars into the phloem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Many channels form as a dividing cell builds its new wall around strands of endoplasmic reticulum, others are opened later through walls that already exist, and either kind may branch as the wall grows.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The common textbook comparison with gap junctions holds for what the two do but not for how they are built, because a gap junction is a pore made of proteins while a plasmodesma is a membrane-lined tunnel through a wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Nor are plasmodesmata simply open holes, since a soluble protein the size of green fluorescent protein spreads freely between some leaf cells but not others, and young leaves let far more through than old ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Plants narrow the channels by laying down callose, a glucan built from glucose units, in the wall around each pore, and the enzymes that make and break callose sit at the channels themselves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Viruses exploit the route with movement proteins that widen the pores, and a movement protein carrying a fluorescent tag escaped from about half of the mature leaf cells making it, against 2 percent for a similar tag alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Shutting the channels is also a defence, because plants attacked by bacteria or fungi make more callose at the pores and so cut routes that a pathogen could otherwise use to spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.6 Connections between Cells and Cellular Activities,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-6-connections-between-cells-and-cellular-activities. [Open access]","Ziqiang Patrick Li, Andrea Paterlini, Marie Glavier, and Emmanuelle M. Bayer, “Intercellular Trafficking via Plasmodesmata: Molecular Layers of Complexity,” <i>Cellular and Molecular Life Sciences</i> 78, no. 3 (2021): 799–816, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00018-020-03622-8. [Open access]","Tessa M. Burch-Smith, Solomon Stonebloom, Min Xu, and Patricia C. Zambryski, “Plasmodesmata during Development: Re-examination of the Importance of Primary, Secondary, and Branched Plasmodesmata Structure versus Function,” <i>Protoplasma</i> 248, no. 1 (2011): 61–74, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00709-010-0252-3. [Open access]","Patricia Zambryski, “Cell-to-Cell Transport of Proteins and Fluorescent Tracers via Plasmodesmata during Plant Development,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 164, no. 2 (2004): 165–68, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2172327/. [Open access]","Liam German, Richa Yeshvekar, and Yoselin Benitez-Alfonso, “Callose Metabolism and the Regulation of Cell Walls and Plasmodesmata during Plant Mutualistic and Pathogenic Interactions,” <i>Plant, Cell &amp; Environment</i> 46, no. 2 (2023): 391–404, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10107507/. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can plant cells not join up the way animal cells do?","a":"Because each plant cell is wrapped in a wall that keeps long stretches of its plasma membrane from touching the membrane of the next cell, so the cytoplasm of neighbours can only meet through channels that cross the wall.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How is a plasmodesma different from a gap junction?","a":"A gap junction is a channel built from connexin proteins in the two facing membranes. A plasmodesma is a tunnel through the cell wall, lined by plasma membrane that is continuous between the two cells, with a squeezed strand of endoplasmic reticulum running down its centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do plants close their plasmodesmata when they are attacked?","a":"Because the channels are a route that viruses, bacteria and fungi can use to spread. Plants respond by depositing callose in the wall around the pores, which narrows them and restricts movement between cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Plasmodesma"},
    "bio-132": {"questions":["In heart muscle each wave of electrical excitation passes from one cell to the next through a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> built from connexins, which is why the whole muscle contracts in order.","Antibodies against desmoglein make the skin blister in pemphigus because they break the kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that ties the intermediate filaments of neighbouring cells together."],"abstract":"A <b>cell junction</b> is a specialised point of contact where neighbouring animal cells, or a cell and the matrix beneath it, are held or linked by proteins that reach across the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Vertebrates have four kinds between cells, namely desmosomes, adherens junctions, tight junctions and gap junctions, and all of them are needed to build the layers and organs of the embryo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A tight junction presses neighbouring cells together so closely, through proteins called claudins and occludins, that material cannot leak between them, which is how the lining of the bladder keeps urine in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Desmosomes and adherens junctions are anchors, the first tying the intermediate filaments of neighbouring cells together through cadherins and the second their actin filaments, while hemidesmosomes fasten a cell to the basal lamina.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Gap junctions are clusters of channels, each a pair of connexons built from six connexin proteins, which let ions and small molecules pass straight from one cell's cytoplasm into the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Gap junctions are why a heart contracts as one, because they couple its muscle cells electrically so that each wave of excitation spreads in order, and changes to them are linked to arrhythmias.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Desmosomes are the strongest anchors, and in intact tissue most of them hold in a calcium-independent, very adhesive state, whereas almost all studies of cultured cells have used weaker, calcium-dependent ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They are also a late arrival, present in vertebrates as early-branching as hagfishes but apparently absent from the closest invertebrate chordates, which points to a role in the evolution of tough skin and a powerful heart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> When the immune system makes antibodies against desmoglein, a desmosomal cadherin, as in the disease pemphigus, the cells of the skin come apart and blisters form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The textbook habit of listing junctions as separate units is misleading, since they cooperate physically and functionally, and cell-to-cell links have also arisen elsewhere, such as the protein junctions between the cells of filament-forming cyanobacteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.6 Connections between Cells and Cellular Activities,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-6-connections-between-cells-and-cellular-activities. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “4.2 Epithelial Tissue,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-2-epithelial-tissue. [Open access]","“Cell Adhesion and Cell Communication,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/cell-adhesion-and-cell-communication-14050486/. [Open access]","Kathleen J. Green, Avinash Jaiganesh, and Joshua A. Broussard, “Desmosomes: Essential Contributors to an Integrated Intercellular Junction Network,” <i>F1000Research</i> 8 (2019): F1000 Faculty Rev-2150, https://doi.org/10.12688/f1000research.20942.1. [Open access]","David Garrod, “Desmosomes In Vivo,” <i>Dermatology Research and Practice</i> 2010 (2010): 212439, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2905707/. [Open access]","Nicholas J. Severs, Alexandra F. Bruce, Emmanuel Dupont, and Stephen Rothery, “Remodelling of Gap Junctions and Connexin Expression in Diseased Myocardium,” <i>Cardiovascular Research</i> 80, no. 1 (2008): 9–19, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2533424/. [Open access]","Ziqiang Patrick Li, Andrea Paterlini, Marie Glavier, and Emmanuelle M. Bayer, “Intercellular Trafficking via Plasmodesmata: Molecular Layers of Complexity,” <i>Cellular and Molecular Life Sciences</i> 78, no. 3 (2021): 799–816, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00018-020-03622-8. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does urine not leak out of the bladder between its lining cells?","a":"Because the epithelial cells lining it are joined by tight junctions, where proteins called claudins and occludins hold neighbouring membranes so close that material cannot pass through the gap between the cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the heart depend on gap junctions?","a":"Because gap junctions couple heart muscle cells electrically, giving each wave of excitation a direct path from cell to cell, so the muscle contracts in an orderly, synchronous way. Disturbed gap junctions are linked to arrhythmias.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are desmosomes thought to be a vertebrate innovation?","a":"Because they are found even in hagfishes but appear to be missing from the closest invertebrate chordates. Their strong adhesion is especially important in tissues under mechanical stress, such as the skin and heart muscle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cell junction"},
    "bio-133": {"questions":["Cells sense how stiff the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> around them is, so when its collagen becomes abnormally cross-linked the stiffer surroundings can feed fibrosis and change how cancer cells behave.","Integrins, membrane proteins found in every animal down to sponges, bind molecules of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> outside the cell and actin filaments inside it through adaptor proteins."],"abstract":"The <b>extracellular matrix</b> is the material that cells release into the space around them, a network of proteins and carbohydrates in which the most abundant protein, collagen, is interwoven with sugar-rich proteoglycans.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In connective tissue it makes up most of the bulk, a ground substance crossed by protein fibres that is usually fluid but becomes mineralised and solid in bone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Under every epithelium lies a thin sheet of it, the basement membrane, which anchors the cells above, and because no blood vessel crosses it those cells are fed by diffusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its other ingredients include glycoproteins such as fibronectin and laminin and the long polymer hyaluronic acid, which mixes readily with water and holds it in the tissue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Cells grip the matrix through integrins, membrane proteins present in every animal down to sponges, which bind matrix molecules outside the cell and, through adaptor proteins, actin filaments inside it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The older picture of the matrix as inert packing is wrong, because when a matrix molecule binds a receptor on the cell surface the change is relayed inward until genes in the nucleus are switched on or off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its stiffness carries information too, since cells sense how rigid their surroundings are and respond with changes in how they multiply, differentiate and express their genes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The matrix is constantly broken down and rebuilt by protein-cutting enzymes, and catalogues of its components, called the matrisome, assign more than 200 human genes to it, among them about 28 collagens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When that turnover goes wrong, abnormal breakdown and cross-linking of collagen contribute to fibrosis and heart failure, and a stiffened matrix changes how cancer cells and their neighbours behave.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It even reaches into the cell's own surface, since links between the matrix, the cytoskeleton and membrane proteins restrain how freely those proteins drift within the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.6 Connections between Cells and Cellular Activities,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-6-connections-between-cells-and-cellular-activities. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “4.3 Connective Tissue Supports and Protects,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-3-connective-tissue-supports-and-protects. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “4.2 Epithelial Tissue,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/4-2-epithelial-tissue. [Open access]","“Cell Adhesion and Cell Communication,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/cell-adhesion-and-cell-communication-14050486/. [Open access]","Kevin Dzobo and Collet Dandara, “The Extracellular Matrix: Its Composition, Function, Remodeling, and Role in Tumorigenesis,” <i>Biomimetics</i> 8, no. 2 (2023): 146, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10123695/. [Open access]","Khairunnisa Mohd Kamal et al., “The Extracellular Matrix: Structure, Composition, Biological Functions, Diseases, and Therapeutic Targets,” <i>Molecular Biomedicine</i> 7 (2026): 38, https://doi.org/10.1186/s43556-026-00436-1. [Open access]","Garth L. Nicolson, “Update of the 1972 Singer-Nicolson Fluid-Mosaic Model of Membrane Structure,” <i>Discoveries</i> 1, no. 1 (2013): e3, https://doi.org/10.15190/d.2013.3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How can material outside a cell change what happens inside it?","a":"When a matrix molecule binds a receptor on the cell surface, the receptor changes shape and passes the change to the actin filaments just inside the membrane. Signals then reach the nucleus and switch genes on or off. Integrins also relay signals in both directions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What does the extracellular matrix have to do with blood clotting?","a":"Damaged cells lining a blood vessel display a receptor called tissue factor. When it binds a factor in the matrix, platelets stick to the vessel wall, the vessel constricts and the steps that produce clotting factors begin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are epithelial cells fed by diffusion rather than by blood vessels?","a":"Because they sit on a basement membrane of matrix that no blood vessel crosses, so nutrients must diffuse up from the connective tissue below or be absorbed from the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Extracellular matrix"},
    "bio-134": {"questions":["Water drawn into the large central vacuole presses outward on the cellulose wall of a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and that turgor pressure is what keeps a well-watered stem or leaf from wilting.","Depending on its tissue, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may hold green chloroplasts, white leucoplasts or coloured chromoplasts, all of them plastids that develop from small undifferentiated proplastids."],"abstract":"A <b>plant cell</b> is a eukaryotic cell with the usual nucleus, ribosomes, mitochondria and peroxisomes, plus three things an animal cell lacks: a cell wall, chloroplasts and other plastids, and a large central vacuole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The wall is made mainly of cellulose, long chains of glucose bundled into microfibrils and set in a hydrated web of other polysaccharides, and it is assembled outside the cell by enzymes associated with the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Because the cytoplasm holds many dissolved molecules, water flows in and swells the vacuole against the wall, and the resulting turgor pressure keeps a well-watered plant from wilting.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The vacuole typically fills about 30 percent of the cell but can occupy up to 90 percent, and it also stores proteins, sugars and other metabolites.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Plant cells are not sealed off from one another, since channels called plasmodesmata cross the walls and connect the cytoplasm of most of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A common misreading of the comparison is that chloroplasts take the place of mitochondria, but plant cells have mitochondria as well and use them to make ATP just as animal cells do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor does every plant cell hold chloroplasts, because plastids begin as undifferentiated proplastids and develop into white leucoplasts, green chloroplasts or yellow, orange and red chromoplasts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The rule that plant cells lack lysosomes is only half true as well, since their digestion takes place in vacuoles, which share degradative enzymes and other functions with lysosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The vacuole is also central to growth, because a plant cell enlarges largely by taking water into it while the wall loosens, and restricting the vacuole limits how far a cell can elongate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most plant cells have no centrosome yet divide normally, and in a young embryo all the cells share one continuous cytoplasm through plasmodesmata before groups of them are isolated into separate domains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","“Plant Cells, Chloroplasts, and Cell Walls,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/plant-cells-chloroplasts-and-cell-walls-14053956/. [Open access]","Sabrina Kaiser and David Scheuring, “To Lead or to Follow: Contribution of the Plant Vacuole to Cell Growth,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 11 (2020): 553, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2020.00553. [Open access]","Heebak Choi, Taegyu Yi, and Sun-Hwa Ha, “Diversity of Plastid Types and Their Interconversions,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 12 (2021): 692024, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2021.692024. [Open access]","Chunhua Zhang, Glenn R. Hicks, and Natasha V. Raikhel, “Plant Vacuole Morphology and Vacuolar Trafficking,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 5 (2014): 476, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2014.00476. [Open access]","Ziqiang Patrick Li, Andrea Paterlini, Marie Glavier, and Emmanuelle M. Bayer, “Intercellular Trafficking via Plasmodesmata: Molecular Layers of Complexity,” <i>Cellular and Molecular Life Sciences</i> 78, no. 3 (2021): 799–816, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00018-020-03622-8. [Open access]","Tessa M. Burch-Smith, Solomon Stonebloom, Min Xu, and Patricia C. Zambryski, “Plasmodesmata during Development: Re-examination of the Importance of Primary, Secondary, and Branched Plasmodesmata Structure versus Function,” <i>Protoplasma</i> 248, no. 1 (2011): 61–74, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00709-010-0252-3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a plant wilt when it runs short of water?","a":"Because a plant cell stays firm through turgor pressure, the push of a water-filled central vacuole against the cell wall. When water is short the vacuole shrinks and the pressure falls, although the walls still help hold stems and leaves together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Do plant cells have mitochondria as well as chloroplasts?","a":"Yes. Mitochondria make ATP, the cell’s main energy-carrying molecule, in both plant and animal cells; chloroplasts are an addition that carries out photosynthesis, not a replacement.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does a plant cell break down material without lysosomes?","a":"Its digestive processes take place in vacuoles. Plant vacuoles are related to lysosomes and contain degradative enzymes for breaking down large molecules, and lytic vacuoles can sit in the same cell as storage vacuoles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Plant cell"},
    "bio-135": {"questions":["Bacterial carboxysomes, protein shells that enclose the enzyme RuBisCO, show that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is not confined to eukaryotes, even though the walls of these compartments contain no lipid membrane.","The nucleolus and stress granules extend the cell's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> beyond membranes, since they form by phase separation as droplets that hold their molecules apart without any enclosing lipid layer."],"abstract":"<b>Compartmentalisation</b> is the division of a cell's interior into separate spaces, each with its own molecules and reactions, and in a eukaryotic cell most of those spaces are organelles walled off by membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The partitions let different kinds of biochemical reaction run side by side in different organelles, much as interior walls separate the rooms of a house.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scale is large, because the total area of a eukaryotic cell's internal membranes far exceeds that of the plasma membrane around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A lipid membrane makes a good partition because its nonpolar core does not let water-soluble molecules through freely, and without it they would diffuse away from wherever they were concentrated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The compartments still trade with one another, since the endoplasmic reticulum, the Golgi apparatus and the lysosomes exchange membrane in an organised way, guided by sorting signals on proteins that work like postal codes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Lysosomes show the gain, because their digestive enzymes work at a much lower pH than the cytoplasm, where many reactions could not run at a low pH, so both proceed only because they are kept apart.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The nuclear envelope does the same for DNA, separating it from the protein-making machinery in the cytoplasm and giving the cell finer control over which proteins it makes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The textbook rule that only eukaryotes have compartments is wrong, for many bacteria build microcompartments whose boundary is not a lipid membrane but a shell of proteins with pores 0.4 to 0.7 nanometres across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Their genes occur in 23 of the 29 established bacterial phyla, and the shells include carboxysomes, which enclose the carbon-fixing enzyme RuBisCO, and others that contain a toxic intermediate while it is broken down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Eukaryotic cells also hold compartments with no membrane at all, such as the nucleolus and stress granules, which form by liquid–liquid phase separation, condensing out of the surrounding fluid as droplets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["“Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/eukaryotic-cells-14023963/. [Open access]","Mike Adams, “Discovering the Lipid Bilayer,” <i>Nature Education</i> 3, no. 9 (2010): 20, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/discovering-the-lipid-bilayer-14225438/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Henning Kirst and Cheryl A. Kerfeld, “Bacterial Microcompartments: Catalysis-Enhancing Metabolic Modules for Next Generation Metabolic and Biomedical Engineering,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 17 (2019): 79, https://doi.org/10.1186/s12915-019-0691-z. [Open access]","Yangxin Li et al., “Membraneless Organelles in Health and Disease: Exploring the Molecular Basis, Physiological Roles and Pathological Implications,” <i>Signal Transduction and Targeted Therapy</i> 9 (2024): 305, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41392-024-02013-w. [Open access]","“Cell Membranes,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/cell-membranes-14052567/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a cell need separate compartments at all?","a":"Because some reactions need conditions that would stop others. The enzymes inside lysosomes work at a much lower pH than the cytoplasm, where many reactions could not run at a low pH, so membranes let both kinds of chemistry go on in the same cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is a lipid membrane a good wall between compartments?","a":"Because the interior of a cell is watery and most of the molecules it depends on are water-soluble. A nonpolar lipid barrier does not dissolve in water and does not let those molecules diffuse straight through it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Do bacteria have organelles?","a":"Some do. Bacterial microcompartments are organelles whose boundary is a selectively permeable shell of proteins rather than a lipid membrane; carboxysomes enclose RuBisCO, and other kinds confine a toxic intermediate while it is processed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cellular compartment"},
    "bio-136": {"questions":["Only 5 to 10 nanometres thick, about a thousandth of the width of a red blood cell, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is all that separates a cell's contents from its surroundings.","In a typical human cell protein makes up about half the mass of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, lipid about 40 percent and carbohydrate the remaining tenth, though the myelin around nerves is mostly lipid."],"abstract":"The <b>cell membrane</b>, also called the plasma membrane, is the boundary that encloses every cell, a thin and potentially fragile layer that is all that stands between the cell's interior and its surroundings.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is a double layer of phospholipids, their heads facing the watery fluid on either side and their fatty-acid tails meeting in the middle, studded with proteins and, in animal cells, cholesterol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> At 5 to 10 nanometres thick it is about a thousandth of the width of a human red blood cell, which measures roughly 8 micrometres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small nonpolar molecules and gases such as oxygen and carbon dioxide cross it quickly and water and ethanol more slowly, while ions, sugars and amino acids pass only through transport proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its outer face carries carbohydrate chains that act as identity markers, letting the immune system tell the body's own cells from foreign ones, along with receptors that pass hormonal signals inward.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> It is not a uniform film of fat, for in a typical human cell protein makes up about half its mass, lipid about 40 percent and carbohydrate the remaining 10, while myelin has only 18 percent protein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Nor is it a static bag, since its lipids and proteins drift sideways within it, and in the eukaryotic cells studied its inner face is lined almost everywhere by a meshwork of actin filaments about 25 nanometres deep.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Its shape follows its work, and in the cells lining the small intestine it folds into microvilli that enlarge the absorbing surface, which the immune reaction of coeliac disease damages.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Its markers can also be turned against it, because HIV docks on CD4, a glycoprotein on the surface of T cells, and the virus's own recognition sites change so fast by mutation that antibodies fail to keep up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even its chemistry is not universal, since archaeal membrane lipids use ether rather than ester linkages and branched chains, and some archaea have a single layer of lipid rather than two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.1 Components and Structure,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-1-components-and-structure. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.4 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-4-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","“Cell Membranes,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/cell-membranes-14052567/. [Open access]","Mike Adams, “Discovering the Lipid Bilayer,” <i>Nature Education</i> 3, no. 9 (2010): 20, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/discovering-the-lipid-bilayer-14225438/. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “3.3 Unique Characteristics of Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-3-unique-characteristics-of-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Akihiro Kusumi et al., “Cholesterol- and Actin-Centered View of the Plasma Membrane: Updating the Singer–Nicolson Fluid Mosaic Model to Commemorate Its 50th Anniversary,” <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i> 34, no. 5 (2023): pl1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10162409/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can oxygen cross the cell membrane easily when sodium ions cannot?","a":"Because small nonpolar molecules and gases slip through the oily interior of the lipid bilayer, while charged ions are held back by it. Ions, sugars and amino acids cross only through specific transport proteins, which lets the cell control how much of each gets in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does the membrane let the immune system recognise the body’s own cells?","a":"Carbohydrate chains attached to membrane proteins and lipids form a sugar coat, the glycocalyx, on the outer surface. Its patterns act as identity markers, so immune cells can tell “self” from “non-self”, which is also why a donated organ can be rejected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Is every cell membrane built from the same kind of lipid?","a":"No. Bacteria and eukaryotes use phospholipids with ester linkages and straight chains, but archaeal phospholipids have ether linkages and branched chains, and some archaeal membranes are a single lipid layer rather than a bilayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cell membrane"},
    "bio-137": {"questions":["Phospholipids dropped into water assemble a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> without any energy input, lining up tail to tail so that their hydrocarbon chains are hidden from the water on both sides.","Flippases spend ATP keeping phosphatidylserine on the inner face of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so the two leaflets of a plasma membrane differ in which lipids they hold and how tightly they pack."],"abstract":"A <b>lipid bilayer</b> is a sheet two molecules thick, formed when lipids with a water-loving head and water-fearing tails line up with their tails facing inward and their heads facing the water on either side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It assembles with no input of energy, because each lipid is two-faced and in water the molecules align of their own accord so that their hydrocarbon tails are kept away from it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a phospholipid the head is a phosphate-containing group on a three-carbon glycerol, the two other carbons carry fatty-acid tails, and a double bond in a tail puts a kink in it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The result is a barrier with a nonpolar core that does not wash away in the watery surroundings and does not let water-soluble molecules diffuse freely out of the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bilayers of this kind form the plasma membranes of bacteria and eukaryotes and the membranes around organelles, although some archaea build their plasma membranes from a single layer of lipid instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> The case for a double layer came in 1925, when lipid extracted from a known number of red blood cells and spread on water covered about twice the area of the cells' surfaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Red blood cells suited the test because they have no nucleus or internal membranes, and two errors in the work, incomplete extraction and an underestimate of cell area, happened to cancel out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Electron microscopes later showed membranes as three layers, two dark lines around a pale core, the pattern produced by a bilayer's two head-group surfaces and the tails between them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The textbook drawing of two identical halves is wrong, because the outer leaflet of a mammalian plasma membrane is more saturated, richer in sphingolipids and more tightly packed, while phosphatidylserine sits almost entirely in the inner one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Enzymes called flippases spend ATP to keep phosphatidylserine on the inside, and when scramblases mix the leaflets the exposed lipid takes part in blood clotting and in the programmed death of cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mike Adams, “Discovering the Lipid Bilayer,” <i>Nature Education</i> 3, no. 9 (2010): 20, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/discovering-the-lipid-bilayer-14225438/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.1 Components and Structure,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-1-components-and-structure. [Open access]","“Cell Membranes,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/cell-membranes-14052567/. [Open access]","J. H. Lorent et al., “Plasma Membranes Are Asymmetric in Lipid Unsaturation, Packing and Protein Shape,” <i>Nature Chemical Biology</i> 16, no. 6 (2020): 644–52, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7246138/. [Open access]","Margherita Caputo et al., “Lipid Asymmetry and Membrane Trafficking: Transbilayer Distribution of Structural Phospholipids as Regulators of Exocytosis and Endocytosis,” <i>Journal of Biological Chemistry</i> 301, no. 8 (2025): 110441, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbc.2025.110441. [Open access]","Carolina Campos Muñiz and Francisco José Fernández Perrino, “Evolution of the Concepts of Architecture and Supramolecular Dynamics of the Plasma Membrane,” <i>Membranes</i> 13, no. 6 (2023): 547, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10305044/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “3.3 Lipids,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “3.3 Unique Characteristics of Prokaryotic Cells,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-3-unique-characteristics-of-prokaryotic-cells. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a lipid bilayer form by itself in water?","a":"Because a membrane lipid has a hydrophilic head and hydrophobic tails. In water the molecules line up of their own accord, heads outward and tails inward, which hides the tails from the water without any energy being spent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were red blood cells used to show that membranes are bilayers?","a":"Because they have no nucleus or other membrane-bound organelles, so all the lipid extracted from them must come from the plasma membrane. Comparing the area that lipid covered with the cells’ total surface gave a ratio of about two.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the two halves of a plasma membrane bilayer different?","a":"Because lipids flip between leaflets only slowly on their own, and ATP-driven flippases actively move phosphatidylserine to the inner side. The outer leaflet ends up richer in sphingolipids and saturated chains and more tightly packed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lipid bilayer"},
    "bio-138": {"questions":["When a mouse cell was fused with a human cell, their labelled surface proteins mixed within 40 minutes at body temperature, as the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of membrane structure predicted.","Actin fences, cholesterol-rich rafts and molecular crowding have all been added to the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, whose original picture of proteins drifting through a uniform lipid sea proved too simple."],"abstract":"The <b>fluid mosaic model</b> describes a cell membrane as a mosaic of phospholipids, cholesterol, proteins and carbohydrates whose parts can flow and change position while the sheet as a whole holds together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It was proposed in 1972 and displaced a 1935 picture of the membrane as a sandwich, a lipid layer between two coats of protein, which had been the first model widely accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The new model built on the way membrane lipids form bilayers of their own accord and set globular integral proteins into the oily core, alongside peripheral proteins held at the surfaces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The fluid half of the name means that lipids and many proteins diffuse sideways within the sheet, which is fluid at body temperature and turns gel-like when cooled.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Kinked, unsaturated fatty-acid tails preserve that fluidity in the cold by holding neighbouring lipids apart, and many organisms, fish among them, adapt to cold by changing the proportion of unsaturated fats in their membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Striking early evidence came from fusing a mouse cell with a human cell after labelling the surface proteins of each with a different fluorescent colour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At first the two sets of proteins stayed in separate halves of the hybrid cell, but after 40 minutes at 37 °C (99 °F) they had completely mixed, and the mixing slowed at lower temperatures.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The model's picture of proteins drifting through a uniform sea of lipid has needed correcting, because real membranes are crowded and their parts are held back by one another, by the cytoskeleton and by the matrix outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Beneath the plasma membrane an actin meshwork lined with rows of anchored proteins divides it into small compartments, so that molecules diffuse within one and only now and then hop into the next.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Small cholesterol-rich domains called rafts are a further proposed refinement, though whether they are true phases in the physical sense, and how best to describe them, is still disputed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.1 Components and Structure,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-1-components-and-structure. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.4 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-4-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Garth L. Nicolson, “Update of the 1972 Singer-Nicolson Fluid-Mosaic Model of Membrane Structure,” <i>Discoveries</i> 1, no. 1 (2013): e3, https://doi.org/10.15190/d.2013.3. [Open access]","Carolina Campos Muñiz and Francisco José Fernández Perrino, “Evolution of the Concepts of Architecture and Supramolecular Dynamics of the Plasma Membrane,” <i>Membranes</i> 13, no. 6 (2023): 547, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10305044/. [Open access]","Akihiro Kusumi et al., “Cholesterol- and Actin-Centered View of the Plasma Membrane: Updating the Singer–Nicolson Fluid Mosaic Model to Commemorate Its 50th Anniversary,” <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i> 34, no. 5 (2023): pl1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10162409/. [Open access]","Luis A. Bagatolli and Ole G. Mouritsen, “Is the Fluid Mosaic (and the Accompanying Raft Hypothesis) a Suitable Model to Describe Fundamental Features of Biological Membranes? What May Be Missing?,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 4 (2013): 457, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2013.00457. [Open access]","“Cell Membranes,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/cell-membranes-14052567/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"What picture of the membrane did the fluid mosaic model replace?","a":"A model of 1935 in which the membrane was a sandwich, a lipid layer coated on both sides by protein, based on the “railroad track” look of early electron micrographs. The 1972 model instead set proteins into the lipid bilayer itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the fused mouse and human cells support the model?","a":"Because proteins that began in separate halves of the hybrid cell had completely mixed after 40 minutes at 37 °C (99 °F). That gave direct evidence that proteins move sideways in the plane of the membrane, and the mixing slowed when the cells were cooler.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do fish living in cold water change the fats in their membranes?","a":"Because cold compresses membranes made largely of straight, saturated tails, making them less fluid and more likely to rupture. Kinked unsaturated tails keep space between neighbouring lipids, so shifting the proportion of them in response to cold helps keep the membrane fluid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fluid mosaic model"},
    "bio-139": {"questions":["About 27 percent of human proteins are helical transmembrane proteins, and because each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> sits where the cell meets its surroundings, most drug targets belong to this group.","A peripheral <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is bound to the surface of the lipid bilayer rather than inserted into it, usually by attaching itself to an integral protein that crosses the membrane."],"abstract":"A <b>membrane protein</b> is a protein that sits in or on a biological membrane, and together such proteins account for roughly half the mass of most cell membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Integral proteins are embedded in the lipid bilayer, their hydrophobic stretches lying among the lipid tails while hydrophilic regions project into the watery fluid on one or both sides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One that crosses the membrane once usually does so with a hydrophobic segment of 20 to 25 amino acids, some complex proteins weave back and forth with up to 12 segments, and others span it as a barrel of beta-sheets.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Peripheral proteins are not inserted into the bilayer but bound to its surfaces, usually by attachment to integral proteins, and some form a filament network just beneath the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between them they serve as channels and pumps, receptors, enzymes, recognition markers and anchors for the cytoskeleton, and some on intestinal cells digest nutrients before absorption.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A survey of the human genome estimated that 27 percent of human proteins are helical transmembrane proteins, and it identified 6,718 of them and sorted most into families.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The largest classified groups were receptors, transporters and enzymes, and shape tracks function: 77 percent of transporters cross the membrane more than six times, while most receptors outside one huge family cross it only once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Because they sit where the cell meets its surroundings, membrane proteins are key points in hormonal and nerve signalling, and the majority of drug targets are membrane proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> They do not drift wherever they like, for many are tethered to proteins outside the cell, to the cytoskeleton inside it or to both, and rows anchored to the actin meshwork act as pickets that fence in their neighbours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Even their shapes record the bilayer around them, since the stretches that cross a plasma membrane are lopsided in a way that matches the different packing of its two leaflets, a pattern found across the eukaryotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.1 Components and Structure,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-1-components-and-structure. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","“Cell Membranes,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/cell-membranes-14052567/. [Open access]","Markus Sällman Almén, Karl J. V. Nordström, Robert Fredriksson, and Helgi B. Schiöth, “Mapping the Human Membrane Proteome: A Majority of the Human Membrane Proteins Can Be Classified According to Function and Evolutionary Origin,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 7 (2009): 50, https://doi.org/10.1186/1741-7007-7-50. [Open access]","Akihiro Kusumi et al., “Cholesterol- and Actin-Centered View of the Plasma Membrane: Updating the Singer–Nicolson Fluid Mosaic Model to Commemorate Its 50th Anniversary,” <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i> 34, no. 5 (2023): pl1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10162409/. [Open access]","J. H. Lorent et al., “Plasma Membranes Are Asymmetric in Lipid Unsaturation, Packing and Protein Shape,” <i>Nature Chemical Biology</i> 16, no. 6 (2020): 644–52, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7246138/. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.4 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-4-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How can a protein sit in the oily middle of a membrane?","a":"Because its membrane-spanning stretches are hydrophobic and lie comfortably among the lipid tails, while its hydrophilic parts stick out into the watery fluid on either side. A single crossing typically takes a hydrophobic segment of 20 to 25 amino acids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are so many drugs aimed at membrane proteins?","a":"Because membrane proteins form key nodes in the cell’s dealings with its surroundings, including hormonal and nerve signalling. Receptors, transporters and enzymes in the membrane are reachable from outside the cell, and the majority of drug targets are membrane proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What separates an integral protein from a peripheral one?","a":"An integral protein is embedded in the bilayer, with hydrophobic regions among the lipid tails. A peripheral protein is not inserted into the bilayer at all but is attached to its surface, usually to an integral protein, sometimes to lipids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Membrane protein"},
    "bio-140": {"questions":["Built from four fused carbon rings and made in the liver, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is the starting material for steroid hormones and bile salts as well as a major membrane lipid.","Bacteria have no <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in their membranes, and many use hopanoids instead, ring-shaped lipids made from the same precursor without the oxygen that building a sterol requires."],"abstract":"<b>Cholesterol</b> is a steroid, a lipid built from four fused carbon rings with a short tail, and it is the most common steroid in the body.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The liver makes it, and it is the starting material for steroid hormones such as testosterone and oestradiol and for the bile salts that help emulsify fats in the gut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In a membrane it lies alongside the phospholipids in the hydrophobic core, and it is the only sterol and the single most abundant lipid in animal cell membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup> Estimates of its share run from about a fifth of the lipid in an animal plasma membrane to 35 to 45 percent of all the lipid molecules there.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is absent from bacterial membranes and from those of mitochondria, while plants make related phytosterols and fungi make ergosterol.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Introductory accounts often say simply that cholesterol adds to a membrane's fluidity, but its real effect is to buffer it, keeping it from stiffening in the cold and from growing too fluid in the heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At the molecular scale it packs neighbouring lipids more tightly, condensing a fluid bilayer into a more ordered state, and raising it to half the lipid roughly triples the bending stiffness of one common unsaturated lipid membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> With sphingomyelin it helps gather lipids and proteins into ordered domains called lipid rafts, although how such domains behave in living membranes is still being worked out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"9\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Making a sterol takes several steps that need oxygen, whereas many prokaryotes use hopanoids, sterol-like lipids closed into rings from the same precursor in one oxygen-free step, and a yeast that lives without oxygen borrowed the bacterial enzyme to make them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Its bad name in everyday speech is misleading, because the body cannot work without it, and what is linked to plaque in the arteries is a high level of low-density lipoprotein, one of the particles in which cholesterol moves through the blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.1 Components and Structure,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-1-components-and-structure. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “3.3 Lipids,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids. [Open access]","“Cell Membranes,” in <i>Essentials of Cell Biology</i>, Scitable (Cambridge, MA: Nature Education, 2014), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/cell-membranes-14052567/. [Open access]","Fathima T. Doole, Teshani Kumarage, Rana Ashkar, and Michael F. Brown, “Cholesterol Stiffening of Lipid Membranes,” <i>Journal of Membrane Biology</i> 255, no. 4–5 (2022): 385–405, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00232-022-00263-9. [Open access]","Akihiro Kusumi et al., “Cholesterol- and Actin-Centered View of the Plasma Membrane: Updating the Singer–Nicolson Fluid Mosaic Model to Commemorate Its 50th Anniversary,” <i>Molecular Biology of the Cell</i> 34, no. 5 (2023): pl1, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10162409/. [Open access]","Bhagyashree Dasari Rao et al., “Horizontal Acquisition of Prokaryotic Hopanoid Biosynthesis Reorganizes Membrane Physiology Driving Lifestyle Innovation in a Eukaryote,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 3291, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11976957/. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “24.3 Lipid Metabolism,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/24-3-lipid-metabolism. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Garth L. Nicolson, “Update of the 1972 Singer-Nicolson Fluid-Mosaic Model of Membrane Structure,” <i>Discoveries</i> 1, no. 1 (2013): e3, https://doi.org/10.15190/d.2013.3. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Does cholesterol make a membrane more fluid or less?","a":"Both, depending on temperature. It stops low temperatures from making the membrane rigid and stops high temperatures from making it too fluid, which widens the range in which the membrane works. At the molecular level it packs lipids more tightly and stiffens the bilayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"If cholesterol is necessary, why does it have such a bad reputation?","a":"Because blood levels of low-density lipoprotein, one of the particles that carry cholesterol and fats through the blood, are linked to plaque in the arteries. Cholesterol itself is needed for membranes, steroid hormones and bile salts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"How do bacteria manage without cholesterol?","a":"Many use hopanoids, which are made from the same squalene precursor as sterols but in a single step that needs no oxygen. They act as sterol surrogates, regulating membrane properties under harsh conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cholesterol"},
    "bio-141": {"questions":["Pushing on seawater harder than its osmotic pressure drives fresh water out through a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that holds back the salt, the principle of reverse-osmosis desalination plants.","Oxygen slips through a cell's <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> almost freely while sodium ions barely cross it unaided, a spread of permeabilities across the bare bilayer of nearly ten billion times."],"abstract":"A <b>semipermeable membrane</b> is any material that lets some molecules or ions through and holds others back, sorting them by size, shape, polarity or charge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Examples range from the membranes around living cells to the dialysis tubing used to remove metabolic wastes from blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Some work as sieves, with pores small enough to pass only small molecules, while in others a molecule dissolves into the membrane material and diffuses across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because cell membranes are so thin, typically 6.5 to 10 nanometres across, diffusion through them can be fast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When pure water and a solution are separated by a membrane that passes only the water, water flows into the solution, and pressing on the solution harder than its osmotic pressure reverses the flow, which is how reverse osmosis desalinates seawater.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> In that idealised experiment only the solvent crosses, but biology textbooks use the names semipermeable and selectively permeable for the cell membrane interchangeably, and it is a far more discriminating barrier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Small uncharged molecules such as oxygen and carbon dioxide slip straight through its oily core, while ions such as sodium, potassium and chloride, and polar molecules such as sugars, need proteins to carry them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Measured permeabilities of a bare lipid bilayer span a range of nearly ten billion times, with dissolved gases at the fast end and charged ions at the slow end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A rule of thumb known as the Overton rule holds that the more readily a compound dissolves in oil rather than water, the faster it crosses, which fits the easy entry of the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K and of fat-soluble drugs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The membranes inside the body, from the pleura to the cell surface, are permeable to water and to some solutes, so the fluids on either side tend to even out by moving solutes, water or both.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Paul Flowers, Klaus Theopold, Richard Langley, and William R. Robinson, “11.4 Colligative Properties,” in <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2019), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties. [Open access]","Paul Peter Urone and Roger Hinrichs, “12.7 Molecular Transport Phenomena: Diffusion, Osmosis, and Related Processes,” in <i>College Physics 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/12-7-molecular-transport-phenomena-diffusion-osmosis-and-related-processes. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.2 Passive Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport. [Open access]","Ron Milo and Rob Phillips, “What Is the Permeability of the Cell Membrane?,” in <i>Cell Biology by the Numbers</i> (New York: Garland Science), http://book.bionumbers.org/what-is-the-permeability-of-the-cell-membrane/. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.5 Passive Transport,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-5-passive-transport. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “41.1 Osmoregulation and Osmotic Balance,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/41-1-osmoregulation-and-osmotic-balance. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do oxygen and carbon dioxide cross a cell membrane so easily?","a":"Because they are small and carry no charge, so they pass through the oily interior of the lipid bilayer by simple diffusion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Ions are held back by their charge and polar molecules by the hydrophobic core, so they need channel or carrier proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can a membrane be used to make drinking water from seawater?","a":"By reverse osmosis: a membrane that passes water but not salt separates the seawater from fresh water, and pushing on the seawater with more than its osmotic pressure drives water out of it through the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Between pure water and seawater that osmotic pressure is about 25.9 atmospheres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are fat-soluble vitamins absorbed so readily?","a":"Because the more a compound prefers an oily solvent to water, the faster it crosses a lipid bilayer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble, so they pass readily through the plasma membranes of the digestive tract and other tissues.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Semipermeable membrane"},
    "bio-142": {"questions":["Because the time needed for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> grows with the square of the distance, a protein that crosses a bacterium in a hundredth of a second would need weeks to cover a centimetre.","Oxygen passes from the air in the lungs into the blood by simple <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, moving down its partial pressure gradient without the body spending any energy to push it."],"abstract":"<b>Diffusion</b> is the net spreading of a substance from where it is more concentrated to where it is less, driven by nothing more than the random thermal motion of its molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> No molecule senses the gradient; each moves at random, but a crowded region has more molecules to leave it than an emptier one has to send back, so the net flow runs from high to low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Once the concentration has evened out the molecules keep moving, but as many cross one way as the other, a state called dynamic equilibrium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Across a cell membrane the same process carries oxygen in and carbon dioxide out, and these gases cross the lipid bilayer so readily that the still layer of water hugging the membrane slows them more than the membrane itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The rate rises with a steeper gradient and a higher temperature, and falls with heavier molecules, a denser medium and a longer distance to cover.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The fact most often left out is that the distance a molecule diffuses grows only with the square root of the time, so covering ten times the distance takes about a hundred times as long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A typical protein crosses a bacterium one micrometre wide in about a hundredth of a second, and a human cell twenty micrometres wide in about 10 seconds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A glucose molecule needs about 21 hours, on average, to diffuse a single centimetre through water, which is why sugar is stirred into a drink rather than left to spread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By diffusion alone a molecule would take about 2 weeks to travel the length of a nerve axon one centimetre long, so long cells need other means of transport, such as motor proteins that cover it in 2 to 3 hours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the lungs oxygen and carbon dioxide cross between air and blood by simple diffusion down their partial pressure gradients, and in larger animals a circulation carries material over distances where diffusion alone would be far too slow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Paul Peter Urone and Roger Hinrichs, “12.7 Molecular Transport Phenomena: Diffusion, Osmosis, and Related Processes,” in <i>College Physics 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/12-7-molecular-transport-phenomena-diffusion-osmosis-and-related-processes. [Open access]","Ron Milo and Rob Phillips, “What Are the Time Scales for Diffusion in Cells?,” in <i>Cell Biology by the Numbers</i> (New York: Garland Science), http://book.bionumbers.org/what-are-the-time-scales-for-diffusion-in-cells/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.2 Passive Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport. [Open access]","Paul Flowers, Klaus Theopold, Richard Langley, and William R. Robinson, “9.4 Effusion and Diffusion of Gases,” in <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2019), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/9-4-effusion-and-diffusion-of-gases. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.5 Passive Transport,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-5-passive-transport. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “22.4 Gas Exchange,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/22-4-gas-exchange. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Ron Milo and Rob Phillips, “What Is the Permeability of the Cell Membrane?,” in <i>Cell Biology by the Numbers</i> (New York: Garland Science), http://book.bionumbers.org/what-is-the-permeability-of-the-cell-membrane/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does diffusion run from high to low concentration if molecules move at random?","a":"Because a crowded region simply has more molecules to wander out of it than an emptier region has to wander in, so the net flow runs downhill although no molecule senses the gradient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Once the concentrations match, molecules still move, but as many go each way.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a cell not rely on diffusion over long distances?","a":"Because diffusion time grows with the square of the distance.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A protein crosses a bacterium in about a hundredth of a second, but would need about 2 weeks to diffuse the length of a nerve axon one centimetre long.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does warmth speed diffusion up?","a":"Because molecules move faster at higher temperatures, so they spread more quickly, as perfume does through a warm room.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Diffusion constants rise with temperature for the same reason, while heavier molecules and denser media slow the process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Diffusion"},
    "bio-143": {"questions":["A transport process called <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> levels off at a maximum rate once every carrier protein in the membrane is occupied, so steepening the gradient beyond that point changes nothing.","Insulin speeds the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of glucose into muscle and fat more than tenfold, chiefly by moving GLUT4 transporters from stores inside the cell up to its surface."],"abstract":"<b>Facilitated diffusion</b> is the movement of a substance across a membrane down its concentration gradient with the help of a membrane protein, and like all diffusion it costs the cell no energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The substances that need it are those the oily interior of the lipid bilayer turns away: charged ions such as sodium, and molecules too large or too polar to slip through, such as glucose and amino acids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The proteins come in two kinds, channels that open a water-filled pore through the membrane and carriers that bind their passenger and change shape to release it on the other side.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Channels are by far the faster, passing tens of millions of molecules a second against a thousand to a million for a carrier.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A current of about one picoampere through a single open ion channel corresponds to roughly ten million ions a second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Facilitated means helped, not powered, and the help changes only the rate: the direction is still set by the gradient, and a carrier will move its substance the other way if the gradient reverses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> What marks it out from simple diffusion is saturation, because a membrane holds a finite number of carriers, and once all are occupied a steeper gradient no longer raises the rate; a saturated sugar transporter typically turns over about a hundred molecules a second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The glucose transporters of the GLUT family usually carry hexose sugars, and they work by alternately opening a cleft to the inside or to the outside of the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In fat, heart and skeletal muscle, insulin raises the maximum rate of glucose uptake as much as tenfold, almost entirely by moving GLUT4 transporters from stores inside the cell to its surface rather than by changing how tightly they bind glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Water crosses many membranes through channels of the same passive kind, the aquaporins, and a single one can pass thousands of millions of water molecules a second while turning away ions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.2 Passive Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Geoffrey D. Holman, “Structure, Function and Regulation of Mammalian Glucose Transporters of the SLC2 Family,” <i>Pflügers Archiv</i> 472, no. 9 (2020): 1155–75, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7462842/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Ron Milo and Rob Phillips, “How Many Ions Pass through an Ion Channel per Second?,” in <i>Cell Biology by the Numbers</i> (New York: Garland Science), http://book.bionumbers.org/how-many-ions-pass-through-an-ion-channel-per-second/. [Open access]","Ron Milo and Rob Phillips, “What Are the Rates of Membrane Transporters?,” in <i>Cell Biology by the Numbers</i> (New York: Garland Science), http://book.bionumbers.org/what-are-the-rates-of-membrane-transporters/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2003: Popular Information,” NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2003/popular-information/. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.5 Passive Transport,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-5-passive-transport. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does facilitated diffusion need no ATP?","a":"Because the substance still moves down its own concentration gradient, so it is a passive process.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The protein only gives it a route around the oily core of the membrane, and a carrier will move it the other way if the gradient reverses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the rate stop rising at high concentrations?","a":"Because a membrane holds only a finite number of carrier proteins, and once all of them are bound to their passenger the rate is at its maximum, so a steeper gradient adds nothing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do channels move substances faster than carriers?","a":"Because a channel opens a continuous water-filled pore, while a carrier must bind each molecule and change shape to release it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Channels pass tens of millions of molecules a second, carriers a thousand to a million.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Facilitated diffusion"},
    "bio-144": {"questions":["Cells given a newly isolated red-cell membrane protein swelled by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in dilute solution while cells without it did not, which identified the first known water channel.","Seawater separated from pure water by a membrane that holds back salt draws water across by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> until its surface stands about 268 metres (880 feet) above the pure water."],"abstract":"<b>Osmosis</b> is the net movement of water across a membrane that lets water through but holds back a dissolved substance, from the side with less solute to the side with more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is a special case of diffusion, and it runs constantly in living things, because cells are surrounded by membranes of this kind.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water keeps crossing until the concentrations on the two sides match or until the pressure building up on the more concentrated side balances the osmotic pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That pressure depends on how many solute particles there are rather than on what they are, which is why salts that split into ions have a bigger effect, gram for gram, than sugars that stay whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It can be large: seawater separated from pure water by a membrane that holds back salt draws water across until its surface stands about 268 metres (880 feet) higher, an osmotic pressure of about 25.9 atmospheres.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Balance does not mean stillness, because at equilibrium water molecules go on crossing in both directions at equal rates and only the net flow stops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water can slip between the lipid tails of a membrane, but in many cells the fast route is through channel proteins, the aquaporins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Such channels were suspected from the middle of the 19th century, yet it was not until 1988 that one was isolated, from red blood cells, and recognised soon afterwards as the long-sought water channel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The decisive test was simple: cells given the protein swelled by osmosis in dilute solution while cells without it did not, and mercury, already known to stop cells taking up water, blocked the protein too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the kidneys, aquaporins return about four-fifths of the water in some 170 litres (45 US gallons) of filtrate a day to the blood, so that only about a litre leaves the body as urine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.2 Passive Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport. [Open access]","Paul Flowers, Klaus Theopold, Richard Langley, and William R. Robinson, “11.4 Colligative Properties,” in <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2019), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties. [Open access]","Paul Peter Urone and Roger Hinrichs, “12.7 Molecular Transport Phenomena: Diffusion, Osmosis, and Related Processes,” in <i>College Physics 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/12-7-molecular-transport-phenomena-diffusion-osmosis-and-related-processes. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2003: Popular Information,” NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2003/popular-information/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2003: Press Release,” 8 October 2003, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2003/press-release/. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.5 Passive Transport,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-5-passive-transport. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “41.1 Osmoregulation and Osmotic Balance,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/41-1-osmoregulation-and-osmotic-balance. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do salts change osmotic pressure more than sugars do, gram for gram?","a":"Because osmotic pressure depends on the number of dissolved particles, not on their size or identity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> A salt such as sodium chloride splits into separate ions in water, so it adds more particles per gram than glucose, which stays whole.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does water not stop crossing once the two sides are balanced?","a":"Because the molecules keep moving at random. At equilibrium water crosses in both directions at equal rates, so only the net flow stops, and a cell in an isotonic fluid still exchanges water with it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the kidneys depend on water channels?","a":"Because they must reclaim nearly all of some 170 litres of filtrate a day. One aquaporin returns about 70 per cent of that water to the blood along the tubule, and a second, controlled by antidiuretic hormone, about another 10 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Osmosis"},
    "bio-145": {"questions":["Dissolving more sugar in a plant cell's cytoplasm lowers its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so water flows in by osmosis and the cell's turgor pressure rises against its stiff wall.","Pure water at atmospheric pressure is assigned a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of zero by convention, so the sap of a living plant cell, rich in dissolved solutes, sits at a negative value."],"abstract":"<b>Water potential</b> is a measure of the potential energy of water, expressed relative to pure water at atmospheric pressure, which is given the value zero by convention.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is written with the Greek letter psi and measured in units of pressure, megapascals, and water always moves from a higher water potential to a lower one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four things set it: dissolved solutes, pressure, gravity, and the binding of water to insoluble materials such as cell walls, which counts most in dry seeds and drought-affected soils.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Solutes always lower it, so the solute potential of a cell's cytoplasm is typically −0.5 to −1.0 megapascals, its size following from the concentration of solute particles and the temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Pressure can push it either way, and the turgor pressure held in by a plant cell wall is typically 0.6 to 0.8 megapascals and can reach 1.5, about six times the pressure in a car tyre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> What makes the measure useful is that water responds only to the total, not to any single component, so it moves from soil to air only while each compartment along the way, root, stem and leaf, sits lower than the one before.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A plant cell that gathers more solute lowers its own water potential, draws water in by osmosis and so raises its turgor pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> When the water potential outside falls below the cell's, water leaves instead, the membrane pulls away from the wall and soft-stemmed plants wilt.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The water in a leaf's xylem is normally under tension, a negative pressure, and the standard way to measure it is to seal a cut leaf in a pressure chamber and raise the pressure until sap just appears at the cut stalk.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The water potential at which leaves lose turgor has become a widely used measure of drought tolerance, a more negative value widening the range over which the leaves keep working.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “30.5 Transport of Water and Solutes in Plants,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/30-5-transport-of-water-and-solutes-in-plants. [Open access]","Paul Flowers, Klaus Theopold, Richard Langley, and William R. Robinson, “11.4 Colligative Properties,” in <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2019), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties. [Open access]","Celia M. Rodriguez-Dominguez et al., “Leaf Water Potential Measurements Using the Pressure Chamber: Synthetic Testing of Assumptions towards Best Practices for Precision and Accuracy,” <i>Plant, Cell & Environment</i> 45, no. 7 (2022): 2037–61, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC9322401/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Jonathan M. Banks and Andrew D. Hirons, “Alternative Methods of Estimating the Water Potential at Turgor Loss Point in <i>Acer</i> Genotypes,” <i>Plant Methods</i> 15 (2019): 34, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC6448274/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Friedrich-Wilhelm Bentrup, “Water Ascent in Trees and Lianas: The Cohesion-Tension Theory Revisited in the Wake of Otto Renner,” <i>Protoplasma</i> 254, no. 2 (2017): 627–33, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5591614/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.2 Passive Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is pure water given a water potential of zero?","a":"By convention, because what matters for water movement is the difference between two systems rather than the absolute energy, so every value is expressed relative to pure water at atmospheric pressure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a wilting plant recover when it is watered?","a":"Because watering raises the water potential outside its cells above that inside, so water moves back in by osmosis and restores the turgor pressure that holds a soft-stemmed plant upright.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"How is the water potential of a leaf measured?","a":"Usually with a pressure chamber: a cut leaf is sealed inside with its stalk poking out, and the pressure is raised until sap appears at the cut surface, balancing the tension the water was under in the plant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Water potential"},
    "bio-146": {"questions":["Because urea crosses cell membranes freely, adding it can raise a fluid's osmolarity without changing its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the property that decides whether cells bathed in it swell or shrink.","Intravenous fluids are matched to blood in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because a large volume of hypotonic fluid makes red blood cells swell and burst, the damage known as haemolysis."],"abstract":"<b>Tonicity</b> describes how a solution changes the volume of a cell bathed in it, by setting the direction in which water crosses the cell membrane by osmosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A hypotonic solution makes a cell take in water and swell, a hypertonic one draws water out so that it shrinks, and an isotonic one leaves its volume unchanged, though water still moves in and out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In living systems the point of reference is always the cytoplasm, so a hypotonic fluid is one with fewer dissolved particles than the cell it surrounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A red blood cell in a strongly hypotonic fluid swells until its membrane bursts, while in a hypertonic one it shrivels, a change called crenation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plant, fungal and bacterial cells resist bursting because a wall limits their swelling, so a plant cell in a hypotonic medium builds turgor pressure instead, while protists without walls expel the excess water with contractile vacuoles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Tonicity often tracks osmolarity, the total concentration of dissolved particles, but the two are not the same thing, because only particles that the membrane holds back can draw water across it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Urea is the standard example, since it passes so freely across plasma membranes that it has no osmotic effect on cells, and American diabetes guidelines leave it out when they calculate serum osmolality for that reason.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Glucose shows the gap from the other side: a 0.30-molar glucose solution for intravenous use has an osmotic pressure close to the 7.7 atmospheres of body fluids, yet fluid-therapy reviews class 5 per cent glucose in water as a hypotonic fluid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Getting this right matters at the bedside, because a large volume of hypotonic fluid makes red cells swell and burst, and hypotonic fluids are avoided in patients at risk of swelling of the brain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Human body fluids are normally held between about 280 and 300 milliosmoles, and the kidneys in particular work to keep the body's cells bathed in an isotonic fluid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.2 Passive Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport. [Open access]","Paul Flowers, Klaus Theopold, Richard Langley, and William R. Robinson, “11.4 Colligative Properties,” in <i>Chemistry 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2019), https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties. [Open access]","Ketan K. Dhatariya and Priyathama Vellanki, “Treatment of Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA)/Hyperglycemic Hyperosmolar State (HHS): Novel Advances in the Management of Hyperglycemic Crises (UK Versus USA),” <i>Current Diabetes Reports</i> 17, no. 5 (2017): 33, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5375966/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Annemieke Smorenberg, Can Ince, and AB Johan Groeneveld, “Dose and Type of Crystalloid Fluid Therapy in Adult Hospitalized Patients,” <i>Perioperative Medicine</i> 2 (2013): 17, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3964340/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “41.1 Osmoregulation and Osmotic Balance,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/41-1-osmoregulation-and-osmotic-balance. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does urea not count towards a fluid's tonicity?","a":"Because it passes freely across plasma membranes and so has no osmotic effect on cells, which is why American guidelines calculate serum osmolality without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do plant cells not burst in fresh water?","a":"Because the cell wall limits how far the membrane can expand, so the water flowing in builds turgor pressure instead of rupturing the cell, and that pressure stiffens the walls and supports soft-stemmed plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are hypotonic drips avoided after a head injury?","a":"Because hypotonic fluids can lower the osmolality of the blood and worsen swelling of the brain, so reviews of fluid therapy treat them as contraindicated where brain swelling threatens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isotonic fluids such as normal saline are preferred instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Tonicity"},
    "bio-147": {"questions":["Metabolic poisons that cut a cell's supply of ATP soon halt <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the pumping that holds ions and molecules away from equilibrium against the steady leak across the membrane.","At rest about a third of all the ATP a person makes is spent on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by a single membrane pump that moves three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions in."],"abstract":"<b>Active transport</b> is the movement of a substance across a membrane against its concentration or electrochemical gradient, which cannot happen without an input of energy, usually from ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The proteins that do it are often called pumps, and they hold the concentrations a cell needs in the face of the constant passive movement of ions and molecules across its membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In primary active transport the pump itself splits ATP, as the sodium-potassium pump of animal cells does when it moves three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A whole family of such pumps, including the calcium pump of skeletal muscle and the hydrogen-potassium pump that makes stomach acid, is phosphorylated during each cycle, and its members are grouped as P-type ATPases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In secondary active transport no ATP is split at the carrier itself; one substance is moved uphill by letting another, often sodium, run downhill through the same protein, so the energy comes at one remove from a pump elsewhere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The gradients pumps build can be steep: calcium ions are kept about ten thousand times more concentrated outside a cell than inside, a difference usually held either by splitting ATP or by coupling to the inflow of three sodium ions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Holding them is costly, and at rest the sodium-potassium pump alone uses about a third of all the ATP a person makes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In the grey matter of the brain, one estimate puts housekeeping work such as building proteins at only a quarter of the energy used, with the rest consumed by sodium-potassium pumps.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Plant cells have no sodium-potassium pump and energise their membranes with a proton gradient instead, which is why the plant poisons that block the animal pump do not harm the plants that make them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Because the energy comes from metabolism, active transport fails when the ATP supply fails, so it is sensitive to the many metabolic poisons that interfere with making ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.3 Active Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-3-active-transport. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.6 Active Transport,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-6-active-transport. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1997: Press Release,” 15 October 1997, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1997/press-release/. [Open access]","Ron Milo and Rob Phillips, “What Is the Permeability of the Cell Membrane?,” in <i>Cell Biology by the Numbers</i> (New York: Garland Science), http://book.bionumbers.org/what-is-the-permeability-of-the-cell-membrane/. [Open access]","Michael V. Clausen, Florian Hilbers, and Hanne Poulsen, “The Structure and Function of the Na,K-ATPase Isoforms in Health and Disease,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 8 (2017): 371, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5459889/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Kirk L. Hamilton, “Robert K. Crane—Na+-Glucose Cotransporter to Cure?,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 4 (2013): 53, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3605518/fullTextXML. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does moving a substance against its gradient need energy?","a":"Because a substance only moves down its gradient on its own, so carrying it the other way takes work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Pumps draw that energy mainly from ATP made by the cell's metabolism, which is why poisons that stop ATP production stop them too.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"What separates primary from secondary active transport?","a":"In primary active transport the pump splits ATP itself, as the sodium-potassium pump does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In secondary active transport a carrier spends a gradient a pump has already built, letting sodium run back in to drive another substance uphill, as in the uptake of glucose by gut cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are the plant poisons that block the sodium pump harmless to the plants?","a":"Because plant cells have no sodium-potassium pump for the poisons to block; they energise their membranes with a proton gradient instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Active transport"},
    "bio-148": {"questions":["Moving three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions in, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> consumes about a third of all the ATP a resting human body makes.","Digitalis and similar plant poisons block the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in animal cells, and animals that feed on such plants carry mutations that leave this transporter insensitive to them."],"abstract":"The <b>sodium-potassium pump</b> is an enzyme in the plasma membrane of animal cells that splits ATP and uses the energy to move three sodium ions out of the cell and two potassium ions in.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It keeps the inside of a cell rich in potassium and poor in sodium, the reverse of the fluid outside; in a typical neuron sodium is about twelve times more concentrated outside and potassium nearly forty times more concentrated inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Each cycle begins with three sodium ions and ATP binding inside; a phosphate passes to the protein, which changes shape, releases the sodium outside, binds two potassium ions and carries them in once the phosphate is removed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It was found in 1957 in finely ground crab nerve membranes, as an ATP-splitting enzyme stimulated by sodium and potassium together, the first enzyme shown to drive directed transport across a membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That discovery won the 1997 Nobel Prize in Chemistry, and many similar ion pumps have been found since.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> A common textbook account credits the pump's unequal exchange with making the inside of the cell negative, but the pump is only weakly electrogenic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Most of the resting voltage comes instead from potassium leaking out through a membrane far more permeable to potassium than to sodium, leaving negative charges behind; the pump keeps up the gradients that make the leak possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Those gradients drive much else, since sodium flowing back in powers the uptake of glucose and amino acids and the export of calcium, and if the pump stops the cell swells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The cost is large: at rest the pump uses about a third of all the ATP a person makes, and in the grey matter of the brain an estimated three-quarters of the energy budget.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cardiotonic steroids from at least twelve plant families and some toads block it, digitalis among them is still given in heart failure, and animals that make or eat them carry mutations that leave their own pumps insensitive.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.3 Active Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-3-active-transport. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1997: Press Release,” 15 October 1997, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1997/press-release/. [Open access]","Michael V. Clausen, Florian Hilbers, and Hanne Poulsen, “The Structure and Function of the Na,K-ATPase Isoforms in Health and Disease,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 8 (2017): 371, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5459889/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Kirk L. Hamilton, “Robert K. Crane—Na+-Glucose Cotransporter to Cure?,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 4 (2013): 53, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3605518/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “35.2 How Neurons Communicate,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/35-2-how-neurons-communicate. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Ron Milo and Rob Phillips, “What Are the Concentrations of Different Ions in Cells?,” in <i>Cell Biology by the Numbers</i> (New York: Garland Science), http://book.bionumbers.org/what-are-the-concentrations-of-different-ions-in-cells/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does lack of oxygen to the brain cause unconsciousness so quickly?","a":"Because without oxygen ATP formation stops, the pump stops with it, and the voltage across nerve-cell membranes that nerve signals depend on cannot be kept up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Is the pump what makes the inside of a cell negative?","a":"Only in small part. The pump is weakly electrogenic, but most of the resting voltage comes from potassium leaking out through channels that let it pass far more easily than sodium, leaving negative charges behind inside the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"How do other transporters use the pump's work?","a":"Sodium kept low inside the cell runs back in through other carriers, and that inflow powers the uptake of glucose and amino acids and the exchange of sodium for calcium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the gut, for example, the sodium-glucose cotransporter relies on the gradient the pump maintains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Sodium–potassium pump"},
    "bio-149": {"questions":["A carrier that lets sodium run down its gradient into the cell while dragging glucose in against its own is carrying out <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, a form of secondary active transport.","First proposed at a symposium in Prague in 1960 to explain how the gut absorbs glucose, the idea of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> coupled an ion's flow to a nutrient's through one carrier."],"abstract":"<b>Co-transport</b> is the movement of two different substances across a membrane by the same carrier protein, one of them running down its gradient and paying for the other to be moved, often against its own gradient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is also called secondary active transport, because the carrier splits no ATP itself and relies instead on a gradient that a primary pump has already built.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the two substances cross in the same direction the carrier is a symporter, and when they cross in opposite directions it is an antiporter.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In animal cells the downhill partner is usually sodium, kept low inside by the sodium-potassium pump, while many other transporters, including those that carry sucrose, are coupled to hydrogen ions instead.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The sodium-hydrogen antiporter, for example, uses sodium flowing in to push hydrogen ions out and so helps hold the pH of the cell's interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The idea that the flows of an ion and a nutrient could be coupled through one carrier was first put forward at a symposium in Prague in 1960, to explain how the small intestine absorbs glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the cells lining the gut a sodium-glucose co-transporter, SGLT1, carries glucose in from the gut with sodium, the glucose leaves the far side of the cell through a GLUT2 transporter, and water follows the absorbed salt and sugar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The protein was cloned in 1987, 27 years after it was predicted, and mice bred without it absorb glucose and galactose poorly, though they thrive on a diet free of those sugars.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The same physiology explains oral rehydration therapy, a drink of water, salt and sugar that keeps the gut absorbing water during cholera and other severe diarrhoea, and that has sharply cut deaths from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In 1978 <i>The Lancet</i> called the finding that glucose speeds the absorption of salt and water potentially the most important medical advance of the century, though one history of the treatment argues that empirical clinical work counted for more than the tidy laboratory-to-bedside story suggests.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.3 Active Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-3-active-transport. [Open access]","Kirk L. Hamilton, “Robert K. Crane—Na+-Glucose Cotransporter to Cure?,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 4 (2013): 53, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3605518/fullTextXML. [Open access]","David R. Nalin, “The History of Intravenous and Oral Rehydration and Maintenance Therapy of Cholera and Non-Cholera Dehydrating Diarrheas: A Deconstruction of Translational Medicine: From Bench to Bedside?,” <i>Tropical Medicine and Infectious Disease</i> 7, no. 3 (2022): 50, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC8949912/fullTextXML. [Open access]","V. Gorboulev et al., “Na+-D-Glucose Cotransporter SGLT1 Is Pivotal for Intestinal Glucose Absorption and Glucose-Dependent Incretin Secretion,” <i>Diabetes</i> 61, no. 1 (2012): 187–96, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3237647/fullTextXML. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Ron Milo and Rob Phillips, “What Are the Rates of Membrane Transporters?,” in <i>Cell Biology by the Numbers</i> (New York: Garland Science), http://book.bionumbers.org/what-are-the-rates-of-membrane-transporters/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is co-transport called secondary active transport?","a":"Because the carrier itself splits no ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It spends a gradient, usually of sodium, that a primary pump such as the sodium-potassium pump has built with ATP, so the energy is used at one remove.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does an oral rehydration drink contain sugar as well as salt?","a":"Because the sodium-glucose co-transporter in the gut lining takes sodium and glucose in together, and water follows the absorbed salt and sugar, so the drink keeps the body absorbing water during severe diarrhoea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How was the predicted sodium-glucose carrier confirmed?","a":"The protein, SGLT1, was cloned in 1987, and the crystal structure of a related bacterial sodium-galactose carrier later added support.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mice bred without SGLT1 develop a glucose-galactose malabsorption syndrome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}]},
    "bio-150": {"questions":["A neutrophil engulfing a bacterium and a cell gulping droplets of the surrounding fluid are both carrying out <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, folding the plasma membrane inwards and pinching it off as a vesicle.","Many viruses and bacterial toxins get inside a cell by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, binding receptors on its surface and being drawn in through the same coated pits that bring in nutrients."],"abstract":"<b>Endocytosis</b> is the uptake of material by a cell that folds part of its plasma membrane inwards around it and pinches the pocket off as a vesicle inside the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It brings in what is too large to cross the membrane by any other route, from proteins and fluid to whole bacteria, and because it takes energy it is counted as a form of active transport.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In phagocytosis, ‘cell eating’, a cell such as a neutrophil engulfs a large particle like an invading microorganism and destroys it, often by fusing the vesicle with a lysosome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In pinocytosis, ‘cell drinking’, the cell takes in fluid together with whatever is dissolved in it, with little selection among its contents.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Receptor-mediated endocytosis is the selective form, in which receptors gather their cargo into pits coated on their inner face with the protein clathrin, which bud off as coated vesicles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Clathrin cannot grip the membrane on its own, so adaptor proteins link the coat both to the membrane and to the cargo receptors, and the order in which the proteins assemble is strikingly regular.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The best-known cargo is cholesterol: particles of low-density lipoprotein bind receptors in coated pits, are carried in and delivered to lysosomes, while the receptors are recycled to the cell surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In familial hypercholesterolaemia the receptors are missing or faulty, so the particles stay in the blood, and the discovery of the receptor and its route won the 1985 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The same entrance is used by intruders, since clathrin-mediated uptake also carries certain toxins, viruses and bacteria into cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Macropinocytosis, the gulping of micrometre-sized droplets, works differently, with large cups of membrane pushed out by the actin cytoskeleton, closely related to the cups of phagocytosis, and it occurs in animals and amoebae but apparently not in plants or fungi.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.4 Bulk Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-4-bulk-transport. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","Nobel Assembly at the Karolinska Institute, “The Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 1985: Press Release,” NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1985/press-release/. [Open access]","Camilla Godlee and Marko Kaksonen, “From Uncertain Beginnings: Initiation Mechanisms of Clathrin-Mediated Endocytosis,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 203, no. 5 (2013): 717–25, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3857488/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Jason S. King and Robert R. Kay, “The Origins and Evolution of Macropinocytosis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 374, no. 1765 (2019): 20180158, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC6304743/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Marta Miaczynska and Harald Stenmark, “Mechanisms and Functions of Endocytosis,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 180, no. 1 (2008): 7–11, full text in Europe PMC, https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2213624/fullTextXML. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.6 Active Transport,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-6-active-transport. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does familial hypercholesterolaemia raise blood cholesterol?","a":"Because the receptors that should take cholesterol-carrying LDL particles into cells by endocytosis are missing or faulty, so the particles are not cleared and stay in the blood.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is endocytosis counted as active transport?","a":"Because large particles cannot pass through the membrane even with energy supplied, so the cell must fold its membrane around them and pinch it off, and that work takes energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Textbooks therefore class endocytosis among the active processes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does clathrin need adaptor proteins?","a":"Because clathrin cannot bind the lipids or proteins of the plasma membrane directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Adaptors link the coat to the membrane and also bind the cargo, so the cargo is gathered into the forming vesicle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Endocytosis"},
    "bio-151": {"questions":["When calcium floods into a nerve ending, transmitter-filled vesicles fuse with its membrane, and this calcium-triggered <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> releases the transmitter into the narrow gap between two cells.","Four helices from vesicle and target proteins zip together and pull two membranes into contact, the molecular step at the heart of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, by which cells release hormones and enzymes."],"abstract":"<b>Exocytosis</b> is the export of material from a cell inside a membrane-bound vesicle that fuses with the plasma membrane and opens its contents to the outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Proteins bound for export are made on the rough endoplasmic reticulum and travel onwards in transport vesicles, which is why cells that secrete a great deal of protein, such as liver cells, are rich in that membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When the vesicle fuses, its contents pass into the fluid around the cell and its membrane becomes part of the cell membrane, so the event that releases a cargo also adds to the surface of the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pancreatic cells release digestive enzymes this way, endocrine cells release hormones, certain immune cells release histamine, and other cells export the proteins of the extracellular matrix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Like its reverse, endocytosis, it moves material too large to pass through the membrane itself, and it costs the cell energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The familiar picture of a cell expelling its waste is only one case, since much exocytosis delivers products the body needs, from milk proteins to the transmitters that pass a signal between nerve cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Fusion is driven by SNARE proteins, one anchored in the vesicle and two in the target membrane, which wind together into a bundle of four helices that draws the two bilayers close enough to merge.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some secretion is constitutive and needs no trigger, while regulated secretion holds filled vesicles back until calcium rises inside the cell, and lactating mammary cells were found to release the same milk proteins by both routes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At a synapse the arriving impulse opens voltage-gated calcium channels, the incoming calcium promotes fusion of the transmitter vesicles, and the transmitter crosses the gap to the next cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fusion is not always total, for many vesicles open only partly and close again, and a wounded cell reseals a tear in its surface within about half a minute by calcium-triggered exocytosis of its lysosomes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.4 Bulk Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-4-bulk-transport. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.4 The Endomembrane System and Proteins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “3.1 The Cell Membrane,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “12.5 Communication Between Neurons,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/12-5-communication-between-neurons. [Open access]","Jing Han, Kristyna Pluhackova, and Rainer A. Böckmann, “The Multifaceted Role of SNARE Proteins in Membrane Fusion,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 8 (2017): 5, https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2017.00005. [Open access]","M. D. Turner et al., “Proteins Are Secreted by Both Constitutive and Regulated Secretory Pathways in Lactating Mouse Mammary Epithelial Cells,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 117, no. 2 (1992): 269–78, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.117.2.269. [Open access]","Christina Tam et al., “Exocytosis of Acid Sphingomyelinase by Wounded Cells Promotes Endocytosis and Plasma Membrane Repair,” <i>Journal of Cell Biology</i> 189, no. 6 (2010): 1027–38, https://doi.org/10.1083/jcb.201003053. [Open access]","Lisa J. Mellander, Raphaël Trouillon, Maria I. Svensson, and Andrew G. Ewing, “Amperometric Post Spike Feet Reveal Most Exocytosis Is via Extended Kiss-and-Run Fusion,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 2 (2012): 907, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep00907. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does exocytosis make the surface of a cell bigger?","a":"Because the vesicle membrane does not vanish when it fuses: it becomes part of the plasma membrane, so every release of cargo also adds a patch of membrane to the surface.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What makes a regulated vesicle wait before it fuses?","a":"A rise in calcium inside the cell. In lactating mammary cells a drug that raised calcium doubled protein secretion, while constitutive secretion carried on at any calcium level.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At a nerve ending the calcium comes in through channels opened by the arriving impulse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does exocytosis help a torn cell survive?","a":"Wounded cells reseal their plasma membrane within about half a minute, and the repair depends on calcium from outside and on lysosomes fusing with the membrane near the wound.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> An enzyme the lysosomes release then helps the cell take the damaged patch back inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Exocytosis"},
    "bio-152": {"questions":["When a stimulus lifts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of a nerve cell past a threshold near −55 mV, voltage-gated sodium channels open and an action potential follows.","Many cancer cells carry a less negative <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> than the mature cells around them, a bioelectric difference linked to how readily cells divide and differentiate."],"abstract":"The <b>membrane potential</b> is the voltage across the membrane of a cell, which arises because ions such as sodium, potassium, calcium and chloride are unequally distributed between the cytoplasm and the fluid outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is measured with one electrode inside the cell and one outside, and it is quoted in millivolts for the inside relative to the outside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A typical neuron at rest sits at about −70 mV, although the figure varies with the type of neuron and the species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because ions carry charge, each is pushed by its concentration gradient and by the voltage at the same time, and that combined force is called its electrochemical gradient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The Goldman–Hodgkin–Katz equation predicts the voltage from the concentrations of the main ions on both sides and from how permeable the membrane is to each.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The sodium–potassium pump expels three sodium ions for every two potassium ions it brings in, so each cycle removes one net positive charge, yet saying that the pump creates the resting potential leaves out the step that matters most.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A resting neuron has far more potassium leak channels than sodium ones, so potassium diffuses out faster than sodium leaks in, and that outward leak of positive charge makes the inside negative while the pump maintains the gradients.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In nerve and muscle, a stimulus that lifts the voltage to a threshold near −55 mV opens voltage-gated sodium channels, and the membrane swings to about +30 mV before potassium channels bring it back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Cells that never fire hold a membrane potential too, and terminally differentiated cells tend to be strongly negative while many cancer cells are depolarised, a difference linked to how readily cells divide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plant cells charge their plasma membrane by pumping protons out, and that voltage drives the uptake of potassium, while bacteria and mitochondria store energy as a proton gradient across a membrane and use it to make ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “35.2 How Neurons Communicate,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/35-2-how-neurons-communicate. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “5.3 Active Transport,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-3-active-transport. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “12.4 The Action Potential,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/12-4-the-action-potential. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.3 Cellular Respiration,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-3-cellular-respiration. [Open access]","Ming Yang and William J. Brackenbury, “Membrane Potential and Cancer Progression,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 4 (2013): 185, https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2013.00185. [Open access]","Adrianna Michalak, Anna Wdowikowska, and Małgorzata Janicka, “Plant Plasma Membrane Proton Pump: One Protein with Multiple Functions,” <i>Cells</i> 11, no. 24 (2022): 4052, https://doi.org/10.3390/cells11244052. [Open access]","Ruben G. Contreras, Antonio Torres-Carrillo, Catalina Flores-Maldonado, Liora Shoshani, and Arturo Ponce, “Na+/K+-ATPase: More than an Electrogenic Pump,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 25, no. 11 (2024): 6122, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms25116122. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does potassium, not the pump, set most of the resting voltage?","a":"Because a resting neuron has far more potassium leak channels than sodium ones, so potassium diffuses out faster than sodium leaks in and more positive charge leaves than enters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pump moves one net charge per cycle and keeps the gradients in place, which is why it matters even though the leak does the immediate work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is an action potential described as all or none?","a":"Because once the membrane reaches threshold, near −55 mV, enough voltage-gated sodium channels open for the change to run all the way to about +30 mV. A stronger stimulus does not make a bigger action potential, and one that falls short of threshold produces none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Do cells other than neurons use their membrane potential?","a":"Yes. Cells that never fire use the voltage as a long-term signal linked to division and differentiation, and many cancer cells are depolarised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Plant cells pump protons out to charge their membrane and use the voltage to take up potassium.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Membrane potential"},
    "bio-153": {"questions":["Bacteria practise a kind of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> called quorum sensing, releasing a molecule that accumulates as their numbers grow until the whole population switches its genes together.","A mutation that locks a growth switch permanently on corrupts the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that tells a cell when to divide, and such failures lie behind many cancers."],"abstract":"<b>Cell signalling</b> is the communication by which cells detect and respond to chemical messages, usually a molecule called a ligand that binds a specific receptor protein on or inside a target cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In multicellular organisms the signals are grouped by the distance they travel: paracrine signals act on nearby cells, endocrine signals are hormones carried in the blood, autocrine signals act on the cell that released them, and some pass directly between neighbours through gap junctions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Water-soluble signals cannot cross the lipid bilayer and act through receptors on the cell surface, while steroid hormones, made from cholesterol, diffuse through the membrane to receptors inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A synapse is a specialised case in which a neuron releases a transmitter across a gap of a few tens of nanometres to receptors on the next cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The response may be a change in gene expression, in metabolism, in growth or division, or even the controlled death of the cell, which is called apoptosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Signalling is not confined to multicellular life, for bacteria use quorum sensing, releasing a signal that builds up as their numbers rise until, past a threshold concentration, the whole population changes its gene expression together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It was first described in the glowing marine bacterium <i>Vibrio fischeri</i>, in which a signal of the acyl-homoserine lactone family controls light production once the culture is dense enough.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The same signal can mean different things to different cells, because the response depends on the cell and its conditions, and in muscle cells adrenaline sets off the breakdown of glycogen into glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cells do not normally divide unless signals from other cells stimulate them, and many growth factors act through receptor tyrosine kinases, of which humans have 58 in twenty subfamilies.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the signalling machinery itself is mutated, cells can divide unprompted, and the switch protein Ras, changed at one of a few sites, is locked on in many tumours, including about nine in ten pancreatic cancers in one survey.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “9.1 Signaling Molecules and Cellular Receptors,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/9-1-signaling-molecules-and-cellular-receptors. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “9.3 Response to the Signal,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/9-3-response-to-the-signal. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “17.2 Hormones,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/17-2-hormones. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “12.5 Communication Between Neurons,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/12-5-communication-between-neurons. [Open access]","Rhea G. Abisado, Saida Benomar, Jennifer R. Klaus, Ajai A. Dandekar, and Josephine R. Chandler, “Bacterial Quorum Sensing and Microbial Community Interactions,” <i>mBio</i> 9, no. 3 (2018): e02331-17, https://doi.org/10.1128/mBio.02331-17. [Open access]","Mark A. Lemmon and Joseph Schlessinger, “Cell Signaling by Receptor Tyrosine Kinases,” <i>Cell</i> 141, no. 7 (2010): 1117–34, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2010.06.011. [Open access]","Ian A. Prior, Paul D. Lewis, and Carla Mattos, “A Comprehensive Survey of Ras Mutations in Cancer,” <i>Cancer Research</i> 72, no. 10 (2012): 2457–67, https://doi.org/10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-11-2612. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do some hormones need a receptor on the cell surface?","a":"Because water-soluble hormones cannot diffuse through the lipid bilayer, so they bind a receptor at the surface and a second messenger such as cyclic AMP carries the signal inside.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Steroid hormones are derived from cholesterol and pass through the membrane to receptors within the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can one signal cause different responses in different cells?","a":"Because the outcome depends on the type of cell and on its conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In muscle cells adrenaline raises cyclic AMP, which leads to glycogen being broken down to glucose while its synthesis is switched off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How do bacteria tell when there are enough of them?","a":"They release a signal molecule continuously, and it accumulates as the population grows. Once it passes a threshold concentration it binds a receptor protein, and the whole population changes its gene expression together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cell signaling"},
    "bio-154": {"questions":["Kinases adding phosphate groups and second messengers such as cyclic AMP are the links of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the relay that carries a message from a surface receptor into the cell.","A Ras protein that can no longer hydrolyse its GTP stays switched on, so <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> along its pathway never stops and the cell keeps dividing without control."],"abstract":"<b>Signal transduction</b> is the relay by which a signal received at the surface of a cell is passed into it, typically through a chain of proteins that ends in a change in what the cell does.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A ligand binding a surface receptor changes the receptor, and that activates components inside the cell in a series called a signalling pathway or cascade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Surface receptors fall into three broad groups, those linked to ion channels, to G proteins and to enzymes, and every G-protein-linked receptor crosses the membrane seven times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A common link in the chain is phosphorylation, in which an enzyme called a kinase adds a phosphate group to a protein and a phosphatase later removes it, switching the protein on or off.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Small molecules called second messengers, among them calcium ions and cyclic AMP, spread the signal through the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> A cascade is more than a relay, because each activated enzyme can activate many copies of the next component, so one ligand at one receptor can produce a greatly amplified response.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In hormone action this is why thousands of events can start at once in response to a very low concentration of hormone in the blood, though the signal is brief because cyclic AMP is quickly broken down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Pathways also branch and converge, so that several signals can be required before a cell commits to a response.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Humans have 58 receptor tyrosine kinases, which are activated when the ligand brings two receptors together, so that one phosphorylates the other and the phosphorylated receptor becomes a docking site for signalling proteins.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Faults and drugs both act on these relays, for about a third of the drugs approved in the United States act on G-protein-coupled receptors, and mutations that keep the switch protein Ras bound to GTP drive many cancers.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “9.2 Propagation of the Signal,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/9-2-propagation-of-the-signal. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “9.1 Signaling Molecules and Cellular Receptors,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/9-1-signaling-molecules-and-cellular-receptors. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “17.2 Hormones,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/17-2-hormones. [Open access]","Alexander S. Hauser, Misty M. Attwood, Mathias Rask-Andersen, Helgi B. Schiöth, and David E. Gloriam, “Trends in GPCR Drug Discovery: New Agents, Targets and Indications,” <i>Nature Reviews Drug Discovery</i> 16, no. 12 (2017): 829–42, https://doi.org/10.1038/nrd.2017.178. [Open access]","Mark A. Lemmon and Joseph Schlessinger, “Cell Signaling by Receptor Tyrosine Kinases,” <i>Cell</i> 141, no. 7 (2010): 1117–34, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2010.06.011. [Open access]","Ian A. Prior, Paul D. Lewis, and Carla Mattos, “A Comprehensive Survey of Ras Mutations in Cancer,” <i>Cancer Research</i> 72, no. 10 (2012): 2457–67, https://doi.org/10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-11-2612. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a cascade amplify a signal?","a":"Because at the start one ligand binds one receptor, but a receptor-linked enzyme can activate many copies of the next component in the chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In hormone signalling this lets thousands of events begin at once in response to a very low concentration of hormone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What does phosphorylation do to a signalling protein?","a":"A kinase adds a phosphate group to a serine, threonine or tyrosine, and that can switch an enzyme on or off or create a site where the next protein in the pathway binds. A phosphatase removes the phosphate and reverses the effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are G-protein-coupled receptors such common drug targets?","a":"They form the largest family of human membrane proteins, with about 800 members, and a 2017 count found 481 approved drugs, around 34 per cent of all drugs approved in the United States, acting at 107 of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Signal transduction"},
    "bio-155": {"questions":["Penicillin harms bacteria but not their host because its <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the enzyme that cross-links the bacterial cell wall, builds a structure that human cells never make.","A drug that crosses membranes easily but binds many proteins besides its intended <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> tends to cause more side effects than a selective one, however well it is absorbed."],"abstract":"A <b>biological target</b> is the molecule in the body, usually a protein, that a drug binds to produce its effect, and the interaction between the two is the drug's mechanism of action.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A survey published in 2017 counted 893 targets for 1,578 drugs approved in the United States, among them 667 human proteins and 189 proteins of pathogens.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Four families, the G-protein-coupled receptors, ion channels, protein kinases and nuclear hormone receptors, make up 44 per cent of the human targets and account for the effect of about 70 per cent of small-molecule drugs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most receptors do not exist to bind drugs at all: they normally bind the body's own neurotransmitters and hormones, and a drug works by imitating such a signal or by occupying the site and blocking it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The more selectively a drug binds its target, the fewer side effects it tends to cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Much of this action happens at the cell membrane, since G-protein-coupled receptors form the largest family of human membrane proteins and about a third of approved drugs act at one of them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Transporters are targets too, and the antidepressants called selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors block the pump that returns serotonin to the nerve ending, so the transmitter accumulates in the synapse.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cardiac glycosides such as ouabain, plant compounds long known to inhibit it, act on the sodium–potassium pump that every animal cell depends on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A drug must also cross membranes to reach its target, and lipid-soluble drugs cross them quickly and spread widely, while the barrier around the brain keeps out many others, including many antimicrobials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Antibiotics show the logic of a good target most clearly, since penicillin and its relatives block the enzymes that cross-link the bacterial cell wall, a structure that human cells do not make.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Rita Santos et al., “A Comprehensive Map of Molecular Drug Targets,” <i>Nature Reviews Drug Discovery</i> 16, no. 1 (2017): 19–34, https://doi.org/10.1038/nrd.2016.230. [Open access]","Alexander S. Hauser, Misty M. Attwood, Mathias Rask-Andersen, Helgi B. Schiöth, and David E. Gloriam, “Trends in GPCR Drug Discovery: New Agents, Targets and Indications,” <i>Nature Reviews Drug Discovery</i> 16, no. 12 (2017): 829–42, https://doi.org/10.1038/nrd.2017.178. [Open access]","Tina Barbour-Taylor, Leah Mueller (Sabato), Donna Paris, and Dorie Weaver, “2.2 Pharmacokinetics and Pharmacodynamics,” in <i>Pharmacology for Nurses</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2024), https://openstax.org/books/pharmacology/pages/2-2-pharmacokinetics-and-pharmacodynamics. [Open access]","Tina Barbour-Taylor, Leah Mueller (Sabato), Donna Paris, and Dorie Weaver, “13.1 Antidepressants,” in <i>Pharmacology for Nurses</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2024), https://openstax.org/books/pharmacology/pages/13-1-antidepressants. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “14.3 Mechanisms of Antibacterial Drugs,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/14-3-mechanisms-of-antibacterial-drugs. [Open access]","Ruben G. Contreras, Antonio Torres-Carrillo, Catalina Flores-Maldonado, Liora Shoshani, and Arturo Ponce, “Na+/K+-ATPase: More than an Electrogenic Pump,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 25, no. 11 (2024): 6122, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms25116122. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do drugs act on receptors that never evolved to bind them?","a":"Because a drug is a chemical that can fit the same receptor site as one of the body's own neurotransmitters or hormones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An agonist imitates the natural signal and an antagonist occupies the site and blocks it, so one receptor can be pushed either way by medicines.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can some drugs reach the brain and others not?","a":"Because a drug must cross cell membranes to reach its target, and lipid-soluble drugs cross them quickly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The blood–brain barrier admits drugs that are lipid-soluble or poorly bound to blood proteins, such as sedatives, but keeps out many antimicrobials, so they fail against infections of the central nervous system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do so many drugs act on so few protein families?","a":"Because a few families have repeatedly yielded successful drugs: G-protein-coupled receptors, ion channels, kinases and nuclear receptors make up 44 per cent of human drug targets but account for about 70 per cent of small-molecule drugs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> G-protein-coupled receptors alone carry about 34 per cent of approved drugs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Biological target"},
    "bio-156": {"questions":["With an overall equation that reverses photosynthesis, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns glucose and oxygen into carbon dioxide and water while capturing about a third of the energy released as ATP.","The textbook total of 38 ATP per glucose is only a theoretical ceiling for <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, since a proton gradient never delivers a whole-number yield and real cells make fewer."],"abstract":"<b>Cellular respiration</b> is the set of chemical reactions by which cells break down food molecules such as sugars and fats and transfer part of their energy to ATP, the molecule that powers most of their work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its overall equation for glucose is the reverse of photosynthesis, glucose and oxygen giving carbon dioxide and water, but it runs in many steps rather than one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of those steps are oxidation–reduction reactions, in which electrons stripped from the fuel are handed to carriers such as NAD+ and FAD.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the aerobic route, glycolysis splits glucose into pyruvate, a transition reaction and the Krebs cycle oxidise the fragments to carbon dioxide, and an electron transport chain passes the electrons to a final inorganic acceptor, which is oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Glycolysis runs in the cytoplasm, while the later stages run in the mitochondria of eukaryotes and in the cytoplasm and cell membrane of prokaryotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Respiration in this sense is not breathing, which only brings the oxygen in, for the reactions themselves take place inside cells, in the mitochondria of a eukaryote.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A cell does not burn its sugar in one flash as a flame does, and releasing the same energy through a series of small oxidations lets part of it be caught in carrier molecules instead of being lost as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Even so, only about 34 per cent of the energy in glucose is captured, and the rest is released as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> About 90 per cent of the ATP made in aerobic glucose breakdown comes from chemiosmosis, in which the electron transport chain pumps protons across a membrane and their flow back through ATP synthase drives the enzyme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That is why the familiar figure of 38 ATP per glucose is only a theoretical maximum, for the real yield varies with the cell, and since a gradient is not a whole-number reaction the count per glucose is never an exact integer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.1 Energy in Living Systems,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-1-energy-in-living-systems. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “4.1 Energy and Metabolism,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/4-1-energy-and-metabolism. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “4.3 Citric Acid Cycle and Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/4-3-citric-acid-cycle-and-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.2 Catabolism of Carbohydrates,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-2-catabolism-of-carbohydrates. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.3 Cellular Respiration,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-3-cellular-respiration. [Open access]","Nature Education, “Cell Energy, Cell Functions,” Scitable (2010), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/cell-energy-and-cell-functions-14024533/. [Open access]","Nick Lane, “Why Are Cells Powered by Proton Gradients?,” <i>Nature Education</i> 3, no. 9 (2010): 18, Scitable, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/why-are-cells-powered-by-proton-gradients-14373960/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the cell release energy in small steps instead of all at once?","a":"Burning sugar directly releases the same total energy, but none of it is stored in carrier molecules and most is lost as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A chain of small oxidations hands electrons to carriers such as NADH, whose energy can later be used to make ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Where does most of the ATP from respiration come from?","a":"About 90 per cent comes from chemiosmosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> As electrons pass along the electron transport chain, protons are pumped across a membrane, and their flow back through ATP synthase drives ATP formation, while glycolysis and the Krebs cycle make only a few ATP directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the ATP yield of glucose not a fixed number?","a":"Because it depends on how many protons the chain pumps, which varies between species, and on how electrons from glycolysis enter the mitochondria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A gradient is not a whole-number reaction, so estimates run from about 28 to 38 ATP per glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cellular respiration"},
    "bio-157": {"questions":["A sprinter's lactate comes from fermentation, not <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because no electron transport chain is involved and the electrons end up on pyruvate rather than on an inorganic acceptor.","In waterlogged wetlands, bacteria that reduce sulphate to hydrogen sulphide by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gain more energy than methane producers and so hold down the release of that greenhouse gas."],"abstract":"<b>Anaerobic respiration</b> is respiration in which the electron transport chain hands its electrons to an inorganic molecule other than oxygen, such as nitrate, sulphate, ferric iron or carbon dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is found in many bacteria and archaea, and it keeps the defining machinery of respiration, a chain of carriers in a membrane that pumps protons across it, with the flow of those protons back through ATP synthase making ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Denitrifying soil bacteria reduce nitrate and nitrite to nitrogen gas, and <i>Escherichia coli</i> switches to nitrate as its final acceptor when oxygen runs out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Methanogenic archaea reduce carbon dioxide to methane, and sulphate-reducing bacteria reduce sulphate to hydrogen sulphide, which they release as they decompose algae in coastal waters.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Their electron transfer systems build smaller electrochemical gradients than the aerobic chain, so less ATP is made from each molecule of fuel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The lactic acid made by a muscle short of oxygen comes from fermentation, which biologists separate sharply from anaerobic respiration although both work without oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In fermentation an organic molecule, usually pyruvate, takes the electrons, no electron transport chain is involved, and no ATP is made beyond the two molecules that glycolysis yields per glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Lactate fermentation regenerates the NAD+ that glycolysis needs, which is what lets red blood cells, which have no mitochondria, and skeletal muscle short of oxygen keep making ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Anaerobic respiration shapes whole ecosystems, and in wetlands sulphate reduction can account for up to half of the anaerobic breakdown of organic carbon, holding down methane release because it is energetically favoured over methane production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Some bacteria even respire onto solids, and <i>Geobacter</i> species pass electrons through proteins spanning their outer membrane to iron oxide minerals that cannot enter the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.3 Cellular Respiration,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-3-cellular-respiration. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.4 Fermentation,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-4-fermentation. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.5 Metabolism without Oxygen,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-5-metabolism-without-oxygen. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Michael Pester, Klaus-Holger Knorr, Michael W. Friedrich, Michael Wagner, and Alexander Loy, “Sulfate-Reducing Microorganisms in Wetlands: Fameless Actors in Carbon Cycling and Climate Change,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 3 (2012): 72, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2012.00072. [Open access]","Yimo Liu et al., “A Trans-Outer Membrane Porin-Cytochrome Protein Complex for Extracellular Electron Transfer by <i>Geobacter sulfurreducens</i> PCA,” <i>Environmental Microbiology Reports</i> 6, no. 6 (2014): 776–85, https://doi.org/10.1111/1758-2229.12204. [Open access]","Nick Lane, “Why Are Cells Powered by Proton Gradients?,” <i>Nature Education</i> 3, no. 9 (2010): 18, Scitable, https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/why-are-cells-powered-by-proton-gradients-14373960/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is lactate production in muscle not anaerobic respiration?","a":"Because anaerobic respiration uses an electron transport chain that ends on an inorganic acceptor such as nitrate or sulphate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Lactate comes from fermentation, in which pyruvate itself takes the electrons, no transport chain is involved and no ATP is made beyond glycolysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does anaerobic respiration yield less ATP than aerobic respiration?","a":"Because the electron transfer systems that end on other acceptors build smaller electrochemical gradients across the membrane, and less ATP is made from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Microbes that respire anaerobically usually keep a complete Krebs cycle, so they still harvest the energy carried by NADH and FADH2.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can a bacterium respire onto a mineral it cannot take in?","a":"<i>Geobacter</i> species oxidise organic compounds inside the cell and pass the electrons outwards through cytochromes and a protein complex spanning the outer membrane to iron oxides, which are poorly soluble and stay outside the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Anaerobic respiration"},
    "bio-158": {"questions":["Lacking mitochondria, a mature red blood cell makes all its ATP by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the cytoplasm, splitting glucose into pyruvate without using oxygen.","Tumour cells that take up glucose greedily and turn it into lactate even with oxygen to spare are relying on <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> far more heavily than the healthy tissue around them."],"abstract":"<b>Glycolysis</b> is the pathway that splits one molecule of glucose into two molecules of pyruvate, a three-carbon compound, in ten steps, each catalysed by its own enzyme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs in the cytoplasm, does not use oxygen directly, and is carried out by nearly all living organisms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The first half is an investment, in which two ATP molecules are spent to phosphorylate the sugar before it is split into two three-carbon pieces.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The second half oxidises those pieces, making four ATP and two NADH, so the net gain from one glucose is two ATP, two NADH and two pyruvate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In animals and most microbes the route is the Embden–Meyerhof–Parnas pathway, but some bacteria use alternatives such as the Entner–Doudoroff pathway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> Glycolysis is usually taught as the opening stage of aerobic respiration, but it is more general than that, an ancient pathway that for many microbes unable to respire is the only way of making ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Mature mammalian red blood cells have no mitochondria and rely on glycolysis alone, and if a cell cannot break pyruvate down further it gains only two ATP from each glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> To keep running without oxygen a cell must regenerate the NAD+ that glycolysis consumes, which is what fermentation to lactate does in a muscle short of oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Reactions resembling those of glycolysis run without any enzyme in water modelled on the iron-rich oceans of the Archean, a hint that the chemistry of the pathway may be older than the enzymes that now catalyse it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Many tumours lean heavily on glycolysis, taking up glucose at a high rate and turning it into lactate even when oxygen is present, an observation first made in the 1920s and known as the Warburg effect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.2 Glycolysis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-2-glycolysis. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.5 Metabolism without Oxygen,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-5-metabolism-without-oxygen. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “4.2 Glycolysis,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/4-2-glycolysis. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “24.2 Carbohydrate Metabolism,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/24-2-carbohydrate-metabolism. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.2 Catabolism of Carbohydrates,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-2-catabolism-of-carbohydrates. [Open access]","Markus A. Keller, Alexandra V. Turchyn, and Markus Ralser, “Non-Enzymatic Glycolysis and Pentose Phosphate Pathway-Like Reactions in a Plausible Archean Ocean,” <i>Molecular Systems Biology</i> 10, no. 4 (2014): 725, https://doi.org/10.1002/msb.20145228. [Open access]","Maria V. Liberti and Jason W. Locasale, “The Warburg Effect: How Does It Benefit Cancer Cells?,” <i>Trends in Biochemical Sciences</i> 41, no. 3 (2016): 211–18, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tibs.2015.12.001. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why must glycolysis spend ATP before it makes any?","a":"Because the first steps phosphorylate glucose, which makes it more reactive and traps it inside the cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Only after the sugar has been primed and split into two three-carbon molecules can the second half extract enough energy to pay back the two ATP and leave a profit of two more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does glycolysis stop without a way to regenerate NAD+?","a":"Because one of its steps oxidises the sugar and hands the electrons to NAD+, and without NAD+ that step cannot continue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Without oxygen, fermentation passes the electrons from NADH to pyruvate, making lactate in muscle and freeing NAD+ for glycolysis to go on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might glycolysis be older than enzymes?","a":"Because in water modelled on the iron-rich early oceans, 29 reactions resembling glycolysis and the pentose phosphate pathway ran with no enzymes at all, catalysed by dissolved metals, above all ferrous iron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That suggests the pathway may have taken shape in the chemistry of the prebiotic world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Glycolysis"},
    "bio-159": {"questions":["Children who inherit a faulty pyruvate dehydrogenase complex cannot carry out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> normally, so their pyruvate is turned into lactate and they develop lactic acidosis from birth.","Because no reaction in animals turns acetyl CoA back into pyruvate, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is a one-way gate, and kinases that phosphorylate its enzyme complex can switch it off rapidly."],"abstract":"The <b>link reaction</b>, also called the transition or bridge reaction, joins glycolysis to the Krebs cycle by converting each three-carbon pyruvate into a two-carbon acetyl group carried by coenzyme A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In eukaryotes pyruvate is first carried from the cytoplasm into the mitochondrial matrix, while in prokaryotes the reaction runs in the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A carboxyl group is removed as carbon dioxide, the remaining two-carbon fragment is oxidised with its electrons passed to NAD+ to form NADH, and the acetyl group is then attached to coenzyme A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The product, acetyl CoA, delivers its acetyl group to the Krebs cycle, and coenzyme A itself is made from vitamin B5, pantothenic acid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The reaction runs once for each pyruvate, and so twice for every molecule of glucose that glycolysis splits.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The work is done by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, an assembly of many copies of three enzymes, known as E1, E2 and E3, that depends on thiamine pyrophosphate, lipoic acid and FAD.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It is a one-way gate, since no known reaction in higher eukaryotes turns acetyl CoA back into pyruvate, and the complex is therefore under elaborate control.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its main control is reversible phosphorylation, in which four dedicated kinases switch the first enzyme off and two phosphatases switch it back on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When the complex fails through an inherited mutation, most often in the gene for the alpha subunit of E1, pyruvate is reduced to lactate instead, and children are born with lactic acidosis and often with damage to the brain, which depends heavily on oxidising glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Treatments include a ketogenic diet low in carbohydrate, which avoids loading the blocked step, and dichloroacetate, which inhibits the kinases and so keeps more of the complex active.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.3 Oxidation of Pyruvate and the Citric Acid Cycle,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-3-oxidation-of-pyruvate-and-the-citric-acid-cycle. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “4.3 Citric Acid Cycle and Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/4-3-citric-acid-cycle-and-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “24.2 Carbohydrate Metabolism,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/24-2-carbohydrate-metabolism. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.2 Catabolism of Carbohydrates,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-2-catabolism-of-carbohydrates. [Open access]","Mulchand S. Patel and Todd C. Rideout, “Regulation of Pyruvate Dehydrogenase Complex: Dancing to Different Drums in Cancer,” <i>International Journal of Cancer</i> 158, no. 6 (2026): 1464–80, https://doi.org/10.1002/ijc.70189. [Open access]","Batya Betesh-Abay, Eilon Shany, Orna Staretz-Chacham, Ilan Shelef, and Abed N. Azab, “Pyruvate Dehydrogenase Complex Deficiency: A Review of Treatments and Case Series,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 27, no. 6 (2026): 2732, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms27062732. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the link reaction called an oxidative decarboxylation?","a":"Because two things happen to pyruvate: a carboxyl group is removed and leaves as carbon dioxide, which is the decarboxylation, and the remaining fragment is oxidised, its electrons going to NAD+ to make NADH.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex so tightly controlled?","a":"Because its step cannot be undone: no known reaction in higher eukaryotes converts acetyl CoA back to pyruvate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Phosphorylation of any one of three serine sites on its first enzyme inactivates it rapidly, and all three must be dephosphorylated to restore it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a faulty complex cause lactic acidosis?","a":"Because pyruvate that cannot be oxidised to acetyl CoA is reduced to lactate instead, which yields only a small fraction of the ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The brain depends heavily on the aerobic oxidation of glucose, so children with the deficiency often have serious neurological problems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase"},
    "bio-160": {"questions":["Although the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> makes almost no ATP directly and never uses oxygen itself, it stops when oxygen runs out, because its NADH can no longer be reoxidised.","With intermediates drawn off to build amino acids, nucleotides and lipids, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the mitochondrial matrix is a hub of construction as well as of fuel breakdown."],"abstract":"The <b>Krebs cycle</b>, also called the citric acid cycle or the tricarboxylic acid cycle, is the series of eight reactions that oxidises the acetyl groups delivered by acetyl CoA to carbon dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each turn begins when a two-carbon acetyl group joins the four-carbon oxaloacetate to make six-carbon citrate, and it ends by regenerating the oxaloacetate, which is why the pathway is a closed loop.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Along the way two carbon atoms leave as carbon dioxide, and each turn yields three NADH, one FADH2 and one ATP or GTP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In eukaryotes the cycle runs in the mitochondrial matrix, and in prokaryotes it runs in the cytoplasm.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its enzymes are dissolved in the matrix with the single exception of succinate dehydrogenase, which sits in the inner membrane and is also part of the electron transport chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The cycle makes very little ATP directly and does not use oxygen itself, so its real product is a supply of reduced carriers, whose electrons drive the chemiosmosis that makes about 90 per cent of the ATP from glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It still depends on oxygen, because unless NADH and FADH2 can pass their electrons on to the chain that ends on oxygen, the oxidation steps of the cycle cannot take place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a hub of building as well as breaking down, since its intermediates are drawn off to make amino acids, nucleotides and lipids and must then be replenished, a process called anaplerosis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its enzymes evolved before Earth had oxygen, which suggests that the first role of the cycle was supplying building blocks rather than energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The sequence was worked out in pigeon flight muscle and set out as a complete cycle in 1937, and the discovery earned a share of the 1953 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.3 Oxidation of Pyruvate and the Citric Acid Cycle,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-3-oxidation-of-pyruvate-and-the-citric-acid-cycle. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “4.3 Citric Acid Cycle and Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/4-3-citric-acid-cycle-and-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts, Kelly A. Young, James A. Wise, Eddie Johnson, Brandon Poe, Dean H. Kruse, Oksana Korol, Jody E. Johnson, Mark Womble, and Peter DeSaix, “24.2 Carbohydrate Metabolism,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/24-2-carbohydrate-metabolism. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.2 Catabolism of Carbohydrates,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-2-catabolism-of-carbohydrates. [Open access]","Inmaculada Martínez-Reyes and Navdeep S. Chandel, “Mitochondrial TCA Cycle Metabolites Control Physiology and Disease,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 11 (2020): 102, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-019-13668-3. [Open access]","“Hans Krebs: Facts,” NobelPrize.org, Nobel Prize Outreach, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/1953/krebs/facts/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the Krebs cycle a cycle rather than a line?","a":"Because its last step regenerates the molecule its first step uses: oxaloacetate accepts an acetyl group to become citrate, and the later steps turn the carbon skeleton back into oxaloacetate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The cycle can therefore keep running as long as acetyl CoA and the other reactants are supplied.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the cycle depend on oxygen if it never uses any?","a":"Because its NADH and FADH2 must hand their electrons to the electron transport chain, which ends on oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If that transfer stops, the carriers are not regenerated and the oxidation steps of the cycle stop as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the cycle need refilling?","a":"Because its intermediates are drawn away for building other molecules, citrate for lipids and oxaloacetate for nucleotides, and the cycle must be replenished to keep turning.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Pyruvate and glutamine are two important sources of the replacement carbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Citric acid cycle"},
    "bio-161": {"questions":["Cyanide jams the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at its fourth complex, where electrons normally reach oxygen, so proton pumping halts, the gradient across the membrane collapses and ATP synthesis ceases.","Electrons from FADH2 join the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at complex II and bypass the first proton pump, which is why each pair supports less ATP than a pair delivered by NADH."],"abstract":"The <b>electron transport chain</b> is a series of protein complexes and mobile carriers that passes electrons from NADH and FADH2 to oxygen, the last stage of aerobic respiration and the only part of glucose breakdown that uses oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In eukaryotes many copies of it sit in the inner membrane of the mitochondrion, crowded into the folds called cristae, while in prokaryotes the same work is done in the plasma membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Electrons can move along it only from a carrier with a more negative redox potential to one with a more positive potential, which is why oxygen, with the most positive of all, takes them at the end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Complex I takes electrons from NADH and complex II from succinate, ubiquinone carries them to complex III, and the small protein cytochrome c ferries them on to complex IV, which reduces oxygen to water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The energy released on the way is used to pump protons out of the matrix at complexes I, III and IV, while complex II pumps none.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The chain makes no ATP itself: what it produces is a proton gradient across the membrane, which ATP synthase then uses to make ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That indirect link was proposed in 1961 as an alternative to theories that postulated a direct chemical connection between oxidation and phosphorylation, for which no evidence could be found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Because electrons from FADH2 enter at complex II and skip the pump of complex I, they move fewer protons and support less ATP, about 1.5 molecules against about 2.5 for each NADH.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The chain is not perfectly tidy either, since in normal conditions under 0.1 percent of the electrons passing through it leak early onto oxygen and form the reactive superoxide anion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> When a poison such as cyanide blocks complex IV, electron flow stops, no more protons are pumped, the gradient runs down and ATP synthesis ceases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “8.3 Cellular Respiration,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-3-cellular-respiration. [Open access]","Werner Kühlbrandt, “Structure and Function of Mitochondrial Membrane Protein Complexes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 13 (2015): 89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4625866/. [Open access]","Ayesha Judge and Michael S. Dodd, “Metabolism,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 64, no. 4 (2020): 607–47, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7545035/. [Open access]","Véronique Larosa and Claire Remacle, “Insights into the Respiratory Chain and Oxidative Stress,” <i>Bioscience Reports</i> 38, no. 5 (2018): BSR20171492, https://doi.org/10.1042/BSR20171492. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “Press Release: The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1978,” 17 October 1978, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1978/press-release/. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “24.2 Carbohydrate Metabolism,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/24-2-carbohydrate-metabolism. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the chain end with oxygen?","a":"Because electrons only move from a carrier with a more negative redox potential to one with a more positive potential, and oxygen has the most positive of all, so it takes the electrons at the end and is reduced to water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do electrons from FADH2 yield less ATP than those from NADH?","a":"Because they enter at complex II, which pumps no protons, and so bypass the pump in complex I. Fewer protons pumped means less ATP: about 1.5 for each FADH2 against about 2.5 for each NADH.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does cyanide stop ATP production?","a":"Because it blocks complex IV, the step where electrons pass to oxygen. With the chain jammed no more protons are pumped, the gradient across the inner membrane runs down, and ATP synthase has nothing left to drive it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Electron transport chain"},
    "bio-162": {"questions":["Dinitrophenol, once sold as a slimming drug, uncouples <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> by letting protons leak back across the inner mitochondrial membrane without passing through ATP synthase.","Around 1960 nobody could find the direct chemical link that supposedly joined oxidation to ATP-making in <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, because the coupling turned out to run through a proton gradient."],"abstract":"<b>Oxidative phosphorylation</b> is the making of ATP from ADP and phosphate with energy released as electrons from food pass down the respiratory chain to oxygen, and it supplies most of the ATP of an aerobic cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is one of two ways a cell makes ATP, the other being substrate-level phosphorylation, in which an enzyme moves a phosphate group straight from a fuel molecule onto ADP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Oxidative phosphorylation works instead through a membrane: the electron transfers pump protons across it, and ATP synthase harvests the potential energy of that electrochemical gradient as the protons flow back.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In eukaryotes it takes place on the inner mitochondrial membrane and in prokaryotes on the cell membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The scale is large, since a healthy adult makes about 50 kilograms (110 pounds) of ATP a day in the mitochondria, and a long-distance runner considerably more.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> How oxidation and phosphorylation were joined was an open problem around 1960, and most proposals postulated a direct chemical interaction between the enzymes, for which no evidence was ever found.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The answer accepted today is that the two are coupled only through the proton gradient, so anything that lets protons back across the membrane without passing through ATP synthase uncouples them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dinitrophenol is such an uncoupler, making the inner mitochondrial membrane leaky to protons, and it was used as a weight-loss drug until 1938.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Brown fat uncouples with a protein, uncoupling protein 1 or thermogenin, which lets protons leak back across the inner membrane and releases the gradient's energy as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Coupling also sets the pace of respiration: in healthy mitochondria the rate of oxygen use rises sharply when ADP is supplied, so the demand for ATP governs how fast fuel is burned.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Nina Parker et al., “8.3 Cellular Respiration,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-3-cellular-respiration. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.1 Energy in Living Systems,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-1-energy-in-living-systems. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “Press Release: The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1978,” 17 October 1978, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1978/press-release/. [Open access]","Martin D. Brand and David G. Nicholls, “Assessing Mitochondrial Dysfunction in Cells,” <i>Biochemical Journal</i> 435, no. 2 (2011): 297–312, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3076726/. [Open access]","Afshan Ardalan, Matthew D. Smith, and Masoud Jelokhani-Niaraki, “Uncoupling Proteins and Regulated Proton Leak in Mitochondria,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 23, no. 3 (2022): 1528, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8835771/. [Open access]","Werner Kühlbrandt, “Structure and Function of Mitochondrial Membrane Protein Complexes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 13 (2015): 89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4625866/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How does oxidative phosphorylation differ from substrate-level phosphorylation?","a":"In substrate-level phosphorylation an enzyme moves a phosphate straight from a fuel molecule onto ADP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In oxidative phosphorylation the energy of electron transfer is first stored as a proton gradient across a membrane, and ATP synthase then uses the protons' flow back to make ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is brown fat a source of heat?","a":"Because its mitochondria carry uncoupling protein 1, or thermogenin, which lets protons leak back across the inner membrane outside ATP synthase, so the energy of the gradient is released as heat instead of being captured as ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was no direct chemical link between oxidation and ATP synthesis ever found?","a":"Because the two are joined only indirectly, through a gradient of protons across the membrane. That chemiosmotic idea was proposed in 1961, received with scepticism, and generally accepted by 1978.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Oxidative phosphorylation"},
    "bio-163": {"questions":["First met with scepticism in 1961, the theory of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> replaced the search for a direct chemical link between electron transfer and ATP synthesis with a gradient of protons across a membrane.","The same proton-motive force that drives <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in bacteria also powers nutrient uptake and the spinning of flagella, so one gradient across the cell membrane serves several kinds of work."],"abstract":"<b>Chemiosmosis</b> is the movement of ions, almost always protons, down an electrochemical gradient across a membrane, and the coupling of that flow to work such as the making of ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Electron transport pumps protons to one side of an energy-converting membrane, and because they are charged and gather at a higher concentration there, the gradient has both an electrical and a chemical part.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Together the two parts make up the proton-motive force, and in mitochondria the electrical part, the membrane potential, is always the larger.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Protons cannot simply diffuse back through the nonpolar core of the membrane, so their route home is through ATP synthase, which turns like a tiny generator as they pass and makes ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The same principle powers the light reactions of photosynthesis in chloroplasts, and in bacteria the force also drives nutrient uptake and the rotation of flagella.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The idea was proposed in 1961 as an alternative to a direct chemical link between oxidation and phosphorylation, and it was first received with scepticism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Over the next 15 years work in many laboratories showed its basic postulates to be correct, and by 1978, when it won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry, it was generally accepted.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The forces involved are modest: the potential across the inner mitochondrial membrane is about 180 millivolts, and the pH difference between the space outside it and the matrix only about 0.6 to 0.8 units.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Even so, chemiosmosis accounts for about 90 percent of the ATP a cell makes when it breaks glucose down aerobically.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Protons are not the only possible currency, since some bacterial ATP synthases run on a gradient of sodium ions, although nearly all F-type enzymes use protons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “Press Release: The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1978,” 17 October 1978, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1978/press-release/. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “8.3 Cellular Respiration,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-3-cellular-respiration. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Werner Kühlbrandt, “Structure and Function of Mitochondrial Membrane Protein Complexes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 13 (2015): 89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4625866/. [Open access]","Martin D. Brand and David G. Nicholls, “Assessing Mitochondrial Dysfunction in Cells,” <i>Biochemical Journal</i> 435, no. 2 (2011): 297–312, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3076726/. [Open access]","Jasmine A. Nirody, Itay Budin, and Padmini Rangamani, “ATP Synthase: Evolution, Energetics, and Membrane Interactions,” <i>Journal of General Physiology</i> 152, no. 11 (2020): e201912475, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7594442/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can't the protons simply leak back through the membrane?","a":"Because ions cannot cross the nonpolar core of a phospholipid bilayer unaided, so in the inner mitochondrial membrane their way back is through the protein ATP synthase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Is the gradient mainly a difference in pH?","a":"No. The proton-motive force has two parts, a pH difference and an electrical potential, and in mitochondria the electrical part is always the larger; the pH difference across the inner membrane is under one unit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Does chemiosmosis only make ATP?","a":"No. In bacteria the same proton-motive force also drives the uptake of nutrients and the rotation of flagella, and some bacteria run their ATP synthase on a sodium gradient instead of protons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chemiosmosis"},
    "bio-164": {"questions":["When the mitochondrial membrane potential falls too low, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can run in reverse, splitting ATP to pump protons across the inner membrane instead of making it.","Found alike in mitochondria, chloroplasts and bacteria, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> has a rotor ring of 8 c subunits in mammals but as many as 17 in one bacterial species."],"abstract":"<b>ATP synthase</b> is the membrane enzyme that makes most of a cell's ATP, joining ADP and inorganic phosphate with energy taken from protons flowing down their gradient across the membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It sits in the inner membrane of mitochondria, in chloroplasts and in the cell membrane of bacteria, and the same enzyme is found in all three.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It has two parts: Fo in the membrane, through which the protons pass, and F1, a head projecting into the matrix, built of three alpha and three beta subunits with the catalytic sites on the betas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The enzyme can also run backwards, splitting ATP to pump protons, and in mitochondria it does so when the membrane potential falls below a threshold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A common accounting for mammalian mitochondria allows about three protons for each ATP, once the cost of bringing ADP and phosphate into the matrix is included.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> What makes it unusual is that it works by rotation: protons passing through Fo turn a ring of c subunits, and the stalk attached to the ring turns inside the F1 head.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The stalk is asymmetrical, so as it turns it forces each beta subunit through a cycle of shapes in which it binds ADP and phosphate, forms ATP and releases it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> One full turn makes three ATP, one at each beta subunit, and the rotation was confirmed directly when a detached F1 head was seen to spin when driven by ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The number of c subunits sets how many protons a turn costs, from 8 in mammalian mitochondria to 17 in one bacterium, so the protons spent per ATP differ between species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The mechanism was proposed from biochemical evidence in the 1970s and confirmed by the enzyme's structure in the 1990s, work recognised by the 1997 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>","sources":["An I. Jonckheere, Jan A. M. Smeitink, and Richard J. T. Rodenburg, “Mitochondrial ATP Synthase: Architecture, Function and Pathology,” <i>Journal of Inherited Metabolic Disease</i> 35, no. 2 (2012): 211–25, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3278611/. [Open access]","Jasmine A. Nirody, Itay Budin, and Padmini Rangamani, “ATP Synthase: Evolution, Energetics, and Membrane Interactions,” <i>Journal of General Physiology</i> 152, no. 11 (2020): e201912475, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7594442/. [Open access]","Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, “Press Release: The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1997,” 15 October 1997, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1997/press-release/. [Open access]","Werner Kühlbrandt, “Structure and Function of Mitochondrial Membrane Protein Complexes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 13 (2015): 89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4625866/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “8.3 Cellular Respiration,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-3-cellular-respiration. [Open access]","Ayesha Judge and Michael S. Dodd, “Metabolism,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 64, no. 4 (2020): 607–47, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7545035/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does one turn of the rotor make three ATP?","a":"Because the F1 head has three catalytic beta subunits, and the turning stalk drives each one through binding ADP and phosphate, making ATP and releasing it, once per revolution.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can the enzyme run backwards?","a":"Because making and splitting ATP use the same catalytic pathway in reverse. When the mitochondrial membrane potential falls too low the enzyme hydrolyses ATP and pumps protons, which cells restrain because it drains their ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the size of the rotor ring matter?","a":"Because the protons turn the ring one c subunit at a time, so a ring with more subunits needs more protons per turn, and so per ATP. Mammalian mitochondria have 8, one bacterium 17.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"ATP synthase"},
    "bio-165": {"questions":["Rows of paired ATP synthase molecules bend the inner mitochondrial membrane into the tight ridges of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, and yeast lacking the linking subunits fail to form them properly.","Separate <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> within a single mitochondrion can hold different membrane potentials, so one fold can lose its charge while its neighbours go on making ATP."],"abstract":"<b>Cristae</b> are the folds of the inner mitochondrial membrane, infoldings that reach into the matrix and give the organelle its ridged look in electron micrographs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> They hold most, if not all, of the assembled complexes of the electron transport chain and of ATP synthase, which makes them the main site of energy conversion in every eukaryote that does not photosynthesise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Depending on the cell they are disk-like plates, tubes or bags, joined to the rest of the inner membrane by narrow crista junctions about 25 nanometres across.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Tissues with a high energy demand, such as heart and skeletal muscle, have closely stacked cristae filling most of each mitochondrion, while liver and kidney mitochondria have fewer and more room for the matrix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Introductory textbooks usually stop at saying that the folds increase the surface area of the inner membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> They are also a separate compartment, the crista lumen, set off from the rest of the inner membrane by the junctions and by a protein assembly called MICOS that anchors the cristae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their shape is made largely by ATP synthase, which forms rows of dimers along the tightly curved ridges, and yeast lacking the subunits that link the dimers have no normal cristae and grow up to 60 percent more slowly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Shape in turn affects function, since altering cristae structure changes how well the respiratory complexes assemble into supercomplexes and how efficiently the mitochondrion respires.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Different cristae within one mitochondrion can hold different membrane potentials, so they behave as largely independent units and one can fail without dragging down the rest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> One likely advantage is that the folds hold pumped protons close to ATP synthase instead of letting them escape, which may be how mitochondria meet a large demand with a shallow gradient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “4.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]","Werner Kühlbrandt, “Structure and Function of Mitochondrial Membrane Protein Complexes,” <i>BMC Biology</i> 13 (2015): 89, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4625866/. [Open access]","Sara Cogliati et al., “Mitochondrial Cristae Shape Determines Respiratory Chain Supercomplexes Assembly and Respiratory Efficiency,” <i>Cell</i> 155, no. 1 (2013): 160–71, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3790458/. [Open access]","Dane M. Wolf et al., “Individual Cristae within the Same Mitochondrion Display Different Membrane Potentials and Are Functionally Independent,” <i>EMBO Journal</i> 38, no. 22 (2019): e101056, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6856616/. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “3.2 The Cytoplasm and Cellular Organelles,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-2-the-cytoplasm-and-cellular-organelles. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “3.3 Eukaryotic Cells,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/3-3-eukaryotic-cells. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Are cristae just a way of adding surface area?","a":"No. They also form a separate compartment, sealed off by narrow junctions and the MICOS complex, that holds the respiratory chain and ATP synthase, and their shape affects how well those complexes assemble and work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"What gives cristae their shape?","a":"Largely ATP synthase itself, which forms rows of dimers along the curved ridges and bends the membrane; yeast without the subunits that link the dimers lack normal cristae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why might cristae behave as separate units?","a":"Because each can hold its own membrane potential, so a problem that depolarises one crista need not spread to the others in the same mitochondrion.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Crista"},
    "bio-166": {"questions":["Made in the cytoplasm by glycolysis, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane, so shuttles carry its electrons inside, and the shuttle used affects how much ATP results.","Ten molecules of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are filled for each glucose broken down aerobically, and passing their electrons to complex I supplies most of a realistic yield of about 31 ATP."],"abstract":"<b>NADH</b> is the reduced form of nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, the coenzyme that collects electrons from fuel in glycolysis, the link reaction and the Krebs cycle and carries them to the respiratory chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Its oxidised form, NAD+, acts as a universal electron carrier, and NADH is in effect a store of energy in reduced form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Breaking down one glucose molecule fills ten NADH and two FADH2, the other electron carrier, and almost all the ATP of aerobic respiration comes from passing their electrons to oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> NADH hands its electrons to complex I, which pumps protons, while FADH2 enters at complex II, which does not, and so supports less ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The inner mitochondrial membrane is impermeable to polar molecules, so the NADH made in the cytoplasm by glycolysis cannot cross it and its electrons are carried in by shuttle systems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Older textbooks counted three ATP per NADH and two per FADH2, giving a theoretical maximum of 38 ATP for each glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The modern figure is about 2.5 per NADH and 1.5 per FADH2, because each ATP costs the protons that turn ATP synthase plus about one more to bring ADP and phosphate into the matrix.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The ceiling for each fuel is a ratio of ratios, the protons pumped per oxygen atom divided by the protons spent per ATP, and the second depends on the size of ATP synthase's rotor ring.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Shuttles trim the yield further, the malate-aspartate shuttle lowering each cytoplasmic NADH to about 2.25 ATP for a total of roughly 31.5 per glucose, while the route that hands electrons to FAD yields less.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Real mitochondria fall short even of that, because some protons leak back across the inner membrane without passing through ATP synthase, and the pathway captures only about 34 percent of glucose's energy, the rest leaving as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Nina Parker et al., “8.3 Cellular Respiration,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-3-cellular-respiration. [Open access]","Ayesha Judge and Michael S. Dodd, “Metabolism,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 64, no. 4 (2020): 607–47, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7545035/. [Open access]","Martin D. Brand and David G. Nicholls, “Assessing Mitochondrial Dysfunction in Cells,” <i>Biochemical Journal</i> 435, no. 2 (2011): 297–312, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3076726/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Andrey Nikiforov, Veronika Kulikova, and Mathias Ziegler, “The Human NAD Metabolome: Functions, Metabolism and Compartmentalization,” <i>Critical Reviews in Biochemistry and Molecular Biology</i> 50, no. 4 (2015): 284–97, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4673589/. [Open access]","Afshan Ardalan, Matthew D. Smith, and Masoud Jelokhani-Niaraki, “Uncoupling Proteins and Regulated Proton Leak in Mitochondria,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 23, no. 3 (2022): 1528, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8835771/. [Open access]","Jasmine A. Nirody, Itay Budin, and Padmini Rangamani, “ATP Synthase: Evolution, Energetics, and Membrane Interactions,” <i>Journal of General Physiology</i> 152, no. 11 (2020): e201912475, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7594442/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the old figure of 38 ATP per glucose too high?","a":"Because it assumed three ATP per NADH and two per FADH2.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each ATP actually costs the protons that turn ATP synthase plus about one to import ADP and phosphate, giving about 2.5 and 1.5, and some protons leak back without making ATP at all.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does it matter which shuttle a cell uses?","a":"Because NADH made in the cytoplasm cannot enter mitochondria directly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The malate-aspartate shuttle passes its electrons to NAD+ inside at a small cost, while the route that hands them to FAD yields less ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"What is a P/O ratio?","a":"The maximum number of ATP made as a pair of electrons passes from a substrate to oxygen: the protons pumped per oxygen atom divided by the protons needed per ATP, a fixed figure for each fuel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide"},
    "bio-167": {"questions":["The bacteria that sour milk into yoghurt and shredded cabbage into sauerkraut carry out <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the same pathway a sprinting muscle uses when its demand for ATP outruns its oxygen supply.","Long blamed for muscle fatigue and soreness, the lactate made by <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now known to form under fully aerobic conditions and to serve as a fuel for the heart."],"abstract":"<b>Lactic acid fermentation</b> is the reduction of pyruvate to lactate by the enzyme lactate dehydrogenase, a reaction that turns NADH back into NAD+ so that glycolysis can keep running without oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It makes no ATP itself, so a cell relying on it gets only the two ATP per glucose that glycolysis yields by substrate-level phosphorylation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Animals and certain bacteria use it, among them the lactic acid bacteria whose acid sets milk into yoghurt and cheese and sours cabbage into sauerkraut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Mammalian red blood cells, which have no mitochondria, depend on it entirely, and skeletal muscle uses it heavily when ATP is demanded faster than oxygen can be delivered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Lactate leaves the muscle in the blood, and the liver turns it back into glucose in the Cori cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> Lactate was long portrayed as a waste product made only when muscle ran short of oxygen and as the agent of fatigue, and some introductory textbooks still blame its build-up for stiffness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> That picture is now disputed, because in healthy people glycolysis proceeds to lactate under fully aerobic conditions, with oxygen in the muscle well above what mitochondria need.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most of the lactate made, around 75 to 80 percent, is disposed of quickly, oxidised as fuel by working muscle and the heart or taken up by the liver to make glucose.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The reaction that makes lactate consumes a proton instead of releasing one, and blood lactate can climb from about 1.6 to 8.3 millimoles per litre during a short sprint.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The heavy breathing after hard exercise, once described as repaying an 'oxygen debt', pays for more than lactate, since the extra oxygen also restores ATP and creatine phosphate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.5 Metabolism without Oxygen,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-5-metabolism-without-oxygen. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “8.4 Fermentation,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-4-fermentation. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “24.2 Carbohydrate Metabolism,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/24-2-carbohydrate-metabolism. [Open access]","George A. Brooks, “Lactate as a Fulcrum of Metabolism,” <i>Redox Biology</i> 35 (2020): 101454, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7284908/. [Open access]","Julien S. Baker, Marie Clare McCormick, and Robert A. Robergs, “Interaction among Skeletal Muscle Metabolic Energy Systems during Intense Exercise,” <i>Journal of Nutrition and Metabolism</i> 2010 (2010): 905612, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3005844/. [Open access]","Ayesha Judge and Michael S. Dodd, “Metabolism,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 64, no. 4 (2020): 607–47, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7545035/. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “4.4 Fermentation,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/4-4-fermentation. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “10.3 Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-3-muscle-fiber-contraction-and-relaxation. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does muscle make lactate at all?","a":"Because glycolysis needs NAD+, and reducing pyruvate to lactate turns NADH back into NAD+, which lets glycolysis go on making ATP quickly when demand outruns the supply of oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Is lactate a waste product?","a":"No. Most of it, about 75 to 80 percent, is used soon after it is made, burned as fuel by muscle and the heart or turned back into glucose by the liver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Does lactic acid cause muscle soreness?","a":"The idea is disputed. Lactic acid build-up was once believed to cause stiffness, fatigue and soreness, but more recent research challenges this, and lactate forms even in muscle with enough oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Lactic acid fermentation"},
    "bio-168": {"questions":["Juice pressed from yeast cells and free of anything living still carried out the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of sugar, showing that the process was chemistry done by enzymes and not an expression of life.","Vinegar and kombucha count as products of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in the food sense, yet the acetic acid bacteria that make them rely on aerobic metabolism, not the biochemist's oxygen-free pathway."],"abstract":"<b>Fermentation</b>, in the biochemist's sense, is a way of extracting energy from food without an electron transport chain, in which an organic molecule takes the electrons and turns NADH back into NAD+.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Glycolysis splits glucose into pyruvate and makes two ATP, and fermentation then reduces the pyruvate or a product of it, regenerating the NAD+ that glycolysis needs to continue.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It makes no further ATP, so a fermenting cell gets at most two ATP per glucose, far less than respiration yields.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is not the same as anaerobic respiration, in which an electron transport chain passes electrons to an inorganic acceptor such as nitrate or sulphate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The best-known kinds are lactic acid fermentation and alcoholic fermentation, in which yeast turns pyruvate into carbon dioxide and ethanol, the alcohol in drinks and the gas that raises bread.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> In food the word is used more broadly, and an expert panel convened in 2019 defined fermented foods as foods made through desired microbial growth and enzymatic conversions of food components.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That wider sense includes processes that are not fermentation to a biochemist, such as the aerobic metabolism of the moulds behind soy sauce and miso and of the acetic acid bacteria that make vinegar and kombucha.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Fermenting was historically a way to preserve food, since the acids and alcohol made hold back harmful microbes, and more than 5,000 kinds of fermented foods and drinks may be consumed today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> For decades alcoholic fermentation was held to be an expression of life inseparable from living yeast cells, a view Pasteur's work made general.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The view fell when Eduard Buchner showed that juice pressed from yeast, free of living cells, could still ferment sugar, work recognised by the 1907 Nobel Prize in Chemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.5 Metabolism without Oxygen,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-5-metabolism-without-oxygen. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “8.4 Fermentation,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-4-fermentation. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “8.3 Cellular Respiration,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-3-cellular-respiration. [Open access]","Maria L. Marco et al., “The International Scientific Association for Probiotics and Prebiotics (ISAPP) Consensus Statement on Fermented Foods,” <i>Nature Reviews Gastroenterology &amp; Hepatology</i> 18, no. 3 (2021): 196–208, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7925329/. [Open access]","Eirini Dimidi et al., “Fermented Foods: Definitions and Characteristics, Impact on the Gut Microbiota and Effects on Gastrointestinal Health and Disease,” <i>Nutrients</i> 11, no. 8 (2019): 1806, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6723656/. [Open access]","K. A. H. Mörner, “Award Ceremony Speech: The Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1907,” NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/1907/ceremony-speech/. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “4.4 Fermentation,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/4-4-fermentation. [Open access]","Jyoti P. Tamang, Koichi Watanabe, and Wilhelm H. Holzapfel, “Review: Diversity of Microorganisms in Global Fermented Foods and Beverages,” <i>Frontiers in Microbiology</i> 7 (2016): 377, https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2016.00377. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How is fermentation different from anaerobic respiration?","a":"Fermentation has no electron transport chain: an organic molecule takes the electrons, and no ATP is made beyond glycolysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Anaerobic respiration uses a chain ending on an inorganic acceptor such as nitrate or sulphate, and makes more ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is vinegar called fermented if its bacteria need oxygen?","a":"Because food science uses the word for any food made through desired microbial growth and enzymatic conversion, a wider sense than the biochemical one; vinegar's acetic acid bacteria use aerobic metabolism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did fermentation without living cells matter?","a":"Because fermentation had been treated as an expression of life, inseparable from living yeast and closed to chemical study. Showing that cell-free yeast juice still fermented sugar opened the process to chemistry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Fermentation"},
    "bio-169": {"questions":["Protein serves as a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> only after its amino acids lose their nitrogen, which mammals excrete as urea while the carbon skeletons enter glycolysis or the Krebs cycle.","The heart draws most of its energy from fatty acids while the brain depends almost wholly on glucose, so the main <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> differs from one tissue to another."],"abstract":"A <b>respiratory substrate</b> is any organic molecule a cell breaks down in respiration to release energy, and carbohydrates, fats and proteins can all serve.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their pathways converge: sugars enter glycolysis, fatty acids are cut into two-carbon acetyl units by beta-oxidation, and amino acids are converted into intermediates of glycolysis or the Krebs cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Amino acids must first lose their amino group, which in mammals becomes ammonia and then urea, excreted in the urine.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The body has no store of amino acids, so any excess beyond what is needed for building proteins is converted into glucose or ketones or broken down for energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Fats are the densest store of energy, giving about twice as much per gram as carbohydrates or proteins, and that high energy value is why the body keeps its reserve as triglycerides.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> Glucose is the textbook fuel but far from the only one, and the mix varies with the tissue and even with sex, sedentary women burning more fat than sedentary men during moderate exercise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The heart takes 50 to 70 percent of its energy from fatty acids, while the brain relies almost entirely on glucose, using 100 to 120 grams of it a day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In prolonged starvation the liver turns surplus acetyl CoA from fat into ketone bodies, which the brain can use as an alternative fuel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Per molecule the difference is large: complete oxidation of one glucose yields about 31.5 ATP, and of one molecule of the fatty acid palmitate about 108.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Because each substrate uses a different amount of oxygen for the carbon dioxide it releases, the ratio of the two gases reveals the mix being burned, about 1.0 for carbohydrate, 0.69 for fat and 0.82 for protein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.6 Connections of Carbohydrate, Protein, and Lipid Metabolic Pathways,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-6-connections-of-carbohydrate-protein-and-lipid-metabolic-pathways. [Open access]","Ayesha Judge and Michael S. Dodd, “Metabolism,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 64, no. 4 (2020): 607–47, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7545035/. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “24.3 Lipid Metabolism,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/24-3-lipid-metabolism. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “24.4 Protein Metabolism,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/24-4-protein-metabolism. [Open access]","Riddhi Das Gupta et al., “Indirect Calorimetry: From Bench to Bedside,” <i>Indian Journal of Endocrinology and Metabolism</i> 21, no. 4 (2017): 594–99, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5477450/. [Open access]","Nina Parker et al., “8.5 Catabolism of Lipids and Proteins,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-5-catabolism-of-lipids-and-proteins. [Open access]","Antonella Cano et al., “Analysis of Sex-Based Differences in Energy Substrate Utilization during Moderate-Intensity Aerobic Exercise,” <i>European Journal of Applied Physiology</i> 122, no. 1 (2022): 29–70, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00421-021-04802-5. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Can the body store protein as fuel?","a":"No. Amino acids in excess cannot be stored; they are converted into glucose or ketones or broken down, and their nitrogen must first be removed and excreted as urea.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does the brain cope with prolonged starvation?","a":"In prolonged starvation the liver makes ketone bodies from the acetyl CoA of fat, and the brain, which normally relies almost entirely on glucose, can burn them as an alternative fuel.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can the fuel being burned be measured?","a":"From the gases. Carbohydrate releases one carbon dioxide for each oxygen used and fat and protein less, so the ratio, about 1.0, 0.69 or 0.82, shows the mix being oxidised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}]},
    "bio-170": {"questions":["Measured from a resting patient's breath with a canopy and gas analysers, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> shows which fuels the body is oxidising, and a value outside about 0.67 to 1.3 suggests a faulty test.","In the first week of a ketogenic diet the day-long <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> measured in the breath fell from 0.88 to 0.78, a sign that the body had shifted towards burning fat."],"abstract":"The <b>respiratory quotient</b> is the ratio of the carbon dioxide produced to the oxygen consumed, and it shows which fuel is being oxidised.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Burning glucose completely produces one molecule of carbon dioxide for each molecule of oxygen used, a quotient of 1.0, while fat and protein produce less carbon dioxide for the oxygen and give about 0.69 and 0.82.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In practice a quotient near 0.7 points mainly to fat, about 0.8 to a mixed diet and 1.0 to carbohydrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Strictly, the respiratory quotient is the ratio in the cells, and the respiratory exchange ratio is the same ratio measured in breath at the mouth, the two agreeing in a steady state.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is measured by indirect calorimetry, which estimates energy use from gas exchange rather than heat and is the reference method for energy expenditure in critically ill children.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The figure is useful because it is a fuel gauge read from the breath, and calculations of resting energy expenditure from gas exchange depend on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It shifts with diet, and in one study the 24-hour value fell from 0.88 to 0.78 during the first week of a ketogenic diet as the body turned to fat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> It shifts with training too, since fit people show a lower exchange ratio than untrained people at a comparable workload, a sign that they burn more fat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> It even differs between the sexes, a meta-analysis finding that men run a slightly higher exchange ratio than women during moderate exercise.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The breath ratio is easily distorted, so values outside about 0.67 to 1.3 are taken as a sign of a faulty test, and hyperventilation, acidosis or air leaks can push the reading away from what the cells are doing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Riddhi Das Gupta et al., “Indirect Calorimetry: From Bench to Bedside,” <i>Indian Journal of Endocrinology and Metabolism</i> 21, no. 4 (2017): 594–99, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5477450/. [Open access]","Haifa Mtaweh et al., “Indirect Calorimetry: History, Technology, and Application,” <i>Frontiers in Pediatrics</i> 6 (2018): 257, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6157446/. [Open access]","Marta Delsoglio et al., “Indirect Calorimetry in Clinical Practice,” <i>Journal of Clinical Medicine</i> 8, no. 9 (2019): 1387, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6780066/. [Open access]","Arnulfo Ramos-Jiménez et al., “The Respiratory Exchange Ratio Is Associated with Fitness Indicators Both in Trained and Untrained Men: A Possible Application for People with Reduced Exercise Tolerance,” <i>Clinical Medicine: Circulatory, Respiratory and Pulmonary Medicine</i> 2 (2008): 1–9, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2990231/. [Open access]","Antonella Cano et al., “Analysis of Sex-Based Differences in Energy Substrate Utilization during Moderate-Intensity Aerobic Exercise,” <i>European Journal of Applied Physiology</i> 122, no. 1 (2022): 29–70, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00421-021-04802-5. [Open access]","Rodrigo Fernández-Verdejo, Jacob T. Mey, and Eric Ravussin, “Effects of Ketone Bodies on Energy Expenditure, Substrate Utilization, and Energy Intake in Humans,” <i>Journal of Lipid Research</i> 64, no. 10 (2023): 100442, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jlr.2023.100442. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the quotient 1.0 for carbohydrate but lower for fat?","a":"Because complete oxidation of glucose releases one carbon dioxide for every oxygen used, while fat and protein release less carbon dioxide for the oxygen they consume, giving about 0.69 and 0.82.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"What is the difference between the respiratory quotient and the respiratory exchange ratio?","a":"The quotient is the ratio of carbon dioxide to oxygen in the cells; the exchange ratio is the same ratio measured in breath at the mouth. In a steady state the two are equal.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a breath measurement mislead?","a":"Because the gases at the mouth are affected by more than metabolism: hyperventilation, metabolic acidosis, air leaks or recent exercise can push the ratio outside its usual range of about 0.7 to 1.0.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Respiratory quotient"},
    "bio-171": {"questions":["A matching tube holding no living material lets a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> cancel out changes in room temperature and air pressure, so that only the organisms' uptake of oxygen moves the fluid.","Run once with a carbon dioxide absorbent and then once without it, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gives both the oxygen consumed and the carbon dioxide released, and so the respiratory quotient."],"abstract":"A <b>respirometer</b> measures how fast living material respires by following the gas it exchanges inside a sealed space, most often the oxygen it takes up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is not to be confused with a spirometer, since it records the gas exchange of the respiring organisms themselves, read as a sign of their biochemical activity.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The classroom version seals germinating seeds or woodlice above potassium hydroxide solution, which absorbs the carbon dioxide they give off, so any fall in gas volume is oxygen taken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> A matching empty tube acts as a thermobarometer, since changes in temperature or pressure move the air in both tubes alike, so the coloured fluid in the manometer joining them moves only because of the organisms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Run in a water bath and read every 4 minutes, the uptake should roughly double with each 10 °C (18 °F) rise up to about 40 °C (104 °F), above which the enzymes begin to be damaged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Run again without the absorbent, it also gives the carbon dioxide released, because that reading is the gas given out minus the oxygen absorbed, and carbon dioxide divided by oxygen is the respiratory quotient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Research respirometers keep the principle and change the sensor, and one closed system with optical oxygen sensors takes up to 240 measurements at the same time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> For cells and small animals the usual instruments are Clark-type oxygen electrodes and microplate analysers, and blood cells are now tested this way as a measure of mitochondrial function.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Adding an ATP synthase inhibitor, then an uncoupler, then rotenone with antimycin A splits a sample's oxygen uptake into the part that drives ATP synthesis, the part lost to proton leak and the part that has nothing to do with mitochondria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> At the other extreme a whole-room calorimeter works out a person's energy expenditure from the oxygen and carbon dioxide differences between the air going in and coming out.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Royal Society of Biology, “Measuring the Rate of Metabolism,” Practical Biology, https://practicalbiology.org/energy/gas-balance-in-respiration-and-photosynthesis/measuring-the-rate-of-metabolism.html. [Open access]","Royal Society of Biology, “Measuring Respiratory Quotient,” Practical Biology, https://practicalbiology.org/energy/gas-balance-in-respiration-and-photosynthesis/measuring-respiratory-quotient.html. [Open access]","Nicolas Mouret et al., “Application of a High-Throughput Respirometry Technique for Assessing Respiratory Metabolism of Terrestrial and Aquatic Invertebrates,” <i>Journal of Insect Science</i> 26, no. 4 (2026): ieag076, https://doi.org/10.1093/jisesa/ieag076. [Open access]","Nathan Dennis, Campbell W. Gourlay, and Marina Ezcurra, “A Novel <i>C. elegans</i> Respirometry Assay Using Low-Cost Optical Oxygen Sensors,” <i>Biology Methods and Protocols</i> 10, no. 1 (2025): bpaf072, https://doi.org/10.1093/biomethods/bpaf072. [Open access]","Alejandra Romero et al., “Protocol for Isolation and Cryopreservation of Human Peripheral Blood Mononuclear Cells for High-Resolution Respirometry,” <i>STAR Protocols</i> 7, no. 4 (2026): 104881, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xpro.2026.104881. [Open access]","Andrii Zahorodnii et al., “Whole-Room Indirect Calorimetry in the Study of Obesity, Insulin Resistance and Metabolic Flexibility: A Narrative Review,” <i>Nutrients</i> 18, no. 19 (2026): 3287, https://doi.org/10.3390/nu18193287. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the tube need potassium hydroxide in it?","a":"Because respiring material gives off carbon dioxide while it takes up oxygen, and the two changes in volume would partly cancel. The hydroxide absorbs the carbon dioxide, so any fall in gas volume measures the oxygen taken up.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the apparatus have a second tube with nothing alive in it?","a":"Because a volume of gas also changes with temperature and air pressure. The empty tube, a thermobarometer, feels the same changes, so the manometer between the two tubes moves only because of the respiring material.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does a respirometer measure carbon dioxide if the absorbent removes it?","a":"By running twice. With the absorbent the reading is the oxygen taken in; without it the reading is the carbon dioxide released minus the oxygen absorbed. Adding the two gives the carbon dioxide, and dividing by the oxygen gives the respiratory quotient.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Respirometer"},
    "bio-172": {"questions":["Per kilogram the heart and kidneys burn far more energy than resting muscle or fat, so the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of a body depends chiefly on its fat-free organs and tissues.","An overactive thyroid gland, releasing extra thyroxine and triiodothyronine into the blood, raises the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of the resting body and is often accompanied by loss of weight."],"abstract":"The <b>basal metabolic rate</b> is the energy an endothermic animal spends at rest, and in humans it is measured in a person resting in a thermally neutral room in the post-absorptive state, after the last meal has been digested.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Ectotherms are described instead by a standard metabolic rate, and the gap is large: men have a basal rate of 1,600 to 1,800 kilocalories a day, while an alligator's standard rate is about 60.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is measured by indirect calorimetry, in which the oxygen consumed and carbon dioxide produced are converted into energy by the modified Weir equation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In a sedentary person the resting component accounts for roughly 60 to 75 per cent of the day's total expenditure, which makes it the largest part of most people's energy budget.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Per kilogram, small endotherms run much faster than large ones because a small body has more surface for its mass and loses heat sooner, and across species metabolic rate scales with body mass and rises steeply with temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> How the rate changes with age was unclear until a database of 6,421 people aged 8 days to 95 years was analysed, 2,008 of them with basal rates measured.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Adjusted for fat-free mass, daily expenditure climbs to about 46 per cent above adult values at around one year old, falls slowly to adult levels by about 20, stays stable from 20 to 60, pregnancy included, and declines only after 60.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The basal rate is not spread evenly through the body either: per kilogram each day the heart and kidneys burn about 440 kilocalories, the brain 240 and the liver 200, against 13 for skeletal muscle and 4.5 for fat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> That is why fat-free mass is the main determinant of resting expenditure, with a smaller contribution from fat mass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Hormones set the rate as well, and an overactive thyroid, with raised levels of thyroxine and triiodothyronine, increases the basal metabolic rate and often brings loss of weight.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “33.1 Animal Form and Function,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/33-1-animal-form-and-function. [Open access]","J. Gordon Betts et al., “24.6 Energy and Heat Balance,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/24-6-energy-and-heat-balance. [Open access]","Andrii Zahorodnii et al., “Whole-Room Indirect Calorimetry in the Study of Obesity, Insulin Resistance and Metabolic Flexibility: A Narrative Review,” <i>Nutrients</i> 18, no. 19 (2026): 3287, https://doi.org/10.3390/nu18193287. [Open access]","ZiMian Wang et al., “Evaluation of Specific Metabolic Rates of Major Organs and Tissues: Comparison between Nonobese and Obese Women,” <i>Obesity</i> 20, no. 1 (2012): 95–100, https://doi.org/10.1038/oby.2011.256. [Open access]","Herman Pontzer et al., “Daily Energy Expenditure through the Human Life Course,” <i>Science</i> 373, no. 6556 (2021): 808–12, https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abe5017. [Open access]","S. A. Sanduni Samudika De Alwis et al., “The Associations between Resting and Total Energy Expenditure, Physical Activity, and Thyroid Hormone Levels in Adult Females,” <i>Frontiers in Physiology</i> 17 (2026): 1716140, https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2026.1716140. [Open access]","Nicolas Mouret et al., “Application of a High-Throughput Respirometry Technique for Assessing Respiratory Metabolism of Terrestrial and Aquatic Invertebrates,” <i>Journal of Insect Science</i> 26, no. 4 (2026): ieag076, https://doi.org/10.1093/jisesa/ieag076. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the basal rate measured at rest, fasting and in a neutral room?","a":"Because the measurement is meant to capture the energy the body needs for its basic functions alone, in a person at rest, in a neutrally temperate environment and in the post-absorptive state. In a sedentary person that resting component is roughly 60 to 75 per cent of the day's expenditure.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a mouse burn more energy for its size than an elephant?","a":"Because a smaller endotherm has more surface area for its mass, so it loses heat faster and needs more energy to hold its body temperature constant. Per unit of body weight, that gives it a higher basal metabolic rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Does metabolism really slow down from early adulthood?","a":"Not according to a database of 6,421 people from 29 countries. Adjusted for fat-free mass, daily expenditure was stable from about 20 to 60 years of age, even during pregnancy, and declined only after 60.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Basal metabolic rate"},
    "bio-173": {"questions":["Chased to exhaustion, mahi-mahi recovered far more slowly than fish given a gradual speed test, showing at least 1.8 times as much <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> during the hours after the chase.","Once called the oxygen debt, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was long explained by lactate being rebuilt into glycogen, until isotope tracers showed that most lactate is simply oxidised as fuel."],"abstract":"<b>Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption</b> is the raised oxygen uptake that continues once exercise has stopped, measured as the oxygen taken in above the resting baseline until uptake returns to that baseline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Recovery needs that oxygen for several jobs at once: restoring ATP and creatine phosphate in the muscles, converting lactic acid back to pyruvic acid, and turning lactate into glucose or glycogen in the liver.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Other systems that worked during the exercise also draw on it, and together these processes produce the heavy breathing that persists after the effort ends.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Intensity matters, and in young men with obesity interval running produced a greater excess, and more fat burned during recovery, than continuous running of the same energy cost.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is not a human peculiarity, for mahi-mahi chased to exhaustion showed an excess at least 1.8 times that of fish swum to fatigue in a gradual speed test, and even in the speed test 40 per cent of the oxygen cost was paid during recovery.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The older name, the oxygen debt, carried a theory with it, that lactate made during hard exercise is mostly dealt with afterwards by oxidising about a fifth of it and rebuilding the other four-fifths into glycogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In that classical picture lactate was a dead-end waste, the product of too little oxygen and the cause of fatigue, cramps and soreness.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Isotope tracer studies in animals and in people found the reverse of the debt theory, with most lactate disposed of by oxidation, much of it inside the muscles, and little turned into muscle glycogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Lactate is now known to form continuously even when oxygen is plentiful, and it serves as a major fuel, a raw material for making glucose and a signalling molecule.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The soreness felt a day or two after hard, eccentric or unaccustomed exercise is a separate process, building over hours to a peak between 24 and 72 hours and involving microscopic damage to the muscle's contractile units, inflammation and sensitised pain signalling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["J. Gordon Betts et al., “10.3 Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation,” in <i>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2022), https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-3-muscle-fiber-contraction-and-relaxation. [Open access]","Lang Jiang et al., “Acute Interval Running Induces Greater Excess Post-Exercise Oxygen Consumption and Lipid Oxidation than Isocaloric Continuous Running in Men with Obesity,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 14 (2024): 9178, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-59893-9. [Open access]","Rachael M. Heuer et al., “Swimming Performance, Maximum O2 Consumption, EPOC, Post-Exercise Recovery and Tissue Metabolites after Fatigue by Ucrit versus Chase Protocols in Mahi-Mahi (<i>Coryphaena hippurus</i>), a High-Performance Pelagic Teleost,” <i>Journal of Experimental Biology</i> 229, no. 7 (2026): jeb251301, https://doi.org/10.1242/jeb.251301. [Open access]","George A. Brooks, “The Tortuous Path of Lactate Shuttle Discovery: From Cinders and Boards to the Lab and ICU,” <i>Journal of Sport and Health Science</i> 9, no. 5 (2020): 446–60, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jshs.2020.02.006. [Open access]","José Antonio Benítez-Muñoz and Rocío Cupeiro, “Factors Influencing Blood Lactate Concentration during Exercise: A Narrative Review with a Lactate Shuttle Perspective,” <i>Acta Physiologica</i> 241, no. 12 (2025): e70131, https://doi.org/10.1111/apha.70131. [Open access]","Jianxin Li et al., “Effects of Different Recovery and Regeneration Modalities on Delayed-Onset Muscle Soreness and Post-Exercise Muscle Damage Markers: A Systematic Review and Network Meta-Analysis,” <i>Frontiers in Bioengineering and Biotechnology</i> 14 (2026): 1956053, https://doi.org/10.3389/fbioe.2026.1956053. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do we keep breathing hard after exercise has stopped?","a":"Because recovery needs oxygen: to restore ATP and creatine phosphate in the muscles, to convert lactic acid back to pyruvic acid, and in the liver to turn lactate into glucose or glycogen. Other systems that worked during the exercise add their own demand.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is 'oxygen debt' now thought a misleading name?","a":"Because it carried a theory that most of the lactate made during exercise is rebuilt into glycogen afterwards. Isotope tracer studies found the reverse: most lactate is oxidised, much of it inside the muscles, and lactate forms continuously even when oxygen is plentiful.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Does lactic acid cause the muscle soreness felt the next day?","a":"That idea belongs to the old view of lactate as a dead-end waste. Delayed-onset soreness builds over hours to a peak between 24 and 72 hours after hard or unaccustomed exercise, and involves microscopic damage to the contractile units, inflammation and sensitised pain signalling.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Excess post-exercise oxygen consumption"},
    "bio-174": {"questions":["Smoke from house fires and badly processed cassava roots are among the commonest sources of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> poisoning, which doctors treat with hydroxocobalamin, a compound that binds the poison.","Victims of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> can look pink rather than blue, because their venous blood stays unusually rich in oxygen that the poisoned tissues are no longer taking up."],"abstract":"<b>Cyanide</b> means both the cyanide anion and undissociated hydrogen cyanide, and at the pH of the body it exists mainly as hydrogen cyanide, the volatile and more toxic form.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Its classic target is cytochrome c oxidase, or complex IV, the last enzyme of the mitochondrial electron transport chain, which hands electrons on to oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Cyanide binds the ferric iron of the enzyme's cytochrome a3 and copper centre and blocks oxygen from the catalytic site, so the chain can no longer pump protons and ATP synthesis fails.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Cells fall back on fermenting pyruvate to lactate, which builds up in the tissues and the plasma and causes acidosis, and organs with the highest energy demands are the most vulnerable.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In humans a lethal dose lies between about 0.5 and 3.5 milligrams per kilogram of body weight, and loss of consciousness followed by cardiac arrest can come within minutes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> Saying that cyanide starves cells of oxygen is right about the result and wrong about the picture, since the blood stays oxygenated and the raised oxygen in venous blood often shows as a pink skin tone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Nor is complex IV the whole story, because the concentration that kills far exceeds the one needed to inhibit it, and cyanide binds dozens of other enzymes, many of them more sensitive to it than cytochrome c oxidase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Plants are partly shielded by an alternative oxidase, a second terminal enzyme that passes electrons from ubiquinol straight to oxygen, so part of their oxygen uptake carries on when the cytochrome pathway is blocked, at the cost of much less ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The commonest exposures are smoke from house fires and cyanogenic plants, for several thousand species make cyanide precursors, and badly processed cassava, a staple for hundreds of millions, can cause the paralytic disease konzo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> The antidote hydroxocobalamin binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin, which is excreted in the urine, though the evidence for giving it to victims of smoke inhalation is still observational and its benefit there is unproven.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “7.4 Oxidative Phosphorylation,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-4-oxidative-phosphorylation. [Open access]","Vik S. Bebarta and Anjali K. Nath, “Redirecting Intermediary Metabolism to Counteract Cyanide Poisoning,” <i>FASEB Journal</i> 39, no. 12 (2025): e70709, https://doi.org/10.1096/fj.202400230rr. [Open access]","Joanna Izabela Lachowicz, Jan Alexander, and Jan O. Aaseth, “Cyanide and Cyanogenic Compounds: Toxicity, Molecular Targets, and Therapeutic Agents,” <i>Biomolecules</i> 14, no. 11 (2024): 1420, https://doi.org/10.3390/biom14111420. [Open access]","Elif Alan-Albayrak and Ulf Simonsen, “Cyanide beyond Toxicity: A Systematic Review of Its Effects on Vascular Function,” <i>Basic and Clinical Pharmacology and Toxicology</i> 137, no. 5 (2025): e70124, https://doi.org/10.1111/bcpt.70124. [Open access]","Robert Dunne et al., “Intravenous Hydroxocobalamin for Cyanide Poisoning from Smoke Inhalation: A Comprehensive Scoping Review,” <i>Journal of the American College of Emergency Physicians Open</i> 7, no. 2 (2026): 100340, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.acepjo.2026.100340. [Open access]","Wen-Yang Jin et al., “Evidence for Hydroxocobalamin in Cyanide Toxicity Caused by Smoke Inhalation: An Updated Systematic Review,” <i>Emergency Medicine International</i> 2025 (2025): 1779752, https://doi.org/10.1155/emmi/1779752. [Open access]","Greg C. Vanlerberghe, “Alternative Oxidase: A Mitochondrial Respiratory Pathway to Maintain Metabolic and Signaling Homeostasis during Abiotic and Biotic Stress in Plants,” <i>International Journal of Molecular Sciences</i> 14, no. 4 (2013): 6805–47, https://doi.org/10.3390/ijms14046805. [Open access]","Samantha E. Lieberman et al., “Transporter Editing in Cassava Indicates Local Production of Cyanogenic Glucosides in, and Export from, Cassava Roots,” <i>Plant Biotechnology Journal</i> 22, no. 4 (2024): 790–92, https://doi.org/10.1111/pbi.14257. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does blocking one enzyme stop the cell's ATP supply so fast?","a":"Because cytochrome c oxidase is the last step of the electron transport chain, handing electrons to oxygen. When cyanide binds its iron, oxygen cannot reach the catalytic site, the chain can no longer pump protons, and ATP synthase loses the gradient that drives it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can a cyanide victim look pink rather than blue?","a":"Because the blood is still carrying oxygen; it is the tissues that cannot use it. The oxygen saturation of venous blood rises, and that often appears as a pink skin tone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is cassava dangerous if it is not properly processed?","a":"Because it accumulates cyanogenic glucosides, chemical precursors of cyanide, which must be removed before it is eaten. Chronic exposure from poorly processed roots can damage the central nervous system and cause the paralytic disease konzo.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cyanide"},
    "bio-175": {"questions":["The dominant gut bacterium <i>Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron</i> is an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that stops growing almost as soon as it is aerated, even though it carries superoxide dismutase and catalase.","Facultative microbes that use up the oxygen in a wound or a mixed infection create a pocket in which an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, killed by oxygen, is able to grow and flourish."],"abstract":"An <b>obligate anaerobe</b> is a microorganism that is killed by oxygen, unlike an aerotolerant anaerobe, which is indifferent to it, or a facultative anaerobe, which grows with or without it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In a tube of thioglycolate medium, a reducing broth with most of its oxygen driven off by autoclaving, obligate anaerobes grow only at the bottom, out of reach of the oxygen slowly diffusing in from the top.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They live wherever oxygen is absent, in deep soil sediments, still waters and the deep ocean, and the Bacteroidetes among them make up a large fraction of the microbes in the human gut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Life without oxygen is the older condition, since prokaryotes lived for nearly a billion years before photosynthesis made significant amounts of it, and archaea still produce methane in anoxic sediments today.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Louis Pasteur first used the words aerobic and anaerobic, after watching some microbes lose their motility in oxygen, and growing strict anaerobes still needs special techniques such as the roll tube and sealed, gassed vessels.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> The usual textbook explanation is that obligate anaerobes die in air because they lack superoxide dismutase, catalase and peroxidase, the enzymes that destroy reactive oxygen species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> That is not the whole story, for the dominant gut anaerobe <i>Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron</i> has superoxide dismutase, a catalase and an NADH peroxidase, yet stops growing almost at once when aerated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Exposed to air, it produces reactive oxygen species about ten times faster than <i>Escherichia coli</i> does, and these attack its iron-sulphur enzymes while oxygen itself may directly damage a central enzyme of its metabolism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Some obligate anaerobes are not defenceless either, and <i>Clostridioides difficile</i>, a major cause of diarrhoea after antibiotics, carries four enzymes that reduce oxygen and so remove it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In soil, wounds and the gut they also rely on neighbours, as facultative anaerobes use up the oxygen and create local pockets where obligate anaerobes thrive, helped by the oxygen-consuming enzymes that many of them carry.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “9.2 Oxygen Requirements for Microbial Growth,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/9-2-oxygen-requirements-for-microbial-growth. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “22.3 Prokaryotic Metabolism,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-3-prokaryotic-metabolism. [Open access]","Zheng Lu and James A. Imlay, “The Fumarate Reductase of <i>Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron</i>, unlike That of <i>Escherichia coli</i>, Is Configured so that It Does Not Generate Reactive Oxygen Species,” <i>mBio</i> 8, no. 1 (2017): e01873-16, https://doi.org/10.1128/mbio.01873-16. [Open access]","James A. Imlay, “The Barrier Properties of Biological Membranes Dictate How Cells Experience Oxidative Stress,” <i>Molecular Microbiology</i> 123, no. 5 (2025): 454–63, https://doi.org/10.1111/mmi.15353. [Open access]","Léo C. Caulat et al., “Physiological Role and Complex Regulation of O2-Reducing Enzymes in the Obligate Anaerobe <i>Clostridioides difficile</i>,” <i>mBio</i> 15, no. 10 (2024): e01591-24, https://doi.org/10.1128/mbio.01591-24. [Open access]","Nikola Hanišáková, Monika Vítězová, and Simon K.-M. R. Rittmann, “The Historical Development of Cultivation Techniques for Methanogens and Other Strict Anaerobes and Their Application in Modern Microbiology,” <i>Microorganisms</i> 10, no. 2 (2022): 412, https://doi.org/10.3390/microorganisms10020412. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do obligate anaerobes grow only at the bottom of a thioglycolate tube?","a":"Because the medium is reducing and has had most of its oxygen driven off by autoclaving, and oxygen creeps back in only slowly from the top. Organisms killed by oxygen survive only in the deep zone it has not yet reached.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Is lacking superoxide dismutase and catalase what makes an anaerobe obligate?","a":"Not always. The gut anaerobe <i>Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron</i> has superoxide dismutase, a catalase and an NADH peroxidase, yet when aerated it makes reactive oxygen species about ten times faster than <i>E. coli</i>, and they damage enzymes central to its metabolism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are so many bacteria and archaea anaerobic at all?","a":"Because life began without oxygen. Prokaryotes lived for nearly a billion years before photosynthesis produced significant amounts of it, and many species of both bacteria and archaea are still anaerobic, playing important parts in the carbon and nitrogen cycles.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Obligate anaerobe"},
    "bio-176": {"questions":["Taking electrons from hydrogen sulphide or thiosulphate instead of water, some bacteria carry out a form of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that captures light energy without releasing any oxygen at all.","The coal and oil burned today hold energy that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> captured from sunlight between about 350 and 200 million years ago and stored in the bonds of carbon compounds."],"abstract":"<b>Photosynthesis</b> is the process by which the energy of sunlight is captured and stored in the bonds of sugars built from carbon dioxide and water, and it is the only biological process that turns sunlight into chemical compounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plants, algae and cyanobacteria carry it out, all human food traces back to it, and it powers about 99 per cent of Earth's ecosystems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In plants it takes place mainly in the chloroplasts of the mesophyll, the leaf's middle layer, which carbon dioxide reaches through the stomata before an intricate path to the enzyme RuBisCO inside the chloroplast.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> It runs in two stages, the light-dependent reactions in the thylakoid membranes using light to make ATP and NADPH, and the Calvin cycle in the stroma using those compounds to build sugar from carbon dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The energy released by burning coal and oil today is sunlight that photosynthesis captured and stored between about 350 and 200 million years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The summary equation is easy to misread as oxygen coming from carbon dioxide, but the oxygen released comes from water, split to replace the electrons that light drives out of chlorophyll in photosystem II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Splitting one water molecule yields two electrons and one atom of oxygen, so two must be split for each molecule of oxygen gas, and about a tenth of that oxygen is used by the leaf's own mitochondria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Light is spent lavishly, since hundreds of chlorophyll molecules serve each reaction centre and eight to twelve photons are needed for every molecule of oxygen released.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Not all photosynthesis gives off oxygen, because many bacteria take electrons from hydrogen sulphide or thiosulphate instead of water, producing sulphur or sulphate, and use only one of the two photosystems that plants and cyanobacteria use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The oxygen-releasing kind arose in a world without free oxygen, and today it supplies the oxygen that respiring organisms need, with phytoplankton and cyanobacteria in the oceans thought to produce between a third and a half of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “5.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/5-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","“Photosynthetic Cells,” <i>Nature Education</i>, Scitable (Nature Publishing Group, 2010), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/photosynthetic-cells-14025371/. [Open access]","Govindjee, “On the Evolution of the Concept of Two Light Reactions and Two Photosystems for Oxygenic Photosynthesis: A Personal Perspective,” <i>Photosynthetica</i> 61, no. 1 (2023): 37–47, https://doi.org/10.32615/ps.2023.006. [Open access]","James A. Imlay, “The Barrier Properties of Biological Membranes Dictate How Cells Experience Oxidative Stress,” <i>Molecular Microbiology</i> 123, no. 5 (2025): 454–63, https://doi.org/10.1111/mmi.15353. [Open access]","Marjorie R. Lundgren and Andrew J. Fleming, “Cellular Perspectives for Improving Mesophyll Conductance,” <i>Plant Journal</i> 101, no. 4 (2020): 845–57, https://doi.org/10.1111/tpj.14656. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Where does the oxygen given off by a plant come from?","a":"From water, not from carbon dioxide. Light drives electrons out of chlorophyll in photosystem II, and the missing electrons are replaced by splitting water, which releases oxygen; two water molecules are split for each molecule of oxygen gas.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Does all photosynthesis produce oxygen?","a":"No. Plants, algae and cyanobacteria split water and release oxygen, but other photosynthetic bacteria take electrons from hydrogen sulphide or thiosulphate instead, producing sulphur or sulphate and no oxygen, and they use only one photosystem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does nearly all life depend on photosynthesis?","a":"Because it is the only biological process that stores the energy of sunlight in chemical compounds. Heterotrophs eat the sugars that photoautotrophs make, or eat animals that did, so all the food humans eat traces back to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Photosynthesis"},
    "bio-177": {"questions":["Carbon dioxide inside a leaf must still cross cell walls and three membranes to reach RuBisCO, a resistance in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that can limit a crop's photosynthesis as much as the chloroplast's own chemistry.","In the Kranz anatomy of most C4 plants, cells of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> first capture carbon dioxide with PEP carboxylase and pass it inward to bundle-sheath cells holding RuBisCO."],"abstract":"The <b>mesophyll</b>, whose name means ‘middle leaf’, is the tissue between a leaf's upper and lower epidermis, and in plants it is the layer where photosynthesis takes place.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In the leaves of dicots it usually has two arrangements of cells, a palisade of tightly packed, column-shaped cells one to three layers deep and below it a spongy layer of loose, irregular cells.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Both layers contain many chloroplasts, and in plants the cells that carry chloroplasts lie in the mesophyll rather than on the surface of the leaf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The air spaces between the spongy cells connect with the stomata, allowing gases to pass between the leaf and the atmosphere, and in water plants the same spaces help the leaf float.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The two layers also share out the light, for chlorophyll in the palisade absorbs most of the red and blue, letting green light penetrate deeper to power chloroplasts in the spongy cells below.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Getting carbon dioxide from those air spaces to RuBisCO is harder than open stomata suggest, because the gas must cross the cell wall, the plasma membrane and both membranes of the chloroplast in turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Together these barriers define the mesophyll conductance, which is so hard to measure that standard deviations can reach nearly 40 per cent of the mean value.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In African cassava, RuBisCO activity and mesophyll conductance together accounted for 84 per cent of the limitation on photosynthesis at steady state, while the stomata were the main brake in fluctuating light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Raising mesophyll conductance could increase photosynthetic efficiency by an estimated 5 to 10 per cent, which is why the size, shape and spacing of mesophyll cells are now studied as targets for improving crops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In most C4 plants the tissue takes a different job, since in their Kranz anatomy the mesophyll cells hold PEP carboxylase and the bundle-sheath cells around the veins hold RuBisCO, where the breakdown of four-carbon acids raises the carbon dioxide around it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “30.4 Leaves,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/30-4-leaves. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “5.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/5-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Magdalena Trojak et al., “Effects of Partial Replacement of Red by Green Light in the Growth Spectrum on Photomorphogenesis and Photosynthesis in Tomato Plants,” <i>Photosynthesis Research</i> 151, no. 3 (2022): 295–312, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11120-021-00879-3. [Open access]","Marjorie R. Lundgren and Andrew J. Fleming, “Cellular Perspectives for Improving Mesophyll Conductance,” <i>Plant Journal</i> 101, no. 4 (2020): 845–57, https://doi.org/10.1111/tpj.14656. [Open access]","Amanda P. De Souza et al., “Photosynthesis across African Cassava Germplasm Is Limited by Rubisco and Mesophyll Conductance at Steady State, but by Stomatal Conductance in Fluctuating Light,” <i>New Phytologist</i> 225, no. 6 (2020): 2498–2512, https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.16142. [Open access]","Susanne von Caemmerer, Oula Ghannoum, and Robert T. Furbank, “C4 Photosynthesis: 50 Years of Discovery and Innovation,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 68, no. 2 (2017): 97–102, https://doi.org/10.1093/jxb/erw491. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a leaf have two different layers of mesophyll?","a":"Because they do different work. The palisade's tightly packed columns absorb most of the red and blue light, letting green light reach the spongy cells beneath, whose air spaces connect with the stomata for the exchange of gases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are open stomata not enough for fast photosynthesis?","a":"Because carbon dioxide must still diffuse from the air spaces through the cell wall, the plasma membrane and both chloroplast membranes to reach RuBisCO. In cassava, RuBisCO activity and this mesophyll conductance accounted for 84 per cent of the limitation on steady photosynthesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"How does the mesophyll of a C4 plant work differently?","a":"It captures carbon dioxide with PEP carboxylase, and the four-carbon acids are broken down in the gas-tight bundle-sheath cells around the veins, raising the carbon dioxide around RuBisCO there. The arrangement is called Kranz anatomy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}]},
    "bio-178": {"questions":["The early-branching cyanobacterium <i>Gloeobacter violaceus</i> has no <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> at all, so its photosynthetic and respiratory machinery must work in the single layer of its cell membrane.","Drawn in textbooks as separate stacked coins, each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in a land plant's chloroplast is in fact part of one continuous network of grana and connecting stroma lamellae."],"abstract":"A <b>thylakoid</b> is a flattened, disc-shaped membrane sac inside a chloroplast, and its membrane carries chlorophyll and the proteins that make up the photosynthetic electron transport chain.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The membrane encloses an internal space called the lumen, a stack of thylakoids is a granum, and the fluid around the grana is the stroma, where the Calvin cycle builds sugar.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Electron transport pumps protons from the stroma into the lumen, water splitting adds more, and ATP synthase uses the resulting gradient to make ATP, just as the intermembrane space of mitochondria does in respiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In land plants a single granum is usually 5 to 25 thylakoid layers with diameters between 300 and 550 nanometres, and the grana are linked by unstacked stroma lamellae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Stacking is not universal, since red algae have none, brown algae and diatoms group their membranes in twos or threes, and cyanobacteria and many algae have mainly single long lamellae.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> Electron micrographs make the stacks look like piles of coins, but the compartments they form are connected like a maze, and grana and stroma lamellae are continuous parts of one intertwined network.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> That network gives a land plant's chloroplast a huge surface of membrane for its metabolic work, and stroma thylakoids join neighbouring grana in a helical arrangement around their edges.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In mature chloroplasts and in cyanobacteria the thylakoids no longer seem to be joined to the inner envelope or the plasma membrane, whereas the internal membranes of bacteria with anoxygenic photosynthesis stay continuous with the plasma membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A flowering plant grown in darkness forms etioplasts instead of chloroplasts, holding a prolamellar body about 1 to 2 micrometres across, and light disperses it as the mature thylakoid system develops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The cyanobacterium <i>Gloeobacter violaceus</i>, from a lineage that branched off early, lacks thylakoids altogether, so its photosynthesis and respiration must operate in the single layer of its cell membrane, which limits how fast it can grow.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","“Photosynthetic Cells,” <i>Nature Education</i>, Scitable (Nature Publishing Group, 2010), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/photosynthetic-cells-14025371/. [Open access]","Annabel Mechela, Serena Schwenkert, and Jürgen Soll, “A Brief History of Thylakoid Biogenesis,” <i>Open Biology</i> 9, no. 1 (2019): 180237, https://doi.org/10.1098/rsob.180237. [Open access]","Radosław Mazur, Agnieszka Mostowska, and Łucja Kowalewska, “How to Measure Grana: Ultrastructural Features of Thylakoid Membranes of Plant Chloroplasts,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 12 (2021): 756009, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2021.756009. [Open access]","Jianfei Ma et al., “Light-Induced Structural Adaptation of the Bundle-Shaped Phycobilisome from Thylakoid-Lacking Cyanobacterium <i>Gloeobacter violaceus</i>,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 16 (2025): 5956, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-60673-w. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does the thylakoid lumen become acidic in the light?","a":"Because the electron transport chain pumps protons from the stroma into the lumen and water splitting adds more, leaving a low pH inside and a high pH in the stroma. ATP synthase uses that electrochemical gradient to make ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Are the stacks in a chloroplast really separate discs?","a":"No. They look like stacks of coins in electron micrographs, but the compartments they form are connected like a maze, and grana and stroma lamellae are continuous parts of one intertwined network of membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"What happens to a seedling's thylakoids in the dark?","a":"In flowering plants, proplastids kept without light become etioplasts rather than chloroplasts, holding a prolamellar body of plastid lipids and a chlorophyll precursor. Light disperses that body and the mature thylakoid system develops.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Thylakoid"},
    "bio-179": {"questions":["Bound into light-harvesting complexes, a <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> such as zeaxanthin passes absorbed energy to chlorophyll or sheds excess energy as heat, protecting the photosynthetic machinery from strong light.","Most animals must obtain every <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> they carry from their food, but the pea aphid makes its own, using genes that it acquired from fungi by horizontal gene transfer."],"abstract":"A <b>carotenoid</b> is one of a large family of yellow, orange and red pigments found in bacteria, archaea, fungi, algae, plants and animals, and more than 850 natural carotenoids of known structure have been identified.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their 40-carbon skeleton begins as phytoene, made by joining two 20-carbon units head to head, and they fall into carotenes and xanthophylls, which additionally carry one or more oxygen atoms.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With the chlorophylls they are one of the two major classes of photosynthetic pigment in plants and algae, absorbing short-wavelength blue light and reflecting yellow, orange and red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Most are bound to the proteins of reaction centres and light-harvesting complexes, where they gather light and pass the energy to chlorophyll, protect chlorophylls and proteins from strong light, and help hold the complexes together.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In the xanthophyll cycle zeaxanthin takes excess energy from excited chlorophyll and dissipates it as heat, which protects the leaf from the harm that too much light can do.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Carotenoids are in green leaves all the time, but an ordinary leaf contains more chlorophyll than carotenoid, and so it looks green.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In autumn the chlorophylls decline, and the yellows of carotenoids together with the reds of anthocyanins give the season its colours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Beyond the leaf they colour fruit as signals to the animals that disperse seeds, such as lycopene in tomatoes, zeaxanthin in maize and β-carotene in orange peel, and plants also make the hormones abscisic acid and strigolactones from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The usual rule is that animals cannot make carotenoids and must get them from their food, and it holds for most animals.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The pea aphid is a striking exception, having acquired carotenoid-making genes from fungi by horizontal gene transfer, and the green forms of the aphid dominate in cooler climates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Shinichi Takaichi, “Distribution, Biosynthesis, and Function of Carotenoids in Oxygenic Phototrophic Algae,” <i>Marine Drugs</i> 23, no. 2 (2025): 62, https://doi.org/10.3390/md23020062. [Open access]","Jin Miao et al., “Horizontally Transferred Carotenoid Genes Associated with Light-Driven ATP Synthesis to Promote Cold Adaptation in Pea Aphid, <i>Acyrthosiphon pisum</i>,” <i>Insects</i> 16, no. 10 (2025): 1013, https://doi.org/10.3390/insects16101013. [Open access]","Farhat Abbas et al., “Anthocyanins, Carotenoids, and Betalains in Plants: From Biosynthesis to Function,” <i>Plants</i> 15, no. 15 (2026): 2303, https://doi.org/10.3390/plants15152303. [Open access]","Dai Koide et al., “Autumn Leaf Color Brightness of Japanese Alpine Vegetation Is Projected to Decrease under Future Climate Change,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 15 (2025): 29064, https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-14547-2. [Open access]","Yanlong Wang et al., “Transcriptomic and Metabolomic Analysis of Autumn Leaf Color Change in <i>Fraxinus angustifolia</i>,” <i>PeerJ</i> 11 (2023): e15319, https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj.15319. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why are leaves green if they contain yellow and orange pigments?","a":"Because an ordinary leaf holds more chlorophyll than carotenoid. When the chlorophylls decline in autumn, the yellows of the carotenoids, together with the reds of anthocyanins, give the season its colours.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"What do carotenoids do in photosynthesis?","a":"They harvest light and pass the energy to chlorophyll, protect chlorophylls and proteins from strong light, and help assemble and stabilise the pigment-protein complexes. In the xanthophyll cycle zeaxanthin dissipates excess energy as heat.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Can animals make carotenoids?","a":"Most cannot and must obtain them from their diet. The pea aphid is a striking exception: it acquired carotenoid-making genes from fungi by horizontal gene transfer and synthesises carotenoids itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Carotenoid"},
    "bio-180": {"questions":["In each reaction centre a special pair of molecules of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> a gives up an electron when excited, the step at which absorbed light becomes chemical energy.","Some cyanobacteria make forms of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> labelled d and f, which absorb far-red light at longer wavelengths than the a and b forms found in green plants."],"abstract":"<b>Chlorophyll</b> is the green pigment of photosynthesis, embedded in the thylakoid membranes of the chloroplast, where it makes the first interaction between light and the plant.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chlorophyll a is the major pigment, and chlorophyll b, which absorbs blue and red-orange light, is one of several pigments that help channel light energy to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light most strongly and reflects the green between them, which is why the leaves of most plants look green to us.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Each photosystem is served by a light-harvesting complex of 300 to 400 chlorophyll a and b molecules mixed with other pigments, which pass absorbed energy inward to a reaction centre.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There a special pair of chlorophyll a molecules gives up an electron when excited, the step at which light energy becomes an excited electron that will end up stored in carbohydrate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Because chlorophyll reflects green, it is widely assumed that leaves absorb little green light, but that is a misconception drawn from the weak green absorption of chlorophyll extracted from leaves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> In the intact leaf, palisade chlorophyll takes most of the red and blue near the surface, so green light penetrates deeper and drives photosynthesis in the chloroplasts of the spongy mesophyll.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Long before its structures were known, measurements showed that hundreds of chlorophyll molecules serve each reaction centre and that eight to twelve photons are needed for each molecule of oxygen released.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Chlorophyll is not one molecule but a family, and many photosynthetic bacteria use relatives called bacteriochlorophylls, which may be green, purple or red.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Some cyanobacteria also make chlorophylls d and f, which absorb far-red light, and chlorophyll f absorbs most strongly at 707 nanometres against 665 for chlorophyll a.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “5.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/5-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","“Photosynthetic Cells,” <i>Nature Education</i>, Scitable (Nature Publishing Group, 2010), https://www.nature.com/scitable/topicpage/photosynthetic-cells-14025371/. [Open access]","Govindjee, “On the Evolution of the Concept of Two Light Reactions and Two Photosystems for Oxygenic Photosynthesis: A Personal Perspective,” <i>Photosynthetica</i> 61, no. 1 (2023): 37–47, https://doi.org/10.32615/ps.2023.006. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Magdalena Trojak et al., “Effects of Partial Replacement of Red by Green Light in the Growth Spectrum on Photomorphogenesis and Photosynthesis in Tomato Plants,” <i>Photosynthesis Research</i> 151, no. 3 (2022): 295–312, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11120-021-00879-3. [Open access]","Man Qi et al., “Enhancing the Production of Chlorophyll f in the Cyanobacterium <i>Synechocystis</i> sp. PCC 6803,” <i>Physiologia Plantarum</i> 177, no. 2 (2025): e70169, https://doi.org/10.1111/ppl.70169. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do leaves look green?","a":"Because chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light most strongly and reflects green. Leaves still use green light, though: palisade chlorophyll takes most of the red and blue, so green penetrates deeper and drives photosynthesis in the spongy mesophyll.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a photosystem need hundreds of chlorophyll molecules?","a":"Because most of them act as an antenna. A light-harvesting complex of 300 to 400 chlorophyll a and b molecules passes absorbed energy to the reaction centre, where a special pair of chlorophyll a molecules gives up an electron.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Do all photosynthetic organisms use the same chlorophyll?","a":"No. Plants use chlorophylls a and b, many photosynthetic bacteria use bacteriochlorophylls that may be green, purple or red, and some cyanobacteria also make chlorophylls d and f, which absorb far-red light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chlorophyll"},
    "bio-181": {"questions":["Measured on whole leaves rather than on pigment extracts, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of photosynthesis shows green light driving real carbon dioxide uptake, which corrects a common classroom belief.","The steep fall beyond about 685 nanometres in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of photosynthesis, and the extra output when two beams are mixed, together pointed to two photosystems working in series."],"abstract":"An <b>action spectrum</b> is a measurement of how strongly a biological process responds to light of each wavelength, and for photosynthesis it plots the rate of activity against the colour of the light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is a different thing from an absorption spectrum, which records only the wavelengths a pigment takes in and is usually measured on an extract in a spectrophotometer.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Chlorophylls absorb strongly in the blue and the red and weakly in the green, which is why they look green, while carotenoids take light only from the blue and green end.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The first action spectrum of photosynthesis was taken in the early 1880s, when a prism spread sunlight across a filament of green alga held in a chamber with bacteria that swim towards oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The bacteria gathered around the alga in proportion to the local oxygen concentration, so their crowds along the filament gave a direct readout of photosynthesis at each colour.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Read against the absorption curve of a chlorophyll extract, the action spectrum seems to say that green light is nearly useless, and that belief still appears in print.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Action spectra measured on whole leaves have long shown green light driving carbon dioxide uptake, if less efficiently per photon arriving than red light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> A lettuce leaf absorbs about 81 per cent of green light against more than 91 per cent of red or blue, and green light reaches deeper into the leaf, where it is used efficiently in strong light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Every photosynthetic action spectrum also falls away sharply beyond about 685 nm, a 'red drop' first measured in the alga <i>Chlorella</i> in the early 1940s.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Far-red light added to shorter wavelengths gives more photosynthesis than the two beams give apart, and that enhancement led to the discovery of two photosystems working in series.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["Praneet Prakash, Yasa Baig, François J. Peaudecerf, and Raymond E. Goldstein, “Dynamics of an Algae–Bacteria Microcosm: Photosynthesis, Chemotaxis, and Expulsion in Inhomogeneous Active Matter,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 122, no. 12 (2025): e2410225122, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11962504/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Jun Liu and Marc W. van Iersel, “Photosynthetic Physiology of Blue, Green, and Red Light: Light Intensity Effects and Underlying Mechanisms,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 12 (2021): 619987, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2021.619987. [Open access]","G. Govindjee, “On the Evolution of the Concept of Two Light Reactions and Two Photosystems for Oxygenic Photosynthesis: A Personal Perspective,” <i>Photosynthetica</i> 61, no. 1 (2023): 37–47, https://doi.org/10.32615/ps.2023.006. [Open access]","Craig R. Taylor, Wim van Ieperen, and Jeremy Harbinson, “Greater Than the Sum of the Parts: Revisiting the Enhancement Effect in Photosynthesis Using Simulated Sun- and Shade-Light,” <i>Plant, Cell & Environment</i> 48, no. 8 (2025), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223708/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is an action spectrum not the same as an absorption spectrum?","a":"Because an absorption spectrum records only which wavelengths a pigment takes in, measured on an extract in a spectrophotometer,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> while an action spectrum measures the work actually done by the living system at each wavelength, the rate of photosynthesis itself.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How could bacteria reveal which colours of light an alga used?","a":"Because the bacteria swam towards oxygen and gathered around the alga in proportion to the local oxygen concentration, so where they crowded along the filament showed where each colour of the spread-out sunlight was driving photosynthesis fastest.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Is it true that plants cannot use green light?","a":"No. Whole leaves absorb most of the green light that reaches them, about 81 per cent in lettuce, and green light penetrates deeper into the leaf and helps drive carbon dioxide uptake efficiently in strong light. The belief comes from measurements on algae and pigment extracts, which absorb green poorly.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Action spectrum"},
    "bio-182": {"questions":["Isotope studies of the early 1940s showed that the oxygen released by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> comes from water rather than from carbon dioxide, overturning an older and widely held assumption.","Because the carriers made by the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> last only millionths of a second, the carbon-fixing reactions long called 'dark' in fact depend on light being present."],"abstract":"The <b>light-dependent reactions</b> are the first stage of photosynthesis, the steps in which light energy absorbed by pigments is turned into chemical energy held in two short-lived carriers, NADPH and ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In plants and algae they take place in the thylakoid membranes inside the chloroplast, while the fixing of carbon dioxide into sugar happens in the surrounding fluid, the stroma.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Two light-driven protein complexes, photosystem II and photosystem I, act in series and pass electrons from water towards the carrier NADP+, a route called the Z-scheme after its shape on a diagram.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Water is split to replace the electrons lost by photosystem II, and its oxygen is released as a by-product, about a tenth of it used by the leaf's own mitochondria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Hydrogen ions build up inside the thylakoid, and their flow back out through the enzyme ATP synthase attaches a third phosphate to ADP, a process called photophosphorylation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The textbook picture of a 'light' stage feeding a separate 'dark' stage misleads, because the two run together in daylight and each depends on the other.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The carriers the light reactions make last only millionths of a second, so the sugar-building reactions are themselves light dependent, needing a constant fresh supply.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Several enzymes of carbon fixation are also switched on by changes the light reactions make in the stroma, its rising pH and magnesium and the redox state sensed by a protein called thioredoxin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An older idea, that the oxygen given off comes from carbon dioxide, was challenged in the early 1940s by isotope studies finding the released oxygen nearly identical in composition to the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The proton gradient the reactions build both drives ATP synthase and, as the inner space turns acidic, triggers a mechanism that harmlessly dissipates excess absorbed energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “5.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/5-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.3 Using Light Energy to Make Organic Molecules,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","David J. Vinyard and Govindjee Govindjee, “Bicarbonate Is a Key Regulator but Not a Substrate for O2 Evolution in Photosystem II,” <i>Photosynthesis Research</i> 162, no. 1 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1007/s11120-024-01111-8. [Open access]","Atsuko Kanazawa et al., “Chloroplast ATP Synthase Modulation of the Thylakoid Proton Motive Force: Implications for Photosystem I and Photosystem II Photoprotection,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 8 (2017): 719, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2017.00719. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is it misleading to call the sugar-making stage the dark reactions?","a":"Because it depends on the light reactions running at the same time: the ATP and NADPH they supply last only millionths of a second,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and light-driven changes in the stroma's pH, magnesium and redox state switch its enzymes on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Where does the oxygen a plant releases come from?","a":"From water. Photosystem II replaces the electrons that light drives out of its chlorophyll by taking them from water, leaving oxygen behind,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and isotope studies in the early 1940s found the released oxygen nearly identical in composition to the water rather than to carbon dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do the light reactions build a proton gradient?","a":"Because the gradient across the thylakoid membrane stores the energy that ATP synthase uses to attach phosphate to ADP,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and the acidity of the inner space also signals when light is in excess and switches on protective dissipation of energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Photosystem"},
    "bio-183": {"questions":["Each <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> gathers light with an antenna of hundreds of pigment molecules and funnels the energy to a special pair of chlorophylls, where an electron leaves for an acceptor.","Far-red light working poorly alone but well with shorter wavelengths was the clue that a chloroplast uses more than one <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the two working in series from water to NADP+."],"abstract":"A <b>photosystem</b> is a large complex of proteins and pigments embedded in the thylakoid membrane, and it is where the energy of light is first converted into chemical energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each one has an antenna of light-harvesting complexes holding hundreds of chlorophyll and carotenoid molecules, which pass the energy of a captured photon from pigment to pigment.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The energy is funnelled to a special pair of chlorophylls in the reaction centre, and there, as nowhere in the antenna, an excited electron actually leaves its molecule for an acceptor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Photosystem II, whose special pair is called P680 after its absorption peak at 680 nm, refills that pair with electrons taken from water and so releases oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Photosystem I, called P700 for its peak at 700 nm, takes the electrons after a chain of carriers and boosts them high enough to reduce the iron-sulphur protein ferredoxin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> The numbering looks backwards, since electrons go through photosystem II first, but the two were numbered in the order of their discovery, not the order in which they work.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The idea of two light reactions grew in the late 1950s from the finding that far-red light drives photosynthesis poorly on its own but well when mixed with shorter wavelengths.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Key evidence for two systems in series came in 1961, when light of one colour oxidised a carrier between them, cytochrome f, and light of another colour reduced it again.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Breaking chloroplasts into fragments and separating them in a centrifuge then showed that different portions made different products, confirming that there are two photosystems.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Two are needed because moving electrons from water to NADP+ requires two inputs of light energy, and anoxygenic bacteria, which take electrons from easier donors, manage with one.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “5.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/5-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","G. Govindjee, “On the Evolution of the Concept of Two Light Reactions and Two Photosystems for Oxygenic Photosynthesis: A Personal Perspective,” <i>Photosynthetica</i> 61, no. 1 (2023): 37–47, https://doi.org/10.32615/ps.2023.006. [Open access]","Craig R. Taylor, Wim van Ieperen, and Jeremy Harbinson, “Greater Than the Sum of the Parts: Revisiting the Enhancement Effect in Photosynthesis Using Simulated Sun- and Shade-Light,” <i>Plant, Cell & Environment</i> 48, no. 8 (2025), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12223708/. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is photosystem II numbered two when it acts first?","a":"Because the photosystems were numbered in the order of their discovery, not in the order in which electrons pass through them on their way from water to NADP+.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a photosystem need hundreds of antenna pigments?","a":"Because the special pair of chlorophylls on its own absorbs far less light, about a hundredth, than it could process; the antenna widens both the area and the range of colours from which energy is collected and funnelled to it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do plants need two photosystems rather than one?","a":"Because moving an electron from water, a very poor donor, all the way to NADP+ needs two inputs of light energy;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> bacteria that use easier donors such as hydrogen sulphide release no oxygen and use only one photosystem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Photosystem"},
    "bio-184": {"questions":["In cyanobacteria, algae and plants, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of water supplies electrons to photosystem II, while bacteria using hydrogen sulphide as their donor release no oxygen at all.","Despite its name, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> of water is not done by light directly but by an oxidised chlorophyll pair and a manganese and calcium cluster on photosystem II."],"abstract":"The <b>photolysis</b> of water is the step in photosynthesis in which water molecules are split into oxygen, protons and electrons, using energy that light delivers to photosystem II.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each water molecule broken releases a pair of electrons to replace those that light drives out of the reaction-centre chlorophyll, and its hydrogen ions are left in the thylakoid's inner space.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Those protons add to the gradient across the thylakoid membrane that powers the making of ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Splitting two molecules of water yields one molecule of oxygen gas, and about a tenth of that oxygen is used by the leaf's own mitochondria while the rest escapes to the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Organisms that split water this way carry out oxygenic photosynthesis, while other bacteria use donors such as hydrogen sulphide and release no oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The name suggests that light breaks water directly, but it does not: light ejects an electron from the chlorophyll pair P680, leaving an oxidant strong enough to pull electrons from water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The splitting itself is done by a cluster of four manganese ions and one calcium ion linked by oxygen bridges, bound to the side of photosystem II facing the thylakoid interior.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Each flash of light moves only one electron while a molecule of oxygen takes four, so the cluster stores charge step by step and releases oxygen on every fourth flash, a pattern first measured in 1969.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That water rather than carbon dioxide is the source of the oxygen was concluded in the early 1940s, when isotope measurements found the released oxygen nearly identical in composition to the water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> A rival view that bicarbonate supplies the oxygen persisted for decades and has recently resurfaced, but researchers now agree that bicarbonate regulates photosystem II without being split.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “5.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/5-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Atsuko Kanazawa et al., “Chloroplast ATP Synthase Modulation of the Thylakoid Proton Motive Force: Implications for Photosystem I and Photosystem II Photoprotection,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 8 (2017): 719, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2017.00719. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Wolfgang Lubitz, Maria Chrysina, and Nicholas Cox, “Water Oxidation in Photosystem II,” <i>Photosynthesis Research</i> 142, no. 1 (2019), https://doi.org/10.1007/s11120-019-00648-3. [Open access]","David J. Vinyard and Govindjee Govindjee, “Bicarbonate Is a Key Regulator but Not a Substrate for O2 Evolution in Photosystem II,” <i>Photosynthesis Research</i> 162, no. 1 (2024), https://doi.org/10.1007/s11120-024-01111-8. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does it take four flashes of light to release one oxygen molecule?","a":"Because each flash drives only one electron out of photosystem II, while making one oxygen molecule from two waters removes four electrons;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> the manganese cluster stores the charges one at a time and releases oxygen on the fourth.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the name photolysis slightly misleading?","a":"Because light does not break the water itself: it removes an electron from the chlorophyll pair P680, and the resulting strong oxidant, acting through the manganese cluster as a catalyst, pulls the electrons from water.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"How do we know the oxygen comes from water and not carbon dioxide?","a":"Isotope studies in the early 1940s found the oxygen plants release nearly identical in composition to the water they were given, and although a bicarbonate theory persisted for decades, researchers now agree the oxygen comes from water alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Photodissociation"},
    "bio-185": {"questions":["Protons flowing back through a rotor ring of 14 subunits in the thylakoid membrane power <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the chloroplast's way of making ATP from the energy of absorbed light.","When only photosystem I is used, cyclic <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> makes ATP without NADPH, while the noncyclic form, running from water to NADP+, yields both carriers together."],"abstract":"<b>Photophosphorylation</b> is the making of ATP from ADP and phosphate with energy captured from light, the photosynthetic counterpart of the oxidative phosphorylation that runs in mitochondria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Electron flow through the thylakoid membrane, together with the splitting of water, loads protons into the thylakoid's inner space, leaving it acidic and the surrounding stroma alkaline.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That proton motive force has two parts, a difference in proton concentration and an electrical field across the membrane, and both drive the synthesis of ATP.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Protons return to the stroma through ATP synthase, a rotary motor whose membrane rotor in the chloroplast is a ring of 14 subunits, and at full rate it makes more than 200 ATP molecules a second.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In noncyclic photophosphorylation electrons pass from water to NADP+ and both ATP and NADPH are made, while the cyclic form uses only photosystem I and yields ATP alone.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> When a light-driven process making ATP without oxygen release was found in isolated chloroplasts in 1954, the very idea that chloroplasts made ATP met with scepticism.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> How light could make ATP was explained by the chemiosmotic theory, which treated the chloroplast's electron chain, like the respiratory chain of mitochondria, as a carrier of protons across a membrane.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Fast electrical changes measured across illuminated thylakoid membranes in the 1960s supported that picture, and its central principle has since been amply confirmed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The proton gradient is also a signal, because an acidic inner space triggers the dissipation of excess absorbed energy and slows electron flow, protecting both photosystems in strong light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> In darkness the same enzyme could turn backwards and break down ATP, and a light-dependent chemical switch in one of its subunits restrains it at night.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.2 The Light-Dependent Reactions of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Atsuko Kanazawa et al., “Chloroplast ATP Synthase Modulation of the Thylakoid Proton Motive Force: Implications for Photosystem I and Photosystem II Photoprotection,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 8 (2017): 719, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2017.00719. [Open access]","Jay-How Yang, Dewight Williams, Eaazhisai Kandiah, Petra Fromme, and Po-Lin Chiu, “Structural Basis of Redox Modulation on Chloroplast ATP Synthase,” <i>Communications Biology</i> 3 (2020): 482, https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-020-01221-8. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Dario Leister and Toshiharu Shikanai, “Complexities and Protein Complexes in the Antimycin A-Sensitive Pathway of Cyclic Electron Flow in Plants,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 4 (2013): 161, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2013.00161. [Open access]","Peter Mitchell, “David Keilin’s Respiratory Chain Concept and Its Chemiosmotic Consequences,” Nobel Lecture, 8 December 1978, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/mitchell-lecture.pdf. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How does a proton gradient let light make ATP?","a":"Light-driven electron flow and the splitting of water pack protons into the thylakoid's inner space,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and their return to the stroma through the rotary ATP synthase turns the motor that joins phosphate to ADP, more than 200 times a second at full rate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was ATP-making in chloroplasts doubted at first?","a":"Because the claim was new: when a light-driven process making ATP in isolated chloroplasts was reported in 1954, the concept of ATP production in chloroplasts met scepticism, which has since disappeared as the evidence accumulated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must the chloroplast's ATP synthase be restrained at night?","a":"Because without a light-driven proton gradient the motor can rotate in the opposite direction and hydrolyse the ATP made during the day; a light-dependent change in one of its subunits limits that wasteful reverse action in the dark.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Photophosphorylation"},
    "bio-186": {"questions":["Because the Calvin cycle needs ATP and NADPH in a ratio of 1.5 while the linear route supplies less, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is thought to make up the chloroplast's shortfall of ATP.","Rice plants impaired in the main route of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> suffered damage to photosystem I under fluctuating light and grew less, showing that the pathway also protects the photosynthetic machinery."],"abstract":"<b>Cyclic electron flow</b> is a route in the light reactions of photosynthesis in which electrons from photosystem I are passed from ferredoxin back to plastoquinone instead of on to NADP+.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> On the way round they pass through the cytochrome b6f complex, which still moves protons into the thylakoid's inner space, so ATP is made although no NADPH is produced.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The more familiar linear route takes electrons from water to NADP+, and a cell whose need for ATP outweighs its need for NADPH can run more of the cyclic route.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Flowering plants have two partly redundant cyclic routes, a main one depending on the proteins PGR5 and PGRL1 and a minor one through a complex resembling a respiratory NADH dehydrogenase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> A light-driven process making ATP without releasing oxygen was identified in isolated chloroplasts in 1954, and it was later found to need ferredoxin and to be blocked by the inhibitor antimycin A.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The usual case for the pathway is arithmetic: each three-carbon sugar built in the Calvin cycle costs ATP and NADPH in a ratio of 1.5, while linear flow is thought to supply about 1.28.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In C3 plants roughly a tenth of all electron flow is estimated to be cyclic, though the share may be much higher when light first comes on or under certain stresses.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Its second role is protective, because the proton gradient it builds slows electron flow at the cytochrome complex and switches on the dissipation of excess energy, shielding photosystem I in sudden bright light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Rice plants with the main route impaired lost photosynthetic rate under fluctuating light, suffered damage at photosystem I and grew less, and in <i>Arabidopsis</i> the same protein is essential for survival in the field.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The pathway is still hard to measure directly in leaves, and exactly how PGR5 and PGRL1 take part in it remains debated.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Wataru Yamori, Amane Makino, and Toshiharu Shikanai, “A Physiological Role of Cyclic Electron Transport around Photosystem I in Sustaining Photosynthesis under Fluctuating Light in Rice,” <i>Scientific Reports</i> 6 (2016): 20147, https://doi.org/10.1038/srep20147. [Open access]","Dario Leister and Toshiharu Shikanai, “Complexities and Protein Complexes in the Antimycin A-Sensitive Pathway of Cyclic Electron Flow in Plants,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 4 (2013): 161, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2013.00161. [Open access]","Marjaana Suorsa, “Cyclic Electron Flow Provides Acclimatory Plasticity for the Photosynthetic Machinery under Various Environmental Conditions and Developmental Stages,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 6 (2015): 800, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2015.00800. [Open access]","Atsuko Kanazawa et al., “Chloroplast ATP Synthase Modulation of the Thylakoid Proton Motive Force: Implications for Photosystem I and Photosystem II Photoprotection,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 8 (2017): 719, https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2017.00719. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"How can cyclic flow make ATP without making NADPH?","a":"Its electrons go from ferredoxin back to plastoquinone instead of to NADP+, yet they still pass through the cytochrome b6f complex, which moves protons into the thylakoid and so builds the gradient that drives ATP synthase.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does a chloroplast need extra ATP at all?","a":"Because the Calvin cycle spends 9 ATP and 6 NADPH for each three-carbon sugar, a ratio of 1.5, while linear electron flow from water to NADP+ is thought to supply them in a ratio of only about 1.28.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does losing the pathway hurt plants most in flickering light?","a":"Because a sudden burst of light can overload photosystem I; the proton gradient from cyclic flow slows the electron supply to it,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and rice lacking the main route suffered photosystem I damage and grew less under fluctuating light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Photochemistry"},
    "bio-187": {"questions":["Besides the Calvin cycle used by plants and algae, six further pathways of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> are known, and every one of them is found only in bacteria and archaea.","Often called the dark reactions, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stage of photosynthesis in fact depends on light, since its enzymes are switched on by light-driven changes in the chloroplast stroma."],"abstract":"<b>Carbon fixation</b> is the conversion of inorganic carbon, as carbon dioxide, into organic molecules, and it is the step through which carbon enters the living world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In plants it happens in the stroma of the chloroplast, where the enzyme RuBisCO joins carbon dioxide to a five-carbon sugar and the product splits into two three-carbon molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Photosynthesis converts about 200 billion tonnes of carbon dioxide a year into organic compounds, and it is the primary input of energy to the global food chain on which nearly all living things depend.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Most of that passes through the Calvin–Benson–Bassham cycle, run on land mainly by plants and in the oceans mainly by microscopic algae and cyanobacteria, which contribute about equally to global production.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Six other pathways are known, all found only in bacteria and archaea, among them the reverse tricarboxylic acid cycle and the reductive acetyl-CoA pathway, and genome surveys keep finding them in more groups than expected.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The reactions are often called light-independent or 'dark' reactions, a label that misleads because it implies they happen at night or without light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The ATP and NADPH they spend last only millionths of a second, so fixation in a plant keeps going only while the light reactions keep supplying them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Light also switches the enzymes on, by raising the pH and the magnesium level of the stroma and changing its redox state through a protein called thioredoxin.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Not all fixation needs light, because many chemoautotrophs drive the Calvin cycle with energy from chemical reactions, and most bacteria using the alternative pathways are chemoautotrophic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Researchers have also assembled new carbon-fixing cycles from enzymes in the laboratory, and one was made roughly ten times more productive by machine-learning-guided optimisation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.3 Using Light Energy to Make Organic Molecules,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “5.3 The Calvin Cycle,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/5-3-the-calvin-cycle. [Open access]","Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Alessandro N. Garritano, Weizhi Song, and Torsten Thomas, “Carbon Fixation Pathways across the Bacterial and Archaeal Tree of Life,” <i>PNAS Nexus</i> 1, no. 5 (2022): pgac226, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9802188/. [Open access]","Yinon M. Bar-On and Ron Milo, “The Global Mass and Average Rate of Rubisco,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 10 (2019): 4738–43, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410859/. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Sarah Bierbaumer et al., “Enzymatic Conversion of CO2: From Natural to Artificial Utilization,” <i>Chemical Reviews</i> 123, no. 9 (2023), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10176493/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is 'dark reactions' a misleading name for carbon fixation in plants?","a":"Because the reactions depend on ATP and NADPH from the light reactions, which last only millionths of a second,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and light-driven changes in the stroma's pH, magnesium and redox state switch the enzymes on.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"How can some organisms fix carbon in complete darkness?","a":"Chemoautotrophs power fixation with energy from chemical reactions rather than light. Many of them use the Calvin cycle itself, and most bacteria that use the alternative fixation pathways are chemoautotrophic.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does most of the world's carbon fixation pass through one cycle?","a":"Because plants on land and algae and cyanobacteria in the sea, which share global production roughly equally, all run the Calvin–Benson–Bassham cycle,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> whose enzyme RuBisCO carries out the vast majority of global carbon fixation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Biological carbon fixation"},
    "bio-188": {"questions":["Of every six three-carbon sugars made in the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, only one is exported, while the other five are rebuilt into the five-carbon acceptor that takes up carbon dioxide.","Switching off the light made phosphoglyceric acid pile up while ribulose diphosphate vanished, linking the two compounds as stages of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> that fixes carbon dioxide."],"abstract":"The <b>Calvin cycle</b> is the circular pathway that turns carbon dioxide into a three-carbon sugar, running in the chloroplast stroma of plants and algae and in the cytoplasm of photosynthetic bacteria.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It runs in three stages, fixation, reduction and regeneration, beginning when the enzyme RuBisCO joins carbon dioxide to the five-carbon sugar ribulose bisphosphate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The six-carbon product splits at once into two molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate, which ATP and NADPH then reduce to glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate in steps that reverse part of glycolysis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Of every six molecules of that sugar made from three carbon dioxides, only one leaves the cycle, and the other five are rebuilt into three molecules of ribulose bisphosphate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Fixing six carbons, enough for one glucose, takes 18 ATP and 12 NADPH, and most of the world's carbon dioxide is thought to be fixed through this pathway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The pathway was traced in the green alga <i>Chlorella</i>, given carbon dioxide labelled with carbon-14 for set times before its compounds were separated on paper and located with photographic film.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In exposures of a fraction of a second, a single compound held eighty or ninety per cent of the fixed radioactive carbon, and that compound was phosphoglyceric acid.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> When the light was switched off, phosphoglyceric acid briefly piled up while ribulose diphosphate disappeared, which clearly linked the two in a cycle driven by products of the light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Several of its enzymes also work in glycolysis, and changes the light makes in the stroma's pH, magnesium and redox state regulate them, so that sugar is not built and broken down at once.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Regenerating ribulose bisphosphate can limit the cycle, and boosting that stage has raised photosynthesis in experimental plants, including tobacco given extra copies of regeneration enzymes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup>","sources":["Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.3 Using Light Energy to Make Organic Molecules,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules. [Open access]","Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “5.3 The Calvin Cycle,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/5-3-the-calvin-cycle. [Open access]","Alessandro N. Garritano, Weizhi Song, and Torsten Thomas, “Carbon Fixation Pathways across the Bacterial and Archaeal Tree of Life,” <i>PNAS Nexus</i> 1, no. 5 (2022): pgac226, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9802188/. [Open access]","Melvin Calvin, “The Path of Carbon in Photosynthesis,” Nobel Lecture, 11 December 1961, NobelPrize.org, https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2017/03/calvin-lecture.pdf. [Open access]","Roberta Croce et al., “Perspectives on Improving Photosynthesis to Increase Crop Yield,” <i>The Plant Cell</i> 36, no. 10 (2024), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11449117/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the pathway called a cycle?","a":"Because five of every six three-carbon sugars it makes are used to rebuild ribulose bisphosphate, the five-carbon molecule that accepts carbon dioxide, so the starting material is regenerated on every turn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why were the algae exposed to labelled carbon dioxide for so short a time?","a":"Because in 30 seconds the label had already spread into a wide variety of compounds, and only by shortening the exposure to fractions of a second could the first product, phosphoglyceric acid, be seen holding most of it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the cycle not simply run on in the dark?","a":"Because it depends on ATP and NADPH from the light reactions; pre-illuminated algae could fix some carbon dioxide briefly in the dark from stored compounds,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> but light-driven changes in the stroma also regulate the cycle's enzymes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Calvin cycle"},
    "bio-189": {"questions":["Managing only one to ten reactions a second and sometimes seizing oxygen instead of carbon dioxide, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> makes up a large share of all the soluble protein in a leaf.","Speed and a preference for carbon dioxide over oxygen that vary little across some 300 organisms suggest that <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is tightly constrained by the chemistry of telling the two gases apart."],"abstract":"<b>RuBisCO</b>, ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase, is the enzyme that adds carbon dioxide to the five-carbon sugar ribulose bisphosphate, the step at which carbon enters the Calvin cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It comes in several forms, and the one in plants, green algae and cyanobacteria is a complex of eight large and eight small subunits whose large subunits are encoded in the chloroplast's own genome.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> It is slow, since more than 95 per cent of measured rates fall between one and ten reactions a second, while a typical enzyme of central metabolism manages about eighty.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It also reacts with oxygen in about a fifth of its catalytic cycles, yielding 2-phosphoglycolate, which has to be recycled at a cost in energy and carbon through photorespiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Leaves carry it in great quantity, from about a fifth to a half of their soluble protein by different estimates.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> RuBisCO is often called the most abundant protein on Earth, a claim that rested for decades on a single-paragraph estimate of about 0.04 billion tonnes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> A recalculation in 2019 put its total mass at about 0.7 billion tonnes, more than an order of magnitude higher, with over 90 per cent of it in the leaves of land plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> In the wild each molecule works far below its laboratory maximum, averaging about 0.03 reactions a second on land, roughly a hundredfold slower than in the test tube.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Across about 300 organisms its speed and its ability to tell carbon dioxide from oxygen vary very little, which suggests the enzyme is tightly constrained by the chemistry of separating the two gases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> An older argument held that every RuBisCO is nearly perfectly tuned to its surroundings, but the larger data set no longer supports that claim, and a model fitted to it sets no fixed ceiling on its speed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Xiaoxiao Shi and Arnold Bloom, “Photorespiration: The Futile Cycle?,” <i>Plants</i> 10, no. 5 (2021): 908, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8147352/. [Open access]","Avi I. Flamholz et al., “Revisiting Trade-offs between Rubisco Kinetic Parameters,” <i>Biochemistry</i> 58, no. 31 (2019): 3365–76, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6686151/. [Open access]","Sarah Bierbaumer et al., “Enzymatic Conversion of CO2: From Natural to Artificial Utilization,” <i>Chemical Reviews</i> 123, no. 9 (2023), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10176493/. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.6 Photosynthesis,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-6-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Yinon M. Bar-On and Ron Milo, “The Global Mass and Average Rate of Rubisco,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 116, no. 10 (2019): 4738–43, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410859/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do leaves contain so much of this one enzyme?","a":"Because each molecule is slow, typically one to ten reactions a second against about eighty for an ordinary metabolic enzyme, and it loses time reacting with oxygen,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> so leaves hold it in bulk, a fifth to a half of their soluble protein.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why was the old 'most abundant protein' figure revised?","a":"Because it came from a single paragraph of a longer paper and gave about 0.04 billion tonnes; a systematic estimate built from the global leaf mass and RuBisCO's share of it reached about 0.7 billion tonnes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does RuBisCO react with oxygen at all?","a":"Because carbon dioxide and oxygen both attack the same activated form of ribulose bisphosphate at its active site, and across hundreds of species the enzyme's ability to tell them apart varies very little,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> so about a fifth of its reactions use oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"RuBisCO"},
    "bio-190": {"questions":["Hot weather increases <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> in crops such as wheat and rice, because warm leaves hold relatively less carbon dioxide and RuBisCO's preference shifts towards oxygen.","Shorter synthetic bypasses installed in tobacco chloroplasts to reduce the cost of <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> raised biomass by roughly a fifth to over a third in replicated field trials."],"abstract":"<b>Photorespiration</b> is the pathway that begins when RuBisCO adds oxygen instead of carbon dioxide to ribulose bisphosphate, producing one molecule of 3-phosphoglycerate and one of 2-phosphoglycolate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The 2-phosphoglycolate inhibits enzymes of photosynthesis, so it is converted to glycolate and passed through two other organelles, the peroxisome and the mitochondrion, before glycerate returns to the chloroplast to rejoin the Calvin cycle.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> For every two molecules of 2-phosphoglycolate recycled, one molecule of carbon dioxide and one of ammonium are released, and ATP and reducing power are consumed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Oxygenation is common because a leaf holds about 25 times as much dissolved oxygen as carbon dioxide, so normally RuBisCO carboxylates only three or four times for each oxygenation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Heat makes it worse, because a warm leaf holds less carbon dioxide relative to oxygen and RuBisCO's affinity and maximum rate both shift in favour of the oxygen reaction.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> Photorespiration is usually treated as waste, and estimates put its cost at a loss of 20 to 50 per cent of the yield of C3 crops such as soybean, rice and wheat, depending on the growing temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> It is also necessary, since it disposes of a compound toxic to photosynthetic enzymes, and nitrogen assimilation, one-carbon metabolism and sulphur assimilation all depend on it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The costly step is the oxygenation itself, which no known RuBisCO excludes, because the enzyme's ability to tell carbon dioxide from oxygen varies very little across species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Synthetic shortcuts for glycolate installed in tobacco chloroplasts, with export to the native route blocked, raised biomass by 19 to 37 per cent in replicated field trials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Nearly every photosynthetic lineage has instead evolved some way of concentrating carbon dioxide around RuBisCO, which favours carboxylation over oxygenation and so keeps photorespiration low.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>","sources":["Matthew P. Johnson, “Photosynthesis,” <i>Essays in Biochemistry</i> 60, no. 3 (2016): 255–73, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5264509/. [Open access]","Xiaoxiao Shi and Arnold Bloom, “Photorespiration: The Futile Cycle?,” <i>Plants</i> 10, no. 5 (2021): 908, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8147352/. [Open access]","Roberta Croce et al., “Perspectives on Improving Photosynthesis to Increase Crop Yield,” <i>The Plant Cell</i> 36, no. 10 (2024), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11449117/. [Open access]","Sarah Bierbaumer et al., “Enzymatic Conversion of CO2: From Natural to Artificial Utilization,” <i>Chemical Reviews</i> 123, no. 9 (2023), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10176493/. [Open access]","Paul F. South, Amanda P. Cavanagh, Helen W. Liu, and Donald R. Ort, “Synthetic Glycolate Metabolism Pathways Stimulate Crop Growth and Productivity in the Field,” <i>Science</i> 363, no. 6422 (2019): eaat9077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7745124/. [Open access]","Avi I. Flamholz et al., “Revisiting Trade-offs between Rubisco Kinetic Parameters,” <i>Biochemistry</i> 58, no. 31 (2019): 3365–76, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6686151/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does RuBisCO react with oxygen so often in a leaf?","a":"Because the leaf holds about 25 times as much dissolved oxygen as carbon dioxide,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> and no known RuBisCO tells the two gases fully apart,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> so normally only three or four carboxylations occur for each oxygenation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Is photorespiration simply wasteful?","a":"Not entirely. It removes 2-phosphoglycolate, a compound toxic to photosynthetic enzymes, and essential processes such as nitrogen assimilation, one-carbon metabolism and sulphur assimilation depend on it, so it may be less wasteful than once believed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do hot days make photorespiration worse?","a":"Because rising temperature lowers the carbon dioxide concentration in the leaf relative to oxygen, reduces RuBisCO's affinity for carbon dioxide compared with oxygen, and speeds up oxygenation relative to carboxylation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Photorespiration"},
    "bio-191": {"questions":["Having evolved independently more than sixty times from C3 ancestors, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is one of the most striking cases of convergence in plants, often reached through an intermediate C2 stage.","Long read as a desert adaptation, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> was in grasses favoured first by open, sunny habitats rather than by dry ones, with the move into arid ground coming later."],"abstract":"<b>C4 photosynthesis</b> is a way of fixing carbon in which carbon dioxide is first captured in a four-carbon acid and then released again around the enzyme RuBisCO, so that the enzyme works in a gas far richer in carbon dioxide than the air outside the leaf.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Most land plants use the C3 pathway instead, whose efficiency is cut by photorespiration, the wasteful reaction of RuBisCO with oxygen, which grows worse at high temperature and low carbon dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In most C4 plants the work is split between two kinds of cell, mesophyll cells that fix carbon dioxide with the enzyme PEP carboxylase and bundle-sheath cells around the veins, where RuBisCO is kept.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Seen in cross-section, the bundle-sheath cells form a wreath-like ring around each vein, a pattern called Kranz anatomy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The four-carbon acid diffuses into the bundle sheath and is broken down there to release carbon dioxide in high concentration, and the plants differ in which enzyme does the releasing.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The pay-off is less photorespiration, better use of water and nitrogen, and the potential for higher rates of photosynthesis in strong light, and maize and sugarcane are among the crops that use it.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The pathway is not a single inheritance but a convergence, having evolved independently from C3 ancestors more than 60 times among flowering plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Many lineages seem to have passed through an intermediate stage, a photorespiratory carbon pump called C2 photosynthesis, which put them on a slope towards the full C4 pathway.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> It is usually pictured as an adaptation to drought, but a comparative study of grasses found that it was favoured in open habitats regardless of water supply, and that C4 lineages only later moved into arid ground faster than C3 ones.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Its grasses diversified most strongly in the mid to late Miocene as open grasslands spread, while in C3 crops today photorespiration can still cut yields by 20 to 50 per cent depending on temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Colin P. Osborne and Robert P. Freckleton, “Ecological Selection Pressures for C4 Photosynthesis in the Grasses,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 276, no. 1663 (2009): 1753–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2674487/. [Open access]","Ivan Reyna-Llorens and Julian M. Hibberd, “Recruitment of Pre-Existing Networks during the Evolution of C4 Photosynthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 372, no. 1730 (2017): 20160386, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5566883/. [Open access]","Julia Mallmann et al., “The Role of Photorespiration during the Evolution of C4 Photosynthesis in the Genus <i>Flaveria</i>,” <i>eLife</i> 3 (2014): e02478, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4103682/. [Open access]","Yu Wang, Andrea Bräutigam, Andreas P. M. Weber, and Xin-Guang Zhu, “Three Distinct Biochemical Subtypes of C4 Photosynthesis? A Modelling Analysis,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 65, no. 13 (2014): 3567–78, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4085956/. [Open access]","Elizabeth L. Spriggs, Pascal-Antoine Christin, and Erika J. Edwards, “C4 Photosynthesis Promoted Species Diversification during the Miocene Grassland Expansion,” <i>PLoS ONE</i> 9, no. 5 (2014): e97722, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4023962/. [Open access]","Paul F. South, Amanda P. Cavanagh, Helen W. Liu, and Donald R. Ort, “Synthetic Glycolate Metabolism Pathways Stimulate Crop Growth and Productivity in the Field,” <i>Science</i> 363, no. 6422 (2019): eaat9077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7745124/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does concentrating carbon dioxide around RuBisCO help the plant?","a":"Because RuBisCO also reacts with oxygen, and this photorespiration wastes carbon and grows worse when it is hot or carbon dioxide is scarce.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Bathing the enzyme in carbon dioxide suppresses the oxygen reaction, and it also improves the plant's use of water and nitrogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does C4 photosynthesis need two different kinds of cell?","a":"Because capture and fixation are kept apart. PEP carboxylase in the mesophyll makes a four-carbon acid that moves into the bundle sheath, where RuBisCO sits and the acid is broken down to release concentrated carbon dioxide.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it misleading to call C4 simply a drought adaptation?","a":"Because across the grasses its origins track open habitats rather than dry ones, and C4 lineages moved into arid habitats faster only afterwards, which makes drought tolerance a later consequence rather than the first cause.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"C4 carbon fixation"},
    "bio-192": {"questions":["Named after the succulent family in which it was first discovered, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> is now known from over 400 genera across 36 families of vascular plants.","Pineapple, agave and vanilla all owe their high water-use efficiency to <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, which is also run by about half of all orchid and bromeliad species."],"abstract":"<b>Crassulacean acid metabolism</b> is a mode of photosynthesis in which a plant opens its stomata to take in carbon dioxide at night and keeps them closed through the heat of the day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> At night the enzyme PEP carboxylase fixes the incoming carbon dioxide into oxaloacetate, which is converted to malic acid and stored in the vacuoles of the leaf cells until dawn.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> By day the malic acid is broken down again, and the carbon dioxide it releases is fixed a second time by RuBisCO in the ordinary C3 way while the stomata stay shut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Gas exchange in the cooler night costs much less water, so plants such as cacti can gather carbon dioxide after dark and photosynthesise by day without opening their pores.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The name comes from the Crassulaceae, the succulent family in which the pathway was first discovered.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The picture of it as a desert cactus trick is far too narrow, because it occurs in over 400 genera across 36 families of vascular plants and evolved independently many times.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> About half of all orchid and bromeliad species use it, many of them epiphytes perched in moist tropical forests, and some freshwater isoetids run it in submerged rosettes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Nor is it always all or nothing, since some species mix C3 and CAM, and facultative CAM plants switch on the night-time cycle only when drought or salt stresses them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Its outstanding water-use efficiency makes CAM crops such as pineapple, agave, aloe, vanilla and <i>Opuntia</i> cacti candidates for land too dry for most farming.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Engineering it into C3 crops is now a research goal, and leaf models suggest that its water saving depends strongly on daytime weather and is capped by how much acid the vacuoles can store.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Xiaohan Yang et al., “The Kalanchoë Genome Provides Insights into Convergent Evolution and Building Blocks of Crassulacean Acid Metabolism,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 8 (2017): 1899, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5711932/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.3 Using Light Energy to Make Organic Molecules,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules. [Open access]","Karolina Heyduk, “Evolution of Crassulacean Acid Metabolism in Response to the Environment: Past, Present, and Future,” <i>Plant Physiology</i> 190, no. 1 (2022): 19–30, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9434201/. [Open access]","Ulrich Lüttge, “Ability of Crassulacean Acid Metabolism Plants to Overcome Interacting Stresses in Tropical Environments,” <i>AoB PLANTS</i> 2010 (2010): plq005, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3000696/. [Open access]","Sarah C. Davis et al., “Undervalued Potential of Crassulacean Acid Metabolism for Current and Future Agricultural Production,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 70, no. 22 (2019): 6521–37, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6883259/. [Open access]","Nadine Töpfer, Thomas Braam, Sanu Shameer, R. George Ratcliffe, and Lee J. Sweetlove, “Alternative Crassulacean Acid Metabolism Modes Provide Environment-Specific Water-Saving Benefits in a Leaf Metabolic Model,” <i>The Plant Cell</i> 32, no. 12 (2020): 3689–705, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7721317/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do CAM plants open their stomata at night?","a":"Because the air is cooler then, so taking in carbon dioxide costs far less water than it would by day.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The carbon is parked as malic acid in the vacuole and handed to RuBisCO the next day while the stomata stay shut.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is CAM not just a desert adaptation?","a":"Because it turns up in over 400 genera across 36 families,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> including about half of all orchid and bromeliad species, many of them epiphytes of moist tropical forest, and even submerged freshwater isoetids.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why are researchers interested in putting CAM into crops?","a":"Because its high water-use efficiency could let crops yield on dry or marginal land,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> although leaf models show that its water saving depends strongly on daytime weather and on how much acid the vacuoles can hold.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Crassulacean acid metabolism"},
    "bio-193": {"questions":["An <i>Elodea</i> shoot whose bubbling stopped rising with brighter light was reread in 1905 as a case where carbon dioxide supply had become the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> on its photosynthesis.","For a seedling in deep forest shade, light is usually the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, so more carbon dioxide or warmth will barely raise its rate of photosynthesis until the light improves."],"abstract":"A <b>limiting factor</b> is the one condition, among the several a process depends on, whose supply sets the rate of the whole, so that improving any of the others makes no difference.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The idea was put as an axiom in 1905, that when a process depends on a number of separate factors, its rate is limited by the pace of the slowest factor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For a chloroplast at work it listed five obvious controlling factors, the carbon dioxide available, the water available, the intensity of light, the amount of chlorophyll and the temperature.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Any of these can be the limit, which is why a leaf whose stomata close on a hot, dry day photosynthesises more slowly as the carbon dioxide inside it runs down.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> In forests, light is the greatest limiting factor for the survival and growth of seedlings, and plants that need less light to break even fare better in shade.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> <br><br> The principle was aimed at the textbook habit of quoting fixed optimum temperatures, light levels and carbon dioxide levels for photosynthesis, much of which its author had found by experiment to be incorrect.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It reread an older experiment, in which the bubbling of an <i>Elodea</i> shoot stopped rising with light and held steady through a further tenfold increase, as a case where carbon dioxide had become the limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The school graph of one factor taking over sharply from another is a simplification, and modern leaf models describe three biochemical limits, the speed of RuBisCO, the regeneration of its substrate and the use of the sugars made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> Experiments that altered single enzymes of the Calvin cycle found no single limiting step, the control being shared among several steps and shifting with conditions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> At preindustrial carbon dioxide levels the investment in RuBisCO and in regenerating its substrate may have been balanced in co-limitation, but at today's levels crops appear to over-invest in RuBisCO.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>","sources":["F. F. Blackman, “Optima and Limiting Factors,” <i>Annals of Botany</i> 19, no. 74 (1905): 281, 289–90, https://archive.org/details/annalsofbotany191905balf. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Liang Shi et al., “Ectomycorrhizal Fungi Reduce the Light Compensation Point and Promote Carbon Fixation of <i>Pinus thunbergii</i> Seedlings to Adapt to Shade Environments,” <i>Mycorrhiza</i> 27, no. 8 (2017): 823–30, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5645441/. [Open access]","Mauricio Tejera-Nieves, Do Young Seong, Lucas Reist, and Berkley J. Walker, “The Dynamic Assimilation Technique Measures Photosynthetic CO2 Response Curves with Similar Fidelity to Steady-State Approaches in Half the Time,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 75, no. 10 (2024): 2819–28, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11103103/. [Open access]","Johannes Kromdijk and Stephen P. Long, “One Crop Breeding Cycle from Starvation? How Engineering Crop Photosynthesis for Rising CO2 and Temperature Could Be One Important Route to Alleviation,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 283, no. 1826 (2016): 20152578, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4810849/. [Open access]","Andrew J. Simkin, Patricia E. López-Calcagno, and Christine A. Raines, “Feeding the World: Improving Photosynthetic Efficiency for Sustainable Crop Production,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 70, no. 4 (2019): 1119–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6395887/. [Open access]","Berkley J. Walker et al., “Uncertainty in Measurements of the Photorespiratory CO2 Compensation Point and Its Impact on Models of Leaf Photosynthesis,” <i>Photosynthesis Research</i> 132, no. 3 (2017): 245–55, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5443873/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does adding carbon dioxide sometimes fail to speed up photosynthesis?","a":"Because the rate is limited by the pace of the slowest factor.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> If light or temperature is the factor in shortest supply, extra carbon dioxide cannot raise the rate until that other condition improves.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did the 1905 paper attack fixed optimum values?","a":"Because textbooks gave definite optima of temperature, light and carbon dioxide for photosynthesis, and its author had found by experiment that much of this treatment was incorrect and that giving such values was misleading.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is a single limiting factor too simple a picture of a real leaf?","a":"Because control is shared. Altering single Calvin cycle enzymes has shown no single limiting step,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> and leaf models recognise separate limits set by RuBisCO, by regenerating its substrate and by using the sugars that are made.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Limiting factor"},
    "bio-194": {"questions":["Clamped to a leaf through a small cuvette, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> calculates photosynthesis from the difference in carbon dioxide between the air flowing in and the air flowing out.","Replacing the old method of bubbling chamber air through absorbing solutions and titrating them, the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> allowed continuous and more precise monitoring of a plant's carbon dioxide exchange."],"abstract":"An <b>infrared gas analyser</b> measures the concentration of a gas such as carbon dioxide by passing infrared light through a sample and recording how much light of a particular wavelength is absorbed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Carbon dioxide absorbs strongly in a band at about 4.26 μm, so a filter in front of the detector admits only that wavelength, and the loss of light reaching it reveals the concentration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> In the common non-dispersive design, all the light passes through the sample and is filtered only just before the detector, instead of being split into a spectrum by a prism or grating.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Because few other gases absorb at that wavelength the method is selective and sensitive, and it has become the most widely used way of measuring carbon dioxide in real time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Earlier plant physiologists measured uptake by passing air from a plant chamber through absorbing solutions and titrating them, and infrared analysers brought greater precision and continuous monitoring of an air stream.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> <br><br> In photosynthesis research the analyser is joined to a cuvette clamped over a small area of a single leaf, or to whole-plant chambers sampled one after another through a gas-switching system.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Air flows in and out continuously, and the leaf's assimilation is calculated from the carbon dioxide entering and leaving, the flow rates and the leaf area.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> That calculation assumes a steady state, so each point on a carbon dioxide response curve has traditionally needed minutes to settle, while newer dynamic methods ramp the gas and finish such curves in about half the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Portable systems of this kind are carried into fields to compare crops, and chambers coupled to analysers have followed the photosynthesis and respiration of Scots pine through the seasons.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The engineered tobacco whose altered photorespiration raised its biomass in field trials was checked with such an analyser, which measured its response to carbon dioxide and its compensation point.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Sudhir Kumar Pandey and Ki-Hyun Kim, “The Relative Performance of NDIR-Based Sensors in the Near Real-Time Analysis of CO2 in Air,” <i>Sensors</i> 7, no. 9 (2007): 1683–96, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3841839/. [Open access]","Aaron J. Saathoff and Jon Welles, “Gas Exchange Measurements in the Unsteady State,” <i>Plant, Cell & Environment</i> 44, no. 11 (2021): 3509–23, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9292621/. [Open access]","Katharina Kölling et al., “A Whole-Plant Chamber System for Parallel Gas Exchange Measurements of <i>Arabidopsis</i> and Other Herbaceous Species,” <i>Plant Methods</i> 11 (2015): 48, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4609071/. [Open access]","Mauricio Tejera-Nieves, Do Young Seong, Lucas Reist, and Berkley J. Walker, “The Dynamic Assimilation Technique Measures Photosynthetic CO2 Response Curves with Similar Fidelity to Steady-State Approaches in Half the Time,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 75, no. 10 (2024): 2819–28, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11103103/. [Open access]","Pasi Kolari et al., “Field and Controlled Environment Measurements Show Strong Seasonal Acclimation in Photosynthesis and Respiration Potential in Boreal Scots Pine,” <i>Frontiers in Plant Science</i> 5 (2014): 717, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4264410/. [Open access]","Paul F. South, Amanda P. Cavanagh, Helen W. Liu, and Donald R. Ort, “Synthetic Glycolate Metabolism Pathways Stimulate Crop Growth and Productivity in the Field,” <i>Science</i> 363, no. 6422 (2019): eaat9077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7745124/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is infrared light suitable for measuring carbon dioxide?","a":"Because carbon dioxide absorbs strongly in a narrow band near 4.26 micrometres where other gases absorb little, so the loss of light at that wavelength tracks its concentration selectively and sensitively.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why must a leaf chamber measure the air both entering and leaving?","a":"Because assimilation is calculated from the difference, the carbon dioxide carried in minus that carried out, scaled by the flow rates and divided by the area of leaf enclosed.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did steady-state measurement make response curves slow?","a":"Because the standard calculation assumes the chamber's carbon dioxide is stable, so each point needed minutes to settle;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> ramping the gas and correcting for the change gives curves of similar quality in about half the time.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Infrared gas analyzer"},
    "bio-195": {"questions":["Splitting water and fixing nitrogen in heterocysts, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> began the oxygenation of Earth's atmosphere long before any land plant existed.","Securely fossilised as filaments by about 2.7 billion years ago, <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may have made oxygen in local oases for roughly a billion years before the air itself turned oxic."],"abstract":"<b>Cyanobacteria</b> are a large and diverse phylum of bacteria that carry out oxygenic photosynthesis, using the same chlorophyll as plants and algae and releasing oxygen as a by-product of using water as their source of hydrogen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Their blue-green colour comes from accessory pigments such as phycocyanin, held on folded internal membranes called thylakoids that closely resemble the photosynthetic membranes of plants.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> They live as single cells, colonies or filaments in seas, lakes, soil and even inside rocks, and many fix atmospheric nitrogen in specialised cells called heterocysts.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Their old name 'blue-green algae' is a misnomer, because they are bacteria, related to eukaryotic algae only through the chloroplast that the algae inherited from them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> That chloroplast is a former cyanobacterium, taken into an ancestral heterotrophic eukaryote by primary endosymbiosis, which is why plastids of primary origin have two membranes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> <br><br> The ancestors of today's cyanobacteria began the oxygenation of Earth's atmosphere, and the rapid rise of oxygen at the end of the Archaean is attributed to them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> When they first appeared is much harder to say, since Precambrian fossils are scarce, offer few features for identification and are poorly preserved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> An older museum account calls them the oldest known fossils, at 3.5 billion years, but the biological origin of the most ancient claimed cyanobacterium-like fossils has since been effectively challenged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Filamentous forms are securely recorded by about 2.7 billion years ago, and one gene-based estimate puts the origin of the group, and so of oxygenic photosynthesis, at about 3.6 billion years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> If so, oxygen was made locally for something like a billion years before it filled the air, an era of 'oxygen oases', and genome-scale evidence suggests multicellular filaments had evolved before the oxygenation.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “4.3 Nonproteobacteria Gram-Negative Bacteria and Phototrophic Bacteria,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/4-3-nonproteobacteria-gram-negative-bacteria-and-phototrophic-bacteria. [Open access]","Bettina E. Schirrmeister, Muriel Gugger, and Philip C. J. Donoghue, “Cyanobacteria and the Great Oxidation Event: Evidence from Genes and Fossils,” <i>Palaeontology</i> 58, no. 5 (2015): 769–85, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4755140/. [Open access]","Ferran Garcia-Pichel et al., “Timing the Evolutionary Advent of Cyanobacteria and the Later Great Oxidation Event Using Gene Phylogenies of a Sunscreen,” <i>mBio</i> 10, no. 3 (2019): e00561-19, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6529634/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “22.1 Prokaryotic Diversity,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-1-prokaryotic-diversity. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “23.1 Eukaryotic Origins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins. [Open access]","University of California Museum of Paleontology, “Introduction to the Cyanobacteria: Architects of Earth's Atmosphere,” https://ucmp.berkeley.edu/bacteria/cyanointro.html. [Open access]","University of California Museum of Paleontology, “Cyanobacteria: Fossil Record,” https://ucmp.berkeley.edu/bacteria/cyanofr.html. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is 'blue-green algae' a misleading name?","a":"Because cyanobacteria are bacteria, not eukaryotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> Their only kinship with algae runs through the chloroplast, which descends from a cyanobacterium taken into an ancestral eukaryotic cell.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is the origin of cyanobacteria so hard to date?","a":"Because Precambrian fossil deposits are scarce, early forms have few distinguishing features and microfossils preserve poorly,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> and the oldest claimed examples have had their biological origin challenged.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did oxygen take so long to build up after cyanobacteria appeared?","a":"Because, on one gene-based estimate, oxygenic photosynthesis arose about 3.6 billion years ago, long before the Great Oxidation Event, implying roughly a billion years in which oxygen existed only in local oases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Cyanobacteria"},
    "bio-196": {"questions":["Russian sulphur isotopes place the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> before the Palaeoproterozoic glaciation, ruling out the idea that a snowball Earth triggered the evolution of oxygen-making photosynthesis.","Produced by cyanobacteria, the oxygen of the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> formed an ozone layer and opened the way for aerobic respiration, yet its build-up was fitful and unevenly recorded worldwide."],"abstract":"The <b>Great Oxidation Event</b> was the first significant build-up of oxygen in Earth's atmosphere, which began early in the Palaeoproterozoic era, about 2.4 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Before it the atmosphere was anoxic, so the only organisms able to live were those that could grow without oxygen.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> The oxygen came from cyanobacteria, the organisms that perform oxygenic photosynthesis, which are credited with the rapid oxygenation of the air at the end of the Archaean.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> In the rocks the event is defined by sulphur, as the interval when enough oxygen had accumulated to stop sediments recording a peculiar mass-independent fractionation of sulphur isotopes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The new oxygen formed ozone, which absorbs damaging ultraviolet light, and it allowed more efficient oxygen-using pathways such as aerobic respiration to spread among prokaryotes.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> <br><br> Calling it an 'event' suggests a sudden, worldwide switch, but sulphides from Western Australia show that sulphur signal vanishing and returning several times between about 2.45 and 2.31 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Dating in southern Africa places the first global glaciation and the onset of oxygenation between about 2.46 and 2.43 billion years ago, with the build-up continuing until about 2.06 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Sulphur isotopes from north-west Russia confine the event there to between 2.50 and 2.43 billion years ago, before the glaciation, ruling out ideas in which a snowball Earth caused the evolution of oxygenic photosynthesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Models of the atmosphere show that large swings in oxygen were possible during the rise, so the change may have been fitful rather than abrupt and permanent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Cyanobacteria are probably far older than the event, which implies a long era in which oxygen existed only in local 'oases', and oxygen levels like today's arose only within the last 700 million years.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup>","sources":["Ashley P. Gumsley et al., “Timing and Tempo of the Great Oxidation Event,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 114, no. 8 (2017): 1811–16, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5338422/. [Open access]","Matthew R. Warke et al., “The Great Oxidation Event Preceded a Paleoproterozoic “Snowball Earth”,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 117, no. 24 (2020): 13314–20, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7306805/. [Open access]","Pascal Philippot et al., “Globally Asynchronous Sulphur Isotope Signals Require Re-Definition of the Great Oxidation Event,” <i>Nature Communications</i> 9 (2018): 2245, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5993798/. [Open access]","Nicholas F. Wogan, David C. Catling, Kevin J. Zahnle, and Mark W. Claire, “Rapid Timescale for an Oxic Transition during the Great Oxidation Event and the Instability of Low Atmospheric O2,” <i>Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences</i> 119, no. 37 (2022): e2205618119, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9477391/. [Open access]","Bettina E. Schirrmeister, Muriel Gugger, and Philip C. J. Donoghue, “Cyanobacteria and the Great Oxidation Event: Evidence from Genes and Fossils,” <i>Palaeontology</i> 58, no. 5 (2015): 769–85, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4755140/. [Open access]","Ferran Garcia-Pichel et al., “Timing the Evolutionary Advent of Cyanobacteria and the Later Great Oxidation Event Using Gene Phylogenies of a Sunscreen,” <i>mBio</i> 10, no. 3 (2019): e00561-19, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6529634/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “22.1 Prokaryotic Diversity,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-1-prokaryotic-diversity. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “23.1 Eukaryotic Origins,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why do sulphur isotopes reveal when oxygen rose?","a":"Because the event is defined as the interval when enough oxygen had accumulated to stop the generation and preservation of mass-independent fractionation of sulphur isotopes in sedimentary rocks, so the signal's disappearance marks the change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is 'event' a misleading word for it?","a":"Because Western Australian sulphides show the signal disappearing and returning several times between about 2.45 and 2.31 billion years ago,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> atmospheric models allow large swings of oxygen during the rise,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> and the build-up continued until about 2.06 billion years ago.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why did oxygen not build up as soon as cyanobacteria evolved?","a":"Because on gene-based estimates cyanobacteria are far older than the event, so for perhaps a billion years oxygen existed only locally, in 'oases', before it accumulated in the atmosphere.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Great Oxidation Event"},
    "bio-197": {"questions":["The tubeworm <i>Riftia</i>, which lacks any digestive system, depends entirely on symbiotic bacteria in its trophosome, whose <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> turns the energy of sulphide into the worm's food.","In Antarctic desert soils some bacteria carry out atmospheric <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, fixing carbon with energy from trace hydrogen and carbon monoxide drawn straight from the air."],"abstract":"<b>Chemosynthesis</b> is the making of organic compounds from carbon dioxide or methane with energy released by oxidising inorganic chemicals, instead of energy from light.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The organisms that live this way are chemoautotrophs, mostly bacteria, among them hydrogen-, sulphur-, iron-, nitrogen- and carbon monoxide-oxidising species.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Their energy comes from inorganic compounds such as hydrogen sulphide and reduced iron, a way of life called lithotrophy, from the Greek for ‘rock’, which is unique to the microbial world.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Like plants they are autotrophs, but where photoautotrophs take their energy from sunlight, chemoautotrophs take it from inorganic molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Energy therefore enters living systems by two routes, as sunlight for phototrophs or as inorganic molecules for chemoautotrophs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup> <br><br> The best-known chemosynthetic ecosystems are at hydrothermal vents on the deep-sea floor, where no sunlight penetrates and bacteria use hydrogen sulphide from the vents as a source of chemical energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> There the dominant animals all depend on chemosynthetic bacteria, which account for almost all primary production, and many carry the bacteria inside their bodies, where the symbionts supply most of the host's organic carbon.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> The giant tubeworm <i>Riftia pachyptila</i> has no digestive system and relies completely on symbionts packed into an organ called the trophosome, yet its tube grows by more than 85 centimetres (33 inches) a year.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> The picture of vents as the only home of chemosynthesis is too narrow, since symbioses of the same kind are found at cold seeps, in seagrass beds and on whale falls.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Even cold desert soils in Antarctica, the Arctic and Tibet hold bacteria that fix carbon with energy from trace hydrogen and carbon monoxide in the air, a process called atmospheric chemosynthesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “46.2 Energy Flow through Ecosystems,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-2-energy-flow-through-ecosystems. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.1 Energy, Matter, and Enzymes,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-1-energy-matter-and-enzymes. [Open access]","Guus Roeselers and Irene L. G. Newton, “On the Evolutionary Ecology of Symbioses between Chemosynthetic Bacteria and Bivalves,” <i>Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology</i> 94, no. 1 (2012): 1–10, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3304057/. [Open access]","Tjorven Hinzke et al., “Host-Microbe Interactions in the Chemosynthetic <i>Riftia pachyptila</i> Symbiosis,” <i>mBio</i> 10, no. 6 (2019): e02243-19, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6918071/. [Open access]","Jillian M. Petersen et al., “Chemosynthetic Symbionts of Marine Invertebrate Animals Are Capable of Nitrogen Fixation,” <i>Nature Microbiology</i> 2 (2016): 16195, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6872982/. [Open access]","Angelique E. Ray et al., “Atmospheric Chemosynthesis Is Phylogenetically and Geographically Widespread and Contributes Significantly to Carbon Fixation throughout Cold Deserts,” <i>The ISME Journal</i> 16, no. 11 (2022): 2547–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9561532/. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.7 Biogeochemical Cycles,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-7-biogeochemical-cycles. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why can chemosynthesis support life where there is no light?","a":"Because chemoautotrophs take their energy from inorganic molecules such as hydrogen sulphide rather than from sunlight, and use it to build organic molecules that also feed the rest of the ecosystem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does <i>Riftia</i> need no digestive system?","a":"Because its bacterial symbionts, packed into the trophosome, oxidise sulphide for energy and fix carbon, and the worm relies completely on them for nutrition;<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> the host in turn supplies the sulphide and oxygen the bacteria use.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it wrong to think chemosynthesis happens only at vents?","a":"Because chemosynthetic symbioses are also found at cold seeps, in seagrass beds and on whale falls,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and cold desert soils hold bacteria that fix carbon using trace gases from the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Chemosynthesis"},
    "bio-198": {"questions":["With the harvest index of rice and wheat near its biological limit, breeders now look to raising <span class=\"blank\">_____</span>, the share of absorbed sunlight that a crop converts into biomass.","Because photorespiration can cut C3 yields by a fifth to a half, engineering a shortcut around it raised the <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> and field biomass of tobacco."],"abstract":"<b>Photosynthetic efficiency</b> is the fraction of the light energy reaching a plant that ends up stored as chemical energy in its biomass.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> For plants that fix carbon dioxide only through the Calvin cycle and RuBisCO, the theoretical maximum is about 4.6 per cent, and in the field less than half of that is achieved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plants with the C4 pathway have a higher potential efficiency, because their carbon-concentrating mechanism largely suppresses photorespiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> In C3 plants photorespiration releases carbon at roughly a quarter of the rate of gross carbon dioxide fixation, which lowers the efficiency with which absorbed light is used.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> RuBisCO is also slow, so leaves must invest large amounts of nitrogen in the enzyme to keep up reasonable rates of photosynthesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> <br><br> For food this matters because, of the four things that set a crop's yield potential, light availability, light capture, energy conversion and plant architecture, only energy conversion is still below its potential maximum in rice, wheat and maize.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The gains of the Green Revolution came largely from raising the harvest index, the share of a plant's biomass that goes into the grain, which is now close to its biological limit.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Global crop productivity rose by 135 per cent in the 45 years after 1960, yet the conversion of absorbed sunlight into biomass was barely improved over the same period.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Photorespiration can cut C3 crop yields by 20 to 50 per cent depending on temperature, and tobacco engineered with a synthetic route around it gained 19 to 37 per cent in biomass in replicated field trials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Results like these have made photosynthetic efficiency a breeding objective in its own right, though whether similar gains will carry over into grain crops has yet to be shown.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>","sources":["Andrew J. Simkin, Patricia E. López-Calcagno, and Christine A. Raines, “Feeding the World: Improving Photosynthetic Efficiency for Sustainable Crop Production,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 70, no. 4 (2019): 1119–40, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6395887/. [Open access]","Paul F. South, Amanda P. Cavanagh, Helen W. Liu, and Donald R. Ort, “Synthetic Glycolate Metabolism Pathways Stimulate Crop Growth and Productivity in the Field,” <i>Science</i> 363, no. 6422 (2019): eaat9077, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7745124/. [Open access]","Johannes Kromdijk and Stephen P. Long, “One Crop Breeding Cycle from Starvation? How Engineering Crop Photosynthesis for Rising CO2 and Temperature Could Be One Important Route to Alleviation,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 283, no. 1826 (2016): 20152578, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4810849/. [Open access]","Yu Wang, Andrea Bräutigam, Andreas P. M. Weber, and Xin-Guang Zhu, “Three Distinct Biochemical Subtypes of C4 Photosynthesis? A Modelling Analysis,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 65, no. 13 (2014): 3567–78, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4085956/. [Open access]","Berkley J. Walker and Asaph B. Cousins, “Influence of Temperature on Measurements of the CO2 Compensation Point: Differences between the Laisk and O2-Exchange Methods,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 64, no. 7 (2013): 1893–905, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3638825/. [Open access]","Ivan Reyna-Llorens and Julian M. Hibberd, “Recruitment of Pre-Existing Networks during the Evolution of C4 Photosynthesis,” <i>Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society B</i> 372, no. 1730 (2017): 20160386, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5566883/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is the theoretical maximum efficiency of photosynthesis so low?","a":"Because energy is lost along the way. In C3 plants photorespiration alone releases carbon at about a quarter of the rate of gross fixation,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> and RuBisCO's slowness forces a heavy investment of nitrogen in the enzyme.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup> The ceiling for C3 plants is about 4.6 per cent.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can breeders no longer rely on the harvest index?","a":"Because the Green Revolution already pushed the share of biomass going into grain close to its biological limit, while the efficiency of turning sunlight into biomass was barely improved.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does photorespiration make an attractive target for engineering?","a":"Because it can cut C3 yields by 20 to 50 per cent, and tobacco given synthetic glycolate pathways raised its photosynthetic quantum yield by 20 per cent and its biomass by 19 to 37 per cent in field trials.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Photosynthetic efficiency"},
    "bio-199": {"questions":["Pine seedlings partnered with root fungi broke even in much dimmer light, their <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> falling from about 200 to 40–70 micromoles of photons per square metre per second.","Written Γ* when day respiration is excluded, the carbon dioxide <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> marks the concentration at which RuBisCO's fixation is cancelled by the carbon released through photorespiration."],"abstract":"A <b>compensation point</b> is a condition at which a plant's photosynthesis exactly balances its respiration, so that its net exchange of carbon dioxide with the air is zero.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> The light compensation point is the light intensity at which the photosynthetic rate of leaves equals their respiration rate, so below it a leaf loses more carbon than it gains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> It is read from a light response curve as the point where net photosynthesis crosses zero, and above it net photosynthesis rises as the light increases.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> The carbon dioxide compensation point is the matching idea for the gas, the concentration at which carbon gained through RuBisCO is cancelled by carbon lost through respiration and photorespiration.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> With day respiration left out of the balance it is written Γ*, a key parameter in the models used to predict how leaves will respond to climate change.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> <br><br> The comparison it captures is often taught as if plants photosynthesise by day and respire only at night, when in fact every living thing, plants included, must break carbohydrate down by respiration to power its life functions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> The two processes run as a cycle, photosynthesis building carbohydrate in chloroplasts and aerobic respiration releasing its energy in the cytoplasm and mitochondria, and both use electron transport chains.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> A low light compensation point is a key trait for seedlings in shade, and pine seedlings with partner fungi on their roots broke even at 40 to 70 micromoles of photons per square metre per second, against about 200 without them.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> Plants with the C4 pathway draw the carbon dioxide inside the leaf down to lower concentrations than C3 plants do, and carbon dioxide compensation points have been used to place species along the evolutionary path from C3 to C4.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Measuring Γ* is not simple, since different gas-exchange and laboratory methods give values that diverge as temperature changes, which in turn alters modelled photosynthesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>","sources":["Liang Shi et al., “Ectomycorrhizal Fungi Reduce the Light Compensation Point and Promote Carbon Fixation of <i>Pinus thunbergii</i> Seedlings to Adapt to Shade Environments,” <i>Mycorrhiza</i> 27, no. 8 (2017): 823–30, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5645441/. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.3 Using Light Energy to Make Organic Molecules,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules. [Open access]","Berkley J. Walker and Asaph B. Cousins, “Influence of Temperature on Measurements of the CO2 Compensation Point: Differences between the Laisk and O2-Exchange Methods,” <i>Journal of Experimental Botany</i> 64, no. 7 (2013): 1893–905, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3638825/. [Open access]","Berkley J. Walker et al., “Uncertainty in Measurements of the Photorespiratory CO2 Compensation Point and Its Impact on Models of Leaf Photosynthesis,” <i>Photosynthesis Research</i> 132, no. 3 (2017): 245–55, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5443873/. [Open access]","Julia Mallmann et al., “The Role of Photorespiration during the Evolution of C4 Photosynthesis in the Genus <i>Flaveria</i>,” <i>eLife</i> 3 (2014): e02478, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4103682/. [Open access]","Colin P. Osborne and Robert P. Freckleton, “Ecological Selection Pressures for C4 Photosynthesis in the Grasses,” <i>Proceedings of the Royal Society B</i> 276, no. 1663 (2009): 1753–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2674487/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why does a leaf below its light compensation point lose carbon?","a":"Because respiration goes on in the light as well.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Below the compensation point photosynthesis cannot keep pace with the carbon dioxide that respiration releases, so the leaf's net exchange turns negative.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why is it wrong to say plants respire only at night?","a":"Because all living things, plants included, get their energy by breaking down carbohydrate in respiration; plants make carbohydrate by photosynthesis but must still break it down to power their life functions.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why does the carbon dioxide compensation point matter to climate modelling?","a":"Because Γ* links RuBisCO's kinetics to the carbon released by photorespiration, making it a key parameter in models that predict leaf carbon dioxide exchange,<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> and its measured temperature response differs between methods.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Compensation point"},
    "bio-200": {"questions":["Named from Greek words meaning ‘self-feeder’, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> stands at the base of every food chain, since even a wolf's energy traces back through deer to plants.","Defined by its carbon source rather than its energy source, an <span class=\"blank\">_____</span> may live in a lightless vent or Antarctic soil as long as it fixes inorganic carbon."],"abstract":"An <b>autotroph</b> is an organism that can produce its own food, and the Greek roots of the word mean ‘self’ and ‘feeder’.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> More exactly, autotrophs convert inorganic carbon dioxide into organic carbon compounds, while heterotrophs rely on more complex organic compounds first made by autotrophs.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"5\"></sup> Photoautotrophs, such as plants, algae and cyanobacteria, use sunlight as their energy source, whereas chemoautotrophs use the energy of inorganic molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup> Autotrophs are critical for all ecosystems, since without them energy would not be available to other living organisms and life itself would not be possible.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Photosynthesis powers 99 per cent of Earth's ecosystems, and even a wolf eating a deer is at the end of an energy path that runs back through the plants the deer ate.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"4\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup> <br><br> The common shorthand that an autotroph is an organism that uses sunlight is wrong, because the prefix refers to the source of carbon, not the source of energy.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chemoautotrophs fix carbon with no light at all, using hydrogen, sulphur, iron, nitrogen or carbon monoxide compounds, while photoheterotrophs such as the green and purple non-sulphur bacteria use light yet take their carbon from organic compounds.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup> Chemoautotrophs are mainly bacteria of places sunlight does not reach, such as dark caves and hydrothermal vents on the ocean floor, where many use hydrogen sulphide released from the vents and in turn supply energy to the rest of the ecosystem.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup> Some live inside animals, converting carbon dioxide or methane into organic compounds for their hosts, and others in cold desert soils draw their energy from trace gases in the air.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"7\"></sup><sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"8\"></sup> Carbon passes between autotrophs and heterotrophs mainly as atmospheric carbon dioxide, the fully oxidised form that autotrophs build into glucose and other energy-rich molecules.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"6\"></sup>","sources":["Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “5.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/5-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “46.2 Energy Flow through Ecosystems,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-2-energy-flow-through-ecosystems. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.1 Energy, Matter, and Enzymes,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-1-energy-matter-and-enzymes. [Open access]","Mary Ann Clark, Matthew Douglas, and Jung Choi, “8.1 Overview of Photosynthesis,” in <i>Biology 2e</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2018), https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis. [Open access]","Samantha Fowler, Rebecca Roush, and James Wise, “20.1 Energy Flow through Ecosystems,” in <i>Concepts of Biology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2013), https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/20-1-energy-flow-through-ecosystems. [Open access]","Nina Parker, Mark Schneegurt, Anh-Hue Thi Tu, Philip Lister, and Brian M. Forster, “8.7 Biogeochemical Cycles,” in <i>Microbiology</i> (Houston: OpenStax, Rice University, 2016), https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/8-7-biogeochemical-cycles. [Open access]","Guus Roeselers and Irene L. G. Newton, “On the Evolutionary Ecology of Symbioses between Chemosynthetic Bacteria and Bivalves,” <i>Applied Microbiology and Biotechnology</i> 94, no. 1 (2012): 1–10, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3304057/. [Open access]","Angelique E. Ray et al., “Atmospheric Chemosynthesis Is Phylogenetically and Geographically Widespread and Contributes Significantly to Carbon Fixation throughout Cold Deserts,” <i>The ISME Journal</i> 16, no. 11 (2022): 2547–60, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9561532/. [Open access]"],"why":[{"q":"Why is 'an organism that uses sunlight' a poor definition of an autotroph?","a":"Because the prefix refers to the carbon source. Chemoautotrophs fix carbon without light, and photoheterotrophs use light but take carbon from organic compounds, so the energy source and the carbon source must be judged separately.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"3\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why do heterotrophs depend on autotrophs even when they eat other animals?","a":"Because the food of every heterotroph traces back to autotrophs; a wolf eating a deer gets energy that the deer took from plants, which captured it by photosynthesis.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"1\"></sup>"},{"q":"Why can some ecosystems survive without any sunlight?","a":"Because chemoautotrophs at hydrothermal vents and in dark caves use inorganic molecules such as hydrogen sulphide as their energy source, building organic molecules that feed the rest of the community.<sup class=\"fn\" data-fn=\"2\"></sup>"}],"wiki":"Autotroph"}
  };
  (window.CARD_EXTRA_IN = window.CARD_EXTRA_IN || []).push({ CARD_EXTRA: CARD_EXTRA });
})();
